window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"RE-1":{"unit":"RE-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01","title":"Reproductive, Placental, and Breast Embryology","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows one bipotential gonad, two pairs of ducts, and one set of external primordia as they become male or female organs, then covers descent of the testis, formation of the placenta and umbilical cord, and development of the breast. Board questions usually give a karyotype, a missing hormone or enzyme, or a malformation and ask you to predict the anatomy. The reasoning rests on a short chain: SRY decides the gonad, the gonad's hormones decide the ducts and genitalia, and each embryonic step that fails leaves a predictable defect. Learn which cell makes which signal and which structure that signal builds or removes.","prerequisites":["Germ layers and the intermediate mesoderm (RE-3)","Formation of the mesonephros and ureteric bud (UR-1)","Implantation and the first two weeks of development (RE-3)","Steroid hormone synthesis and the androgen receptor (RE-8)","Layers of the anterior abdominal wall"],"connections":[{"to":"Urinary embryology (UR-1)","how":"The mesonephric duct guides the paramesonephric duct and gives off the ureteric bud, so renal and Mullerian anomalies often occur together."},{"to":"Reproductive hormone biochemistry (RE-8)","how":"Defects in steroidogenesis, 5-alpha-reductase, or the androgen receptor alter external and internal anatomy in predictable ways."},{"to":"Reproductive genetics (RE-10)","how":"Sex chromosome aneuploidy and SRY translocation or deletion explain most disorders of gonadal development."},{"to":"Congenital reproductive diseases (RE-13-C) and placental disease (RE-13-H)","how":"Hypospadias, cryptorchidism, uterine anomalies, and abnormal placentation are the clinical forms of the steps taught here."},{"to":"Inguinal anatomy (RE-4, RE-5)","how":"The path of testicular descent creates the inguinal canal, the spermatic cord coverings, and the gonadal blood supply and lymph drainage."}],"terms":[{"term":"Bipotential (indifferent) gonad","def":"The early gonad, before about week 7, whose supporting cells can still become either Sertoli or granulosa cells."},{"term":"SRY","def":"Sex-determining region Y, a gene on the short arm of the Y chromosome encoding the transcription factor that starts testis development."},{"term":"Sertoli cell","def":"The supporting cell of the testis cords, which organizes the testis and secretes anti-Mullerian hormone."},{"term":"Anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH)","def":"A TGF-beta family glycoprotein from Sertoli cells that causes regression of the paramesonephric ducts."},{"term":"Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct","def":"The duct of the embryonic kidney, which androgens convert into the epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicle, and ejaculatory duct."},{"term":"Paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct","def":"A duct formed beside the mesonephric duct that becomes the uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina when AMH is absent."},{"term":"5-alpha-reductase type 2","def":"The enzyme in genital skin and the urogenital sinus that converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT)."},{"term":"Gubernaculum","def":"A mesenchymal cord from the lower pole of the gonad to the labioscrotal swelling that guides testicular descent."},{"term":"Processus vaginalis","def":"An outpouching of peritoneum that precedes the testis through the inguinal canal and normally closes after descent."},{"term":"Chorion frondosum","def":"The villous part of the chorion facing the decidua basalis, which forms the fetal part of the placenta."},{"term":"Decidua basalis","def":"The endometrium beneath the implantation site, which forms the maternal part of the placenta."},{"term":"Mammary ridge","def":"A band of thickened surface ectoderm from axilla to groin on each side, from which the breast develops."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C01","obj":"RE-1.1","title":"SRY and determination of the gonad","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The gonad begins in week 5 as the genital (gonadal) ridge, a thickening of coelomic epithelium and underlying intermediate mesoderm on the medial side of the mesonephros. Primordial germ cells, which arise in the epiblast and gather in the yolk sac wall, migrate along the dorsal mesentery of the hindgut and enter the ridges in week 6. Epithelial cells proliferate into primitive sex cords around the germ cells. Until about week 7 the gonad is bipotential: its supporting cells can become Sertoli cells or granulosa cells, and both duct pairs are present."},{"type":"p","text":"SRY (sex-determining region Y) lies on the short arm of the Y chromosome, near the boundary of the pseudoautosomal region. It encodes a transcription factor with an HMG DNA-binding domain, also called testis-determining factor. SRY acts in the supporting cells at about week 7, where it switches on SOX9. SOX9 then maintains its own expression through a positive feedback loop involving FGF9 and prostaglandin D2, which is why the gonad stays committed once SOX9 is established. SOX9-positive cells become Sertoli cells, which enclose the germ cells in testis (seminiferous) cords, a dense tunica albuginea forms beneath the surface, and Sertoli cell signals induce interstitial mesenchyme to become Leydig cells. The Sertoli cell is the organizer of the testis, and every later male step depends on it."},{"type":"p","text":"Without SRY, the supporting cells become granulosa cells, the cords break into clusters around oogonia in the cortex, and an ovary forms. The ovary is often called the default pathway, but it is actively maintained by WNT4, RSPO1 with beta-catenin, and FOXL2, which repress SOX9. Ovarian maintenance also needs two X chromosomes: in 45,X Turner syndrome oocytes are lost by apoptosis and the ovaries become fibrous streaks."},{"type":"p","text":"Prediction follows one rule: SRY decides the gonad, whatever the rest of the karyotype. A 46,XY embryo with an SRY deletion or inactivating mutation forms streak gonads (complete gonadal dysgenesis, Swyer syndrome). Streaks make neither AMH nor testosterone, so the Mullerian ducts persist, the Wolffian ducts regress, and the external genitalia are female. Puberty does not occur because there is no ovarian estrogen, and streak gonads containing Y material carry a high risk of gonadoblastoma and dysgerminoma. A 46,XX embryo with SRY translocated to an X chromosome, usually by abnormal X and Y exchange during paternal meiosis, forms testes and a male phenotype. The testes are small and produce no sperm because the azoospermia factor (AZF) genes on the long arm of the Y are absent, and gonadotropins are high (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism)."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the gonad from SRY status","cols":["Karyotype and SRY","Gonad","Internal ducts","External genitalia","Key feature"],"rows":[["46,XY, SRY normal","Testes","Wolffian derivatives; Mullerian ducts regressed","Male","Reference pattern"],["46,XX, no SRY","Ovaries","Mullerian derivatives; Wolffian ducts regressed","Female","Reference pattern"],["46,XY, SRY deleted or mutated","Streak gonads","Uterus, tubes, upper vagina","Female","No puberty; tumor risk in Y-containing streaks"],["46,XX, SRY translocated to X","Small testes","Wolffian derivatives; no uterus","Male","Azoospermia from missing Yq AZF genes"],["47,XXY (Klinefelter)","Testes, later hyalinized","Wolffian derivatives","Male","One Y with SRY is enough to make testes"],["45,X (Turner)","Ovaries that become streaks","Mullerian derivatives","Female","Oocyte survival needs two X chromosomes"]],"caption":"The presence of SRY, not the number of X chromosomes, decides whether a testis forms. Ducts and genitalia then follow the gonad's hormones."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 46,XX man has small firm testes, no uterus, and azoospermia. Did his fetal gonads make AMH, and why is he infertile?","explain":"Yes. The translocated SRY made Sertoli cells, which secreted AMH and removed the Mullerian ducts, and Leydig cell androgens masculinized him. Sperm are absent because the AZF spermatogenesis genes on the long arm of the Y were not translocated with SRY.","check":"The SRY translocation paragraph and the karyotype table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C02","obj":"RE-1.2","title":"AMH, testosterone, and DHT: three separate male signals","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Once a testis exists, two cell types and one enzyme carry out the rest of male development. Each signal acts on a different target, so each can fail alone, and most board questions on disorders of sex development depend on that independence."},{"type":"table","title":"The three male signals","cols":["Signal","Source","Target","Result","If it fails in a 46,XY fetus"],"rows":[["AMH","Sertoli cells","Mesenchyme around the paramesonephric ducts","Mullerian regression; appendix testis remains","Uterus and tubes persist in a virilized male (persistent Mullerian duct syndrome)"],["Testosterone","Leydig cells, driven by placental hCG, later fetal LH","Mesonephric ducts, by local diffusion","Epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicle, ejaculatory duct","Wolffian ducts regress"],["DHT","Testosterone converted by 5-alpha-reductase type 2 in target tissue","Genital tubercle, urogenital folds, labioscrotal swellings, urogenital sinus","Penis, scrotum, prostate","Ambiguous or female external genitalia; small or absent prostate"]],"caption":"AMH removes, testosterone keeps, and DHT builds. Testosterone and DHT act through the same androgen receptor."},{"type":"p","text":"AMH, a TGF-beta family glycoprotein, binds AMH receptor type 2 on the mesenchyme surrounding each paramesonephric duct, which triggers apoptosis of the duct epithelium. The duct responds only during a short window, about weeks 8 to 10, so AMH arriving later cannot remove it. Testosterone stabilizes the mesonephric duct by acting locally: it diffuses from the testis to the duct on the same side at a concentration far above plasma levels. That is why a 46,XX fetus exposed to adrenal androgens in 21-hydroxylase deficiency has virilized external genitalia but no Wolffian derivatives, and why an individual with a testis on one side and an ovary on the other has male ducts only on the testis side."},{"type":"p","text":"Genital skin and the urogenital sinus express 5-alpha-reductase type 2. DHT binds the androgen receptor with higher affinity and dissociates more slowly than testosterone, amplifying the signal in tissues that are far from the testis. DHT drives growth of the genital tubercle, fusion of the urogenital folds and labioscrotal swellings, and budding of the prostate from urogenital sinus endoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"In the female, the absence of AMH lets the paramesonephric ducts persist, and the absence of testosterone lets the mesonephric ducts regress, leaving remnants such as the epoophoron and the Gartner duct, which can form a cyst in the lateral vaginal wall. Female ducts and external genitalia need no gonadal hormone, so a fetus with no functioning gonads develops female anatomy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Male sexual differentiation","steps":["SRY expressed in gonadal supporting cells","SOX9 turns them into Sertoli cells; testis forms","Sertoli cell AMH removes the Mullerian ducts","Leydig cell testosterone keeps the Wolffian ducts","5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT in genital tissue","DHT forms penis, scrotum, and prostate"],"caption":"Each arrow is a separate gene product, so a single defect stops the chain at a predictable point."},{"type":"example","title":"Three 46,XY patients, three defects","text":"Patient A has female external genitalia, a short blind vagina, no uterus, and inguinal testes. Testes formed and AMH removed the uterus, but neither testosterone nor DHT acted: complete androgen insensitivity (androgen receptor defect). Breasts develop at puberty because abundant testosterone is aromatized to estradiol. Patient B has ambiguous genitalia at birth, a normal epididymis and ductus deferens, and virilizes at puberty. Testosterone acted on the Wolffian ducts but DHT was lacking: 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency. Patient C is a normally virilized male whose uterus and tubes are found during inguinal surgery. Androgens worked but AMH did not: a defect in AMH or its receptor. For each case, ask which structure is present, then which hormone builds or removes it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C03","obj":"RE-1.3","title":"Paramesonephric ducts and uterine fusion anomalies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The paramesonephric ducts form around week 6 as longitudinal invaginations of coelomic epithelium on the lateral surface of each mesonephros. Their cranial ends stay open to the coelomic cavity, and their caudal growth follows the adjacent mesonephric duct, which acts as a guide. Each duct runs downward, crosses ventral to the mesonephric duct, and meets its partner in the midline."},{"type":"p","text":"The cranial, unfused parts become the uterine tubes, with the open ends forming the infundibulum and fimbriae. The caudal parts fuse into the uterovaginal canal, which forms the body and cervix of the uterus and the upper vagina. As the ducts move medially they raise a transverse peritoneal fold, the broad ligament. The solid tip of the canal reaches the posterior wall of the urogenital sinus at the sinus tubercle and induces the sinovaginal bulbs, which form a solid vaginal plate that later canalizes into the lower vagina; the hymen marks the junction. A single uterine cavity therefore requires three steps in order: growth of both ducts, fusion in the midline, and resorption of the septum left where their walls meet."},{"type":"flow","title":"Building one uterus from two ducts","steps":["Paramesonephric ducts form lateral to the mesonephric ducts","Cranial unfused parts become the uterine tubes","Caudal parts fuse into the uterovaginal canal","Midline septum resorbs, leaving one cavity","Canal meets sinovaginal bulbs; vaginal plate canalizes"],"caption":"Failure of growth, fusion, or resorption produces a distinct malformation."},{"type":"p","text":"The defect identifies the step that failed. If the ducts never fuse, each forms its own uterus and cervix (uterus didelphys), often with a longitudinal vaginal septum. If fusion is incomplete at the fundus, two horns remain with an indented outer contour (bicornuate uterus). If fusion is complete but the septum persists, the outer fundal contour is smooth while a fibromuscular wall divides the cavity (septate uterus), the most common of these anomalies. The septum is relatively poorly vascularized and an embryo implanting on it is more likely to be lost, so septate uterus carries the highest risk of recurrent pregnancy loss among them. If one duct fails to grow, a single horn results (unicornuate uterus); if both fail, the uterus and upper vagina are absent (Mullerian agenesis, Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome)."},{"type":"table","title":"Mullerian anomalies by failed step","cols":["Anomaly","Step that failed","Outer fundal contour","Cavity and cervix"],"rows":[["Uterus didelphys","Fusion, completely","Two separate uteri","Two cavities, two cervices, often a vaginal septum"],["Bicornuate uterus","Fusion, at the upper part","Indented, heart-shaped","Two horns; usually one cervix"],["Septate uterus","Septum resorption","Smooth, normal","Cavity divided by a septum; one cervix"],["Unicornuate uterus","Growth of one duct","Single narrow horn","One horn, one cervix"],["Mullerian agenesis (MRKH)","Growth of both ducts","Uterus absent","No cervix; upper vagina absent"]],"caption":"An outer notch means a fusion problem; a smooth outside with a wall inside means a resorption problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Two consequences follow from the anatomy. The ovaries arise from the gonadal ridge, not the ducts, so ovarian estrogen, breast development, and pubic hair are normal; Mullerian agenesis presents as primary amenorrhea in a 46,XX woman with normal secondary sex characteristics. The mesonephric duct both guides the paramesonephric duct and gives off the ureteric bud, so renal agenesis or a pelvic kidney often accompanies these defects, typically on the side of a missing horn."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C04","obj":"RE-1.4","title":"External genitalia from shared primordia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The external genitalia begin identically in both sexes. In weeks 3 to 4, mesenchyme around the cloacal membrane forms the cloacal folds, which meet cranially as the genital tubercle. When the urorectal septum reaches the cloacal membrane at about the end of week 6, the folds divide into the urethral (urogenital) folds in front and the anal folds behind, and a second pair of elevations, the labioscrotal (genital) swellings, appears lateral to the urethral folds. Differences appear in about week 9, and the genitalia are clearly male or female by about week 12. Whether the primordia follow the male or the female path depends on DHT acting through the androgen receptor, not on the karyotype."},{"type":"p","text":"In the male, DHT makes the genital tubercle elongate into the phallus, which pulls the urethral folds forward to form the walls of the urethral groove over the endodermal urethral plate. The folds then fuse over the groove from proximal to distal, closing the spongy (penile) urethra and leaving the penile raphe on the ventral surface; the urethra in the glans is completed last. The labioscrotal swellings enlarge, move caudally, and fuse in the midline as the scrotum, marked by the scrotal raphe."},{"type":"p","text":"In the female, without DHT, the genital tubercle elongates only slightly to form the clitoris, the urethral folds stay separate as the labia minora, and the labioscrotal swellings stay separate as the labia majora, joined in front at the mons pubis and behind at the posterior labial commissure. The urogenital groove remains open as the vestibule, which receives the urethra and vagina."},{"type":"table","title":"Indifferent primordia and their derivatives","cols":["Primordium","Male","Female"],"rows":[["Genital tubercle","Glans penis and corpora cavernosa","Glans clitoris and corpora of the clitoris"],["Urogenital (urethral) folds","Ventral penile shaft enclosing the spongy (penile) urethra","Labia minora"],["Labioscrotal swellings","Scrotum","Labia majora"],["Urogenital sinus","Prostate, bulbourethral glands, prostatic and membranous urethra","Paraurethral (Skene) glands, greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands, lower vagina, urethra"]],"caption":"Structures in the same row are homologues. Fusion in the male and non-fusion in the female explain each pair."},{"type":"p","text":"Timing and site explain the common errors. Androgen exposure of a 46,XX fetus, most often from 21-hydroxylase deficiency, before about week 12 fuses the labioscrotal swellings and urethral folds, whereas exposure after that time can only enlarge the clitoris. In hypospadias, the most common penile anomaly, the urethral folds fail to fuse completely, so the urethra opens on the ventral surface anywhere from the glans to the perineum; deficient androgen production or action is one cause, and cryptorchidism often coexists. Epispadias is a different error: the genital tubercle forms too far caudally, near the urorectal septum, so the urethral opening lies on the dorsal surface. It often accompanies bladder exstrophy, a defect of the ventral body wall."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C05","obj":"RE-1.5","title":"Testicular descent, the processus vaginalis, and inguinal defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The testis develops high on the posterior abdominal wall, beside the mesonephros, and keeps the vessels and lymphatics it acquired there. The testicular artery arises from the abdominal aorta below the renal arteries, the left testicular vein drains to the left renal vein, and testicular lymph drains to para-aortic nodes, whereas scrotal skin drains to superficial inguinal nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"The gubernaculum is a mesenchymal cord running from the lower pole of the testis through the developing abdominal wall to the labioscrotal swelling. Descent has two phases. In the transabdominal phase (about weeks 8 to 15), insulin-like peptide 3 (INSL3) from Leydig cells makes the gubernaculum swell, holding the testis near the future deep inguinal ring while the lumbar region grows away from it. In the inguinoscrotal phase (about weeks 25 to 35), androgen-dependent migration of the gubernaculum draws the testis through the inguinal canal into the scrotum, usually by birth. The scrotum keeps the testis about 2 to 3 °C (3.6 to 5.4 °F) below core temperature, which spermatogenesis requires, so an undescended testis (cryptorchidism, about 3% of term boys and more of preterm boys) has impaired spermatogenesis and a higher risk of germ cell tumors."},{"type":"flow","title":"Testicular descent","steps":["Testis forms on the posterior abdominal wall","Gubernaculum links its lower pole to the labioscrotal swelling","INSL3 swells the gubernaculum; testis held near deep ring","Androgen-dependent phase draws testis into the scrotum","Processus closes; tunica vaginalis remains"],"caption":"The testis passes behind the processus vaginalis, so the tunica vaginalis covers its front and sides."},{"type":"p","text":"Ahead of the testis, a pouch of peritoneum, the processus vaginalis, evaginates along the gubernaculum, and each abdominal wall layer it passes through forms a covering of the spermatic cord. After descent the connection between the processus and the peritoneal cavity normally closes, at about birth or within the first year, leaving the tunica vaginalis as a closed serous sac."},{"type":"table","title":"Abdominal wall layers and the coverings they form","cols":["Abdominal wall layer","Covering of the cord and testis"],"rows":[["Peritoneum","Processus vaginalis, later tunica vaginalis"],["Transversalis fascia (deep ring)","Internal spermatic fascia"],["Transversus abdominis","No covering; it arches over the canal"],["Internal oblique","Cremaster muscle and cremasteric fascia"],["External oblique aponeurosis (superficial ring)","External spermatic fascia"]],"caption":"An indirect hernia follows the same route and acquires all three spermatic fasciae."},{"type":"p","text":"If the processus stays open, the width of the channel decides what can follow it. A wide channel admits bowel or omentum, producing an indirect (congenital) inguinal hernia that enters the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels and runs within the spermatic cord, sometimes into the scrotum. It is the most common groin hernia in both sexes. A narrow channel admits only peritoneal fluid, producing a communicating hydrocele. In the female, the gubernaculum becomes the ovarian ligament and the round ligament of the uterus, and a patent processus (canal of Nuck) can produce an indirect hernia or hydrocele into the labium majus."},{"type":"table","title":"Inguinal and scrotal swellings compared","cols":["Lesion","Processus vaginalis","Contents","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Indirect inguinal hernia","Widely patent","Bowel or omentum","Enters deep ring lateral to inferior epigastric vessels; congenital"],["Communicating hydrocele","Narrowly patent","Peritoneal fluid","Size varies through the day; transilluminates"],["Noncommunicating hydrocele","Closed","Fluid within the tunica vaginalis","Fixed size; transilluminates"],["Direct inguinal hernia","Not involved","Bowel or omentum","Through inguinal (Hesselbach) triangle medial to inferior epigastric vessels; acquired weakness"]],"caption":"Indirect hernia and communicating hydrocele are the same embryologic defect differing only in width."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 2-year-old has a scrotal swelling that transilluminates, is small each morning, and enlarges by evening. What structure is open, and why does bowel not enter it?","explain":"A narrow patent processus vaginalis lets peritoneal fluid drain down when upright and return when supine (communicating hydrocele). It is too narrow for bowel; a wider channel gives an indirect hernia.","check":"The processus vaginalis paragraph and the swellings table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C06","obj":"RE-1.6","title":"Placenta and umbilical cord","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The placenta has a fetal part, the chorion frondosum, and a maternal part, the decidua basalis. Placental formation begins at implantation (about days 6 to 8 after fertilization), when the trophoblast differentiates into two layers. The inner cytotrophoblast is mononuclear and mitotically active, a stem layer. The outer syncytiotrophoblast forms by fusion of cytotrophoblast cells; it is multinucleated, does not divide, invades the endometrium, and is the endocrine layer that secretes hCG, which keeps the corpus luteum making progesterone until the placenta takes over at about weeks 8 to 12. By about day 12, lacunae in the syncytium open into eroded maternal sinusoids, starting the uteroplacental circulation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Maturation of chorionic villi","steps":["Primary villus: cytotrophoblast core under syncytiotrophoblast (week 2)","Secondary villus: extraembryonic mesoderm enters the core (week 3)","Tertiary villus: fetal capillaries form in the core (end of week 3)","Cytotrophoblast shell anchors villi to the decidua","Villi at the embryonic pole branch as chorion frondosum"],"caption":"Once tertiary villi connect to the embryonic heart, exchange with maternal blood can begin."},{"type":"p","text":"Villi at first cover the whole chorion. As the embryo grows, villi beneath the thinning decidua capsularis lose their blood supply and degenerate, leaving the smooth chorion laeve, while villi facing the decidua basalis branch richly as the chorion frondosum. Decidual cells, enlarged endometrial stromal cells filled with glycogen and lipid, limit trophoblast invasion; where decidua basalis is deficient, as over a prior uterine scar, villi attach to or invade the myometrium (placenta accreta spectrum)."},{"type":"table","title":"Decidua and umbilical cord structures","cols":["Structure","Location or course","Role or fate"],"rows":[["Decidua basalis","Beneath the implanted conceptus","Maternal part of the placenta (basal plate)"],["Decidua capsularis","Over the conceptus, facing the uterine cavity","Thins and fuses with decidua parietalis"],["Decidua parietalis","Rest of the uterine lining","Obliterates the uterine cavity on fusion"],["Umbilical arteries (2)","From fetal internal iliac arteries to placenta","Carry deoxygenated blood; become medial umbilical ligaments"],["Umbilical vein (1)","From placenta to fetal liver and ductus venosus","Carries oxygenated blood; becomes ligamentum teres hepatis"],["Wharton jelly","Surrounds the cord vessels","Mucoid connective tissue that resists compression"]],"caption":"Umbilical vessels are named by direction relative to the fetal heart, not by oxygen content."},{"type":"p","text":"By the fourth month the placenta has a fetal chorionic plate and a maternal basal plate. Decidual septa divide the maternal surface into about 15 to 20 cotyledons. Maternal blood from the spiral arteries fills the intervillous space and bathes the villi directly, so the human placenta is hemochorial, yet fetal and maternal blood do not mix. They are separated by the placental membrane, which late in pregnancy thins to syncytiotrophoblast, a discontinuous cytotrophoblast, basal lamina, connective tissue, and fetal capillary endothelium. Extravillous cytotrophoblast remodels the spiral arteries into wide, low-resistance vessels; shallow remodeling is a central mechanism of preeclampsia (RE-13-H). At term the placenta weighs about 500 to 600 g."},{"type":"p","text":"The umbilical cord forms from the connecting stalk and is covered by amnion. It normally holds two umbilical arteries and one umbilical vein (the left; the right regresses early) in Wharton jelly, a mucoid connective tissue rich in hyaluronic acid that cushions the vessels, together with remnants of the allantois and vitelline duct. A single umbilical artery, found in about 1% of cords, is associated with renal, cardiac, and chromosomal anomalies, notably trisomy 18."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In placenta accreta, which layer is missing, and why does the placenta fail to separate after delivery?","explain":"The decidua basalis is deficient, so villi anchor directly in the myometrium. Normal separation occurs within the decidua basalis; without it there is no cleavage plane.","check":"The decidual cells paragraph and the decidua rows of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-01-01-C07","obj":"RE-1.7","title":"Breast development and the milk line","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The breast is a modified apocrine sweat gland: its ducts and alveoli come from surface ectoderm, and its stroma and fat from the underlying mesoderm. Early in the second month, a band of thickened ectoderm, the mammary ridge or milk line, appears on each side of the ventral body wall, running from the axilla to the inguinal region."},{"type":"p","text":"Most of the ridge then regresses. Only a short segment over the pectoral region persists and sinks into the mesenchyme as a primary bud, which sends out about 15 to 20 secondary sprouts. These canalize late in fetal life to form the lactiferous ducts, each draining one lobe. At birth the ducts open into a shallow epithelial pit, which proliferation of the underlying mesenchyme raises into the nipple; failure of this eversion leaves an inverted nipple."},{"type":"flow","title":"From mammary ridge to nipple","steps":["Ectodermal mammary ridge forms from axilla to groin","Ridge regresses except over the pectoral region","Pectoral segment sinks into mesenchyme as a bud","Bud sprouts about 15 to 20 cords","Cords canalize into lactiferous ducts","Mesenchyme raises the epithelial pit into the nipple"],"caption":"A failure of regression anywhere along the ridge leaves accessory nipple or breast tissue on that line."},{"type":"p","text":"Until puberty the breast in both sexes is a small branched duct system. A newborn's breasts may swell and secrete a little milk for a few weeks because maternal estrogen crossed the placenta, then regress as it falls. At puberty, ovarian estrogen drives duct elongation, branching, and stromal growth, progesterone adds lobuloalveolar development, and full secretory differentiation waits for pregnancy (RE-6)."},{"type":"p","text":"Any segment of the ridge that fails to regress can form a nipple (supernumerary nipple, polythelia) or glandular tissue (polymastia) anywhere along the line from axilla to groin. Accessory nipples most often lie on the chest or upper abdomen below the normal breast and are easily mistaken for nevi, while accessory glandular tissue is most common in the axilla. Because it is true breast tissue, it responds to hormones, swelling before menstruation and during pregnancy and lactation, and it can develop the same diseases as the normal breast, including fibroadenoma and carcinoma. Absence of the breast (amastia) or nipple (athelia) is rare and may accompany absence of the pectoralis major in Poland syndrome."},{"type":"table","title":"Developmental anomalies of the breast","cols":["Anomaly","Mechanism","Typical finding"],"rows":[["Polythelia","Persistent ridge segment forms a nipple","Small pigmented papule on the milk line, often below the breast"],["Polymastia","Persistent ridge segment forms glandular tissue","Axillary mass that enlarges in pregnancy or lactation"],["Inverted nipple","Epithelial pit not everted by mesenchyme","Nipple retracted since birth"],["Amastia or athelia","Ridge fails to form or persist at the pectoral level","Absent breast or nipple; may occur with Poland syndrome"]],"caption":"An inverted nipple present since birth is developmental; a newly retracted nipple suggests disease."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy, physiology, and genetics meet in this chapter. The gonad's location on the posterior abdominal wall explains the aortic origin of the testicular artery, the left testicular vein draining to the renal vein, and para-aortic spread of testicular tumors. The intermediate mesoderm builds both the urinary and genital systems, so the mesonephric duct links renal agenesis to Mullerian anomalies. Biochemistry decides the phenotype: SRY and SOX9 are transcription factors, AMH is a TGF-beta ligand, testosterone and DHT share one nuclear receptor, and 21-hydroxylase or 5-alpha-reductase deficiency changes anatomy by changing which steroid reaches which tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from each failed step. Streak gonads with Y material give germ cell tumors, cryptorchid testes give infertility and tumors, a patent processus gives hernias and hydroceles, shallow trophoblast invasion gives preeclampsia and excessive invasion gives accreta, and persistent ridge tissue develops ordinary breast disease. The models here are simplified: the ovarian pathway is active rather than passive, the origin of the vagina is still debated, and timing values vary between texts, so reason from the sequence of steps rather than exact weeks."}],"summary":["SRY on Yp switches on SOX9, which makes Sertoli cells and a testis; without SRY an actively maintained ovary forms.","46,XY with SRY deletion gives streak gonads and female anatomy; 46,XX with SRY translocation gives small azoospermic testes and a male phenotype.","Sertoli cell AMH removes the Mullerian ducts; Leydig cell testosterone keeps the Wolffian ducts by local action; DHT builds the penis, scrotum, and prostate.","Androgen insensitivity gives testes, no uterus, and female external genitalia; 5-alpha-reductase deficiency gives male ducts with ambiguous genitalia.","Mullerian ducts form the tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina; the lower vagina comes from the urogenital sinus.","No fusion gives didelphys, partial fusion gives bicornuate, and failed septum resorption gives septate uterus, the type with the most pregnancy loss.","Genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings give the glans, ventral shaft and spongy urethra, and scrotum in males, and the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora in females.","Hypospadias is ventral from unfused urogenital folds; epispadias is dorsal from a misplaced genital tubercle.","The gubernaculum guides the testis through the inguinal canal; a wide patent processus vaginalis gives an indirect hernia and a narrow one a communicating hydrocele.","Chorion frondosum and decidua basalis form the placenta; the cord holds two arteries and one vein in Wharton jelly; extra nipples lie on the milk line from axilla to groin."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, sections on the genital system and descent of the testis"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Month to Birth: The Fetus and Placenta"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Integumentary System, section on mammary glands"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, inguinal region and spermatic cord"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Female Reproductive System, section on the placenta"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System; The Female Genital Tract"}]},"RE-10":{"unit":"RE-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01","title":"Reproductive Genetics","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how errors in chromosome number and mutations in single genes produce reproductive disease. It begins with sex chromosome aneuploidy and X inactivation, then follows the molecular path from high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) to cervical carcinoma, and closes with the inherited DNA repair syndromes (BRCA1, BRCA2, and Lynch syndrome) and the receptor-based classification of breast cancer. Board questions usually hand you a karyotype, a viral protein, a family history, or a receptor profile and ask for the mechanism and the expected phenotype. The common thread is that loss of a control system, whether chromosome segregation, a cell cycle checkpoint, or a repair pathway, has predictable consequences.","prerequisites":["Stages of meiosis I and meiosis II and the difference between homologs and sister chromatids","The G1 to S checkpoint: cyclin D and CDK4/6, Rb, E2F, p53, and p21","DNA repair pathways: mismatch repair, homologous recombination, and nonhomologous end joining","Tumor suppressor genes versus oncogenes and the two-hit hypothesis","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and feedback by sex steroids and inhibin"],"connections":[{"to":"Gonadal development and disorders of sex development (RE embryology units)","how":"The Y chromosome gene SRY and the number of X chromosomes determine whether gonads become testes, ovaries, or dysgenetic streaks."},{"to":"Female reproductive pathology (RE-13)","how":"Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, endometrial carcinoma, high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma, and breast carcinoma are the clinical endpoints of the mechanisms taught here."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and renal anatomy","how":"Turner syndrome links a sex chromosome error to bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta, and horseshoe kidney."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathology","how":"Lynch syndrome is the most common inherited colorectal cancer syndrome, so the same mismatch repair defect explains both colon and endometrial tumors."},{"to":"Microbiology and immunology","how":"HPV oncoproteins show how a DNA virus commandeers host cell cycle machinery, and persistent infection reflects failed immune clearance."}],"terms":[{"term":"Nondisjunction","def":"Failure of homologous chromosomes (meiosis I) or sister chromatids (meiosis II or mitosis) to separate, giving daughter cells with an extra or missing chromosome."},{"term":"Aneuploidy","def":"A chromosome number that is not an exact multiple of the haploid set, such as 47,XXY or 45,X."},{"term":"Mosaicism","def":"The presence of two or more cell lines with different karyotypes in one person, all derived from a single zygote."},{"term":"X inactivation","def":"Transcriptional silencing of all but one X chromosome in each somatic cell, which equalizes X-linked gene dosage between XX and XY cells."},{"term":"Barr body","def":"The condensed, inactive X chromosome seen as a dense chromatin mass against the inner nuclear membrane of interphase cells."},{"term":"Pseudoautosomal region","def":"Segments at the tips of the X and Y chromosomes that are homologous, pair and recombine in male meiosis, and largely escape X inactivation."},{"term":"E2F","def":"A family of transcription factors that activate genes needed for DNA synthesis and S phase entry, held inactive by hypophosphorylated Rb."},{"term":"Homologous recombination","def":"An accurate repair pathway for double-strand DNA breaks that copies the missing sequence from the intact sister chromatid."},{"term":"Mismatch repair","def":"A pathway that recognizes and removes nucleotides wrongly paired or looped out during DNA replication, using the parental strand as template."},{"term":"Microsatellite instability","def":"Variation in the length of short tandem repeat sequences in tumor DNA, the signature of defective mismatch repair."},{"term":"Loss of heterozygosity","def":"Loss of the remaining normal allele in a cell that inherited one mutant allele, the usual second hit in tumor suppressor syndromes."},{"term":"Triple-negative breast cancer","def":"Breast carcinoma lacking estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2 overexpression or amplification."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01-C01","obj":"RE-10.1","title":"Nondisjunction and the sex chromosome aneuploidies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In meiosis I, homologous chromosomes separate (in the male, X and Y pair through their pseudoautosomal regions and then part); in meiosis II, sister chromatids separate. Nondisjunction is a failure of either step. A meiosis I error places both homologs in one gamete, and a meiosis II error places two copies of the same chromosome in one gamete. The partner gamete from that division carries no sex chromosome."},{"type":"table","title":"How sex chromosome gametes combine","cols":["Abnormal gamete","Normal partner gamete","Zygote","Where the error can arise"],"rows":[["XX egg","Y sperm","47,XXY","Maternal meiosis I or II"],["XY sperm","X egg","47,XXY","Paternal meiosis I only"],["XX sperm","X egg","47,XXX","Paternal meiosis II"],["YY sperm","X egg","47,XYY","Paternal meiosis II"],["Egg or sperm with no sex chromosome","X gamete","45,X","Either parent; usually the father"]],"caption":"An XY sperm arises only from paternal meiosis I."},{"type":"p","text":"Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) affects about 1 in 500 to 1,000 males. About half of cases arise from a paternal meiosis I error and half from maternal errors, and only the maternal fraction rises with maternal age. The extra X leads to germ cell loss, tubular hyalinization, and Leydig cell failure, producing primary hypogonadism (flow below). Gynecomastia reflects a high estradiol to testosterone ratio, long legs reflect delayed epiphyseal closure and an extra SHOX dose, and male breast cancer risk is increased."},{"type":"p","text":"Turner syndrome (45,X) occurs in about 1 in 2,500 live-born females, but most 45,X conceptuses are lost in pregnancy. In about three quarters of cases the paternal sex chromosome was lost, through nondisjunction or anaphase lag, so risk does not rise with maternal age. Oocytes undergo accelerated atresia, leaving streak ovaries, so estrogen and inhibin fall and FSH and LH rise. Short stature reflects loss of one SHOX copy; webbed neck and peripheral lymphedema reflect fetal lymphatic obstruction (cystic hygroma); bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation, and horseshoe kidney are characteristic associations."},{"type":"flow","title":"Klinefelter syndrome from gamete to phenotype","steps":["Nondisjunction yields an XX egg or an XY sperm","Fertilization gives 47,XXY","Germ cells lost, tubules hyalinize, Leydig cells fail","Inhibin B and testosterone fall","Loss of negative feedback raises FSH and LH","Small firm testes, azoospermia, gynecomastia"],"caption":"The endocrine pattern is primary (testicular) hypogonadism: low testosterone with high gonadotropins."},{"type":"h","text":"Mosaicism"},{"type":"p","text":"When nondisjunction or anaphase lag happens in a mitotic division after fertilization, only the descendants of that cell are abnormal. The result is mosaicism, such as 45,X/46,XX or 46,XY/47,XXY. Because a normal cell line is present, the phenotype is usually milder and depends on which tissues carry the abnormal line: a 45,X/46,XX woman may have spontaneous puberty and menstrual cycles before early ovarian failure, and a 46,XY/47,XXY man may retain some spermatogenesis. A 45,X/46,XY mosaic is a special case because Y chromosome material in a dysgenetic gonad carries a risk of gonadoblastoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Working out the parental origin of an error","text":"A 47,XXY boy carries one maternal X, one paternal X, and his father's Y. Ask which gamete carried two sex chromosomes. An XX egg would have given two maternal X chromosomes, so the egg was normal and the sperm carried both paternal X and Y. X and Y are homologs that separate in meiosis I, so the error was paternal meiosis I, and maternal age is irrelevant. By the same logic, a 47,XXY boy with two different maternal X chromosomes points to maternal meiosis I, and two identical maternal X chromosomes (at loci near the centromere) points to maternal meiosis II."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A father has nondisjunction of the sex chromosomes in meiosis II. What karyotypes can his children have if the abnormal sperm fertilize normal eggs?","explain":"Sister chromatids fail to separate, so the sperm carries XX, YY, or no sex chromosome, giving 47,XXX, 47,XYY, or 45,X.","check":"Compare with the gamete table; note that an XY sperm cannot arise in meiosis II."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01-C02","obj":"RE-10.2","title":"X inactivation, XIST, and Barr bodies","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A female cell with two active X chromosomes would make twice as much of each X-linked gene product as a male cell. Dosage compensation solves this by silencing all but one X in every somatic cell. The choice is made early in embryonic life, around implantation, and in the embryo proper it is random: in some cells the maternal X is silenced and in others the paternal X. Once made, the choice is stable and is inherited by every daughter cell, so every woman is a mosaic of two cell populations. This is why a heterozygous carrier of an X-linked disorder can show patchy or partial signs, and why skewed inactivation can occasionally make a carrier fully affected."},{"type":"p","text":"Silencing is controlled by the X inactivation center on the long arm of the X, which contains the XIST gene. XIST is transcribed only from the X that will be silenced, and its product is a long noncoding RNA that stays on that chromosome and coats it in cis. The RNA recruits Polycomb repressive complexes that place repressive histone marks (H3K27 trimethylation), the histone variant macroH2A is incorporated, and CpG islands of promoters become methylated. The chromosome condenses into facultative heterochromatin, replicates late in S phase, and sits against the nuclear envelope as the Barr body. Once DNA methylation is established, continued XIST expression is no longer required to keep most genes off."},{"type":"flow","title":"Steps in X inactivation","steps":["Cell counts X chromosomes in the early embryo","One X is chosen to remain active","XIST is transcribed from each other X","XIST RNA coats its own X in cis","Repressive histone marks and CpG methylation follow","Condensed, late-replicating X forms the Barr body"],"caption":"Every X beyond the first is silenced, so the count of Barr bodies tracks the count of X chromosomes."},{"type":"p","text":"The rule for prediction follows directly: the number of Barr bodies equals the number of X chromosomes minus one, regardless of how many Y chromosomes are present. A Y chromosome is never inactivated and forms no Barr body."},{"type":"table","title":"Barr bodies by karyotype","cols":["Karyotype","Condition","Active X","Barr bodies"],"rows":[["46,XY","Typical male","1","0"],["46,XX","Typical female","1","1"],["47,XXY","Klinefelter syndrome","1","1"],["45,X","Turner syndrome","1","0"],["47,XXX","Triple X","1","2"],["48,XXXY","Klinefelter variant","1","2"]],"caption":"One X is always active; each additional X is inactivated."},{"type":"h","text":"Why aneuploidy still matters"},{"type":"p","text":"If inactivation were complete, extra or missing X chromosomes would have little effect. It is not. About 15% of X-linked genes escape inactivation and a further group escapes variably. The pseudoautosomal genes, including SHOX, have functional copies on the Y, so normal development expects two active copies in both sexes. A 45,X cell has one dose of these genes, which explains short stature and the other Turner features, while a 47,XXY cell has three. Both X chromosomes are also active in the earliest embryo and in oocytes, where two X chromosomes are needed to maintain the follicle pool, which helps explain ovarian failure in Turner syndrome. When one X is structurally abnormal, such as an isochromosome Xq, the abnormal X is preferentially inactivated in surviving cells."},{"type":"stop","predict":"How many Barr bodies are in a buccal cell from a 49,XXXXY individual, and why does this karyotype cause more severe intellectual disability than 47,XXY?","explain":"Three Barr bodies (four X minus one). Silencing is incomplete, so each extra X adds more active escape gene dosage.","check":"Compare with the Barr body rule and the paragraph on genes that escape inactivation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01-C03","obj":"RE-10.3","title":"High-risk HPV E6 and E7 and the loss of cell cycle checkpoints","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Two tumor suppressor proteins guard the G1 to S transition. Rb, when hypophosphorylated, binds E2F transcription factors and keeps S phase genes off. Growth signals induce cyclin D, and cyclin D with CDK4 or CDK6 (then cyclin E with CDK2) phosphorylates Rb, which releases E2F and commits the cell to DNA replication. p53 responds to DNA damage: kinases such as ATM stabilize it by freeing it from its ubiquitin ligase MDM2, and p53 then induces p21, a CDK inhibitor that keeps Rb hypophosphorylated and arrests the cycle, as well as GADD45 for repair and BAX for apoptosis if the damage is beyond repair."},{"type":"p","text":"HPV infects basal keratinocytes through microabrasions, most often at the cervical transformation zone, where immature metaplastic squamous cells are especially susceptible. The virus needs the host DNA synthesis machinery but infects cells that would normally stop dividing as they differentiate, so its early proteins E6 and E7 force them back into S phase. High-risk types (most importantly 16 and 18, together responsible for about 70% of cervical carcinomas) make E6 and E7 that bind their targets with high affinity; low-risk types 6 and 11, which cause condylomata, bind weakly."},{"type":"table","title":"High-risk HPV oncoproteins","cols":["Oncoprotein","Host target","Molecular action","Consequence"],"rows":[["E6","p53","Recruits the ubiquitin ligase E6AP, tagging p53 for proteasomal degradation","No G1 arrest or apoptosis after DNA damage"],["E6","Telomerase (TERT)","Activates TERT expression","Cells avoid replicative senescence"],["E7","Hypophosphorylated Rb (and p107, p130)","Binds Rb, displacing E2F, and promotes Rb degradation","E2F drives S phase entry without growth signals"],["E7","p21 and p27","Inactivates these CDK inhibitors","Cyclin-CDK activity cannot be braked"]],"caption":"E6 pairs with p53 and E7 with Rb; together they remove both arms of the G1 checkpoint."},{"type":"p","text":"In a productive infection, the viral genome persists as a circular episome and the viral E2 protein represses transcription of E6 and E7, so expression stays modest and the lesion is usually a low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (CIN 1); most such infections are cleared by the immune system within one to two years. In high-grade lesions and carcinomas the viral DNA has often integrated into a host chromosome, and integration usually breaks the E2 gene. Without E2, E6 and E7 are overexpressed. The cell now proliferates without growth signals and continues dividing despite DNA damage, so mutations accumulate with each cycle. Persistent infection, smoking, and immunosuppression such as HIV infection raise the odds of progression."},{"type":"flow","title":"From high-risk HPV infection to invasive carcinoma","steps":["HPV 16 or 18 infects transformation zone basal cells","Persistent infection escapes immune clearance","Viral DNA integrates, disrupting E2","E6 and E7 are overexpressed","p53 degraded; Rb inactivated, E2F released","Mutations accumulate: CIN 2 to CIN 3","Invasion through basement membrane: squamous cell carcinoma"],"caption":"Viral oncoproteins start the process; host mutations acquired over years complete it."},{"type":"p","text":"Two diagnostic findings follow from this mechanism. Koilocytes, squamous cells with a wrinkled nucleus and a clear perinuclear halo, mark productive viral replication in the upper epithelium. Strong diffuse staining for p16, a CDK4/6 inhibitor, marks transformation: with Rb inactivated, the negative feedback that normally limits p16 is lost, so p16 accumulates even though it can no longer stop the cycle. HPV 18 is relatively more common in endocervical adenocarcinoma, and the same E6 and E7 mechanism underlies HPV-related vulvar, anal, penile, and oropharyngeal carcinomas."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01-C04","obj":"RE-10.4","title":"BRCA1, BRCA2, and hereditary breast and ovarian cancer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A double-strand break is the most dangerous DNA lesion because neither strand is left to serve as a template. After replication, during S and G2 phases, the cell can repair it accurately by homologous recombination, which copies the missing sequence from the intact sister chromatid. BRCA1 acts early: it helps resect the broken ends to expose single-stranded DNA and coordinates checkpoint signaling. Through the bridging protein PALB2 it recruits BRCA2, which loads the recombinase RAD51 onto the single strands so they can invade the sister chromatid and copy it. Without either protein the cell falls back on nonhomologous end joining and other error-prone pathways that delete or rearrange sequence, so the genome becomes progressively unstable."},{"type":"p","text":"BRCA1 (chromosome 17q) and BRCA2 (chromosome 13q) are tumor suppressor genes. Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer follows an autosomal dominant pattern in families, yet the defect is recessive at the level of the cell: one inherited mutant allele is present in every cell, and a tumor arises only after the second allele is lost in a breast or ovarian epithelial cell, usually by deletion that produces loss of heterozygosity. Because a second hit is likely but not certain, penetrance is high but incomplete, cancers appear at younger ages, and they are more often bilateral or multiple. Certain founder mutations are frequent in Ashkenazi Jewish families."},{"type":"flow","title":"BRCA two-hit tumorigenesis","steps":["Germline BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation in every cell","Second allele lost in one epithelial cell","Homologous recombination fails","Double-strand breaks repaired by error-prone joining","Chromosomal instability; TP53 mutation is common","Clonal expansion to carcinoma"],"caption":"Inheritance is dominant in the family, but tumor formation requires loss of both alleles in a cell."},{"type":"table","title":"BRCA1 versus BRCA2","cols":["Feature","BRCA1","BRCA2"],"rows":[["Chromosome","17q","13q"],["Role in repair","End resection, checkpoint signaling, recruits BRCA2 via PALB2","Loads RAD51 onto single-stranded DNA"],["Female breast cancer, lifetime","About 60 to 70%","About 55 to 70%"],["Ovarian (tubo-ovarian) cancer, lifetime","About 40%","About 15 to 20%"],["Typical breast tumor type","Triple-negative, basal-like, high grade","Usually ER-positive, luminal"],["Male breast cancer","Slightly increased","Clearly increased, about 6 to 7% lifetime"],["Other cancers","Prostate (modest), pancreatic","Prostate (often aggressive), pancreatic, melanoma"],["Biallelic germline mutation","Usually lethal in the embryo; very rare survivors","Fanconi anemia (FANCD1)"]],"caption":"Risk figures are approximate cumulative risks to about age 80 and differ between studies; general population breast cancer risk is about 12%."},{"type":"p","text":"The ovarian cancer in BRCA carriers is almost always high-grade serous carcinoma, and many of these tumors begin in the fimbriated end of the fallopian tube as serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma before seeding the ovary and peritoneum. TP53 mutation is nearly universal in these tumors. BRCA2 is the gene to suspect when a family includes male breast cancer, prostate cancer, or pancreatic cancer. Why loss of a ubiquitous repair gene targets the breast and tube so selectively is not fully understood; hormonal effects on proliferating epithelium are a leading explanation."},{"type":"p","text":"The same repair defect makes BRCA-deficient tumor cells unusually dependent on backup pathways. Inhibiting poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP), which repairs single-strand breaks, lets those breaks become double-strand breaks at replication forks; normal cells repair them by homologous recombination, but BRCA-deficient cells cannot and die. This is synthetic lethality: loss of a repair pathway is both the cause of the tumor and its vulnerability."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01-C05","obj":"RE-10.5","title":"Lynch syndrome: mismatch repair, microsatellite instability, and gynecologic cancer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"DNA polymerase occasionally inserts a wrong base or, in repetitive sequence, slips so that the new strand gains or loses a repeat unit. Mismatch repair corrects these errors just behind the replication fork. The MSH2 and MSH6 heterodimer recognizes base mismatches and small loops (MSH2 with MSH3 handles larger loops), the MLH1 and PMS2 heterodimer is recruited and nicks the newly synthesized strand, and the error is excised and resynthesized using the parental strand as template. Without this system the mutation rate rises by roughly a hundredfold to a thousandfold."},{"type":"p","text":"Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer) is caused by a germline mutation in MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2, or by a deletion in EPCAM that silences the neighboring MSH2 gene. It affects roughly 1 in 300 people and is inherited as autosomal dominant. As with BRCA, every cell carries one defective allele and a tumor arises after the second allele is lost. The tumor then accumulates mutations in microsatellites, short tandem repeats of 1 to 6 base pairs scattered through the genome, and the lengths of these repeats vary in tumor DNA compared with normal tissue. This is microsatellite instability (MSI). When a microsatellite lies in the coding sequence of a growth-regulating gene, such as the TGF-beta receptor type II or BAX, a frameshift inactivates it, which drives the tumor forward."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mismatch repair loss to carcinoma","steps":["Germline mutation in one mismatch repair allele","Second allele lost in an epithelial cell","Replication slippage errors go uncorrected","Microsatellite lengths change: MSI-high","Frameshifts inactivate genes such as TGFBR2 and BAX","Accelerated progression to carcinoma"],"caption":"Adenomas in Lynch syndrome progress to carcinoma faster than sporadic adenomas because mutations accumulate rapidly."},{"type":"p","text":"In women with Lynch syndrome, the lifetime risk of endometrial carcinoma (roughly 40 to 60% for MLH1 and MSH2 carriers) equals or exceeds the risk of colorectal carcinoma, and endometrial cancer is often the first, or sentinel, cancer. These are usually endometrioid carcinomas, sometimes arising in the lower uterine segment. Ovarian cancer risk rises to roughly 10%, with endometrioid and clear cell types more common than in BRCA carriers. Colorectal cancers arise younger (often in the 40s), favor the right colon, and carry dense tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes because frameshift mutations generate abnormal peptides the immune system recognizes. Stomach, small bowel, upper urinary tract, and biliary tract are also at risk, and sebaceous skin tumors define the Muir-Torre variant."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading mismatch repair immunohistochemistry","text":"An endometrial carcinoma from a 44-year-old woman shows MSI and loss of MLH1 and PMS2 staining, with MSH2 and MSH6 retained. PMS2 is unstable without its partner MLH1, so paired loss points to MLH1 as the primary defect; isolated PMS2 loss would point to PMS2 itself. By the same logic, MSH2 loss carries MSH6 with it. MLH1 loss, however, has two causes. In Lynch syndrome it reflects a germline mutation, but many sporadic MSI tumors silence MLH1 by somatic hypermethylation of its promoter. Testing the tumor for MLH1 promoter methylation (and BRAF V600E in colon tumors) separates the two: methylation favors a sporadic tumor, while its absence calls for germline testing."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinction from BRCA syndromes is in the repair pathway and the signature it leaves. BRCA loss produces gross chromosomal rearrangements and copy number changes (chromosomal instability), whereas mismatch repair loss produces many small insertions, deletions, and point mutations with a near-normal karyotype."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-10-01-C06","obj":"RE-10.6","title":"Receptor-based classification of breast carcinoma and the BRCA1 link","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Breast epithelium has two cell layers: inner luminal cells, many of which express estrogen receptor (ER) and progesterone receptor (PR), and outer basal or myoepithelial cells, which express basal keratins such as CK5/6 and lack hormone receptors. Gene expression profiling sorts invasive carcinomas into molecular groups that resemble these cell types. In practice, immunohistochemistry for ER, PR, HER2, and the proliferation marker Ki-67 approximates the groups, which matters because each group depends on a different growth pathway and behaves differently."},{"type":"p","text":"ER is a nuclear transcription factor; estrogen binding drives expression of genes for proliferation, including PR itself, which is why a PR-positive, ER-negative tumor is uncommon. HER2 is a receptor tyrosine kinase of the epidermal growth factor receptor family, encoded by ERBB2 on chromosome 17q. In HER2-positive tumors the gene is amplified, so the cell makes many copies of the receptor, which dimerize without ligand and signal continuously through the RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways. HER2 status is therefore read as protein overexpression by immunohistochemistry or gene amplification by in situ hybridization."},{"type":"table","title":"Molecular groups of invasive breast carcinoma","cols":["Group","Receptor profile","Biology","Approximate share and behavior"],"rows":[["Luminal A","ER+, PR+, HER2 negative, low Ki-67","Low grade, estrogen-driven","About 40 to 55%; best prognosis, late recurrences possible"],["Luminal B","ER+, PR lower or negative, high Ki-67, HER2 negative or positive","Higher grade, estrogen-driven but more proliferative","About 15 to 20%; intermediate prognosis"],["HER2-enriched","ER and PR negative, HER2 amplified","Driven by constant HER2 signaling","About 10 to 15%; high grade, aggressive biology"],["Basal-like","ER, PR, and HER2 negative (triple-negative); CK5/6 and EGFR often positive","High grade, TP53 mutated, genomic instability","About 10 to 15%; younger women, early recurrence"]],"caption":"Triple-negative and basal-like overlap heavily but are not identical: most basal-like tumors are triple-negative, and most triple-negative tumors are basal-like."},{"type":"p","text":"About 70 to 80% of breast cancers in BRCA1 carriers are triple-negative and basal-like, whereas BRCA2 tumors are usually ER-positive and resemble sporadic luminal cancers. The leading explanation is that BRCA1 is needed for luminal progenitor cells to differentiate into mature ER-positive luminal cells. When BRCA1 is lost, progenitors accumulate, and the resulting tumors keep an undifferentiated, ER-negative program with basal features. Combined with the loss of homologous recombination and nearly universal TP53 mutation, this produces high-grade tumors with large, pleomorphic nuclei, many mitoses, pushing borders, and frequent lymphocytic infiltrates. Triple-negative tumors are more common in younger women and in women of African ancestry, recur early (mostly within about 3 to 5 years), and spread to viscera and brain more than to bone."},{"type":"p","text":"Receptor status explains why prognosis differs. ER-positive tumors stay dependent on estrogen signaling, and HER2-positive tumors on HER2 signaling, so each has an identifiable driver pathway. Triple-negative tumors lack both, and because so many BRCA1 cancers are triple-negative, a triple-negative breast cancer, especially in a young woman, is a recognized reason to look for a germline BRCA1 mutation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 36-year-old woman has a high-grade breast carcinoma that is ER, PR, and HER2 negative, and her mother had high-grade serous ovarian cancer. Which gene is most likely mutated, and what repair process has failed?","explain":"BRCA1. Homologous recombination repair of double-strand breaks has failed, and loss of BRCA1 also blocks luminal differentiation, giving a basal-like tumor.","check":"Compare with the BRCA1 versus BRCA2 table and the paragraph on the BRCA1 link."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disorder in this chapter is the failure of a control system that normally protects genetic information. Meiotic segregation protects chromosome number, X inactivation protects gene dosage, the p53 and Rb checkpoints protect the cell cycle, and homologous recombination and mismatch repair protect sequence. Anatomy and endocrinology show the consequences at the organ level (streak ovaries, hyalinized tubules, and high gonadotropins), microbiology shows how a virus disables the checkpoints from outside, and pathology shows that inherited repair defects leave recognizable signatures: chromosomal instability with BRCA loss and microsatellite instability with mismatch repair loss."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits matter for reasoning. A peripheral blood karyotype can miss tissue mosaicism. Not all MSI or MLH1 loss is inherited, and not all triple-negative cancers carry BRCA1 mutations; most do not. Risk figures for hereditary syndromes vary with the specific gene, the study population, and the age to which risk is calculated, so exam answers depend on the direction and relative size of risk rather than an exact percentage."}],"summary":["Meiosis I nondisjunction sends both homologs into one gamete; meiosis II nondisjunction sends both sister chromatids. An XY sperm arises only from paternal meiosis I.","47,XXY causes primary hypogonadism (low testosterone and inhibin B, high FSH and LH); 45,X causes streak ovaries and is usually due to loss of the paternal sex chromosome, unrelated to maternal age.","Errors after fertilization produce mosaicism, which usually gives a milder phenotype; Y material in 45,X/46,XY gonads carries gonadoblastoma risk.","XIST RNA coats the X it is transcribed from and silences it in cis; Barr bodies equal the number of X chromosomes minus one.","Genes that escape inactivation, including pseudoautosomal SHOX, explain why sex chromosome aneuploidy still causes a phenotype.","High-risk HPV E6 targets p53 for ubiquitin-mediated degradation and E7 binds Rb to release E2F; integration that disrupts E2 raises their expression.","BRCA1 and BRCA2 are autosomal dominant tumor suppressors required for homologous recombination; tumors need loss of the second allele.","BRCA1 tumors are typically triple-negative and basal-like; BRCA2 adds male breast, prostate, and pancreatic cancer.","Lynch syndrome is germline mismatch repair deficiency with microsatellite instability, and endometrial cancer risk equals or exceeds colorectal risk in women.","Breast carcinomas group by ER, PR, and HER2 status; HER2 positivity reflects ERBB2 gene amplification."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, cytogenetic disorders involving sex chromosomes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, oncogenic DNA viruses and defects in DNA repair"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract and The Breast"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"DNA structure, replication, and repair"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Gametogenesis, chromosomal abnormalities"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Human Cancer Viruses, papillomaviruses"}]},"RE-11":{"unit":"RE-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01","title":"Immune Responses in Reproductive Disease","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The reproductive tract asks the immune system to do contradictory things: ignore sperm and a half-foreign fetus, arm the newborn with maternal antibody, and still defend mucosal surfaces against infection. This chapter explains how testicular immune privilege, fetal tolerance, placental IgG transfer, milk IgA, and the vaginal microbiota achieve this, and what happens when they fail: antisperm antibodies, autoimmune orchitis, Rh hemolytic disease, bacterial vaginosis, persistent HPV infection, and tubal scarring. Board questions typically give a clinical outcome such as infertility, hydrops, or ectopic pregnancy and ask for the immune mechanism behind it.","prerequisites":["Central and peripheral tolerance, regulatory T cells, and NK cell activating and inhibitory receptors (IM-13)","Antibody isotypes, their structure, and class switching (IM-5)","The four types of hypersensitivity (IM-15-C)","Histology of the seminiferous epithelium, cervix, vagina, and fallopian tube (RE-2)","Structure of the placenta and trophoblast layers (RE-1, RE-3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive genetics (RE-10)","how":"HPV E6 and E7 act on p53 and Rb only in infections that cell-mediated immunity fails to clear."},{"to":"Reproductive pathogens and STDs (RE-12, RE-13-K)","how":"The organisms behind bacterial vaginosis, salpingitis, and cervical neoplasia are the same ones whose host responses this chapter explains."},{"to":"Diseases of the placenta (RE-13-H)","how":"Interactions between uterine NK cells and extravillous trophoblast shape spiral artery remodeling, which fails in preeclampsia."},{"to":"Diseases of blood cell lysis (HE-9-B)","how":"Hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn is an antibody-mediated extravascular hemolysis, detected by antiglobulin testing."},{"to":"Acquired immunodeficiency (IM-15-B)","how":"CD4 T cell loss in HIV allows HPV persistence, which is why invasive cervical carcinoma is an AIDS-defining illness."}],"terms":[{"term":"Immune privilege","def":"The capacity of a site, such as the testis or the maternal-fetal interface, to tolerate antigens that would trigger rejection elsewhere."},{"term":"Blood-testis barrier","def":"Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells that seal meiotic and postmeiotic germ cells away from blood and lymph."},{"term":"Antisperm antibodies","def":"Antibodies against sperm surface antigens that agglutinate sperm and impair motility and fertilization."},{"term":"Semiallogeneic","def":"Carrying antigens half from self and half from a genetically different individual, as the fetus does with paternal HLA."},{"term":"HLA-G","def":"A nonclassical, minimally polymorphic HLA class I molecule on extravillous trophoblast that engages inhibitory receptors on NK cells and T cells."},{"term":"Missing-self recognition","def":"NK cell killing of cells that lack the class I molecules that normally engage NK inhibitory receptors."},{"term":"FcRn","def":"The neonatal Fc receptor, an MHC class I-like molecule that binds IgG at acidic pH and transports it across the placenta."},{"term":"Secretory IgA","def":"Dimeric IgA carried across epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor and protected by the attached secretory component."},{"term":"Fetomaternal hemorrhage","def":"Passage of fetal red cells into the maternal circulation, most often at delivery."},{"term":"Hydrops fetalis","def":"Abnormal fluid in two or more fetal compartments (skin, pleura, pericardium, peritoneum), the end stage of severe fetal anemia or heart failure."},{"term":"Clue cell","def":"A vaginal squamous cell whose borders are obscured by an adherent coat of Gardnerella and other bacteria."},{"term":"Virus-like particle","def":"A self-assembled shell of viral capsid protein that mimics the virion surface but contains no viral genome."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C01","obj":"RE-11.1","title":"Testicular immune privilege and its failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Spermatogenesis begins at puberty, long after central tolerance has been established in the thymus. Spermatocytes, spermatids, and sperm express new proteins that were never presented for deletion, so to the immune system they are foreign. The testis survives this through immune privilege, which has an anatomical layer and an active layer."},{"type":"p","text":"The anatomical layer is the blood-testis barrier. Tight junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells, near their bases, divide the seminiferous epithelium into a basal compartment, which holds spermatogonia and is open to interstitial fluid, and an adluminal compartment, which holds spermatocytes and spermatids. Germ cells cross the junctions as new junctions form beneath them, so antigen-bearing cells never contact blood or lymph. The active layer is local immunosuppression: Sertoli cells, Leydig cells, and resident macrophages of an anti-inflammatory type release TGF-beta and IL-10, Sertoli cells express Fas ligand that can kill activated Fas-bearing T cells, intratesticular androgen dampens inflammation, and regulatory T cells restrain responses that do start. Active suppression matters because the barrier is weaker in the rete testis and excurrent ducts, where sperm antigens can leak."},{"type":"flow","title":"Barrier breach to antisperm antibodies","steps":["Trauma, torsion, vasectomy, or orchitis","Blood-testis barrier or duct wall is breached","Sperm antigens reach antigen-presenting cells","B cells make antisperm IgG and IgA","Sperm agglutinate and move poorly","Reduced fertility despite sperm production"],"caption":"Antisperm antibodies follow any event that exposes sequestered sperm antigens to the systemic immune system."},{"type":"p","text":"After vasectomy, spermatogenesis continues; sperm that leak from the distended epididymis and vas are phagocytosed, and antisperm antibodies develop in most men. They matter when fertility is sought after reversal. Antibodies bound to sperm cause head-to-head or tail-to-tail agglutination, block penetration of cervical mucus, and interfere with the acrosome reaction and zona pellucida binding, so fertility falls even when the count is normal."},{"type":"p","text":"Mumps orchitis follows parotitis in a minority of postpubertal males. Viral replication causes interstitial inflammation and edema, and because the tunica albuginea cannot stretch, pressure injures the seminiferous tubules, which later atrophy. It is usually unilateral, so sterility is uncommon, but subfertility and antisperm antibodies can follow."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune orchitis is T cell-mediated inflammation of the testis, isolated or with systemic autoimmunity (vasculitis, autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes). Tubular destruction lowers sperm output and Sertoli cell inhibin B, so FSH rises; if Leydig cells are lost, testosterone falls and LH rises. This is primary (hypergonadotropic) hypogonadism."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading testicular injury from laboratory values","cols":["Lesion","Sperm","Inhibin B, FSH","Testosterone, LH"],"rows":[["Antisperm antibodies alone","Count often normal; agglutination, poor motility","Normal","Normal"],["Tubular damage (germ and Sertoli cells)","Oligospermia or azoospermia","Low inhibin B, high FSH","Normal"],["Tubular plus Leydig damage","Oligospermia or azoospermia","Low inhibin B, high FSH","Low testosterone, high LH"]],"caption":"FSH tracks the seminiferous tubules through inhibin B; LH tracks Leydig cells through testosterone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After vasectomy reversal, semen contains sperm in normal numbers but they clump and swim poorly. What explains this?","explain":"The vasectomy exposed sperm antigens and induced antisperm antibodies, which persist after the duct is reopened and agglutinate sperm.","check":"Compare with the flow on barrier breach and the paragraph on vasectomy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C02","obj":"RE-11.2","title":"Maternal tolerance of the semiallogeneic fetus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Half of the fetal genome, including HLA alleles, comes from the father, so the fetus is a semiallogeneic graft that by ordinary rules should be rejected. It survives because the trophoblast, the fetal tissue that contacts maternal blood and decidua, presents little for maternal T cells to attack and actively inhibits maternal NK cells, and because the maternal response at the interface is regulated. Tolerance is local, not a general shutdown: many multiparous women make antibodies against paternal HLA, which shows the mother does see fetal antigens."},{"type":"p","text":"Villous syncytiotrophoblast, which is bathed in maternal blood in the intervillous space, expresses no classical HLA class I or class II, so maternal cytotoxic T cells find no paternal peptide-HLA complex to recognize. Extravillous trophoblast, which invades the decidua and remodels the spiral arteries, lacks the highly polymorphic HLA-A and HLA-B and all class II, but expresses HLA-C and the nonclassical molecules HLA-E and HLA-G. HLA-G is minimally polymorphic, so it offers few foreign epitopes."},{"type":"p","text":"Silencing class I creates a new risk, because natural killer (NK) cells kill cells that lack class I (missing-self recognition), and uterine NK cells are the most abundant leukocytes in early decidua. HLA-G engages inhibitory receptors such as LILRB1 (also present on T cells and macrophages), and HLA-E, which presents leader peptides derived from HLA-G and HLA-C, engages the inhibitory receptor CD94/NKG2A. Instead of killing, uterine NK cells secrete cytokines and angiogenic factors that help remodel spiral arteries into wide, low-resistance vessels. Signaling between maternal NK receptors (KIR) and fetal HLA-C is thought to tune this remodeling, and inadequate remodeling is linked to preeclampsia (RE-13-H)."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers of protection at the maternal-fetal interface","cols":["Mechanism","Effect"],"rows":[["No HLA-A, HLA-B, or class II on trophoblast","No target for maternal CD8 or CD4 T cells"],["HLA-G and HLA-E on extravillous trophoblast","Inhibit uterine NK cells; prevent missing-self killing"],["Complement regulators (CD46, CD55, CD59) on trophoblast","Limit complement injury from any bound antibody"],["Indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase","Tryptophan depletion suppresses local T cell proliferation"],["Expanded maternal regulatory T cells","Suppress responses to paternal antigens"],["Decidual stromal cells","Limit recruitment of effector T cells into the decidua"]],"caption":"No single mechanism is sufficient; tolerance depends on concealment, inhibition, and regulation together."},{"type":"p","text":"This protection shields the trophoblast, not fetal cells that leave the placenta: fetal red cells entering the maternal circulation are fully exposed, which is why maternal alloantibodies against red cell antigens can still cause disease (C04)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C03","obj":"RE-11.3","title":"Passive immunity of the newborn","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The newborn's adaptive immune system is naive, so for its first months it relies on antibody made by its mother. Two routes deliver it: IgG across the placenta before birth, giving systemic protection, and secretory IgA in milk after birth, protecting the gut surface."},{"type":"p","text":"The neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) is an MHC class I-like molecule paired with beta2-microglobulin. Syncytiotrophoblast takes up maternal plasma by fluid-phase endocytosis; as the endosome acidifies to about pH 6, FcRn binds the Fc region of IgG and carries it across the cell, releasing it at the neutral pH of the fetal side, while unbound proteins go to lysosomes. The same pH-dependent recycling protects IgG from degradation throughout life and gives most IgG a half-life of about 3 weeks. Placental transfer is active and selective: it begins near the end of the first trimester, rises steeply in the third, and at term cord IgG often equals or exceeds maternal IgG. IgG1 crosses most efficiently and IgG2 least, and very preterm infants are born with low IgG."},{"type":"p","text":"IgM, a pentamer of about 900 kDa, has no FcRn binding site, and IgA, IgD, and IgE do not cross either. Any IgM in cord or neonatal blood was therefore made by the fetus and means the fetus met an antigen in utero, as in congenital infection. Maternal IgG decays over months while the infant's own IgG production starts slowly, so total IgG reaches a low point at about 3 to 6 months (physiologic hypogammaglobulinemia)."},{"type":"p","text":"FcRn cannot tell protective from harmful IgG. Maternal IgG autoantibodies and alloantibodies cause transient neonatal disease that fades as the IgG is cleared over weeks to months, a time course that itself identifies the mechanism."},{"type":"table","title":"What crosses and what it does","cols":["Antibody","Route to infant","Consequence"],"rows":[["Protective IgG","Placenta (FcRn), mainly third trimester","Systemic protection for about 6 months"],["IgM","Does not cross","Neonatal IgM is fetal; suggests congenital infection"],["Anti-D and other red cell IgG","Placenta","Hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn"],["Anti-Ro/SSA","Placenta","Neonatal lupus, congenital heart block"],["TSH receptor-stimulating IgG","Placenta","Transient neonatal hyperthyroidism"],["Anti-acetylcholine receptor IgG","Placenta","Transient neonatal myasthenia"],["Secretory IgA","Milk, highest in colostrum","Mucosal protection in the infant gut"]],"caption":"Only IgG crosses the placenta, whether it is protective or pathogenic; IgA reaches the infant through milk."},{"type":"p","text":"Plasma cells in the lactating breast home there from gut-associated lymphoid tissue, so milk IgA targets organisms the mother has met in her own gut. Dimeric IgA joined by J chain is carried across mammary epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor, and the retained secretory component resists digestion. Little is absorbed; it acts in the lumen by immune exclusion, binding microbes and toxins so they cannot attach. Milk also supplies lactoferrin, lysozyme, and oligosaccharides that act as decoy receptors."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting newborn serology","text":"A healthy-appearing newborn has Toxoplasma IgG in cord blood but no Toxoplasma IgM. Step 1: IgG crosses by FcRn, so the IgG may be entirely maternal; it shows the mother was infected at some time, not that the infant was. Step 2: IgM cannot cross, so its absence means there is no evidence the fetus mounted its own response. Step 3: if the IgG is only maternal it will fall steadily over the following months, whereas an infected infant keeps or raises its titer. A positive Toxoplasma IgM in the neonate, by contrast, would point directly to congenital infection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C04","obj":"RE-11.4","title":"Rh alloimmunization and hemolytic disease of the fetus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The D antigen of the Rh system (RhD) is a red cell membrane protein encoded by RHD. People who lack it, usually because both copies of RHD are deleted, are RhD-negative, about 15% of people of European ancestry and fewer in most other populations. RhD is strongly immunogenic, but unlike anti-A and anti-B, anti-D is not naturally present; it forms only after exposure to RhD-positive red cells through pregnancy or transfusion."},{"type":"p","text":"In pregnancy, exposure comes from fetomaternal hemorrhage. The largest bleed usually occurs at delivery, but miscarriage, induced abortion, ectopic pregnancy, amniocentesis, abdominal trauma, and antepartum bleeding can also sensitize. The primary response is slow and mostly IgM, which cannot cross the placenta, and it usually develops after the first infant is born. That pregnancy is therefore usually spared, but memory B cells remain."},{"type":"flow","title":"From sensitization to hydrops","steps":["RhD-negative mother, RhD-positive fetus","Fetal red cells enter mother, usually at delivery","Primary IgM response and memory B cells","Later RhD-positive pregnancy recalls memory","Anti-D IgG crosses placenta by FcRn","Splenic macrophages destroy coated fetal red cells","Fetal anemia, extramedullary hematopoiesis, heart failure","Hydrops fetalis"],"caption":"Sensitization in one pregnancy sets up antibody-mediated hemolysis in the next."},{"type":"p","text":"In a later RhD-positive pregnancy, even a small bleed recalls memory B cells, which now make class-switched IgG anti-D. FcRn carries it to the fetus, where it binds RhD on red cells. This is type II hypersensitivity, antibody against a fixed cell-surface antigen, not immune complex disease. Anti-D activates complement poorly, so destruction is mainly extravascular: fetal splenic macrophages bind the Fc portion of coated cells through Fc gamma receptors and phagocytose them."},{"type":"p","text":"Anemia drives erythropoietin release, so the marrow, liver, and spleen release nucleated red cells (erythroblastosis fetalis), and extramedullary hematopoiesis enlarges the liver and spleen. Severe anemia causes high-output heart failure, and hepatic hematopoiesis and congestion impair albumin synthesis and portal flow; together these produce hydrops fetalis. Before birth the placenta carries unconjugated bilirubin to the mother's liver. After birth the infant's immature UDP-glucuronosyltransferase is overwhelmed, jaundice appears within the first day, and unconjugated bilirubin can cross the blood-brain barrier and stain the basal ganglia (kernicterus). The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is positive on the infant's red cells, and the indirect test detects anti-D in maternal serum."},{"type":"table","title":"Rh versus ABO hemolytic disease","cols":["Feature","RhD","ABO"],"rows":[["Typical setting","RhD-negative mother, RhD-positive fetus","Group O mother, group A or B fetus"],["Antibody origin","Requires prior sensitization","Preexisting IgG anti-A and anti-B in group O"],["First pregnancy","Usually spared","Can be affected"],["Severity","Can be severe, with hydrops","Usually mild; jaundice more than anemia"],["Why","Strong antigen; antibody bound only by red cells","A and B weak on fetal cells; antigen on other tissues absorbs antibody"]],"caption":"Both are type II hypersensitivity mediated by maternal IgG."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the first baby is spared and ABO mismatch protects","text":"An RhD-negative, group O woman has had one RhD-positive baby. Step 1: sensitization needs fetal red cells in her circulation, usually at that delivery, and the response that follows is slow and mostly IgM, which does not cross the placenta, so the first baby escaped. Step 2: if that baby was group A, her natural anti-A would have destroyed the fetal cells before RhD was presented, making sensitization less likely. Step 3: if she was sensitized, memory B cells make IgG anti-D in the next RhD-positive pregnancy. Passive anti-D given at exposure works like step 2, clearing RhD-positive cells before a primary response; it cannot erase memory once formed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C05","obj":"RE-11.5","title":"Vaginal defenses and bacterial vaginosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The vagina is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Estrogen thickens it and loads the intermediate and superficial cells with glycogen. As cells shed, host alpha-amylase breaks glycogen into maltose and related sugars, which lactobacilli (mainly Lactobacillus crispatus, L. gasseri, L. jensenii, and L. iners) ferment to lactic acid. This keeps the pH at about 3.8 to 4.5 in reproductive-age women. Undissociated lactic acid inhibits anaerobes, Gardnerella, and many sexually transmitted pathogens. Lactobacilli also occupy epithelial binding sites, and some strains produce bacteriocins and hydrogen peroxide."},{"type":"p","text":"Cervical mucus, antimicrobial peptides (defensins, secretory leukocyte protease inhibitor, lactoferrin), and antibodies add protection. Secretory IgA is present, but unlike most mucosal surfaces the lower female genital tract also contains substantial IgG, much of it derived from serum."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the defense depends on estrogen, it is weak before puberty and after menopause, when the epithelium is thin, glycogen and lactobacilli are scarce, and the pH is above 4.5. Menstrual blood and semen, both near neutral or alkaline, raise the pH transiently."},{"type":"flow","title":"Loss of lactobacilli to bacterial vaginosis","steps":["Douching, new or multiple partners, menses","Lactobacilli and lactic acid decline","pH rises above 4.5","Gardnerella forms biofilm on epithelial cells","Anaerobes overgrow and produce amines","Clue cells, thin gray discharge, fishy odor"],"caption":"Bacterial vaginosis is a shift in the whole community, not infection by a single new organism."},{"type":"p","text":"In bacterial vaginosis (BV), Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes such as Prevotella, Mobiluncus, and Atopobium vaginae increase many-fold. Gardnerella biofilm coats squamous cells, producing clue cells. Anaerobes generate amines (putrescine and cadaverine by decarboxylating amino acids, and trimethylamine) that raise the pH further; adding potassium hydroxide (KOH) volatilizes them, giving a fishy odor (whiff test). Bacterial sialidases and proteases break down protective mucins and IgA. Neutrophils are few, so this is a vaginosis rather than a vaginitis. Loss of the acid barrier raises the risk of acquiring sexually transmitted infections including HIV, of pelvic inflammatory disease, and of preterm birth."},{"type":"table","title":"Vaginal pH and inflammation by cause","cols":["Condition","pH","Key finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Normal, reproductive age","3.8 to 4.5","Lactobacilli, no clue cells","Lactic acid from glycogen"],["Bacterial vaginosis","Above 4.5","Clue cells, positive whiff test, few neutrophils","Lactobacilli lost; anaerobes and Gardnerella"],["Trichomoniasis","Above 4.5","Motile trichomonads, many neutrophils","Protozoan infection with inflammation"],["Candidiasis","4.5 or below","Pseudohyphae, thick white discharge","Yeast tolerates acid; favored by antibiotics, high estrogen, diabetes"]],"caption":"A raised pH points toward loss of lactobacilli or trichomoniasis; candidiasis occurs at normal pH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C06","obj":"RE-11.6","title":"Immunity to HPV and the L1 virus-like particle vaccine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a nonenveloped double-stranded DNA virus that reaches basal keratinocytes through microabrasions, especially at the cervical transformation zone. Its life cycle follows epithelial differentiation: basal cells keep the genome as an episome and express early proteins (E1, E2, E6, E7) at low levels, while the capsid proteins L1 and L2 are made only in upper differentiating layers, and virions are released as surface cells are shed."},{"type":"p","text":"There is no viremia and no cell lysis, so there is little inflammation; capsid antigen appears only in cells about to be lost; and E6 and E7 dampen interferon signaling. Natural infection therefore induces weak, inconsistent neutralizing antibody. Even so, most infections and low-grade lesions regress, usually within about 1 to 2 years, through cell-mediated immunity: Th1 CD4 T cells and cytotoxic CD8 T cells recognize early proteins such as E2, E6, and E7 on infected keratinocytes, and regressing lesions contain dense T cell infiltrates. Antibody cannot do this, because basal cells hold viral DNA inside the cell and display no capsid."},{"type":"p","text":"L1, the major capsid protein, self-assembles when expressed alone in yeast or insect cells into virus-like particles (VLPs). These display the conformational neutralizing epitopes of the virion but contain no DNA, so they cannot infect or transform. Their dense, repetitive surface strongly activates B cells, producing type-specific neutralizing IgG at titers well above those after natural infection. IgG transudates into cervical mucus and exudes at sites of microtrauma, where virions must first bind the basement membrane before entering basal cells, so the virus is neutralized before entry. The same logic makes the vaccine prophylactic only: it cannot reach an established infection. HPV 16 and 18 cause about 70% of cervical carcinomas, and HPV 6 and 11 cause most anogenital warts."},{"type":"table","title":"Two arms of HPV immunity","cols":["Feature","Neutralizing antibody","Cell-mediated immunity"],"rows":[["Main source","Vaccine; weak after natural infection","Natural infection"],["Target","L1 on free virions","Early proteins (E2, E6, E7) in infected cells"],["Action","Blocks new infection","Clears existing infection and low-grade lesions"],["Lost in HIV","Vaccine responses weaker at low CD4 counts","Impaired as CD4 count falls"]],"caption":"Antibody prevents infection; T cells end it."},{"type":"p","text":"When CD4 T cells are depleted by HIV or T cell function is suppressed after transplantation, HPV infections persist, are often multiple and multifocal, and progress more often to high-grade cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) and invasive carcinoma, and anal and vulvar neoplasia rise as well. Persistence allows HPV DNA to integrate and E6 and E7 to disable p53 and Rb (RE-10). Invasive cervical carcinoma is an AIDS-defining illness."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would vaccine-induced anti-L1 antibody clear an existing low-grade cervical lesion?","explain":"No. Infected basal cells carry viral DNA intracellularly and make no L1, so there is nothing for neutralizing antibody to bind; clearance needs T cells against early proteins.","check":"Compare with the table on the two arms of HPV immunity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-11-01-C07","obj":"RE-11.7","title":"Salpingitis, tubal scarring, infertility, and ectopic pregnancy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis (serovars D to K) and Neisseria gonorrhoeae infect columnar epithelium of the endocervix and can ascend through the endometrium to the fallopian tubes. Salpingitis is the central lesion of pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). Chlamydial infection is asymptomatic in most women, so it often goes untreated, persists, and recurs. Neither organism leaves durable protective immunity, partly because of antigenic variation of gonococcal pili and Opa proteins and the many serovars of chlamydial major outer membrane protein."},{"type":"p","text":"Most tubal damage comes from the host response rather than the organism. Chlamydia is an obligate intracellular bacterium; infected epithelial cells release IL-1, IL-6, IL-8 (CXCL8), and TNF, recruiting neutrophils and then lymphocytes. Clearance requires a Th1 response with IFN-gamma, but that response also injures the mucosa. IFN-gamma induces indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase, which depletes tryptophan; chlamydiae that cannot replicate enter a persistent form that continues to release antigens such as chlamydial heat shock protein 60 (HSP60). On reinfection, sensitized T cells respond faster and more strongly, so each episode does more harm, and antibodies to chlamydial HSP60 are associated with tubal infertility and ectopic pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"N. gonorrhoeae attaches to nonciliated cells by pili and Opa proteins and passes through them. Ciliated cells are killed indirectly: released lipooligosaccharide (LOS) and peptidoglycan fragments induce TNF, which causes ciliated cells to slough. An intense neutrophilic exudate produces acute purulent salpingitis, and pus can leak from the fimbriae into the pelvis (pyosalpinx, tubo-ovarian abscess) or track to the liver capsule (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Salpingitis to ectopic pregnancy or infertility","steps":["Chlamydial or gonococcal cervicitis, often silent","Ascent to the fallopian tube","Cytokines, neutrophils, and Th1 response injure mucosa","Ciliated cells lost; plicae fuse","Fibrosis and adhesions narrow or close the tube","Partial narrowing: embryo implants in tube","Complete blockage: hydrosalpinx, infertility"],"caption":"The same scarring process produces ectopic pregnancy or tubal factor infertility depending on how much of the lumen remains."},{"type":"p","text":"Repair replaces mucosa with fibrous tissue. The plicae (mucosal folds) lose cilia and fuse, the lumen narrows, the fimbriae can close, and adhesions tether the tube to the ovary and pelvic wall. Complete distal occlusion with trapped fluid forms a hydrosalpinx and causes tubal factor infertility. Partial damage favors ectopic pregnancy: fertilization occurs normally in the ampulla, but lost cilia and a distorted lumen slow transport, so the blastocyst is still in the tube when it is ready to implant, about 6 to 7 days after fertilization. The risk of infertility rises with each episode of PID."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a woman with several past chlamydial infections have a higher risk of ectopic pregnancy than one with a single treated infection?","explain":"Each reinfection meets sensitized T cells, producing a stronger inflammatory response and more cilia loss and fibrosis, which slows embryo transport.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on chlamydial pathogenesis and the flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology set the stage. Sertoli cell tight junctions, the layered trophoblast, the glycogen-rich vaginal epithelium, the transformation zone, and the ciliated tubal mucosa each determine where antigen is hidden, where it is exposed, and which cells an infection can reach. Biochemistry supplies the effectors: pH-dependent binding by FcRn, lactic acid from glycogen, amines from anaerobic metabolism, tryptophan depletion by indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase, and bilirubin conjugation that the newborn liver cannot yet keep up with."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and pathology then explain the outcomes. Two kinds of tolerance appear here: sequestration with local suppression in the testis, and concealment with NK inhibition at the placenta. Both are local, so systemic responses still occur when antigen escapes, as in antisperm antibodies and Rh alloimmunization. Antibody and T cells divide the work against HPV, so a T cell deficit predicts persistence, while in the fallopian tube the T cell response that clears Chlamydia is itself the cause of scarring. Many details, such as the relative weight of each tolerance mechanism or the role of chlamydial HSP60, come from experimental and association studies and remain incompletely settled."}],"summary":["Sperm antigens appear after central tolerance forms; Sertoli tight junctions and local TGF-beta, IL-10, Fas ligand, and regulatory T cells keep them tolerated.","Trauma, torsion, vasectomy, or mumps orchitis exposes sperm antigens, and antisperm antibodies agglutinate sperm and impair motility and fertilization.","Autoimmune orchitis is primary hypogonadism: tubular loss raises FSH through low inhibin B, and Leydig loss lowers testosterone and raises LH.","Trophoblast lacks HLA-A, HLA-B, and class II; extravillous trophoblast expresses HLA-G and HLA-E, which inhibit uterine NK cell missing-self killing.","FcRn binds IgG at acidic endosomal pH and releases it at neutral pH, so only IgG crosses the placenta; neonatal IgM is fetal and suggests congenital infection.","Milk secretory IgA from gut-derived plasma cells protects the infant gut by immune exclusion; maternal IgG wanes to a low point at about 3 to 6 months.","Rh disease is type II hypersensitivity: anti-D IgG from a sensitized RhD-negative mother causes extravascular fetal hemolysis, anemia, and hydrops; the first pregnancy is usually spared.","Estrogen-driven glycogen feeds lactobacilli that keep vaginal pH at or below 4.5; their loss allows Gardnerella and anaerobes, giving clue cells, amines, and pH above 4.5.","HPV L1 VLP vaccines induce neutralizing IgG that blocks entry; T cells clear established infection, so HIV and other T cell deficits raise cervical cancer risk.","Repeated chlamydial and gonococcal salpingitis destroys cilia and causes fibrosis, leading to ectopic pregnancy with partial narrowing and infertility with complete blockage."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunologic tolerance; hypersensitivity disorders; immunity at mucosal surfaces"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The male reproductive system, Sertoli cells and the blood-testis barrier"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of infancy and childhood, fetal hydrops and immune hydrops"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The female genital tract, pelvic inflammatory disease and cervical neoplasia"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Chlamydiae; Neisseriae; papillomaviruses"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pregnancy and lactation"}]},"RE-12":{"unit":"RE-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01","title":"Reproductive Pathogens","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the organisms that cause sexually transmitted infections, vaginitis, toxic shock, and infections passed from mother to fetus or newborn. For each organism it explains structure, growth requirements, virulence factors, and how disease develops, then connects those features to the laboratory findings used to identify it: Gram stain, oxidase and sugar tests, selective media, dark-field microscopy, wet mount, KOH preparation, serology, and histology. Board questions usually present a smear, a culture result, a lesion, or a newborn finding and ask you to name the organism or explain the mechanism.","prerequisites":["Bacterial cell envelope: gram-positive and gram-negative walls, peptidoglycan, lipopolysaccharide","Viral structure: envelope, capsid, and genome type","Antigen processing and presentation on MHC class II to CD4 T cells","Complement activation, opsonization, and the membrane attack complex","Cell cycle control by p53 and the retinoblastoma protein (Rb)"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology","how":"This chapter's organisms illustrate IgA protease, complement evasion, superantigens, and placental transfer of IgG but not IgM."},{"to":"Pathology of the female genital tract","how":"High-risk HPV drives cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and carcinoma at the transformation zone."},{"to":"Embryology","how":"The timing of fetal infection relative to organogenesis in weeks 3 to 8 explains why early TORCH infections cause structural defects."},{"to":"Neuroscience","how":"HSV latency in sensory ganglia and the late neurologic lesions of syphilis link microbiology to the peripheral and central nervous systems."},{"to":"Cardiovascular system","how":"Syphilitic aortitis and the patent ductus arteriosus of congenital rubella are cardiovascular outcomes of reproductive pathogens."}],"terms":[{"term":"Oxidase test","def":"A test that detects cytochrome c oxidase; Neisseria species are positive."},{"term":"Antigenic variation","def":"Repeated change in the structure of a surface antigen, such as gonococcal pilin, that lets an organism escape existing antibodies."},{"term":"Elementary body","def":"The small, rigid, metabolically inactive infectious form of Chlamydia that survives outside cells and enters host cells."},{"term":"Reticulate body","def":"The larger, metabolically active intracellular form of Chlamydia that divides by binary fission inside an inclusion."},{"term":"Dark-field microscopy","def":"Microscopy that lights the specimen obliquely so thin organisms such as Treponema pallidum appear bright against a dark background."},{"term":"Latency","def":"Persistence of a viral genome in a host cell with no production of virus, from which the virus can later reactivate."},{"term":"Koilocyte","def":"A squamous cell infected by HPV, with a wrinkled, enlarged nucleus surrounded by a clear perinuclear halo."},{"term":"Clue cell","def":"A vaginal squamous cell so heavily coated with bacteria that its borders are obscured, typical of bacterial vaginosis."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A microbial protein that binds MHC class II and the T cell receptor V-beta region outside the peptide groove, activating a large fraction of T cells."},{"term":"CAMP test","def":"A test in which group B streptococci enhance the hemolysis of Staphylococcus aureus beta-hemolysin, producing an arrowhead zone."},{"term":"Nontreponemal test","def":"A syphilis screening test (RPR, VDRL) that detects antibody to a cardiolipin-lecithin-cholesterol antigen and whose titer tracks disease activity."},{"term":"TORCH","def":"A grouping of congenital infections: Toxoplasma, Other agents, Rubella, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C01","obj":"RE-12.1","title":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus whose paired cocci have flattened facing sides, giving a kidney bean shape. It infects only humans and dies quickly with drying or cold, so it spreads by direct mucosal contact during sex or from the birth canal to a newborn's eyes. It attaches to columnar and cuboidal epithelium (urethra, endocervix, rectum, pharynx, conjunctiva) rather than to the stratified squamous epithelium of the adult vagina."},{"type":"h","text":"Laboratory identification"},{"type":"p","text":"Like all Neisseria, the gonococcus is oxidase positive and catalase positive. It produces acid from glucose but not maltose, while Neisseria meningitidis uses both, which is the classic way to separate the two. It is fastidious and grows best on chocolate agar in about 5% CO2. Thayer-Martin agar is chocolate agar with vancomycin (suppresses gram-positive organisms), colistin (suppresses most other gram-negative organisms), and nystatin (suppresses fungi), with trimethoprim added in the modified version to suppress Proteus, so commensals from genital, rectal, and pharyngeal sites do not overgrow it. In a man with urethral discharge, a Gram stain showing gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils is highly sensitive, but endocervical smears are much less reliable, so nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) is the usual test."},{"type":"table","title":"Gonococcal virulence factors","cols":["Factor","Action","Consequence"],"rows":[["Type IV pili","Attach to nonciliated columnar cells; recombination of silent pilin copies into the expressed gene keeps changing pilin","No lasting immunity, frequent reinfection, no vaccine"],["Opa proteins","Tight binding and entry into epithelial cells; switch on and off (phase variation)","Invasion and further antigenic change"],["Lipooligosaccharide (LOS)","Endotoxin with a short sugar chain instead of an O antigen; host sialic acid added to LOS resists complement","Neutrophil influx, purulent discharge, serum resistance"],["IgA1 protease","Cleaves secretory IgA1 at its hinge","Weakens mucosal antibody defense"],["Transferrin and lactoferrin receptors","Take iron directly from host proteins","Growth on iron-poor mucosa"]],"caption":"Unlike the meningococcus, the gonococcus has no polysaccharide capsule and depends on these surface factors."},{"type":"flow","title":"From mucosal attachment to tubal scarring","steps":["Pili attach to nonciliated columnar epithelial cells","Opa proteins mediate tight binding and transcytosis","Organisms reach subepithelial tissue","LOS and peptidoglycan fragments trigger TNF and neutrophils","Purulent urethritis or cervicitis","Ascent damages ciliated tubal cells: scarring, infertility, ectopic pregnancy"],"caption":"Gonococcal disease is driven largely by the inflammatory response to LOS and released cell wall fragments."},{"type":"p","text":"Men usually have a purulent urethral discharge with dysuria. Women are often asymptomatic or have cervicitis, and ascending infection causes pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) and, occasionally, perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome). Neonatal conjunctivitis typically appears 2 to 5 days after birth. Bacteremic spread causes disseminated gonococcal infection with arthritis, tenosynovitis, and sparse pustular skin lesions. People lacking terminal complement components (C5 to C9) are prone to recurrent disseminated Neisseria infection because the membrane attack complex is the main way serum kills these organisms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient treated for gonorrhea last year is infected again by the same strain type. Why did the first infection not protect her?","explain":"Pilin and Opa proteins change by recombination and phase variation, so antibodies made against last year's surface proteins no longer bind, and IgA protease degrades the mucosal antibody that remains.","check":"The virulence factor table: pili, Opa, and IgA1 protease rows."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C02","obj":"RE-12.2","title":"Chlamydia trachomatis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis is a small gram-negative-type bacterium that can grow only inside living host cells (obligate intracellular). It imports ATP from the host through an ATP/ADP exchange transporter and draws on host metabolites, so it does not grow on cell-free culture media. Its envelope has an outer membrane with lipopolysaccharide but very little peptidoglycan; the rigidity of the infectious form comes instead from outer membrane proteins cross-linked by disulfide bonds. The major outer membrane protein (MOMP) varies between strains and defines the serovars."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Chlamydial developmental cycle","steps":["Elementary body attaches to a columnar epithelial cell","Enters by endocytosis; inclusion avoids lysosomal fusion","Elementary body converts to a reticulate body","Reticulate bodies divide by binary fission using host ATP","Reticulate bodies convert back to elementary bodies","Cell lysis or extrusion releases elementary bodies to infect new cells"],"caption":"The cycle takes about 2 to 3 days. Only the elementary body is infectious; only the reticulate body divides."},{"type":"p","text":"The two forms suit two settings. The elementary body is small and tough enough to survive outside cells, while the reticulate body is fragile but metabolically active. Inclusions are seen in infected cells with Giemsa stain, and C. trachomatis inclusions contain glycogen that stains with iodine. Cell culture is possible but slow, so NAAT of urine or genital swabs is the standard diagnostic test. Much of the tissue damage comes from the host response: repeated or persistent infection drives chronic inflammation and scarring, which explains blinding trachoma and tubal infertility."},{"type":"table","title":"Serovars and their diseases","cols":["Serovars","Target","Disease and mechanism"],"rows":[["A, B, C","Conjunctival epithelium; spread by hands, fomites, and flies","Trachoma: repeated follicular conjunctivitis, eyelid scarring, inturned lashes, corneal scarring, blindness"],["D to K","Genital and conjunctival columnar epithelium","Urethritis, cervicitis, PID, epididymitis, proctitis, reactive arthritis; neonatal conjunctivitis (about 5 to 14 days) and afebrile pneumonia with staccato cough (about 4 to 12 weeks)"],["L1, L2, L3","Lymphatics and mononuclear phagocytes in lymph nodes","Lymphogranuloma venereum: small painless ulcer, then painful inguinal nodes that may suppurate; proctocolitis"]],"caption":"Serovar differences in MOMP and tissue tropism explain why one species causes eye, genital, and lymphatic disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Genital infection with serovars D to K is often silent, especially in women, so ascending infection can scar the fallopian tubes before any symptoms prompt testing. LGV serovars are more invasive: they cross the epithelium, multiply in macrophages, and spread to regional nodes, producing granulomatous lymphadenitis. Because both chlamydia and gonorrhea cause urethritis, cervicitis, PID, and neonatal conjunctivitis, the timing of neonatal eye disease and the absence of organisms on Gram stain help point to Chlamydia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C03","obj":"RE-12.3","title":"Treponema pallidum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum subspecies pallidum, the cause of syphilis, is a tightly coiled spirochete only about 0.1 to 0.2 micrometers wide. Its flagella lie in the periplasmic space between the inner and outer membranes (periplasmic flagella, or endoflagella), so the whole cell rotates like a corkscrew and can bore through viscous tissue and cross endothelium. Its outer membrane carries very few exposed proteins, which gives antibodies little to bind and helps the organism persist for years."},{"type":"p","text":"Because it is thinner than the resolving limit of ordinary light microscopy after Gram staining, it is seen by dark-field microscopy of fresh exudate from a chancre or condyloma latum, where it appears as a bright, motile spiral. Oral lesions are unsuitable because commensal mouth treponemes look the same. In tissue, silver stains or immunohistochemistry show it. It cannot be grown on standard laboratory media: it has a reduced genome, lacks many biosynthetic pathways, is microaerophilic, and divides only about once every 30 hours. Diagnosis therefore rests mainly on serology."},{"type":"p","text":"Transmission is by direct contact with moist primary or secondary lesions, and across the placenta at any stage of pregnancy. Fetal infection is most likely when maternal syphilis is early and organisms are abundant in the blood. The characteristic tissue lesion is endarteritis obliterans (concentric thickening of small arteries) with a plasma cell rich infiltrate, which explains the indurated chancre and the late damage to the aorta and nervous system."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of acquired syphilis","cols":["Stage","Timing","Findings and mechanism"],"rows":[["Primary","About 3 weeks after contact (range about 10 to 90 days)","Painless, indurated, clean-based chancre; painless rubbery regional nodes"],["Secondary","Weeks to a few months later","Spirochetemia: rash including palms and soles, condylomata lata, generalized lymphadenopathy, fever"],["Latent","Months to years","No signs; positive serology only"],["Tertiary","Years to decades","Gummas; aortitis from endarteritis of the vasa vasorum, causing ascending aortic aneurysm; tabes dorsalis and Argyll Robertson pupils"]],"caption":"Neurosyphilis can occur at any stage; tabes dorsalis is a late form."},{"type":"table","title":"Serologic tests","cols":["Feature","Nontreponemal (RPR, VDRL)","Treponemal (FTA-ABS, TP-PA, enzyme or chemiluminescence immunoassays)"],"rows":[["Antigen","Cardiolipin-lecithin-cholesterol, a host-type lipid","T. pallidum proteins"],["Result after cure","Titer falls and often becomes negative","Usually stays positive for life"],["Use","Screening and following activity by titer","Confirming that antibody is specific"],["False positives","Pregnancy, SLE and antiphospholipid antibodies, acute viral infections, injection drug use","Uncommon; other treponemal infections such as yaws"]],"caption":"Nontreponemal titers measure activity; treponemal tests measure past or present exposure."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading syphilis serology","text":"A pregnant woman has a positive RPR at a titer of 1:2 and a negative TP-PA. A treponemal test is the specific one, so the negative result means this RPR is a biological false positive, which pregnancy alone can cause. Now compare a man with a negative RPR and a positive TP-PA. The treponemal antibody shows past or present infection, while the negative nontreponemal test usually means treated past infection, though very early primary or late latent syphilis can look the same. Third case: an RPR of 1:64 with a positive TP-PA means active infection, and a fourfold or greater fall in titer later indicates response. If secondary syphilis is strongly suspected but a very high antibody level gives a falsely negative undiluted RPR (prozone effect), diluting the serum reveals the reaction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C04","obj":"RE-12.4","title":"HSV-2 and human papillomavirus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both viruses have double-stranded DNA genomes and replicate in the host cell nucleus, but they differ in structure, in how they leave the cell, and in what they do over the long term. HSV-2 kills the cells it replicates in and hides in neurons; HPV completes its life cycle without killing cells and, for high-risk types, can drive malignant transformation."},{"type":"table","title":"HSV-2 compared with HPV","cols":["Feature","HSV-2","HPV"],"rows":[["Family","Herpesviridae","Papillomaviridae"],["Envelope","Yes; fragile, so spread needs close contact","No; resists drying and survives on surfaces"],["Genome","Linear dsDNA, about 150 kb","Circular dsDNA, about 8 kb"],["Target cell","Mucosal epithelium, then sensory neurons","Basal keratinocytes reached through microabrasions"],["Effect on cell","Lytic: ballooning, multinucleated giant cells, intranuclear (Cowdry A) inclusions","Productive without lysis: koilocytes in upper layers"],["Long-term behavior","Latency in sacral sensory ganglia, recurrent outbreaks","Persistent infection; high-risk types can transform cells"],["Genital lesion","Grouped painful vesicles on a red base that become shallow ulcers","Warts (types 6, 11) or dysplasia and carcinoma (types 16, 18)"]],"caption":"The envelope explains why HSV is fragile outside the body and HPV is hardy."},{"type":"cycle","title":"HSV-2 latency and recurrence","steps":["Replication in genital epithelium produces painful vesicles","Virus enters sensory nerve endings","Retrograde axonal transport to sacral dorsal root ganglia","Latency: episomal genome, only latency-associated transcripts made","Stress, fever, or immunosuppression reactivates replication","Anterograde transport returns virus to the same dermatome"],"caption":"No viral proteins are made during latency, so infected neurons are invisible to CD8 T cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The first episode is usually the most severe, with fever, tender inguinal nodes, and many lesions, because there is no prior immunity. Recurrences are milder and occur in the same area. Virus is also shed intermittently from normal-looking skin, which accounts for much transmission. HSV-1 now causes a substantial share of genital herpes; it then becomes latent in sacral ganglia, while oral HSV-1 is latent in the trigeminal ganglion. PCR of lesion fluid is the preferred test."},{"type":"p","text":"HPV infects basal keratinocytes but expresses its capsid proteins (L1, L2) and assembles virions only in the differentiating upper layers, so virus is shed with desquamated cells. There is no cell lysis, no viremia, and little inflammation, which lets infection persist. In low-risk types 6 and 11, the E6 and E7 proteins bind p53 and Rb weakly and the genome stays episomal; they cause condyloma acuminatum (exophytic genital warts) and recurrent respiratory papillomatosis. High-risk types 16 and 18 (with others such as 31, 33, and 45) cause most cervical cancers and also anal, oropharyngeal, vulvar, vaginal, and penile carcinomas. Type 16 is the most common type in both squamous carcinoma and adenocarcinoma, while type 18 is relatively more frequent in adenocarcinoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"High-risk HPV transformation","steps":["Viral DNA integrates into the host genome, disrupting E2","Loss of E2 repression increases E6 and E7 expression","E6 targets p53 for ubiquitin-mediated degradation","E7 binds Rb, releasing E2F to drive S phase","Damaged cells keep dividing; mutations accumulate","Dysplasia at the transformation zone progresses to carcinoma"],"caption":"Loss of Rb function causes p16 to accumulate, a tissue marker of high-risk HPV activity."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why do HPV 6 and 11 cause warts but rarely cancer, while 16 and 18 cause cancer?","explain":"Low-risk E6 and E7 bind p53 and Rb weakly and the genome stays episomal under E2 control. High-risk E6 and E7 bind strongly, and integration removes E2 restraint, so p53 and Rb are inactivated.","check":"The transformation flow and the paragraph on low-risk types."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C05","obj":"RE-12.5","title":"Trichomonas, Candida, and Gardnerella","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The healthy vagina is acidic. Estrogen causes vaginal squamous cells to store glycogen, lactobacilli break down glycogen products to lactic acid, and the pH stays at about 3.8 to 4.5. Lactobacilli also make hydrogen peroxide and bacteriocins that hold other organisms back. Each cause of vaginitis disturbs this system differently, so pH, odor, and a quick look under the microscope usually identify it."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the common causes of vaginitis","cols":["Feature","Bacterial vaginosis (Gardnerella and anaerobes)","Candida albicans","Trichomonas vaginalis"],"rows":[["Organism type","Gram-variable rod with mixed anaerobes","Yeast (fungus)","Flagellated protozoan"],["Discharge","Thin, gray, homogeneous, fishy","Thick, white, curdlike","Frothy, yellow-green, often malodorous"],["pH","Above 4.5","4.5 or below (normal)","Above 4.5"],["Amine (whiff) test with KOH","Positive","Negative","Often positive"],["Microscopy","Clue cells on saline wet mount, few neutrophils","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae on KOH preparation","Motile pear-shaped trophozoites on saline wet mount, many neutrophils"],["Inflammation","Minimal","Itching, redness, swelling","Marked; punctate cervical hemorrhages (strawberry cervix)"]],"caption":"Candida leaves the pH acid; bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis raise it."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial vaginosis is a shift in flora rather than a classic infection. Lactobacilli decline and Gardnerella vaginalis and anaerobes overgrow. Anaerobic metabolism produces amines such as trimethylamine, putrescine, and cadaverine, which raise the pH and smell fishy, especially when KOH makes them volatile. Gardnerella forms biofilms on squamous cells, creating clue cells. Because there is little tissue invasion, there are few white cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Trichomonas vaginalis is a pear-shaped protozoan with four anterior flagella, an undulating membrane, and an axostyle. It exists only as a trophozoite with no cyst form, so it cannot survive long outside the body and spreads sexually. It adheres to and damages squamous epithelium, provoking a strong neutrophil response. Motility is lost as the specimen cools, so a wet mount must be read promptly, and even then it misses many cases; NAAT is more sensitive. Infected men are usually asymptomatic."},{"type":"p","text":"Candida albicans is part of normal flora, so candidal vaginitis is usually endogenous rather than sexually transmitted. It grows as budding yeast and, in tissue, forms pseudohyphae (chains of elongated buds with constrictions at the joins) and true hyphae. KOH dissolves epithelial cells and debris so the fungal elements stand out. Overgrowth follows anything that removes lactobacilli or raises glycogen and glucose: antibiotic exposure, pregnancy and high estrogen states, poorly controlled diabetes, and impaired cell-mediated immunity."},{"type":"example","title":"Working from pH and microscopy","text":"A discharge sample has a pH of 5.5. That rules out Candida as the sole cause, because candidiasis keeps the normal acid pH, and leaves bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis. The saline wet mount shows many neutrophils but no clue cells. Bacterial vaginosis causes little inflammation, so heavy neutrophils point toward Trichomonas even if no motile organism is seen, since the wet mount often misses it. A NAAT for Trichomonas is the logical next test. If instead the pH were 4.2 with itching and thick white discharge, the KOH preparation would be the slide to examine for pseudohyphae."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C06","obj":"RE-12.6","title":"Haemophilus ducreyi and the TSST-1 superantigen","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Haemophilus ducreyi and chancroid"},{"type":"p","text":"Haemophilus ducreyi is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that lines up in chains and parallel rows on Gram stain, a pattern described as a school of fish. It is fastidious, requires hemin (X factor), and grows only on enriched media, so culture is difficult and NAAT is used where available. It enters through small breaks in genital skin and provokes an intense neutrophil and macrophage response. The result is a soft, painful ulcer with ragged, undermined edges and a gray-yellow purulent base, often with tender, usually one-sided inguinal nodes (buboes) that may suppurate. Chancroid is uncommon in the United States but, like other ulcer diseases, increases HIV transmission."},{"type":"table","title":"Genital ulcers compared","cols":["Disease","Organism","Ulcer","Nodes"],"rows":[["Primary syphilis","Treponema pallidum","Painless, indurated, clean base, usually single","Painless, rubbery, often both sides"],["Chancroid","Haemophilus ducreyi","Painful, soft, ragged, purulent base","Painful, may suppurate"],["Genital herpes","HSV-2 or HSV-1","Painful grouped vesicles that become shallow ulcers","Tender, mainly in the first episode"],["Lymphogranuloma venereum","Chlamydia trachomatis L1 to L3","Small, painless, often unnoticed","Painful, matted, may suppurate"]],"caption":"Pain and induration of the ulcer, together with the node pattern, separate the four classic genital ulcer diseases."},{"type":"h","text":"Toxic shock syndrome toxin 1"},{"type":"p","text":"A conventional protein antigen must be processed into peptides that sit in the groove of an MHC class II molecule, and only T cells whose receptors fit that particular peptide respond, a tiny fraction of the T cell pool. TSST-1, secreted by some strains of Staphylococcus aureus, skips this step. The intact toxin binds the outside of the MHC class II molecule (mainly the alpha chain), away from the peptide groove, and also binds the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain (V-beta). Every T cell carrying a compatible V-beta segment is activated whatever its peptide specificity, which can be up to about 20% of all T cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"How TSST-1 causes shock","steps":["S. aureus grows on a retained tampon, packing, or wound","TSST-1 is secreted and crosses the mucosa","Toxin links MHC class II to T cell receptor V-beta","A large fraction of T cells activates at once","Massive IL-2, IFN-gamma, TNF, and IL-1 release","Vasodilation and capillary leak: hypotension and multiorgan injury"],"caption":"The disease is a cytokine storm caused by the toxin, not by invasion of the bloodstream."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture follows the cytokines: fever of 38.9 degrees C (102 degrees F) or higher, hypotension, a diffuse macular erythroderma, involvement of three or more organ systems, and desquamation of the palms and soles about 1 to 2 weeks later. Menstrual cases were linked to high-absorbency tampons, which provide air and a near-neutral pH that favor toxin production; nonmenstrual cases follow surgical wounds, nasal packing, and postpartum infection. Blood cultures are usually negative in staphylococcal toxic shock because the bacteria stay local. People who lack antibody to TSST-1 are susceptible, which is why most cases occur in younger patients."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a superantigen activate thousands of times more T cells than a conventional antigen?","explain":"It ignores peptide specificity. Binding outside the groove to MHC class II and to a V-beta segment shared by many clones activates all those clones, instead of the rare clone that recognizes one peptide.","check":"The TSST-1 paragraph on conventional versus superantigen recognition."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-12-01-C07","obj":"RE-12.7","title":"Group B streptococcus and the TORCH organisms","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae (group B streptococcus)"},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus agalactiae is a gram-positive coccus in chains, catalase negative, with the Lancefield group B cell wall carbohydrate. It gives a narrow zone of beta-hemolysis and is resistant to bacitracin, which separates it from bacitracin-sensitive group A Streptococcus pyogenes. In the CAMP test, its CAMP factor acts together with the beta-hemolysin of S. aureus to produce an arrowhead of enhanced hemolysis where the two growths meet. It also hydrolyzes hippurate."},{"type":"p","text":"It colonizes the lower gastrointestinal and genital tracts of about 10% to 30% of pregnant women, often intermittently. Its main virulence factor is a polysaccharide capsule rich in sialic acid, which recruits the complement regulator factor H and limits C3b deposition, so the organism resists opsonization and phagocytosis. Protection depends on maternal IgG against the capsule, which crosses the placenta mainly in the third trimester; a preterm infant or one whose mother lacks this antibody is poorly defended. Screening is done by rectovaginal culture late in pregnancy because carriage fluctuates and only colonization near delivery predicts exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"Neonatal group B streptococcal disease","cols":["Feature","Early onset","Late onset"],"rows":[["Age","First week of life, most within 24 hours","About 1 week to 3 months"],["Acquisition","Ascending or birth canal exposure; aspiration of infected fluid","Vertical or from caregivers and environment"],["Main forms","Sepsis and pneumonia","Bacteremia with meningitis more prominent"],["Risk factors","Preterm birth, membranes ruptured 18 hours or more, intrapartum fever, heavy maternal colonization or GBS bacteriuria, previous affected infant","Preterm birth"]],"caption":"Group B streptococcus is a leading cause of neonatal sepsis and meningitis; it is a perinatal pathogen, not a TORCH agent."},{"type":"h","text":"TORCH organisms"},{"type":"p","text":"Most TORCH organisms reach the fetus during a maternal infection with a blood-borne phase: they infect the placenta and then the fetal circulation. Infection during organogenesis (weeks 3 to 8) is most likely to cause malformations, while later infection causes growth restriction and inflammation of formed organs. Maternal IgG crosses the placenta through the neonatal Fc receptor but IgM does not, so pathogen-specific IgM in a newborn means the fetus made it in response to its own infection. The O group includes syphilis, varicella-zoster virus, parvovirus B19, Listeria, HIV, and Zika virus, and some lists add others. HSV is the exception: most neonatal HSV is acquired during delivery from genital shedding."},{"type":"table","title":"Classic congenital and perinatal infections","cols":["Organism","Main route","Classic findings and mechanism"],"rows":[["Toxoplasma gondii","Transplacental after maternal primary infection","Chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, scattered intracranial calcifications; transmission rises later in pregnancy but severity falls"],["Treponema pallidum","Transplacental, any stage of pregnancy","Early: rhinitis, rash including palms and soles, hepatosplenomegaly, osteochondritis. Late: notched incisors, saddle nose, saber shins, interstitial keratitis, deafness"],["Parvovirus B19","Transplacental","Infects erythroid precursors via P antigen: fetal anemia, high-output heart failure, hydrops"],["Rubella virus","Transplacental; worst in first trimester","Cataracts, sensorineural deafness, patent ductus arteriosus, blueberry muffin rash from dermal hematopoiesis"],["Cytomegalovirus","Transplacental after primary or recurrent maternal infection","Most common congenital infection: sensorineural hearing loss, periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, petechiae"],["HSV-2 or HSV-1","Mostly during delivery","Skin, eye, and mouth vesicles; encephalitis; disseminated disease"]],"caption":"Congenital CMV is the leading infectious, nongenetic cause of sensorineural hearing loss in children."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These organisms connect several Part I disciplines. Microbiology supplies the identifying features, but immunology explains much of the disease: gonococcal antigenic variation and IgA protease defeat mucosal antibody, terminal complement deficiency predisposes to disseminated Neisseria, chlamydial scarring is largely immune mediated, the group B streptococcal capsule recruits factor H, and TSST-1 bypasses antigen processing altogether. Pathology adds the tissue reactions: endarteritis obliterans in syphilis, koilocytes and p53 and Rb inactivation in HPV-driven neoplasia, and placental infection in TORCH disease timed against embryologic organogenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"The laboratory findings are tools with limits. Gram stain is reliable for male urethral gonorrhea but not for endocervical specimens or for Chlamydia and Treponema, which it cannot show well; wet mounts miss many Trichomonas infections; nontreponemal tests give false positives; and newborn findings overlap among TORCH agents, so serology, NAAT, and PCR confirm what the pattern suggests. Several infections often coexist, because the same exposures transmit them and genital ulcers ease the entry of other pathogens, including HIV."}],"summary":["Neisseria gonorrhoeae is an oxidase-positive gram-negative diplococcus that uses glucose but not maltose, grows on Thayer-Martin agar, and evades immunity through pilin and Opa variation, IgA1 protease, and sialylated LOS.","Chlamydia trachomatis is obligately intracellular; the elementary body infects and the reticulate body divides; MOMP defines serovars A to C (trachoma), D to K (genital and neonatal), and L1 to L3 (LGV).","Treponema pallidum is too thin for Gram stain, is seen by dark-field microscopy, cannot be grown on standard media, and crosses the placenta at any stage of pregnancy.","Nontreponemal titers (RPR, VDRL) track activity and can be falsely positive; treponemal tests confirm infection and usually stay positive for life.","HSV-2 is an enveloped, lytic dsDNA virus that becomes latent in sacral sensory ganglia and reactivates in the same dermatome.","HPV is a nonenveloped circular dsDNA virus; types 6 and 11 cause warts, while high-risk 16 and 18 E6 and E7 inactivate p53 and Rb and drive carcinoma.","Vaginitis sorts by pH and microscopy: clue cells and amines in bacterial vaginosis, pseudohyphae on KOH with normal pH in Candida, motile flagellates with heavy inflammation in Trichomonas.","Haemophilus ducreyi is a gram-negative coccobacillus in school of fish chains causing a painful soft ulcer, unlike the painless indurated chancre of syphilis.","TSST-1 is a superantigen that links MHC class II to T cell receptor V-beta outside the groove, activating up to about 20% of T cells and causing a cytokine storm.","Group B streptococcus is beta-hemolytic, bacitracin resistant, and CAMP positive, and causes neonatal sepsis and meningitis; most TORCH agents cross the placenta, while neonatal HSV is mainly acquired at delivery."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Neisseriae; Haemophilus species"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Chlamydiae; Spirochetes"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Herpesviruses; papillomaviruses; staphylococci and streptococci; parasitic protozoa"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases; The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes, section on superantigens"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Birth defects and teratogens, infectious agents"}]},"RE-13-A":{"unit":"RE-13-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01","title":"Reproductive Endocrine Conditions: Amenorrhea, Anovulation, Abnormal Bleeding, and Ovarian Failure","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter applies the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis to the menstrual and fertility disorders that arise from disordered hormone signaling. It covers amenorrhea and how FSH, LH, prolactin, TSH, and estradiol localize it; anovulation and the unopposed estrogen that follows; abnormal uterine bleeding; menopause and primary ovarian insufficiency; androgen excess; menstrual pain; and the main categories of female infertility. Board questions usually give a short history with a hormone panel and ask where the defect lies or what a hormone change will do to the endometrium, bone, or vessels. If you can trace each finding to a broken feedback loop, these questions become predictable.","prerequisites":["Hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis: pulsatile GnRH, FSH, LH, estradiol, inhibin, and progesterone feedback","Follicular, ovulatory, and luteal phases of the ovarian cycle and proliferative and secretory endometrium","Steroidogenesis in theca, granulosa, and adrenal cortex, including aromatase and 21-hydroxylase","Mullerian duct development and the anatomy of the uterus, tubes, and vagina","Prolactin regulation by dopamine and TRH"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrine system (pituitary, thyroid, adrenal)","how":"Prolactinomas, hypothyroidism, Sheehan syndrome, and congenital adrenal hyperplasia all present as menstrual disorders."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal system (bone remodeling)","how":"Estrogen restrains RANKL-driven osteoclast activity, so every low-estrogen state in this chapter causes bone loss."},{"to":"Reproductive pathology (endometrium and myometrium)","how":"Unopposed estrogen leads to endometrial hyperplasia and endometrioid carcinoma, and leiomyomas and adenomyosis are the structural mimics of hormonal bleeding."},{"to":"Reproductive pathogens (pelvic inflammatory disease)","how":"Chlamydia and gonorrhea scar the fallopian tubes, which is the mechanism of tubal infertility and ectopic pregnancy."},{"to":"Genetics (sex chromosome and triplet repeat disorders)","how":"Turner syndrome, androgen insensitivity, and the FMR1 premutation explain several causes of amenorrhea and early ovarian failure."}],"terms":[{"term":"Amenorrhea","def":"Absence of menstruation, classed as primary (menses never started) or secondary (menses stopped after starting)."},{"term":"Anovulation","def":"A cycle in which no oocyte is released, so no corpus luteum forms and no progesterone is made."},{"term":"Unopposed estrogen","def":"Estrogen stimulation of the endometrium without the progesterone that normally converts it to secretory tissue and limits growth."},{"term":"Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism","def":"Low ovarian steroid output with high FSH and LH because the ovary has failed and feedback is lost."},{"term":"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","def":"Low ovarian steroid output with low or inappropriately normal FSH and LH because the hypothalamus or pituitary has failed."},{"term":"Asherman syndrome","def":"Intrauterine adhesions after injury to the basal endometrium, usually from curettage, causing amenorrhea or light menses with normal hormones."},{"term":"PALM-COEIN","def":"The FIGO classification of abnormal uterine bleeding into structural causes (PALM) and nonstructural causes (COEIN)."},{"term":"Menopause","def":"Permanent cessation of menses from follicle depletion, diagnosed after 12 months without menses."},{"term":"Primary ovarian insufficiency","def":"Loss of normal ovarian function before age 40, with high FSH and low estradiol."},{"term":"Hirsutism","def":"Excess terminal hair in a male pattern in a woman, from androgen excess or increased follicle sensitivity to androgens."},{"term":"Virilization","def":"Masculinization beyond hair growth, such as clitoromegaly, voice deepening, and temporal balding, which implies high androgen levels."},{"term":"Mittelschmerz","def":"Unilateral lower abdominal pain at midcycle related to ovulation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C01","obj":"RE-13-A.1","title":"Primary versus secondary amenorrhea","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Regular menses require an intact chain: pulsatile GnRH from the hypothalamus drives FSH and LH, the ovary responds with follicle growth and estradiol, ovulation produces a corpus luteum that secretes progesterone, and the fall of progesterone at the end of the luteal phase sheds the endometrium through an open cervix and vagina. A break anywhere in that chain stops menstruation, and the two definitions of amenorrhea simply describe when the break appeared."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary amenorrhea is no menarche by age 15 in a person with normal secondary sexual characteristics, or no menarche by age 13 when breast development has not begun. The earlier cutoff exists because absent breast development at 13 already shows that estrogen has never been adequate. Secondary amenorrhea is the absence of menses for 3 months in someone who previously had regular cycles, or for 6 months if cycles were irregular."},{"type":"p","text":"Pregnancy is the most common cause of secondary amenorrhea, and it can also present as primary amenorrhea if conception occurs before the first period, so hCG is always the first test. In pregnancy, hCG from the trophoblast acts on the LH receptor to keep the corpus luteum secreting progesterone, so the lining never undergoes withdrawal shedding."},{"type":"h","text":"Using the physical examination as a bioassay"},{"type":"p","text":"Two findings sort primary amenorrhea quickly. Breast development (thelarche) proves that the gonads made estrogen at some point. A normal uterus on examination or ultrasound proves that Mullerian ducts formed, which also means anti-Mullerian hormone from testes was absent during development. Pubic and axillary hair reflect androgen action and help separate the two conditions in which breasts are present but the uterus is missing."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary amenorrhea by breast development and uterus","cols":["Breasts","Uterus","Main causes","Key mechanism or clue"],"rows":[["Absent","Present","Gonadal dysgenesis such as Turner syndrome or 46,XY Swyer syndrome (high FSH); Kallmann syndrome or constitutional delay (low FSH)","No estrogen; FSH level separates ovarian failure from central failure"],["Present","Absent","Mullerian agenesis (46,XX, normal pubic hair); complete androgen insensitivity (46,XY, scant pubic hair)","Mullerian ducts failed to form, or testes made AMH; in androgen insensitivity, testosterone is aromatized to estradiol"],["Present","Present","Imperforate hymen or transverse vaginal septum; otherwise evaluate as for secondary amenorrhea","Outflow obstruction gives cyclic pelvic pain and trapped blood (hematocolpos)"],["Absent","Absent","Rare 46,XY states: testicular regression after AMH has acted, or 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","Testes made AMH, so ducts regressed, but no estrogen was produced"]],"caption":"Gonadal dysgenesis is the most common cause of primary amenorrhea and Mullerian agenesis the second most common."},{"type":"p","text":"Kallmann syndrome deserves special mention because its mechanism is developmental: GnRH neurons arise in the olfactory placode and migrate into the hypothalamus with olfactory axons. When that migration fails, the result is GnRH deficiency with anosmia or hyposmia, low FSH and LH, and absent puberty. Secondary amenorrhea, by contrast, is usually acquired: pregnancy, functional hypothalamic suppression, PCOS, hyperprolactinemia, thyroid disease, ovarian insufficiency, and intrauterine adhesions account for most cases, and the next section shows how hormones sort them."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 16 year old has normal breast development, sparse pubic and axillary hair, and a blind-ending vagina with no palpable uterus. What is her karyotype likely to be, and why are the breasts normal?","explain":"Androgen insensitivity in a 46,XY person: testes secrete AMH, so Mullerian structures regress, and testosterone that cannot act is aromatized to estradiol, which develops the breasts. Sparse hair reflects absent androgen action.","check":"Compare with the second row of the primary amenorrhea table and the paragraph on the physical examination as a bioassay."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C02","obj":"RE-13-A.2","title":"Localizing amenorrhea with FSH, LH, prolactin, TSH, and estradiol","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Once pregnancy is excluded, the hormone panel locates the break in the axis by applying one rule of negative feedback. Estradiol and inhibin B from the ovary hold FSH and LH down. If the ovary fails, the brake is released and gonadotropins rise, so high FSH with low estradiol (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism) places the defect in the ovary. If the hypothalamus or pituitary fails, the ovary is simply unstimulated, so FSH, LH, and estradiol are all low, or gonadotropins are inappropriately normal for the low estradiol (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism)."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolactin and TSH are measured with FSH because both cause central suppression. Prolactin inhibits pulsatile GnRH release, acting at least partly through hypothalamic kisspeptin neurons, so hyperprolactinemia from a prolactinoma, from dopamine antagonist drugs, or from stalk compression lowers FSH and LH. In primary hypothyroidism, raised TRH stimulates lactotrophs as well as thyrotrophs, so prolactin rises and menses stop; restoring thyroid hormone lowers TRH and with it prolactin."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormone patterns that localize amenorrhea","cols":["Level","FSH and LH","Estradiol","Other clues","Examples"],"rows":[["Hypothalamus","Low or normal","Low","Low energy availability, weight loss, stress, anosmia","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, Kallmann syndrome"],["Pituitary","Low","Low","High prolactin, mass effect, postpartum hemorrhage with failure to lactate","Prolactinoma, Sheehan syndrome, pituitary adenoma"],["Ovary","High (FSH above about 25 IU/L)","Low","Low AMH, vasomotor symptoms","Primary ovarian insufficiency, Turner syndrome, menopause"],["Outflow tract","Normal","Normal","Cyclic hormonal symptoms but no bleeding","Asherman syndrome, cervical stenosis"]],"caption":"PCOS usually shows normal FSH, normal or raised LH, normal estradiol, and raised androgens, so it does not fit the low-estrogen rows."},{"type":"flow","title":"Classic stepwise evaluation of secondary amenorrhea","steps":["Negative hCG excludes pregnancy","Measure TSH and prolactin for central suppression","Progestin challenge: bleeding means estrogen present and outflow open","No bleed: give estrogen then progestin","Still no bleed: outflow tract defect such as Asherman syndrome","Bleed after estrogen: hypoestrogenic, so FSH separates ovary from brain"],"caption":"The progestin and estrogen challenges test, in order, whether the endometrium is estrogen primed and whether the outflow tract can respond at all."},{"type":"p","text":"Asherman syndrome is the outflow defect boards favor. Vigorous curettage, especially of a recently pregnant uterus, or endometrial infection, destroys the stratum basalis, the layer that regenerates the functionalis each cycle. Fibrous adhesions then bridge the cavity. Because the ovary and pituitary are untouched, FSH, LH, and estradiol are normal, ovulation continues, and the patient may notice cyclic breast or mood changes without bleeding; the endometrium simply cannot respond."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading a hormone panel","text":"A 27 year old marathon runner with a body mass index of 17 kg/m2 has had no menses for 8 months. hCG is negative, TSH and prolactin are normal, FSH and LH are low normal, and estradiol is low. Step 1: low estradiol means the endometrium is not being built, so a progestin challenge would produce little or no bleeding. Step 2: if the ovary had failed, FSH would be high; it is not, so the ovary is unstimulated rather than broken. Step 3: normal prolactin and TSH exclude the two common pituitary-level suppressors. Step 4: low energy availability, with low leptin and stress-related CRH, slows GnRH pulses. The defect is hypothalamic, and the low estradiol predicts bone loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C03","obj":"RE-13-A.3","title":"Anovulation and the consequences of unopposed estrogen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ovulation depends on a switch in estradiol feedback. Early in the follicular phase, estradiol suppresses FSH and LH. When the dominant follicle raises estradiol above about 200 pg/mL and holds it there for about 2 days, the hypothalamus and pituitary switch to positive feedback, GnRH pulses and pituitary sensitivity rise, and the LH surge follows. Ovulation occurs about 34 to 36 hours after the surge begins. Anovulation results when this sequence fails, either because no follicle matures enough to deliver the estradiol signal, or because the estradiol signal arrives but the hypothalamus cannot respond with a surge."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of anovulation and their mechanisms","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Estrogen status"],"rows":[["Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)","Fast GnRH pulses favor LH; LH and insulin drive theca androgen; relative FSH lack arrests follicles","Present, acyclic"],["Hyperprolactinemia","Prolactin slows GnRH pulses, lowering FSH and LH","Low to normal"],["Hypothyroidism","High TRH raises prolactin; altered SHBG and steroid clearance disturb feedback","Variable"],["Hyperthyroidism","Raised SHBG and altered steroid metabolism disrupt cycles, often with light or absent menses","Variable"],["Low energy availability","Low leptin, raised cortisol, and reduced kisspeptin slow GnRH pulses","Low"],["Physiologic: first years after menarche, perimenopause","Immature positive feedback or failing follicle supply","Present, irregular"]],"caption":"Only causes that leave estrogen present produce the unopposed estrogen effects; severe hypothalamic suppression instead produces low estrogen and bone loss."},{"type":"h","text":"Why PCOS is self-sustaining"},{"type":"p","text":"In PCOS, more frequent GnRH pulses favor LH over FSH. LH stimulates theca cells to make androstenedione and testosterone, and insulin resistance with compensatory hyperinsulinemia amplifies theca androgen output and suppresses hepatic sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), raising free testosterone. Granulosa cells, short of FSH-driven aromatase, cannot convert the androgen load, and the local androgen excess arrests follicles at the small antral stage, producing many small follicles. Androstenedione is aromatized to estrone in adipose tissue, giving a steady, acyclic estrogen level that never rises sharply enough to trigger a surge."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The PCOS loop","steps":["Rapid GnRH pulses raise LH relative to FSH","LH and insulin increase theca androgen","Follicles arrest; no dominant follicle, no LH surge","Adipose aromatization gives steady acyclic estrone","Acyclic estrogen and low progesterone keep GnRH pulses rapid"],"caption":"Without a luteal phase, progesterone never slows GnRH pulse frequency, so the LH predominance persists."},{"type":"h","text":"Unopposed estrogen"},{"type":"p","text":"Without ovulation there is no corpus luteum and therefore no progesterone. Progesterone normally converts proliferative endometrium to secretory endometrium, reduces estrogen receptor expression, and induces 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, which converts estradiol to the weaker estrone. When estrogen acts without that brake, the endometrium keeps proliferating. Clinically this gives irregular cycles with intervals of amenorrhea punctuated by heavy, prolonged bleeding, described in the next section. Over months to years the glands crowd and proliferate abnormally, producing endometrial hyperplasia. Hyperplasia with atypia (endometrial intraepithelial neoplasia) is the direct precursor of endometrioid (type I) endometrial carcinoma, which is why obesity, PCOS, and estrogen-only exposure share the same cancer risk."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a woman with PCOS have a higher risk of endometrial carcinoma than a woman with functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, even though both are anovulatory?","explain":"In PCOS, estrogen is present but never opposed by progesterone, so the endometrium proliferates continuously. In hypothalamic amenorrhea, estrogen itself is low, so the endometrium is thin and inactive.","check":"Compare the estrogen status column of the anovulation table with the paragraph on unopposed estrogen."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C04","obj":"RE-13-A.4","title":"Anovulatory bleeding (AUB-O) versus structural causes of abnormal uterine bleeding","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dysfunctional uterine bleeding is the older name for bleeding caused by ovulatory dysfunction. In the current FIGO PALM-COEIN classification it is called abnormal uterine bleeding from ovulatory dysfunction (AUB-O), and it is diagnosed only after structural causes, pregnancy, and coagulopathy have been excluded."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal menstruation is orderly because it is a progesterone withdrawal event. When the corpus luteum regresses, the fall in progesterone triggers prostaglandin release, spiral arteriole vasoconstriction, and ischemia of the whole functionalis at once. The layer sheds synchronously down to the basalis, and platelet plugs and vasoconstriction stop the bleeding within about 4 to 7 days. In AUB-O, a thick estrogen-driven endometrium without secretory change outgrows its stromal and vascular support. Fragile, dilated vessels break down in patches at different times, so shedding is asynchronous: some areas bleed while others are still proliferating. The result is irregular timing, unpredictable volume, and often prolonged heavy bleeding (estrogen breakthrough bleeding). A fall in estrogen, as when a follicle undergoes atresia, can also trigger bleeding (estrogen withdrawal bleeding). Biopsy shows proliferative or disordered proliferative endometrium with no secretory change, or hyperplasia."},{"type":"table","title":"PALM-COEIN classification of abnormal uterine bleeding","cols":["Category","Cause","Mechanism and typical clue"],"rows":[["P (structural)","Polyp","Localized overgrowth of endometrium with thick-walled vessels; intermenstrual or postmenopausal spotting"],["A (structural)","Adenomyosis","Endometrial glands and stroma within myometrium; enlarged, globular, tender uterus with heavy painful menses"],["L (structural)","Leiomyoma","Benign smooth muscle tumor; submucosal myomas enlarge and distort the cavity and impair contraction, causing heavy menses"],["M (structural)","Malignancy and hyperplasia","Endometrial hyperplasia or carcinoma; postmenopausal bleeding requires exclusion of carcinoma"],["C (nonstructural)","Coagulopathy","von Willebrand disease is the classic inherited cause; heavy bleeding since menarche"],["O (nonstructural)","Ovulatory dysfunction","Anovulation with unopposed estrogen; irregular, unpredictable bleeding"],["E (nonstructural)","Endometrial","Primary disorder of local hemostasis or inflammation in an ovulatory woman"],["I (nonstructural)","Iatrogenic","Exogenous hormones, anticoagulants, intrauterine devices"],["N (nonstructural)","Not yet classified","Rare or poorly defined entities such as arteriovenous malformation"]],"caption":"Structural causes can be seen on imaging or histology; nonstructural causes cannot."},{"type":"p","text":"The pattern of bleeding helps separate the groups. Ovulatory dysfunction produces irregular cycles because the timing of shedding no longer follows a luteal phase. Structural lesions usually leave ovulation intact, so cycles stay regular but menses become heavier (leiomyoma, adenomyosis) or spotting appears between them (polyp). Postmenopausal bleeding is a separate red flag: atrophy is its most common cause, but endometrial carcinoma must be excluded because it occurs mainly after menopause, often driven by unopposed estrone from adipose aromatization."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C05","obj":"RE-13-A.5","title":"Menopause and the effects of estrogen loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A woman is born with about 1 to 2 million oocytes, about 300,000 to 400,000 remain at puberty, and atresia continues throughout life, so only a few hundred are ever ovulated. Menopause occurs when the follicle pool falls below the number needed to sustain cycles, at an average age of about 51 years in the United States. It is diagnosed retrospectively after 12 months without menses. Perimenopause (the menopausal transition) is the preceding period of irregular cycles."},{"type":"p","text":"The first hormonal change is a fall in inhibin B, which granulosa cells of small antral follicles secrete in proportion to their number. Losing inhibin removes selective negative feedback on FSH, so FSH rises in the early follicular phase while estradiol is still normal or even high. Faster follicle recruitment shortens cycles at first; later, cycles lengthen and become anovulatory, which is why perimenopause brings irregular, sometimes heavy, bleeding. After the final follicles are gone, estradiol and progesterone fall, both negative feedback signals are lost, and FSH and LH rise, with FSH rising more than LH. The ovarian stroma continues to secrete androgens, and estrone from adipose aromatization becomes the main circulating estrogen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Feedback changes from perimenopause to menopause","steps":["Follicle pool shrinks","Inhibin B falls; early FSH rise","Cycles shorten, then become anovulatory","Estradiol and inhibin A also fall","FSH and LH remain high; estrone from fat dominates"],"caption":"FSH rises first and most because it alone is controlled by inhibin as well as estradiol."},{"type":"table","title":"Effects of estrogen loss and their mechanisms","cols":["Effect","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats)","Loss of estrogen feedback enlarges hypothalamic KNDy (kisspeptin, neurokinin B, dynorphin) neurons; increased neurokinin B signaling narrows the thermoneutral zone, so small rises in core temperature trigger vasodilation and sweating"],["Atrophic vaginitis (genitourinary syndrome of menopause)","Thin vaginal epithelium stores less glycogen; fewer lactobacilli make less lactic acid, pH rises above about 4.5, causing dryness, dyspareunia, and recurrent urinary infection"],["Osteoporosis","Estrogen normally lowers RANKL and raises osteoprotegerin and induces osteoclast apoptosis; its loss increases osteoclast number and lifespan, with the fastest trabecular bone loss in the first years"],["Higher cardiovascular risk","LDL rises and HDL falls, and loss of estrogen-stimulated endothelial nitric oxide synthase reduces vasodilation"]],"caption":"Each consequence follows from loss of a specific estrogen action, so the same list applies to any hypoestrogenic state."},{"type":"p","text":"Elevated FSH confirms the diagnosis biochemically but is not required in a woman over 45 with typical symptoms, because FSH fluctuates widely during the transition. A woman under 40 with the same profile has primary ovarian insufficiency, described next."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In early perimenopause, why can FSH be raised while estradiol is still normal?","explain":"Inhibin B falls first as the antral follicle pool shrinks. Losing inhibin removes the FSH-specific brake before estradiol output declines.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the first hormonal change and the feedback flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C06","obj":"RE-13-A.6","title":"Primary ovarian insufficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary ovarian insufficiency (POI), also called premature ovarian failure, is loss of ovarian function before age 40. It affects about 1% of women. It is defined by at least 4 months of oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea with FSH above about 25 IU/L on two occasions at least 4 weeks apart, and estradiol is low. Unlike menopause, POI is often intermittent: some women ovulate occasionally and a small minority conceive, which is why the term insufficiency replaced failure."},{"type":"p","text":"The hormone profile is the same as menopause for the same reason: depleted or nonfunctioning follicles make little estradiol and inhibin, so negative feedback is lost and FSH and LH rise. Anti-Mullerian hormone, made by granulosa cells of small growing follicles, is low or undetectable and reflects the shrunken pool. The consequences of hypoestrogenism, including vasomotor symptoms, vaginal atrophy, bone loss, and greater cardiovascular risk, arrive years earlier than in natural menopause."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of primary ovarian insufficiency","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Turner syndrome (45,X and mosaics)","Two X chromosomes are needed to maintain oocytes; accelerated atresia leaves streak gonads, so most present with primary amenorrhea, while mosaics may menstruate briefly"],["FMR1 premutation (about 55 to 200 CGG repeats)","Premutation mRNA is toxic to follicles; a minority of carriers, about 20%, develop POI; the full mutation causing fragile X syndrome does not"],["Autoimmune oophoritis","Lymphocytic attack on steroid-producing theca cells of growing follicles; steroid cell or 21-hydroxylase antibodies mark cases linked to adrenal autoimmunity"],["Chemotherapy and pelvic radiation","Alkylating agents such as cyclophosphamide and ionizing radiation destroy oocytes; risk rises with dose and with age"],["Galactosemia","Galactose metabolites are toxic to the developing ovary"],["Idiopathic","No cause found in most cases"]],"caption":"A karyotype and FMR1 testing are part of the evaluation because Turner mosaicism and premutation carriers are otherwise easily missed."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions are tested. POI is hypergonadotropic, whereas the low-estrogen amenorrhea of hypothalamic suppression is hypogonadotropic, so FSH separates them. And autoimmune POI should prompt thought about the adrenal, because the same autoimmune process can also destroy the adrenal cortex."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C07","obj":"RE-13-A.7","title":"Hirsutism, virilization, and localizing androgen excess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hirsutism is excess terminal hair in a male pattern (upper lip, chin, chest, linea alba, back, inner thighs) in a woman. It occurs when androgens convert fine vellus hairs into coarse pigmented terminal hairs, which depends both on androgen level and on follicle sensitivity, largely through local 5-alpha-reductase conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone. Hirsutism alone indicates mild to moderate androgen excess. Virilization means masculinization beyond hair: clitoromegaly, voice deepening, increased muscle mass, temporal balding, and loss of female body contour. It requires much higher androgen levels, so rapid onset of virilization suggests an androgen-secreting tumor until proven otherwise."},{"type":"p","text":"Localizing the source depends on where each androgen is made. In women, about a quarter of testosterone comes directly from the ovary, about a quarter from the adrenal, and about half from peripheral conversion of androstenedione that both glands secrete. Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate (DHEA-S), by contrast, comes almost entirely from the adrenal zona reticularis, which secretes DHEA largely in its sulfated form. Testosterone therefore reflects total androgen production, while DHEA-S is an adrenal marker."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing androgen excess","cols":["Source","Condition","Testosterone","DHEA-S","Other findings"],"rows":[["Ovary","PCOS","Mildly raised","Normal or mildly raised","Gradual onset from puberty, irregular cycles, insulin resistance"],["Ovary","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor","Markedly raised (above about 150 to 200 ng/dL)","Normal","Rapid virilization, unilateral adnexal mass"],["Adrenal","Nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency)","Mildly raised","Normal or raised","Raised 17-hydroxyprogesterone, rising further after ACTH stimulation"],["Adrenal","Adrenal carcinoma or adenoma","Raised","Markedly raised (above about 700 mcg/dL)","Rapid onset, may also secrete cortisol"]],"caption":"High testosterone with normal DHEA-S points to the ovary; high DHEA-S points to the adrenal."},{"type":"p","text":"In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, cortisol synthesis is impaired, ACTH rises, and the accumulated 17-hydroxyprogesterone is diverted into the androgen pathway. The classic form virilizes a female fetus; the nonclassic form appears after puberty and closely mimics PCOS, which is why 17-hydroxyprogesterone is measured. PCOS remains the most common cause of hirsutism and is diagnosed by the Rotterdam criteria (two of three: ovulatory dysfunction, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovarian morphology) after excluding other causes."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: ovary or adrenal?","text":"A 34 year old has 5 months of worsening facial hair, a deepening voice, and clitoromegaly. Total testosterone is 260 ng/dL, DHEA-S is normal, and 17-hydroxyprogesterone is normal. Step 1: voice change and clitoromegaly are virilization, not simple hirsutism, so androgen levels must be very high, and the short history suggests a tumor. Step 2: testosterone far above about 150 to 200 ng/dL fits a tumor rather than PCOS. Step 3: normal DHEA-S argues against an adrenal source, and normal 17-hydroxyprogesterone excludes CAH. Step 4: the source is ovarian, most classically a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor, and imaging should look for an adnexal mass."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C08","obj":"RE-13-A.8","title":"Primary dysmenorrhea and mittelschmerz","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation without pelvic disease. It usually begins within a year or two after menarche, once cycles become ovulatory, because the mechanism requires a luteal phase followed by progesterone withdrawal. Pain is crampy, lower midline, and may radiate to the back and thighs; it starts with or just before the flow and lasts about 1 to 3 days."},{"type":"p","text":"Progesterone stabilizes lysosomal membranes in endometrial cells. When it falls, phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid, and cyclooxygenase converts it to prostaglandin F2-alpha and prostaglandin E2. Women with dysmenorrhea have higher endometrial and menstrual fluid prostaglandin levels. PGF2-alpha raises resting myometrial tone and the strength and frequency of contractions, and the contractions compress vessels, so the myometrium becomes ischemic, much as exercising muscle does when its blood supply is limited. Prostaglandins also sensitize pain fibers, and when they enter the circulation they cause the nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and headache that often accompany cramps. The relief produced by cyclooxygenase inhibitors confirms this mechanism."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mechanism of primary dysmenorrhea","steps":["Corpus luteum regresses; progesterone falls","Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid","COX produces PGF2-alpha and PGE2","Myometrial hypercontractility and vasoconstriction","Ischemia and sensitized nerve endings cause pain"],"caption":"Anovulatory cycles lack the progesterone withdrawal step, which is why the earliest cycles after menarche are often painless."},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary dysmenorrhea comes from pelvic pathology such as endometriosis, adenomyosis, or leiomyoma. Clues are onset years after menarche, pain that worsens over time or begins before menses, dyspareunia, heavy bleeding, and an abnormal pelvic examination."},{"type":"h","text":"Mittelschmerz"},{"type":"p","text":"Mittelschmerz is unilateral lower abdominal or pelvic pain at midcycle, about 14 days before the next menses, on the side of the ovulating ovary, so it may switch sides from month to month. It lasts from minutes to a day or two. The pain is attributed to stretching of the ovarian capsule by the enlarging follicle just before rupture and to peritoneal irritation by follicular fluid or a small amount of blood released at rupture. Its importance for boards is diagnostic: right-sided mittelschmerz can mimic appendicitis, and timing in the cycle, a negative pregnancy test (to exclude ectopic pregnancy), and the absence of fever help separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-01-C09","obj":"RE-13-A.9","title":"Ovulatory, tubal, and uterine causes of female infertility","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infertility is failure to conceive after 12 months of regular unprotected intercourse, shortened to 6 months for women 35 or older because fecundity falls with age. Conception requires a released oocyte, an open tube where sperm and oocyte meet and the embryo is transported, and a receptive endometrium for implantation; female infertility can be grouped by which of these fails. Male factors contribute in a large share of couples and are evaluated in parallel."},{"type":"table","title":"Female infertility by category","cols":["Category","Examples","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Ovulatory","PCOS, hyperprolactinemia, hypothalamic amenorrhea, primary ovarian insufficiency, thyroid disease","No LH surge or no follicles, so no oocyte is released"],["Tubal","Prior pelvic inflammatory disease, endometriosis, prior pelvic surgery","Scarred, blocked, or deciliated tubes prevent gamete meeting and embryo transport"],["Uterine","Asherman syndrome, submucosal leiomyoma, Mullerian anomalies such as uterine septum","Cavity is obliterated or distorted, so implantation fails or pregnancy is lost"]],"caption":"Ovulatory disorders are the most readily identified group; PCOS is their most common cause."},{"type":"h","text":"Ovulatory infertility"},{"type":"p","text":"Each ovulatory cause works through mechanisms already described: PCOS and hyperprolactinemia prevent a dominant follicle and LH surge, low energy availability slows GnRH pulses, and ovarian insufficiency leaves no follicles to recruit. Ovulation can be confirmed biochemically by a mid-luteal serum progesterone above about 3 ng/mL, which shows that a corpus luteum formed. Regular cycles of 21 to 35 days with premenstrual symptoms also suggest ovulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Tubal infertility"},{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae ascend from the cervix to cause salpingitis. Chlamydia in particular can be silent while inducing a chronic immune response that damages ciliated epithelium and mucosal folds, and repair produces fibrosis, fimbrial adhesions, and distal occlusion with a fluid-filled hydrosalpinx. Partly damaged tubes may still admit sperm yet fail to move the embryo, so the same scarring raises the risk of ectopic pregnancy. Each additional episode of pelvic inflammatory disease increases the risk. Hysterosalpingography shows whether contrast flows through the tubes."},{"type":"h","text":"Uterine infertility"},{"type":"p","text":"In Asherman syndrome, adhesions replace the functionalis and leave little or no endometrium able to undergo decidualization, so implantation fails or early pregnancy is lost. Submucosal leiomyomas project into the cavity, distort its shape, and alter the overlying endometrium and its blood flow, which lowers implantation rates; intramural and subserosal myomas affect fertility much less. Asherman syndrome also causes outflow amenorrhea, and submucosal leiomyoma causes structural heavy bleeding."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The conditions in this chapter are best understood as failures at different levels of one feedback loop. Anatomy and embryology explain the outflow and Mullerian causes, from imperforate hymen to Asherman syndrome. Physiology explains why FSH rises when the ovary fails but falls when the brain fails, and why estrogen without progesterone overstimulates the endometrium. Genetics and immunology explain Turner syndrome, the FMR1 premutation, and autoimmune oophoritis. Microbiology supplies the tubal scarring of pelvic inflammatory disease, and pathology links unopposed estrogen to hyperplasia and endometrioid carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits of this framework matter. Hormone values fluctuate, especially in perimenopause and POI, so a single FSH can mislead, and PCOS does not fit neatly into the high or low gonadotropin categories because it is a disorder of feedback timing rather than of output. Low estrogen and unopposed estrogen are opposite problems with opposite risks: bone loss and cardiovascular risk in the first, endometrial hyperplasia in the second. Deciding which one a patient has is often the key step in a board question."}],"summary":["Primary amenorrhea is no menarche by 15, or by 13 without breast development; secondary is 3 months without menses after regular cycles, and pregnancy is its most common cause.","Breast development shows past estrogen and a uterus shows Mullerian development; together they sort primary amenorrhea.","High FSH with low estradiol localizes amenorrhea to the ovary; low FSH, LH, and estradiol localize it to the hypothalamus or pituitary; normal hormones with no bleeding suggest an outflow defect such as Asherman syndrome.","Prolactin and the high TRH of primary hypothyroidism suppress GnRH pulses, so TSH and prolactin are part of every amenorrhea panel.","Anovulation means no corpus luteum and no progesterone; when estrogen is present, unopposed estrogen causes irregular heavy bleeding, hyperplasia, and endometrioid carcinoma risk.","AUB-O is asynchronous estrogen breakthrough shedding without secretory change, diagnosed after structural PALM causes and coagulopathy are excluded.","Menopause begins with falling inhibin B and early FSH rise; loss of estradiol causes vasomotor symptoms, vaginal atrophy, osteoporosis, and higher cardiovascular risk.","Primary ovarian insufficiency is the menopausal hormone profile before 40, caused by Turner syndrome, FMR1 premutation, autoimmunity, or gonadotoxic therapy, and is often intermittent.","Raised testosterone with normal DHEA-S points to the ovary; raised DHEA-S points to the adrenal; rapid virilization suggests a tumor.","Primary dysmenorrhea is prostaglandin-driven hypercontractility and ischemia after progesterone withdrawal; infertility is ovulatory, tubal, or uterine according to which step of conception fails."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Female Reproductive System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, development of the genital ducts"}]},"RE-13-B":{"unit":"RE-13-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02","title":"Inflammatory Diseases of the Reproductive Tract","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers inflammation of the male and female reproductive tracts and two conditions in which endometrium grows where it does not belong. It begins with local infections of the glans and cervix, covers endometriosis, adenomyosis, endometritis, and orchitis, follows infection upward from the cervix to the tubes and peritoneum, and ends with vulvovaginal disease. NPLEX Part I cases usually give the diagnosis and ask for the organism, the tissue change, the risk factor, or the complication. If you know which epithelium each organism targets, how inflammation heals by scarring, and how estrogen drives misplaced endometrium, you can predict most of the findings.","prerequisites":["Histology of the female tract: squamous vagina and ectocervix, columnar endocervix, ciliated tubal mucosa","Menstrual cycle hormones and the functional and basal layers of the endometrium","Anatomy of the penis, scrotum, testis, epididymis, and vas deferens","Peritoneal spaces: rectouterine pouch and paracolic gutters","Acute and chronic inflammation, healing by fibrosis, and cell-mediated immunity"],"connections":[{"to":"Microbiology of sexually transmitted and opportunistic organisms","how":"The cell biology of Chlamydia, Neisseria, Trichomonas, HSV, and Candida explains which epithelium each infects and what it looks like."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"T-cell immunity controls mucosal Candida, and the immune response to persistent Chlamydia, rather than the organism alone, scars the fallopian tube."},{"to":"Reproductive endocrinology","how":"Estrogen maintains vaginal glycogen and acidity and drives the growth of endometriosis, adenomyosis, and leiomyoma."},{"to":"Pregnancy and the puerperium","how":"Tubal scarring predisposes to ectopic pregnancy, and delivery creates the setting for acute endometritis."},{"to":"Hepatobiliary disease and gynecologic neoplasia","how":"Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis mimics cholecystitis, and lichen sclerosus precedes HPV-independent vulvar squamous cell carcinoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Balanoposthitis","def":"Inflammation of both the glans penis and the inner surface of the foreskin (prepuce)."},{"term":"Phimosis","def":"A foreskin that cannot be retracted over the glans, physiologic in young boys and pathologic when caused by scarring."},{"term":"Mucopurulent cervicitis","def":"Endocervical inflammation with yellow, pus-containing discharge from the os and a friable cervix that bleeds on contact."},{"term":"Cervical ectropion","def":"Columnar endocervical epithelium lying out on the ectocervix, where it is more exposed to infection."},{"term":"Endometrioma","def":"An ovarian cyst lined by endometriotic tissue and filled with old blood, called a chocolate cyst."},{"term":"Coelomic metaplasia","def":"Transformation of peritoneal or ovarian surface mesothelium, derived from coelomic epithelium, into endometrial-type tissue."},{"term":"Chronic endometritis","def":"Persistent endometrial inflammation diagnosed by plasma cells in the endometrial stroma."},{"term":"Epididymo-orchitis","def":"Infection of the epididymis that spreads to the testis, usually ascending from the urethra or infected urine."},{"term":"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)","def":"Ascending infection of the upper female genital tract involving endometrium, tubes, ovaries, or pelvic peritoneum."},{"term":"Hydrosalpinx","def":"A fallopian tube sealed at its fimbriated end and distended with watery fluid after salpingitis."},{"term":"Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome","def":"Inflammation of the liver capsule complicating PID, with right upper quadrant pain and fine capsular adhesions."},{"term":"Pseudohyphae","def":"Chains of elongated yeast cells that stay attached after budding, the invasive form of Candida seen on KOH preparation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C01","obj":"RE-13-B.1","title":"Balanitis and its link to phimosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Balanitis is inflammation of the glans penis. When the inner surface of the foreskin is also inflamed, as it usually is in uncircumcised men, the term is balanoposthitis. The preputial sac between glans and foreskin is warm and moist and collects smegma, a mixture of shed epithelial cells, glandular secretions, and urine residue. When this material is not cleared, it supports overgrowth of organisms that are normally held in check."},{"type":"p","text":"Candida albicans is the most common infectious cause. Bacterial causes include anaerobes, streptococci (groups A and B), Staphylococcus aureus, and Gardnerella vaginalis, sometimes acquired from a sexual partner. Noninfectious balanitis also occurs from irritants, contact dermatitis, and skin diseases such as lichen sclerosus (C10). The inflamed glans is red, swollen, sore, and itchy, sometimes with a discharge under the foreskin; candidal balanitis often shows patchy redness with small papules or a white film, while anaerobic infection tends to be malodorous."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for balanitis and how each works","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Uncircumcised foreskin","Creates a closed, moist preputial sac where smegma and organisms collect"],["Poor hygiene","Smegma and urine residue are not removed from the sac"],["Diabetes mellitus","Glucose in urine residue feeds Candida, and hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil chemotaxis and killing"],["Phimosis","The foreskin cannot be retracted, so the sac cannot be cleaned or drained"]],"caption":"Each risk factor either traps moist secretions under the foreskin or weakens host defense against yeast and bacteria."},{"type":"p","text":"Because diabetes acts as both growth medium and immune defect, recurrent candidal balanitis in an adult man can be the first clue to undiagnosed diabetes mellitus."},{"type":"p","text":"Phimosis is a foreskin that cannot be pulled back over the glans. In infants and young boys it is physiologic, because the inner prepuce is still adherent to the glans, and it usually resolves with age. Acquired phimosis results from scarring. Each episode of balanitis heals by fibrosis, which contracts the preputial opening; the tighter opening traps more secretions, which causes further inflammation and more scarring. This self-perpetuating loop explains why recurrent balanitis and phimosis occur together. If a tight foreskin is forced back behind the glans and cannot be returned (paraphimosis), it acts as a constricting band that blocks venous and lymphatic return and causes painful swelling of the glans. Chronic phimosis with retained smegma and inflammation is also a recognized risk factor for penile squamous cell carcinoma."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Balanitis and phimosis feed each other","steps":["Moist preputial sac traps smegma and urine","Candida or bacteria overgrow, more so with glycosuria","Glans and inner foreskin become inflamed","Healing fibrosis narrows the preputial opening","Phimosis traps more secretions, renewing infection"],"caption":"Scarring from each episode tightens the foreskin, and the tighter foreskin sets up the next episode."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C02","obj":"RE-13-B.2","title":"Mucopurulent cervicitis and its look-alikes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cervix has two epithelia. The ectocervix is covered by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium continuous with the vagina, and the endocervical canal is lined by mucus-secreting simple columnar epithelium. They meet at the squamocolumnar junction. In adolescents, in pregnancy, and with estrogen-containing contraceptives, this junction often lies out on the ectocervix (ectropion), exposing more columnar cells. This matters because the two main causes of cervicitis prefer columnar epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium. Its infectious elementary body enters columnar cells and becomes a replicating reticulate body inside a membrane-bound inclusion, and the cell later releases new elementary bodies. Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram-negative diplococcus, attaches to columnar cells by pili and Opa proteins and is carried across the epithelium, where its lipooligosaccharide triggers a strong neutrophil response. Both cause endocervicitis: mucopurulent discharge from the os and a friable cervix that bleeds when touched with a swab, because the inflamed mucosa is edematous and hypervascular. Many infections, especially chlamydial ones, cause no symptoms, so they persist and can ascend silently (C07). Gonococci may be seen on Gram stain as gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils, though this is less sensitive from the cervix than from the male urethra; chlamydia does not appear on Gram stain, and both are detected by nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT)."},{"type":"p","text":"Two other organisms inflame the cervix but target squamous epithelium and look different. Trichomonas vaginalis, a flagellated protozoan, damages vaginal and ectocervical cells by direct contact and produces punctate subepithelial hemorrhages, the strawberry cervix, with frothy yellow-green malodorous discharge and vaginal pH above 4.5. Herpes simplex virus (HSV-2, and increasingly HSV-1) lyses the squamous cells it infects, producing vesicles that break down into shallow painful ulcers; on the cervix this gives an ulcerative, sometimes necrotic cervicitis, often with vulvar lesions and tender inguinal nodes in a first episode."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the patterns of cervicitis","cols":["Cause","Epithelium targeted","Cervix appearance","Discharge","Laboratory finding"],"rows":[["C. trachomatis","Endocervical columnar","Friable, edematous endocervix, or normal-looking","Mucopurulent, or none","NAAT positive; not seen on Gram stain"],["N. gonorrhoeae","Endocervical columnar","Friable endocervix","Mucopurulent","NAAT positive; gram-negative intracellular diplococci"],["T. vaginalis","Squamous (vagina and ectocervix)","Punctate hemorrhages (strawberry cervix)","Frothy yellow-green; pH above 4.5","Motile flagellated trichomonads on wet mount"],["HSV","Squamous","Vesicles and ulcers, necrotic surface","Variable; ulcers dominate","Multinucleated cells with intranuclear inclusions; PCR positive"]],"caption":"Columnar-cell organisms give mucopurulent endocervicitis; squamous-cell organisms give punctate hemorrhage or ulceration."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C03","obj":"RE-13-B.3","title":"Endometriosis: origin, cyclic bleeding, and symptoms","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Endometriosis is the presence of endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus. It affects about 10% of women of reproductive age and a much larger share of women evaluated for infertility or chronic pelvic pain. The ovaries are the most common site, followed by the uterosacral ligaments, rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas), and pelvic peritoneum; less often it involves bowel, bladder, and surgical scars, and rarely distant sites such as lung. A lesion is confirmed histologically by two of three features: endometrial glands, endometrial stroma, and hemosiderin pigment from old bleeding."},{"type":"table","title":"Theories of origin","cols":["Theory","Proposed mechanism","What it explains best"],"rows":[["Retrograde menstruation","Menstrual tissue flows backward through the tubes and implants on pelvic surfaces","The pelvic and ovarian distribution, concentrated in dependent sites such as the rectouterine pouch"],["Coelomic metaplasia","Peritoneal and ovarian surface mesothelium, derived from coelomic epithelium, differentiates into endometrium","Rare disease in people who have not menstruated"],["Vascular or lymphatic spread","Endometrial cells travel through veins or lymphatics, a benign metastasis","Lesions in lymph nodes, lung, and other distant sites"],["Stem or progenitor cell origin","Circulating progenitor cells, possibly from bone marrow, differentiate at ectopic sites","Distant lesions and the variety of sites"]],"caption":"No single theory explains every case; each accounts best for a different distribution of lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Retrograde menstruation is the most widely accepted explanation, but it occurs in most menstruating women, so implantation and survival depend on other factors. Endometriotic tissue differs from normal endometrium: its stromal cells express aromatase and make their own estrogen, they produce large amounts of prostaglandin E2, which stimulates aromatase further, they resist the growth-restraining effect of progesterone, and they recruit inflammatory cells. Defective peritoneal clearance of refluxed cells and genetic susceptibility also contribute."},{"type":"p","text":"Implants keep their estrogen and progesterone receptors, so they proliferate and bleed with each cycle, but the blood has no way out. Blood and its breakdown products attract macrophages and provoke inflammation followed by fibrosis. Grossly this produces red-blue to yellow-brown nodules (powder-burn lesions). In the ovary, repeated bleeding into a cyst concentrates old blood into an endometrioma, the chocolate cyst. Fibrosis binds tubes, ovaries, uterus, and bowel together and can fix the uterus in retroversion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Endometriosis: from implant to symptoms","steps":["Endometrial glands and stroma lie on ovary or peritoneum","Implants respond to cyclic estrogen and progesterone and bleed","Trapped blood triggers inflammation and prostaglandin release","Fibrosis forms adhesions; ovarian bleeding forms chocolate cysts","Dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, painful defecation, and infertility follow"],"caption":"Cyclic bleeding with no outlet converts hormone-responsive implants into inflammation and scar."},{"type":"p","text":"Each symptom follows from where the inflammation and scarring sit. Dysmenorrhea comes from cyclic bleeding and high local prostaglandin levels; it often begins before menses and is more severe than ordinary cramps. Deep dyspareunia comes from stretching inflamed, scarred uterosacral ligaments and rectouterine pouch during intercourse, and painful defecation means rectal or rectovaginal involvement. Infertility results from adhesions that distort the tube and ovary and block ovum pickup, and from inflammatory peritoneal fluid that impairs gametes, fertilization, and implantation. The uterus itself is normal in size. Symptom severity correlates poorly with the amount of disease. Endometriosis also slightly increases the risk of clear cell and endometrioid ovarian carcinoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"What would happen to endometriotic implants and pelvic pain if ovarian estrogen production stopped, as after menopause?","explain":"Implants depend on estrogen to proliferate and bleed, so they usually regress and cyclic pain lessens, but existing adhesions and fibrosis persist because scar tissue does not depend on hormones.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on hormone receptors in implants and on local aromatase."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C04","obj":"RE-13-B.4","title":"Adenomyosis compared with endometriosis and leiomyoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adenomyosis is the presence of endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, separated from the overlying endometrium by normal muscle. It is usually defined as endometrial tissue more than about 2 to 2.5 mm below the endometrial-myometrial junction. The foci arise from the basal layer (stratum basalis), which grows down into the muscle, and the surrounding smooth muscle undergoes reactive hypertrophy. The result is a globular, uniformly enlarged uterus with a thickened wall, rarely larger than about a 12-week pregnancy. It is most common in parous women in their 40s, and prior uterine surgery such as cesarean delivery or curettage is a risk factor, consistent with disruption of the boundary between endometrium and muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"Heavy menstrual bleeding is explained by an enlarged endometrial surface and by infiltrated myometrium that contracts poorly, since myometrial contraction helps limit menstrual blood loss. Dysmenorrhea comes from prostaglandin release and from swelling and bleeding of foci inside the muscle. Because basalis-derived glands respond less fully to the cycle, the amount of bleeding within foci varies. On examination the uterus is symmetrically enlarged, soft or boggy, and tender, especially around menses."},{"type":"p","text":"Leiomyomas (fibroids) are the most common uterine tumor. They are benign, estrogen-sensitive, clonal proliferations of smooth muscle, often carrying MED12 mutations, and they form discrete, well-circumscribed nodules that push the surrounding muscle aside. That is why they make the uterus irregular and lumpy rather than evenly enlarged. Location predicts symptoms: submucosal tumors distort the cavity and cause heavy bleeding and infertility, while intramural and subserosal tumors press on bladder or rectum. All three conditions are estrogen dependent and often coexist in the same patient."},{"type":"table","title":"Endometriosis, adenomyosis, and leiomyoma","cols":["Feature","Endometriosis","Adenomyosis","Leiomyoma"],"rows":[["Lesion","Glands and stroma outside the uterus","Glands and stroma within the myometrium","Benign clonal smooth muscle tumor"],["Uterus on examination","Normal size; may be fixed and retroverted","Uniformly enlarged, globular, boggy, tender","Irregular, firm, nodular, usually nontender"],["Bleeding","Usually normal amount","Heavy menstrual bleeding","Heavy bleeding, mainly with submucosal tumors"],["Pain","Dysmenorrhea, deep dyspareunia, painful defecation","Dysmenorrhea","Pressure; often none"],["Infertility","Common (adhesions, inflammation)","Possible","With submucosal or cavity-distorting tumors"],["Pathology","Powder-burn implants, chocolate cysts; glands, stroma, hemosiderin","Ill-defined foci deep in hypertrophied muscle; no capsule","Sharply circumscribed whorled gray-white nodules of bland smooth muscle"],["Typical patient","Reproductive age","Parous, about 40 to 50 years","Reproductive age; more common in Black women"]],"caption":"The size and shape of the uterus on examination usually separates the three."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the uterus on examination","text":"A 44-year-old woman with three children has increasingly heavy, painful periods. Step 1: heavy bleeding plus dysmenorrhea narrows the choice to adenomyosis or leiomyoma, since endometriosis does not usually increase menstrual blood loss. Step 2: examine the uterus. A symmetrically enlarged, soft, tender uterus means diffuse infiltration of the wall with reactive hypertrophy, which is adenomyosis. A firm, irregular, lumpy uterus means discrete expanding nodules, which is leiomyoma. Step 3: if instead the uterus were normal in size but fixed, with deep dyspareunia and tender nodularity behind the cervix, the disease would lie outside the uterus, which is endometriosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C05","obj":"RE-13-B.5","title":"Acute and chronic endometritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The closed cervix and its mucus plug normally keep vaginal flora out of the endometrial cavity. Endometritis develops when that barrier is breached and the cavity contains material that supports bacterial growth. The clinical setting and the inflammatory cells separate acute from chronic forms."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute endometritis is most often postpartum. After delivery the placental site is a large raw wound, the decidua is partly necrotic, and blood and lochia fill the cavity, an excellent culture medium. Cesarean delivery is the strongest risk factor; prolonged labor or rupture of membranes, repeated vaginal examinations, chorioamnionitis, manual removal of the placenta, and retained placental fragments add to the risk. Miscarriage or abortion with retained products creates the same setting. The infection is polymicrobial from the lower genital tract: streptococci (including group B), gram-negative rods such as E. coli, Gardnerella, and anaerobes such as Bacteroides and Peptostreptococcus. Group A streptococcus causes rare but rapidly severe puerperal sepsis."},{"type":"p","text":"The patient has fever of 38.0°C (100.4°F) or more in the days after delivery, uterine tenderness, and foul-smelling lochia from anaerobic metabolism. Leukocytosis helps less than usual, because the white cell count normally rises after delivery. Untreated infection extends to the parametrium, pelvic veins (septic pelvic thrombophlebitis), peritoneum, and bloodstream. Histologically, neutrophils infiltrate the endometrium."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic endometritis is persistent low-grade inflammation, often with few symptoms. It is associated with retained products of conception, an intrauterine device, PID (especially chlamydial), and, where it is endemic, tuberculosis, which produces granulomas. Lymphocytes and small lymphoid aggregates are normal in endometrium, and neutrophils are normal during menstruation, so neither proves endometritis. Plasma cells are not normally present; finding them in the stroma, confirmed if needed with CD138 (syndecan-1) staining, marks sustained antigen stimulation and is the diagnostic feature. The inflamed lining sheds and repairs irregularly and is less receptive to an embryo, which explains abnormal uterine bleeding, pelvic pain, infertility, and recurrent pregnancy loss."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute versus chronic endometritis","cols":["Feature","Acute","Chronic"],"rows":[["Setting","After delivery (especially cesarean), miscarriage, or abortion; retained products","Retained products, intrauterine device, PID, tuberculosis"],["Organisms","Polymicrobial genital flora with anaerobes; group A streptococcus rarely","Chlamydia, mixed flora, M. tuberculosis; often none identified"],["Diagnostic histology","Neutrophils infiltrating the endometrium","Plasma cells in the endometrial stroma"],["Presentation","Fever, uterine tenderness, foul lochia","Abnormal bleeding, pelvic pain, infertility"],["Complications","Parametritis, pelvic abscess, septic thrombophlebitis, sepsis","Infertility, recurrent pregnancy loss"]],"caption":"Neutrophils with fever after delivery mean acute endometritis; plasma cells in the stroma mean chronic endometritis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C06","obj":"RE-13-B.6","title":"Mumps orchitis versus bacterial epididymo-orchitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Orchitis is inflammation of the testis. Isolated orchitis is uncommon; most cases are either viral orchitis from mumps or bacterial infection that spreads from the epididymis. The two differ in route, patient age, and consequence."},{"type":"p","text":"Mumps virus, a paramyxovirus, spreads by respiratory droplets, replicates in the upper airway and regional lymph nodes, and reaches glandular organs by viremia. Orchitis develops in roughly a fifth to a third of males who get mumps after puberty, usually about 4 to 8 days after parotitis begins, and is rare before puberty. It is unilateral in most cases. The testis becomes painful and swollen, often with high fever. The lesion is an interstitial orchitis with edema and lymphocytic infiltration. Because the tunica albuginea is a tough, nondistensible capsule, edema raises the pressure inside the testis, compresses the seminiferous tubules, and causes ischemic loss of germinal epithelium. Testicular atrophy follows in a substantial share of affected testes, with reduced sperm counts; complete sterility is uncommon because involvement is usually one-sided. Leydig cells are more resistant than germ cells, so testosterone is less often reduced."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial epididymo-orchitis begins in the epididymis. Organisms ascend backward from the urethra or infected urine along the vas deferens to the tail of the epididymis, then spread to the testis. Age and sexual history predict the organism. In sexually active men under about 35 years, C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae from urethritis predominate, often with dysuria or urethral discharge. In older men, E. coli and other enteric gram-negative rods predominate, reflecting urinary infection from prostatic enlargement, obstruction, or catheterization; enteric organisms also occur in men who have insertive anal intercourse."},{"type":"p","text":"Pain and swelling develop over hours to days, beginning at the posterior epididymis. The cremasteric reflex is preserved and Doppler ultrasound shows increased blood flow from inflammatory hyperemia. Testicular torsion, the key alternative, begins suddenly, abolishes the cremasteric reflex, and shows absent flow. Bacterial infection can cause abscess, testicular infarction from venous compression, and scarring of the epididymal duct, which causes obstructive infertility when bilateral."},{"type":"table","title":"Mumps orchitis and bacterial epididymo-orchitis","cols":["Feature","Mumps orchitis","STI-related epididymo-orchitis","Enteric epididymo-orchitis"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Postpubertal, unvaccinated","Sexually active, under about 35 years","Older men; urinary obstruction or instrumentation"],["Route","Bloodstream (viremia)","Ascending from the urethra","Ascending from infected urine"],["Organism","Mumps virus","C. trachomatis, N. gonorrhoeae","E. coli and other gram-negative rods"],["Clue","Parotitis about a week earlier","Urethritis or urethral discharge","Bacteriuria and pyuria"],["Site first affected","Testis (interstitium)","Epididymis, then testis","Epididymis, then testis"],["Main complication","Testicular atrophy, reduced fertility","Epididymal scarring, infertility","Abscess, infarction"]],"caption":"Bloodstream spread to the testis means mumps; backward spread along the vas means bacterial epididymo-orchitis, with the organism set by age and sexual history."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C07","obj":"RE-13-B.7","title":"Pelvic inflammatory disease as ascending infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is infection of the upper female genital tract: endometrium, fallopian tubes, ovaries, and pelvic peritoneum, alone or together. It begins as an endocervical infection that ascends. C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae start many cases, but the upper tract infection is usually polymicrobial and includes vaginal flora, especially the anaerobes and Gardnerella of bacterial vaginosis, along with Haemophilus influenzae, enteric gram-negative rods, streptococci, and Mycoplasma genitalium. In a substantial share of cases neither gonorrhea nor chlamydia is found."},{"type":"p","text":"The closed cervix and its mucus plug normally limit ascent. Menses weakens this barrier: the os opens slightly, protective mucus is replaced by blood, which is a good culture medium, and retrograde menstrual flow can carry organisms upward. This is why gonococcal PID often begins within a week after a period. Other risks are young age, new or multiple partners, prior PID or STI, bacterial vaginosis, and instrumentation, including intrauterine device insertion, with risk concentrated in the first few weeks after insertion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ascending path of PID","steps":["C. trachomatis or N. gonorrhoeae infects endocervical columnar cells","Menses opens the barrier; vaginal flora join the infection","Organisms ascend into the endometrium (endometritis)","Infection reaches the tubal mucosa (salpingitis)","Pus spills from the fimbriae into the pelvic peritoneum","Healing fibrosis scars tubes and forms adhesions"],"caption":"Each step up the tract adds an organ, a symptom, and a site of later scarring."},{"type":"p","text":"The organisms injure the tube differently. Gonococci attach to nonciliated mucosal cells, and their lipooligosaccharide and peptidoglycan fragments kill neighboring ciliated cells, producing acute suppurative salpingitis with marked pain. Chlamydia often causes mild or no symptoms, yet tubal damage is substantial because persistent or repeated infection drives a cell-mediated immune response, in part directed at chlamydial heat shock protein 60, that scars the mucosa."},{"type":"p","text":"The examination findings map onto the inflamed organs. Moving the cervix pulls on inflamed tubes and parametrium, producing cervical motion tenderness; pressing on the adnexa compresses swollen tubes and ovaries, producing adnexal tenderness; uterine tenderness reflects endometritis; and lower abdominal pain reflects peritoneal inflammation. Fever above about 38.3°C (101°F), purulent cervical discharge, many neutrophils on vaginal wet mount, and raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate or C-reactive protein indicate active inflammation. Ectopic pregnancy produces similar pain, so pregnancy must be excluded with a human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) test."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does chlamydial PID cause as much tubal infertility as gonococcal PID, even though it often causes few symptoms?","explain":"Chlamydial damage comes largely from the host's cell-mediated immune response to persistent or repeated infection, which scars the tubal mucosa without much pain, and because symptoms are mild the infection continues longer before it is recognized.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on how gonococci and chlamydia injure the tube."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C08","obj":"RE-13-B.8","title":"Complications of salpingitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Salpingitis, inflammation of the fallopian tube, is the central lesion of PID. Its complications follow from three facts. The tubal mucosa is a delicate, folded (plicae), ciliated epithelium that must move an ovum and embryo toward the uterus. The fimbriated end opens into the peritoneal cavity. And inflammation at each site heals by fibrosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications of salpingitis and their mechanisms","cols":["Complication","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Pyosalpinx","Pus accumulates in a tube whose fimbriated end has closed","Tender adnexal mass"],["Hydrosalpinx","Fimbrial end stays sealed; pus resorbs and the tube fills with watery secretion","Thin-walled, dilated, sausage-shaped tube; infertility"],["Tubo-ovarian abscess","Inflamed tube adheres to the ovary and a polymicrobial, anaerobe-rich abscess forms","Adnexal mass and fever; rupture causes peritonitis and sepsis"],["Tubal infertility","Cilia destroyed, plicae fused, fimbriae occluded, peritubal adhesions","Risk rises with each episode of PID"],["Ectopic pregnancy","Damaged but open tube passes sperm yet delays the embryo","Tubal implantation, usually in the ampulla; rupture with hemorrhage"],["Chronic pelvic pain","Adhesions and persistent low-grade inflammation","Ongoing pain and dyspareunia"],["Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis","Infected exudate reaches the liver capsule","Right upper quadrant pain; violin-string adhesions"]],"caption":"Closure of the fimbriated end, loss of cilia, and fibrosis account for nearly every complication."},{"type":"p","text":"Ectopic pregnancy shows how partial damage can be more dangerous than complete obstruction. A completely blocked tube prevents pregnancy, but a tube whose lumen stays open while its cilia are lost and its folds are bridged by adhesions lets small sperm reach the ovum yet fails to carry the much larger embryo to the uterus. The embryo implants in the tube, most often the ampulla, whose thin wall cannot support it, and rupture causes intraperitoneal bleeding. Prior salpingitis is one of the most important risk factors for ectopic pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome is inflammation of the liver capsule (perihepatitis) that complicates both chlamydial and gonococcal PID. Infected exudate from the open fimbriae is thought to travel up the right paracolic gutter, the main peritoneal channel from the pelvis to the space around the liver, although lymphatic spread has also been proposed. The capsule and adjacent parietal peritoneum are inflamed but the liver parenchyma is not, so liver enzymes are normal or only mildly raised. Sharp right upper quadrant pain worsens with breathing and may be referred to the right shoulder, because diaphragmatic peritoneum is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5). Fibrinous exudate heals into fine violin-string adhesions between the anterior liver surface and the anterior abdominal wall or diaphragm, seen at laparoscopy."},{"type":"example","title":"Right upper quadrant pain that is not the gallbladder","text":"A 24-year-old woman has sharp right upper quadrant pain that worsens on deep breathing, fever, and lower abdominal pain. Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder, and liver enzymes are near normal. Step 1: pleuritic upper abdominal pain with normal parenchymal tests points to inflamed capsule or peritoneum rather than liver cells or gallbladder. Step 2: lower abdominal pain with fever in a young sexually active woman suggests PID; cervical motion and adnexal tenderness on pelvic examination would confirm the source. Step 3: link them. Exudate from inflamed fimbriae tracks up the right paracolic gutter to the liver capsule, which is Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis. Step 4: predict the laparoscopic finding, violin-string adhesions from liver capsule to abdominal wall."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C09","obj":"RE-13-B.9","title":"Candidal vulvovaginitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adult vagina is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium that stores glycogen under the influence of estrogen. Glycogen from shed cells is broken down to simpler sugars that lactobacilli ferment to lactic acid, holding vaginal pH at about 3.8 to 4.5. Lactobacilli also compete for nutrients and attachment sites. Candida albicans, which causes most cases, lives harmlessly in the vagina of a sizable minority of women, so disease means overgrowth and tissue invasion, not mere presence. Candida tolerates acid and lactobacilli usually persist, so unlike bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis, candidal vaginitis does not raise the pH."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptomatic infection follows a switch from budding yeast to invasive hyphae and pseudohyphae that adhere to and penetrate the epithelium. Hyphae secrete aspartyl proteases and the peptide toxin candidalysin, which damage epithelial cells and trigger neutrophil recruitment; much of the itching, burning, and redness is this host inflammatory response. Mucosal defense depends on Th17 cells, through interleukin 17 and interleukin 22, and on neutrophils."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for candidal vaginitis","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Broad-spectrum antibiotics","Kill lactobacilli, removing competition for nutrients and attachment sites"],["Diabetes mellitus","High glucose in tissue and secretions feeds yeast; neutrophil function is impaired"],["Pregnancy and other high-estrogen states","Estrogen raises epithelial glycogen and promotes yeast adherence and hyphal growth"],["Immunosuppression","Loss of T-cell defense (for example HIV infection or corticosteroids) allows mucosal overgrowth"]],"caption":"Risk factors remove bacterial competition, add fuel for yeast, or weaken T-cell control."},{"type":"p","text":"The discharge is thick, white, and curd-like, resembling cottage cheese, with little odor. The vulva is itchy, red, and swollen, sometimes with satellite lesions, and urine on inflamed skin causes burning on urination. The pH is normal (4.5 or less) and the whiff (amine) test is negative. A 10% potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation dissolves epithelial cells but not fungal cell walls, revealing pseudohyphae and budding yeast. Candida glabrata, a less common cause, forms only budding yeast without pseudohyphae."},{"type":"table","title":"The three common causes of vaginal discharge","cols":["Feature","Candidal vaginitis","Bacterial vaginosis","Trichomoniasis"],"rows":[["Cause","Candida, mostly C. albicans","Gardnerella and anaerobes replace lactobacilli","Trichomonas vaginalis"],["Discharge","Thick, white, curd-like; little odor","Thin, gray-white, fishy odor","Frothy, yellow-green, malodorous"],["Vaginal pH","4.5 or less (normal)","Above 4.5","Above 4.5"],["Whiff test","Negative","Positive","Often positive"],["Microscopy","Pseudohyphae and budding yeast on KOH","Clue cells; few lactobacilli","Motile flagellated trichomonads"],["Inflammation","Marked vulvar itching and redness","Little; few neutrophils","Vaginitis, strawberry cervix, many neutrophils"]],"caption":"A normal pH with itching and curd-like discharge points to Candida; a raised pH points to bacterial vaginosis or trichomoniasis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does candidal vaginitis leave vaginal pH normal while bacterial vaginosis raises it?","explain":"Candida grows well in acid and lactobacilli usually persist, so lactic acid production continues; in bacterial vaginosis lactobacilli are replaced by anaerobes, lactic acid falls, and amine production raises the pH.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph of this section on glycogen, lactobacilli, and pH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-02-C10","obj":"RE-13-B.10","title":"Lichen sclerosus of the vulva","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with a strong preference for anogenital skin. It is most common in postmenopausal women, with a smaller peak in prepubertal girls, and it also affects men, where it involves the glans and foreskin and is a cause of acquired phimosis (C01). Its cause is not settled. It is considered immune mediated, it is associated with autoimmune diseases such as autoimmune thyroid disease and vitiligo, and many patients have autoantibodies against extracellular matrix protein 1."},{"type":"p","text":"The histology explains the appearance. The epidermis is thinned, with loss of rete ridges and degeneration of basal cells. Beneath it, the upper dermis is replaced by a band of pale, homogeneous, nearly acellular (hyalinized) collagen, and below that band lies a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate. Thin epidermis over pale, poorly vascular collagen looks white and shiny and wrinkles like parchment or cigarette paper. Thin skin tears and bruises easily, so purpura and fissures are common."},{"type":"p","text":"Intense itching is the main symptom, and scratching adds further injury. White plaques may encircle the vulva and anus in a figure-of-eight pattern. Progressive sclerosis and contraction distort the anatomy: the labia minora shrink or fuse, the clitoral hood scars over, and the introitus narrows, causing dyspareunia. The vagina, lined by mucosa rather than skin, is typically spared."},{"type":"p","text":"Lichen sclerosus carries a small increase in the risk of vulvar squamous cell carcinoma, often quoted as a few percent over a lifetime. This pathway does not involve human papillomavirus (HPV). Chronic inflammation and epithelial injury in older women lead to TP53 mutations and differentiated vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia, then keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma. HPV-associated vulvar carcinoma, by contrast, arises from high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesions in younger women. A persistent ulcer, nodule, or thickened area arising within lichen sclerosus is therefore suspicious for this progression."},{"type":"table","title":"White or thinned vulvar skin: three causes","cols":["Feature","Lichen sclerosus","Lichen simplex chronicus","Postmenopausal atrophy"],"rows":[["Epidermis","Thin, with loss of rete ridges","Thickened (acanthosis, hyperkeratosis)","Thin"],["Dermis","Band of hyalinized collagen with lymphocytes beneath","Mild chronic inflammation","No sclerosis"],["Appearance","White, shiny, parchment-like; scarring","Leathery, thickened, exaggerated skin markings","Pale, dry, thin"],["Vagina","Spared","Not involved","Involved"],["Cause","Immune mediated; exact cause uncertain","Chronic itch and scratch cycle","Estrogen deficiency"],["Cancer risk","Small increase (HPV-independent squamous cell carcinoma)","Not premalignant by itself","No increase"]],"caption":"Lichen sclerosus thins the epidermis and scars the dermis; lichen simplex chronicus thickens the epidermis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Histology and anatomy predict where each organism settles and where harm accumulates. Chlamydia and gonococci seek columnar epithelium in the endocervix, tubes, and male urethra, while Trichomonas, HSV, and Candida infect squamous surfaces of the vagina, ectocervix, and glans. The open fimbriae connect the female tract to the peritoneal cavity, so cervical infection can reach the pelvis and the liver capsule, while in men organisms travel backward along the vas to the epididymis. Mumps is the exception that arrives by blood. In both sexes healing by fibrosis turns inflammation into lasting damage: phimosis, tubal occlusion, ectopic pregnancy, epididymal obstruction, and pelvic adhesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Endocrinology and immunology explain the rest. Estrogen maintains vaginal glycogen and acidity and drives growth of ectopic and intramyometrial endometrium, so endometriosis and adenomyosis are diseases of the reproductive years, while T-cell immunity governs Candida and the immune response to Chlamydia causes much of the tubal scarring. Pelvic findings overlap widely among PID, ectopic pregnancy, endometriosis, and appendicitis, so a single sign rarely decides the answer; the setting, timing, and laboratory clues do."}],"summary":["Balanitis comes from Candida or bacteria in a moist preputial sac; uncircumcised foreskin, poor hygiene, and diabetes are risks, and scarring from recurrent episodes causes phimosis, which traps more secretions.","C. trachomatis and N. gonorrhoeae infect endocervical columnar cells and cause mucopurulent cervicitis with a friable cervix; Trichomonas gives a strawberry cervix and HSV an ulcerative cervicitis.","Endometriosis is endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus; implants bleed cyclically, causing inflammation, adhesions, chocolate cysts, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility with a normal-sized uterus.","Adenomyosis is endometrium within the myometrium, giving a uniformly enlarged, boggy, tender uterus with heavy painful periods; leiomyoma gives an irregular, firm, nodular uterus.","Acute endometritis is polymicrobial infection after delivery, especially cesarean, or retained products, with fever and uterine tenderness; plasma cells in the stroma define chronic endometritis.","Mumps orchitis is bloodborne, postpubertal, and can cause atrophy; epididymo-orchitis ascends along the vas, from STI organisms in young men and E. coli in older men.","PID is ascending polymicrobial infection started by C. trachomatis or N. gonorrhoeae; cervical motion, adnexal, and uterine tenderness reflect the inflamed organs.","Salpingitis heals by fibrosis, causing hydrosalpinx, tubo-ovarian abscess, tubal infertility, and ectopic pregnancy; spread to the liver capsule causes Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis with violin-string adhesions.","Candidal vaginitis follows antibiotics, diabetes, pregnancy, or immunosuppression and gives thick white discharge, itching, normal pH, and pseudohyphae on KOH.","Lichen sclerosus thins the epidermis over hyalinized dermis, forms white parchment-like plaques in older women, and slightly raises the risk of HPV-independent vulvar squamous cell carcinoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract: vulva, vagina, cervix, endometritis, adenomyosis, endometriosis, leiomyoma, and fallopian tubes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System: penis, testis, and epididymis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Sections on the neisseriae, chlamydiae, herpesviruses, paramyxoviruses, and candidiasis"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the female reproductive tract"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Female Reproductive System: cervix, vagina, uterine tubes, and endometrium"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes: defense against fungi and Th17 responses"}]},"RE-13-C":{"unit":"RE-13-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03","title":"Congenital and Genetic Reproductive Diseases","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers reproductive disorders that are present from conception or birth: chromosomal and single-gene disorders (Turner, Klinefelter, fragile X, androgen insensitivity), disorders of sex development, and structural malformations of the testis, penis, foreskin, uterus, vagina, and hymen. Each one makes sense once you know two things: which embryologic step failed, and what happened to the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal feedback loop as a result. Board questions usually give a karyotype, a physical finding, or a hormone panel and ask you to name the condition or predict the missing piece, so the chapter keeps returning to those two questions.","prerequisites":["Gonadal, ductal, and external genital development, including SRY, AMH, testosterone, and DHT","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis: GnRH, LH, FSH, inhibin B, and sex steroid feedback","Testicular histology: Sertoli cells, Leydig cells, and spermatogenesis","Meiotic nondisjunction, X inactivation, and X-linked inheritance","Adrenal steroid synthesis pathway and the role of ACTH"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive embryology (RE-1)","how":"Every malformation in this chapter is a specific failure of fusion, resorption, canalization, or descent described in normal development."},{"to":"Endocrine system: adrenal disorders","how":"21-hydroxylase deficiency is the commonest cause of 46,XX DSD and links genital ambiguity to cortisol, aldosterone, and ACTH physiology."},{"to":"Genetics: trinucleotide repeat disorders","how":"Fragile X shows how an unstable repeat, promoter methylation, and parent-of-origin expansion produce anticipation."},{"to":"Cardiovascular system: congenital heart disease","how":"Turner syndrome is a classic cause of bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation of the aorta."},{"to":"Reproductive neoplasia: testicular germ cell tumors","how":"Cryptorchidism and gonads with Y material carry a raised risk of germ cell tumors."}],"terms":[{"term":"Disorder of sex development (DSD)","def":"A congenital condition in which chromosomal, gonadal, or anatomic sex is atypical or does not match."},{"term":"Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism","def":"Low gonadal hormone output from primary gonadal failure, with high LH and FSH because negative feedback is lost."},{"term":"Inhibin B","def":"A glycoprotein from Sertoli cells (and granulosa cells in the ovary) that selectively suppresses pituitary FSH secretion."},{"term":"Anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH)","def":"A Sertoli cell hormone that causes regression of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts in the male fetus."},{"term":"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT)","def":"The potent androgen formed from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase that masculinizes the external genitalia and prostate."},{"term":"Streak gonad","def":"A gonad reduced to fibrous ovarian-type stroma without follicles or germ cells."},{"term":"Anticipation","def":"Earlier onset or greater severity of a genetic disorder in successive generations, typically from expansion of a repeat."},{"term":"Premutation","def":"An FMR1 allele of about 55 to 200 CGG repeats that is still expressed but is unstable when transmitted by a female."},{"term":"Hematocolpos","def":"Accumulation of menstrual blood within the vagina behind an obstruction."},{"term":"Testicular dysgenesis syndrome","def":"A proposed grouping of cryptorchidism, hypospadias, poor semen quality, and testicular germ cell tumors attributed to abnormal fetal testis development."},{"term":"SHOX","def":"A growth gene in the pseudoautosomal region of the X and Y chromosomes that escapes X inactivation, so two active copies are normal."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C01","obj":"RE-13-C.1","title":"Cryptorchidism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cryptorchidism is failure of one or both testes to reach the scrotum. Descent occurs in two phases. In the transabdominal phase, insulin-like peptide 3 (INSL3) from Leydig cells enlarges the gubernaculum, which anchors the testis near the deep inguinal ring as the abdomen grows. In the inguinoscrotal phase, androgen-dependent migration of the gubernaculum carries the testis through the inguinal canal, usually reaching the scrotum by the last weeks of gestation."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the final phase is late, preterm infants are affected far more often than term infants, and many undescended testes still descend during the first few months of life, aided by the postnatal surge of gonadotropins and testosterone. Most undescended testes lie in the inguinal canal; fewer are intra-abdominal or high scrotal. The condition is usually unilateral. A retractile testis, which can be brought into the scrotum but is pulled up by an active cremasteric reflex, is a normal variant and must be distinguished. A patent processus vaginalis often accompanies the defect, so an indirect inguinal hernia is a common association."},{"type":"h","text":"Why spermatogenesis and hormone levels change"},{"type":"p","text":"Spermatogenesis needs a temperature about 2 to 3 °C (3.6 to 5.4 °F) below core body temperature, which the scrotum and the pampiniform plexus countercurrent exchanger provide. A testis held in the canal or abdomen is warmer, so germ cells are lost progressively after the first year and the tubules become small and fibrotic. Sertoli cells are also heat sensitive and make less inhibin B, so FSH rises. Leydig cells tolerate heat better, so testosterone is usually normal, particularly when one testis is normal. Infertility is therefore most likely when both testes are undescended."},{"type":"p","text":"Germ cells in an undescended testis mature abnormally, and germ cell neoplasia in situ can develop, giving a several-fold higher risk of testicular germ cell tumor, most often seminoma. The contralateral, normally descended testis also carries a modestly raised risk, which suggests that a shared intrinsic defect of fetal testis development, and not temperature alone, contributes. This idea underlies the testicular dysgenesis syndrome concept."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with bilateral intra-abdominal testes has a semen analysis showing azoospermia. Predict his FSH, inhibin B, LH, and testosterone.","explain":"Inhibin B is low and FSH high because heat damages Sertoli cells and germ cells. Leydig cells tolerate heat better, so testosterone is usually normal or only mildly low, with LH normal or mildly raised.","check":"The paragraph on temperature, Sertoli cells, and Leydig cells."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C02","obj":"RE-13-C.2","title":"Hypospadias and epispadias","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The male external genitalia develop from indifferent structures under the influence of DHT. The genital tubercle elongates to form the phallus, and the urethral folds on its ventral surface fuse in the midline from proximal to distal, enclosing the urethral groove to form the penile urethra. The labioscrotal swellings fuse behind them to form the scrotum. Any failure of androgen production or action during this window can stop fusion part way."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypospadias is an opening of the urethra on the ventral (under) surface of the penis because the urethral folds failed to fuse completely. It is one of the commonest congenital anomalies in boys, at about 1 in 250 live male births. Most openings are distal, near the glans or corona; proximal openings on the shaft, scrotum, or perineum reflect earlier, more severe failure. A ventral curvature of the penis (chordee) and a hooded, incomplete foreskin often accompany it. Because the same androgen-dependent window governs testicular descent and closure of the processus vaginalis, hypospadias is associated with cryptorchidism and inguinal hernia. Severe hypospadias together with an undescended testis raises the possibility of a disorder of sex development (C07), including a virilized 46,XX infant with congenital adrenal hyperplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"Epispadias is an opening on the dorsal (upper) surface. It is not a fusion failure of the urethral folds but a different and much rarer error: the genital tubercle forms in an abnormal position, related to defective development of the infraumbilical abdominal wall and cloacal membrane. For this reason epispadias is usually part of the bladder exstrophy and epispadias complex, in which the bladder is open on the lower abdominal wall and the pubic bones are widely separated. When the defect reaches the bladder neck, the sphincter mechanism is incomplete and urinary incontinence results."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypospadias compared with epispadias","cols":["Feature","Hypospadias","Epispadias"],"rows":[["Meatus","Ventral surface of penis","Dorsal surface of penis"],["Embryologic error","Incomplete fusion of the urethral folds","Abnormal position of the genital tubercle"],["Hormone link","Reduced androgen production or action","Not primarily hormonal"],["Frequency","Common, about 1 in 250 male births","Rare"],["Key associations","Cryptorchidism, inguinal hernia, chordee, DSD when severe","Bladder exstrophy, separated pubic symphysis, incontinence"]],"caption":"Ventral opening means the folds failed to close; dorsal opening means the tubercle was malpositioned."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C03","obj":"RE-13-C.3","title":"Fragile X syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fragile X syndrome is the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability. It is caused by expansion of a CGG trinucleotide repeat in the 5' untranslated region of the FMR1 gene at Xq27.3. Inheritance is X-linked (often described as X-linked dominant with reduced penetrance), so males are more severely affected and females with a full mutation show variable, usually milder, features because of random X inactivation."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal alleles have fewer than about 45 repeats. When an allele exceeds about 200 repeats (the full mutation), the repeat and the FMR1 promoter become hypermethylated, chromatin condenses, and transcription stops. The missing protein, FMRP, is an RNA-binding protein that normally restrains local translation of synaptic messenger RNAs in dendrites. Without it, excess synaptic protein synthesis leaves dendritic spines long, thin, and immature, which underlies the intellectual disability, autism features, and attention problems."},{"type":"flow","title":"From repeat expansion to disease","steps":["Maternal premutation (about 55 to 200 CGG repeats)","Expansion during female meiosis","Full mutation over about 200 repeats in the child","Hypermethylation of repeat and FMR1 promoter","FMR1 silenced, no FMRP made","Unrestrained synaptic translation, immature dendritic spines","Intellectual disability and the fragile X phenotype"],"caption":"The repeat expands through the mother; methylation, not a change in protein sequence, causes loss of function."},{"type":"p","text":"Anticipation follows from the instability of the repeat in female meiosis. A mother with a premutation can pass a full mutation to her child, and the larger her repeat, the higher that risk. A male with a premutation passes it to all his daughters, usually without expansion, and to none of his sons. Premutation alleles are still transcribed, and excess FMR1 messenger RNA is thought to be toxic: female carriers are at risk of primary ovarian insufficiency, and older male carriers of fragile X associated tremor and ataxia syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Affected males have intellectual disability, a long narrow face, a large prominent jaw, large protruding ears, and joint hypermobility, and some have mitral valve prolapse. Macroorchidism, meaning testes clearly larger than normal for age, usually appears after puberty, so it is a clue in an adolescent or adult and not in a young child."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C04","obj":"RE-13-C.4","title":"Imperforate hymen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The upper vagina comes from the fused paramesonephric ducts, and the lower vagina from the sinovaginal bulbs of the urogenital sinus, which form a solid vaginal plate. The plate canalizes, and a thin membrane, the hymen, remains between the vaginal lumen and the urogenital sinus (future vestibule). It normally perforates during late fetal life. Imperforate hymen is persistence of that membrane without an opening, and it is the most common obstructive anomaly of the female genital tract."},{"type":"p","text":"The ovaries, uterus, and hypothalamic-pituitary axis are all normal, so the girl goes through a normal puberty with breast development and pubic hair. The problem appears only when menstruation begins. Endometrium sheds on schedule, but the blood has no exit, so it collects behind the membrane. She presents with primary amenorrhea despite normal secondary sexual characteristics, together with cyclic lower abdominal or pelvic pain that recurs each month. Examination shows a tense, bulging membrane at the introitus that looks bluish because dark blood lies behind it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Imperforate hymen at menarche","steps":["Normal ovulatory cycles begin","Endometrium sheds each month","Blood trapped behind intact hymen","Hematocolpos: vagina distends with blood","Hematometra, then hematosalpinx if untreated","Pelvic mass compresses bladder and rectum"],"caption":"Each cycle adds blood above the obstruction, so pain and mass effect worsen over months."},{"type":"p","text":"As blood accumulates, the distended vagina (hematocolpos) can form a lower abdominal mass, and pressure on the urethra and rectum can cause urinary retention and constipation. Further accumulation distends the uterus and tubes, and retrograde flow through the tubes raises the risk of endometriosis. In a newborn, maternal estrogen can stimulate enough vaginal secretion to produce a bulging mucocolpos, so the anomaly is sometimes found at birth. A transverse vaginal septum (C10) gives the same history, but the obstruction lies higher in the vagina, so no bulging membrane is seen at the introitus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C05","obj":"RE-13-C.5","title":"Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Klinefelter syndrome is the most common chromosomal cause of male hypogonadism and infertility, affecting about 1 in 600 males. The 47,XXY karyotype arises from meiotic nondisjunction in either parent, and mosaic 46,XY/47,XXY forms are milder. The Y chromosome carries SRY, so testes form and the infant looks male, while the extra X is inactivated and seen as a Barr body. Testicular failure is thought to reflect an extra dose of genes that escape X inactivation, which disrupts the germ cells and Sertoli cells of the seminiferous tubules."},{"type":"p","text":"Two separate compartments of the testis fail, and each produces its own hormone signature. Tubular failure (seminiferous tubule dysgenesis) means germ cells are lost and tubules become hyalinized, so Sertoli cells make little inhibin B and FSH rises sharply; the result is azoospermia. Leydig cell dysfunction means testosterone is low or low-normal, so the hypothalamus and pituitary lose negative feedback and LH rises. The Leydig cells often look clumped or hyperplastic on biopsy because the tubules around them have shrunk, but they function poorly."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Feedback loss in Klinefelter syndrome","steps":["Hyalinized tubules: low inhibin B","Pituitary FSH rises","Weak Leydig cells: low testosterone","Hypothalamus and pituitary raise LH","High LH drives aromatase, estradiol rises relative to testosterone","Gonads cannot respond, so gonadotropins stay high"],"caption":"Primary testicular failure: the pituitary signals harder, but the testis cannot answer."},{"type":"p","text":"The physical findings follow from this hormone pattern. The testes are small and firm because tubules are scarred, and this is the most consistent sign. Low testosterone delays closure of the epiphyses, and the extra copy of the pseudoautosomal SHOX gene adds growth, so patients are tall with long legs and an arm span exceeding height (eunuchoid proportions). High LH stimulates aromatization, so estradiol is high relative to testosterone and gynecomastia develops. Facial and body hair may be sparse with a female-type pubic hair pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"Intelligence is usually in the normal range, but language and learning difficulties are more common. The altered estrogen to androgen ratio raises the risk of male breast cancer, low testosterone contributes to osteoporosis, and the risks of extragonadal (mediastinal) germ cell tumors and of some autoimmune diseases such as systemic lupus erythematosus are increased."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is FSH raised more consistently than LH in Klinefelter syndrome, even in men whose testosterone is in the low-normal range?","explain":"FSH is restrained mainly by inhibin B, which falls as soon as the tubules fail. LH is restrained by testosterone, so if Leydig cells still make enough testosterone, LH may be only mildly raised.","check":"The two compartments paragraph and the feedback cycle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C06","obj":"RE-13-C.6","title":"Phimosis and paraphimosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The prepuce (foreskin) is a fold of skin and mucosa that covers the glans. At birth its inner surface is normally adherent to the glans, and its opening is narrow. The adhesions separate gradually, so retractability increases through childhood and is present in most boys by adolescence."},{"type":"p","text":"Phimosis is a foreskin that cannot be retracted over the glans. In infants and young boys it is physiologic, and the foreskin is soft and unscarred. Acquired (pathologic) phimosis results from scarring that forms a tight, often white, fibrous ring at the tip of the foreskin. Causes include recurrent balanoposthitis (inflammation of the glans and foreskin), which is more common with poor hygiene and with diabetes because glucose in urine favors Candida, as well as lichen sclerosus and tears from forceful retraction. The narrow opening traps smegma and urine, causing ballooning of the foreskin on voiding, further infection, and occasionally urinary obstruction. Chronic inflammation under a nonretractile foreskin is a recognized risk factor for penile squamous cell carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Paraphimosis is the opposite situation: a foreskin that has been retracted behind the corona cannot be returned over the glans. It often follows retraction for cleaning, examination, or urinary catheter placement when the foreskin is not replaced. A relatively tight foreskin then acts as a constricting band. Low-pressure veins and lymphatics are compressed first, so the glans and the distal foreskin swell, the swelling tightens the band further, and eventually arterial inflow is threatened. The result is a painful, swollen glans with a tight ring behind it, which can progress to ischemia and necrosis."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The constriction loop in paraphimosis","steps":["Retracted foreskin forms a tight ring behind the corona","Venous and lymphatic outflow obstructed","Glans and distal foreskin become edematous","Swelling tightens the ring further"],"caption":"Edema and constriction reinforce each other until arterial supply is at risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Phimosis compared with paraphimosis","cols":["Feature","Phimosis","Paraphimosis"],"rows":[["Foreskin position","Forward over the glans, cannot be pulled back","Behind the glans, cannot be brought forward"],["Usual cause","Physiologic in infancy; scarring from balanoposthitis or lichen sclerosus","Foreskin left retracted after cleaning, examination, or catheterization"],["Mechanism of harm","Narrow opening traps urine and smegma","Constricting band blocks venous and lymphatic return"],["Complications","Balanoposthitis, ballooning, urinary obstruction, penile cancer risk","Edema, ischemia, necrosis of the glans"],["Urgency","Usually not urgent","Emergency"]],"caption":"Phimosis is a foreskin that will not go back; paraphimosis is one that will not come forward."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C07","obj":"RE-13-C.7","title":"46,XX and 46,XY disorders of sex development","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sexual differentiation proceeds in a fixed order, and each level depends on a different signal. Chromosomes decide the gonad: SRY on the Y chromosome directs the bipotential gonad to become a testis, and without it an ovary forms. The gonad then decides the ducts through its hormones. The external genitalia depend on androgen, specifically DHT, and form female in its absence. A disorder of sex development (DSD) occurs when one of these steps fails, so the gonads and the external genitalia do not match."},{"type":"flow","title":"Normal male sexual differentiation","steps":["Y chromosome carries SRY","Bipotential gonad becomes testis","Sertoli cells secrete AMH: Mullerian ducts regress","Leydig cells secrete testosterone: Wolffian ducts persist","5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT","DHT masculinizes external genitalia and prostate"],"caption":"Without SRY, no AMH, and no androgen, the default is ovaries, Mullerian structures, and female external genitalia."},{"type":"p","text":"In 46,XX DSD the karyotype is female, the gonads are ovaries, and the uterus and tubes are normal because no AMH was ever made. The external genitalia are virilized because the fetus was exposed to androgen. The most common cause is congenital adrenal hyperplasia from 21-hydroxylase deficiency. Low cortisol removes negative feedback on the pituitary, so ACTH rises and drives the adrenal cortex; precursors that cannot become cortisol or aldosterone are shunted into adrenal androgens. The newborn has clitoromegaly and fused labioscrotal folds, and in the salt-wasting form, aldosterone deficiency causes hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and hypovolemia in the first weeks. Excess maternal androgen from an ovarian or adrenal tumor, or exogenous androgen, has the same effect on the fetus. Androgen exposure before about 12 weeks fuses the labioscrotal folds, while later exposure enlarges only the clitoris."},{"type":"p","text":"In 46,XY DSD the karyotype is male and testes are present, so AMH works and Mullerian structures are absent, but the external genitalia are undervirilized because testosterone was not converted or did not act. Androgen insensitivity (C08) is a receptor defect. In 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency, an autosomal recessive disorder, testosterone is normal and builds the Wolffian structures (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles), but too little DHT is made to masculinize the external genitalia, so the infant has ambiguous or female-appearing genitalia. At puberty the large rise in testosterone causes substantial virilization."},{"type":"table","title":"46,XX DSD compared with 46,XY DSD","cols":["Feature","46,XX DSD","46,XY DSD"],"rows":[["Former term","Female pseudohermaphroditism","Male pseudohermaphroditism"],["Gonads","Ovaries","Testes"],["Internal ducts","Uterus and tubes present","Uterus and tubes absent (AMH acts)"],["External genitalia","Virilized","Undervirilized or female"],["Underlying problem","Too much androgen","Too little androgen action"],["Main causes","21-hydroxylase deficiency; maternal androgen-secreting tumor or exogenous androgen","Androgen insensitivity; 5-alpha-reductase deficiency"]],"caption":"The gonad names the category; the external genitalia show which way androgen exposure went wrong."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the testosterone to DHT ratio","text":"A 46,XY infant has ambiguous genitalia, palpable inguinal testes, and no uterus on ultrasound. Testes and absent uterus confirm functioning Sertoli cells and AMH. Testosterone is normal for age, so Leydig cells work. The question is whether androgen is not converted or not sensed. The testosterone to DHT ratio is high, which points to 5-alpha-reductase deficiency: testosterone is present, but little DHT is formed. In androgen insensitivity the ratio is normal and LH is high because the hypothalamus cannot sense testosterone. Wolffian structures help as well: present in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency (testosterone acts directly) but absent or rudimentary in complete androgen insensitivity (no working receptor)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C08","obj":"RE-13-C.8","title":"Complete androgen insensitivity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complete androgen insensitivity is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the androgen receptor gene on the X chromosome, inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern. The person is 46,XY with normal testes, which make normal or high amounts of testosterone and normal AMH. Every tissue fails to respond to testosterone and DHT, so the phenotype is defined by which steps of differentiation need androgen and which do not."},{"type":"p","text":"AMH acts through its own receptor, so the Mullerian ducts regress and the uterus, tubes, and upper vagina are absent. The Wolffian ducts need testosterone acting on the androgen receptor, so they also regress. The external genitalia, lacking DHT action, develop as female, and the lower vagina forms from the urogenital sinus as a short blind pouch. The testes do not descend into a scrotum and lie in the abdomen, inguinal canal, or labia majora; an inguinal hernia containing a testis in an apparently female infant is a classic early presentation."},{"type":"p","text":"Most cases present at puberty with primary amenorrhea in a girl who has developed breasts. Breast development is explained by aromatization: testosterone is converted to estradiol in peripheral tissues, and there is no androgen effect to oppose estrogen at the breast. Pubic and axillary hair depend on androgen, so they are sparse or absent."},{"type":"p","text":"The hormone pattern reflects resistance at the hypothalamus and pituitary. Testosterone normally suppresses LH through the androgen receptor, so with no working receptor the feedback signal is not sensed, LH rises, and the Leydig cells are driven to make testosterone in the male range or higher. More testosterone means more aromatization, so estradiol is high for a male. FSH is often normal or only mildly raised because inhibin B from Sertoli cells still restrains it. The undescended testes carry an increased risk of germ cell tumor that rises after puberty. Partial androgen insensitivity, from a receptor with some residual function, produces a spectrum from ambiguous genitalia to an undervirilized male."},{"type":"table","title":"Which structures need which hormone, and what happens in complete androgen insensitivity","cols":["Structure","Signal required","Result in CAIS"],"rows":[["Testis formation","SRY","Testes present"],["Mullerian regression","AMH","No uterus, tubes, or upper vagina"],["Wolffian development","Testosterone via androgen receptor","Absent or rudimentary"],["Male external genitalia","DHT via androgen receptor","Female external genitalia, blind vaginal pouch"],["Breast development","Estradiol (aromatized testosterone)","Normal breasts at puberty"],["Pubic and axillary hair","Androgen via androgen receptor","Sparse or absent"]],"caption":"Everything that needs the androgen receptor fails; everything driven by AMH or estrogen proceeds."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C09","obj":"RE-13-C.9","title":"Mullerian agenesis compared with complete androgen insensitivity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mullerian agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome) is congenital absence of the uterus and upper vagina in a 46,XX female. The paramesonephric ducts fail to develop or fuse, so the uterus is absent or present only as small rudimentary buds, and the upper two-thirds of the vagina is missing. The lower vagina comes from the urogenital sinus, so a short blind vaginal pouch is present. The cause is not established and most cases are sporadic. It is one of the leading causes of primary amenorrhea, after gonadal dysgenesis such as Turner syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"The ovaries develop from the genital ridge, not from the Mullerian ducts, so they are normal. They ovulate and make estradiol, so breast development, the pubertal growth spurt, and pubic and axillary hair (from normal adrenal and ovarian androgens acting on normal receptors) are all typical of a female. FSH, LH, estradiol, and testosterone are in the normal female range. The only complaint is absent menses, sometimes with cyclic symptoms from normal ovarian cycling. Because the Mullerian and Wolffian ducts develop next to the forming kidney, renal anomalies such as unilateral renal agenesis or a pelvic kidney are common, and vertebral anomalies also occur."},{"type":"p","text":"Complete androgen insensitivity (C08) produces the same picture at first glance: a phenotypic girl with breasts, primary amenorrhea, no uterus, and a blind vaginal pouch. Two findings separate them at the bedside, and two separate them in the laboratory. Pubic and axillary hair is normal in Mullerian agenesis but sparse in androgen insensitivity, and gonads may be palpable in the groin only in androgen insensitivity. Testosterone is in the female range in Mullerian agenesis but in the male range in androgen insensitivity, and the karyotype settles the question."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary amenorrhea with a blind vaginal pouch","cols":["Feature","Mullerian agenesis","Complete androgen insensitivity"],"rows":[["Karyotype","46,XX","46,XY"],["Gonads","Normal ovaries","Testes, undescended"],["Uterus and upper vagina","Absent (ducts failed to form)","Absent (regressed under AMH)"],["Breasts","Normal (ovarian estradiol)","Normal (aromatized testosterone)"],["Pubic and axillary hair","Normal","Sparse or absent"],["Testosterone","Female range","Male range or higher"],["LH","Normal","High"],["Common associations","Renal and vertebral anomalies","Inguinal hernia, gonadal germ cell tumor risk"]],"caption":"Same pelvis, different reason: one never formed the ducts, the other formed testes that removed them."},{"type":"example","title":"Working through primary amenorrhea","text":"A 16-year-old has never menstruated. First ask whether there is breast development, which shows estrogen exposure. No breasts with high FSH means gonadal failure, as in Turner syndrome (C11). Breasts present means estrogen is being made, so look for an outflow problem. If a uterus is present and a bluish bulge is seen at the introitus with monthly pain, think of imperforate hymen (C04). If the uterus is absent and the vagina ends in a blind pouch, check pubic hair and testosterone: normal hair and female-range testosterone mean Mullerian agenesis; sparse hair and male-range testosterone mean complete androgen insensitivity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C10","obj":"RE-13-C.10","title":"Septate uterus and vaginal septa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The uterus and upper vagina form in three steps. The paired paramesonephric ducts grow down and fuse in the midline, so for a time the fused segment has a wall down its center. That midline septum is then resorbed, leaving a single cavity, and the vaginal plate canalizes to open the lower vagina. Each step can fail in its own way, and the step that failed predicts the anatomy."},{"type":"p","text":"A septate uterus results when the ducts fuse normally but the midline septum is not resorbed. The external contour of the fundus is smooth and normal, because fusion was complete, while the cavity is divided by a partial or complete septum. This distinguishes it from a bicornuate uterus, in which fusion itself was incomplete and the fundus is indented. Septate uterus is the most common structural uterine anomaly and the one most strongly linked to recurrent pregnancy loss. The septum is fibromuscular tissue with a poorer blood supply than normal myometrium, so an embryo that implants on it has inadequate placentation and is more likely to be lost, usually in the first or early second trimester. Preterm birth and malpresentation are also more common."},{"type":"p","text":"A longitudinal vaginal septum is a midline wall running down the vagina, produced by incomplete fusion of the lower paramesonephric ducts or failure of the septum between them to resorb. It often coexists with a septate uterus or a uterus didelphys and may cause dyspareunia or difficulty with tampon use. If one side is blocked, menstrual blood collects in an obstructed hemivagina, which is classically associated with absence of the kidney on the same side. A transverse vaginal septum is a horizontal membrane caused by failed fusion or canalization where the Mullerian-derived upper vagina meets the sinus-derived vaginal plate. It obstructs menstrual flow like an imperforate hymen, with primary amenorrhea, cyclic pain, and hematocolpos, but the hymen is normal and no bulge appears at the introitus."},{"type":"table","title":"Which developmental step failed","cols":["Anomaly","Failed step","Key feature"],"rows":[["Septate uterus","Resorption of midline septum","Normal fundal contour; divided cavity; recurrent pregnancy loss"],["Bicornuate uterus (for contrast)","Fusion of the upper ducts","Indented fundus; two horns"],["Longitudinal vaginal septum","Fusion or resorption of lower ducts","Double vaginal canal; may obstruct one side"],["Transverse vaginal septum","Fusion or canalization at the vaginal plate","Obstructed menses with a normal hymen"],["Imperforate hymen (C04)","Perforation of the hymenal membrane","Bulging bluish membrane at the introitus"]],"caption":"Failed fusion of the upper ducts changes the outer uterine contour; failed resorption or canalization changes only the inside."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C11","obj":"RE-13-C.11","title":"Turner syndrome: ovarian dysgenesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Turner syndrome is the absence of all or part of one X chromosome in a phenotypic female, affecting about 1 in 2,500 live-born girls. About half have a 45,X karyotype, usually from loss of the paternal X; the rest are mosaic (for example 45,X/46,XX) or carry a structurally abnormal X such as an isochromosome of the long arm. Most 45,X conceptions are lost in pregnancy. With only one X there is no Barr body. Turner syndrome is the most common cause of primary amenorrhea and the classic example of gonadal dysgenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Germ cells reach the fetal ovary and oocytes form, but maintaining them requires genes on both X chromosomes. With a single X, oocytes undergo accelerated atresia beginning in fetal life, and by early childhood the ovaries are reduced to streak gonads of fibrous stroma with no follicles. Without follicles, the granulosa and theca cells that make estradiol and inhibin are absent."},{"type":"p","text":"The hormone pattern is hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Estradiol is low, so there is no breast development and no menarche, giving primary amenorrhea with absent thelarche. Loss of estradiol and inhibin removes negative feedback, so FSH and LH are both high, with FSH typically higher. The uterus is present because no AMH was made, but it stays small without estrogen. Pubic and axillary hair can appear because adrenal androgens at adrenarche are normal, and low estrogen impairs bone mineralization."},{"type":"p","text":"Mosaic patients may retain enough follicles to start puberty and even menstruate before the ovaries fail early, so Turner syndrome can also present as secondary amenorrhea with premature ovarian insufficiency. When any Y chromosome material is present in a mosaic karyotype, the dysgenetic gonad has a substantial risk of gonadoblastoma."},{"type":"table","title":"Gonadotropin patterns in this chapter","cols":["Condition","Gonadal steroid","Inhibin B","FSH","LH"],"rows":[["Turner syndrome","Low estradiol","Low","High","High"],["Klinefelter syndrome","Low or low-normal testosterone","Low","High","High"],["Bilateral cryptorchidism","Testosterone usually normal","Low","High","Normal or mildly high"],["Complete androgen insensitivity","High testosterone and estradiol","Normal","Normal or mildly high","High"],["Mullerian agenesis","Normal estradiol","Normal","Normal","Normal"]],"caption":"Low inhibin B tracks tubular or follicular loss and raises FSH; loss of steroid feedback or receptor resistance raises LH."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 15-year-old with no breast development has high FSH and LH. Another has no breast development with low FSH and LH. Which has Turner syndrome, and where is the lesion in the other?","explain":"The first. High gonadotropins mean the pituitary is signaling but the gonad cannot respond (primary failure). Low gonadotropins point to the hypothalamus or pituitary, as in constitutional delay or Kallmann syndrome.","check":"The hormone pattern paragraph and the gonadotropin table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-03-C12","obj":"RE-13-C.12","title":"Turner syndrome: associated findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most of the nonovarian findings of Turner syndrome trace to two causes: too little of a dosage-sensitive growth gene, and abnormal development of the fetal lymphatic system. Short stature is the most consistent feature. It results from haploinsufficiency of SHOX, a gene in the pseudoautosomal region of the X chromosome that escapes X inactivation, so a normal female has two working copies and a woman with 45,X has one. Growth is slow throughout childhood with no pubertal spurt, and skeletal signs from the same cause include a wide carrying angle at the elbow (cubitus valgus) and short fourth metacarpals. Klinefelter syndrome shows the converse, with three copies and tall stature (C05)."},{"type":"p","text":"In the fetus, the jugular lymph sacs fail to connect normally to the venous system, so lymph accumulates in the neck as a cystic hygroma, which can be seen on prenatal ultrasound. When it later regresses, it leaves redundant skin folds that form the webbed neck and a low posterior hairline. Generalized lymphatic hypoplasia produces lymphedema of the hands and feet that is often present at birth and is a key neonatal clue. A broad shield chest with widely spaced nipples is also typical."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiovascular anomalies are the most important cause of serious complications. A bicuspid aortic valve is the most common, and coarctation of the aorta is the classic association, which explains hypertension and weak femoral pulses in a girl. One proposed explanation is that distended fetal lymphatics compress the developing outflow tract and reduce flow through the left heart, but this remains a hypothesis. Aortic dilation and dissection are later risks, made more likely by these lesions and by hypertension. The commonest renal anomaly is a horseshoe kidney, in which the lower poles fuse and ascent is halted beneath the inferior mesenteric artery. Autoimmune hypothyroidism and hearing loss are also more common."},{"type":"table","title":"Associated findings of Turner syndrome and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Short stature, cubitus valgus, short fourth metacarpals","SHOX haploinsufficiency"],["Webbed neck, low posterior hairline","Regressed fetal cystic hygroma"],["Lymphedema of hands and feet at birth","Lymphatic hypoplasia"],["Bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta","Abnormal left-sided outflow development"],["Horseshoe kidney","Fused lower poles trapped under the inferior mesenteric artery"],["Primary amenorrhea, absent breasts","Streak ovaries with no estradiol (C11)"]],"caption":"Growth and lymphatic defects account for most of the external phenotype."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains the anatomy in this chapter. The same few signals, SRY, AMH, testosterone, and DHT, decide the gonad, the ducts, and the external genitalia, so a karyotype plus three questions (which gonad, is there a uterus, how virilized are the genitalia) localizes most disorders of sex development. Structural defects follow the specific step that failed: descent in cryptorchidism, fold fusion in hypospadias, tubercle position in epispadias, duct formation in Mullerian agenesis, resorption in septate uterus, and canalization or perforation in vaginal septa and imperforate hymen. Because the paramesonephric ducts develop beside the forming kidney, Mullerian anomalies often travel with renal anomalies."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and genetics explain the laboratory findings. Inhibin B reports on Sertoli and granulosa cells and governs FSH, while sex steroids and their receptors govern LH, so a hormone panel shows which compartment failed. Genetic mechanisms differ in instructive ways: whole chromosome gain or loss with gene dosage effects in Klinefelter and Turner syndromes, an unstable repeat with methylation silencing in fragile X, and a receptor defect in androgen insensitivity. Hormone values vary with age, pubertal stage, and laboratory, so patterns are more reliable than single cutoffs, and the karyotype is the final arbiter when phenotypes overlap."}],"summary":["Cryptorchidism is most often inguinal and more common in preterm infants; heat damages germ cells and Sertoli cells, lowering inhibin B and raising FSH, and germ cell tumor risk is increased.","Hypospadias is a ventral meatus from incomplete urethral fold fusion with cryptorchidism and hernia; epispadias is a dorsal meatus from a malpositioned genital tubercle with bladder exstrophy.","Fragile X is an FMR1 CGG expansion beyond about 200 repeats that is methylated and silenced; it expands through the mother and causes intellectual disability, long face, large jaw and ears, and postpubertal macroorchidism.","Imperforate hymen gives primary amenorrhea with normal puberty, cyclic pain, a bulging bluish membrane, and hematocolpos.","Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) combines tubular failure (low inhibin B, high FSH, azoospermia) with Leydig failure (low testosterone, high LH), giving small firm testes, tall stature, and gynecomastia.","Phimosis is a foreskin that cannot retract, physiologic in infancy or from scarring; paraphimosis is a retracted foreskin trapped behind the glans that obstructs venous return and threatens ischemia.","46,XX DSD means ovaries with virilized genitalia from excess androgen (most often 21-hydroxylase deficiency); 46,XY DSD means testes with undervirilized genitalia from failed androgen action or DHT synthesis.","Complete androgen insensitivity gives female external genitalia, a blind pouch, no uterus, undescended testes, sparse hair, and high testosterone, estradiol, and LH.","Mullerian agenesis (46,XX) has normal ovaries, hair, and female hormone levels with absent uterus and upper vagina, often with renal anomalies.","Turner syndrome (45,X or mosaic) causes streak ovaries with low estradiol and high FSH and LH, plus short stature (SHOX), webbed neck, lymphedema, bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation, and horseshoe kidney."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital system: genital system development"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders; Diseases of Infancy and Childhood; The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System; The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and hormonal functions of the male; female physiology before pregnancy and female hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sexual differentiation"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the male and female reproductive tracts"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and perineum: male and female genital organs"}]},"RE-13-D":{"unit":"RE-13-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04","title":"Benign Conditions of the Penis and Scrotum","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the common nonmalignant disorders of the penis and scrotum: erectile dysfunction, the fluid, blood, cystic, and venous swellings of the scrotum, testicular torsion, priapism, Peyronie disease, and the causes of male infertility. Each is taught from the normal anatomy and physiology that it disrupts, such as the veno-occlusive mechanism of erection, the embryology of the processus vaginalis, and the venous drainage of the testes. Board questions rely on a small set of bedside clues (transillumination, position relative to the testis, change with posture, the cremasteric reflex, nocturnal erections) and on hormone patterns, and each clue makes sense once its mechanism is clear.","prerequisites":["Gross anatomy of the penis, scrotum, testis, epididymis, and spermatic cord (RE-2, RE-4)","Descent of the testis and the processus vaginalis (RE-1)","Autonomic and somatic innervation and venous drainage of the pelvis and genitalia (RE-5)","Hypothalamic, pituitary, and testicular hormones and their feedback (RE-7)","Nitric oxide, cGMP, and smooth muscle relaxation"],"connections":[{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"Atherosclerosis narrows the small penile arteries early, so vascular erectile dysfunction is often a sign of systemic arterial disease."},{"to":"Neoplasms of the urinary tract (UR-12-G)","how":"Renal cell carcinoma that grows into the left renal vein blocks drainage of the left testicular vein and produces a new left varicocele."},{"to":"Hematology (sickle cell disease)","how":"Sickled red cells obstruct outflow from the cavernous sinusoids and are a leading cause of ischemic priapism, especially in children and young men."},{"to":"Congenital and genetic reproductive diseases (RE-13-C)","how":"Cryptorchidism and Klinefelter syndrome are testicular causes of infertility, and CFTR mutations cause absence of the vas deferens."},{"to":"Metabolic endocrine disease (EN-12-D)","how":"Diabetes causes erectile dysfunction through neuropathy, endothelial dysfunction, and atherosclerosis at once, and it can cause retrograde ejaculation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Veno-occlusive mechanism","def":"Compression of the subtunical venules between expanding sinusoids and the rigid tunica albuginea, which traps blood and makes the erect penis rigid."},{"term":"Nocturnal penile tumescence","def":"Involuntary erections that occur mainly during REM sleep and show that the nerves, vessels, and smooth muscle of erection are working."},{"term":"Tunica vaginalis","def":"The closed serous sac, derived from the processus vaginalis, that covers the front and sides of the testis."},{"term":"Processus vaginalis","def":"A pouch of peritoneum that extends through the inguinal canal into the scrotum ahead of the descending testis."},{"term":"Transillumination","def":"Passage of light through a scrotal swelling; clear fluid transmits light, while blood, veins, and solid tissue do not."},{"term":"Pampiniform plexus","def":"The network of veins in the spermatic cord that surrounds the testicular artery and drains into the testicular vein."},{"term":"Bell-clapper deformity","def":"A high attachment of the tunica vaginalis on the spermatic cord that leaves the testis free to rotate inside the sac."},{"term":"Cremasteric reflex","def":"Elevation of the testis by contraction of the cremaster muscle when the skin of the upper medial thigh is stroked (spinal segments L1 and L2)."},{"term":"Ischemic priapism","def":"A persistent, painful, rigid erection caused by failed venous outflow, with hypoxic and acidotic blood trapped in the corpora cavernosa."},{"term":"Tunica albuginea","def":"The dense fibrous coat around each corpus cavernosum (and, separately, around the testis)."},{"term":"Azoospermia","def":"Complete absence of sperm in the ejaculate."},{"term":"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","def":"Gonadal failure caused by too little GnRH, LH, and FSH, so both the gonadotropins and testosterone are low."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C01","obj":"RE-13-D.1","title":"Erectile dysfunction: mechanism of erection and classification of causes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Erection is a neurovascular event. Sexual stimulation activates parasympathetic fibers from S2 to S4 (the pelvic splanchnic nerves), which reach the penis in the cavernous nerves. Their nitrergic endings and the sinusoidal endothelium release nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase in smooth muscle, cyclic GMP (cGMP) activates protein kinase G, cytosolic Ca2+ falls, and the helicine arteries and trabecular smooth muscle relax. Inflow fills the sinusoids, which expand against the tunica albuginea and compress the subtunical venules. This veno-occlusive mechanism, not inflow alone, makes the penis rigid."},{"type":"flow","title":"From stimulation to rigid erection","steps":["Parasympathetic S2 to S4 fibers fire in the cavernous nerves","Nerve endings and endothelium release nitric oxide","Guanylyl cyclase raises cGMP; cytosolic Ca2+ falls","Helicine arteries and trabecular smooth muscle relax","Arterial inflow fills the cavernous sinusoids","Sinusoids compress subtunical venules: blood is trapped"],"caption":"Failure at any step causes erectile dysfunction. Detumescence follows when PDE5 degrades cGMP and sympathetic alpha-1 tone contracts the smooth muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"Detumescence reverses the chain: phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5) hydrolyzes cGMP, and sympathetic fibers from T11 to L2 release norepinephrine onto alpha-1 receptors, contracting smooth muscle so the sinusoids empty. Erectile dysfunction (ED) is the persistent inability to achieve or keep an erection adequate for intercourse, and its causes map onto the links of this chain."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of erectile dysfunction by mechanism","cols":["Category","Mechanism","Examples","Clues"],"rows":[["Vascular","Narrowed penile arteries limit inflow; endothelial damage lowers NO; injured sinusoids leak (veno-occlusive failure)","Atherosclerosis, smoking, hypertension, aortoiliac occlusion (Leriche syndrome)","Gradual onset in all settings; other vascular disease"],["Neurogenic","Parasympathetic outflow or cavernous nerves interrupted","Diabetic autonomic neuropathy, spinal cord injury, radical prostatectomy or other pelvic surgery, multiple sclerosis","Bladder or ejaculatory symptoms"],["Endocrine","Low testosterone lowers libido and NO-dependent signaling; prolactin suppresses GnRH pulses","Primary or secondary hypogonadism, prolactinoma","Low libido, small testes, galactorrhea or headache"],["Drug-induced","Central, autonomic, or hormonal interference","Thiazides, some beta blockers, SSRIs, antiandrogens, dopamine antagonists, opioids, alcohol","Onset after a new drug"],["Psychogenic","Central inhibition and high sympathetic tone override intact peripheral mechanisms","Performance anxiety, depression, relationship stress","Sudden, situational onset; nocturnal erections preserved"]],"caption":"Organic causes often coexist; psychogenic ED is identified by intact nocturnal and morning erections."},{"type":"p","text":"Causes often combine. Diabetes damages autonomic nerves, impairs endothelial NO production through hyperglycemia and oxidative stress, and accelerates atherosclerosis. Because the penile arteries are narrow, plaque limits flow there early, so vascular ED often precedes symptomatic coronary disease by a few years and is regarded as a marker of systemic atherosclerosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Healthy men have about three to five erections a night, mostly during REM sleep, generated by brainstem mechanisms free of emotional inhibition. Preserved nocturnal or morning erections therefore show that nerves, vessels, smooth muscle, and androgen support all work, so the cause is most likely psychogenic. Their absence points to an organic cause; low testosterone also reduces them."},{"type":"p","text":"Two pathways start erection. Psychogenic erection depends on descending input from the brain through thoracolumbar and sacral outflow. Reflexogenic erection uses a sacral arc: pudendal sensory fibers from the genitalia synapse in S2 to S4, which send parasympathetic output back to the penis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with a complete spinal cord transection at T6 is touched on the penis. Can he have an erection?","explain":"Usually yes. The sacral reflex arc (pudendal afferents, S2 to S4 parasympathetic outflow) lies below the lesion and is intact, so reflexogenic erection persists. Psychogenic erection, which needs descending input from the brain, is lost.","check":"The two pathways of erection and the neurogenic row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C02","obj":"RE-13-D.2","title":"Hematocele: blood in the tunica vaginalis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The tunica vaginalis is a closed serous sac. Its visceral layer covers the front and sides of the testis, its parietal layer lines the inner scrotal wall, and a thin film of fluid between them lets the testis move. A hematocele is blood collected in this space."},{"type":"p","text":"Blunt scrotal trauma is the most common cause, and it may rupture the tunica albuginea of the testis (testicular rupture), spilling blood and seminiferous tubules into the sac. Other causes are hemorrhagic infarction in torsion, bleeding from a testicular tumor, scrotal or inguinal surgery, and bleeding disorders or anticoagulation. Because a hematocele can conceal a ruptured, twisted, or neoplastic testis, the clinical question is always what lies beneath it."},{"type":"p","text":"Transillumination explains the key bedside test. A light held against the scrotum in a dark room scatters through clear serous fluid, so a hydrocele glows red. Hemoglobin absorbs visible light and clot is opaque, so a hematocele stays dark, as do solid tumors and veins. A hematocele is usually tense and tender, with overlying bruising, and the testis usually cannot be felt through it."},{"type":"p","text":"Over weeks an untreated hematocele organizes. Fibrin and granulation tissue thicken the sac wall, the clot fibroses, and the wall may calcify. A chronic hematocele can therefore present as a firm, painless mass that mimics a testicular tumor. A large, tense collection can also compress the testis. On ultrasound a hematocele is a complex collection with internal echoes and septations, whereas a simple hydrocele is anechoic (black)."},{"type":"table","title":"Hydrocele compared with hematocele","cols":["Feature","Hydrocele","Hematocele"],"rows":[["Contents of tunica vaginalis","Clear serous fluid","Blood and clot"],["Usual setting","Infancy (patent processus) or idiopathic in adults; infection, trauma, tumor","Trauma, torsion, tumor, surgery, bleeding tendency"],["Onset and pain","Gradual, painless","Often acute and painful, with bruising"],["Transillumination","Yes","No"],["Ultrasound","Anechoic fluid around the testis","Complex fluid with echoes and septations"],["Chronic course","May persist or resolve","Organizes, fibroses, may calcify and mimic tumor"]],"caption":"Both collections lie in the same space; their contents decide whether light passes through."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C03","obj":"RE-13-D.3","title":"Hydrocele: communicating and noncommunicating","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A hydrocele is serous fluid in the tunica vaginalis, and its two types follow from embryology. Before the testis descends, a pouch of peritoneum, the processus vaginalis, extends through the inguinal canal into the scrotal swelling, led by the gubernaculum. The testis descends behind this pouch and normally reaches the scrotum by about birth. The upper processus then closes, usually around birth or within the first year, and only the distal sac around the testis remains as the tunica vaginalis."},{"type":"table","title":"Fate of the processus vaginalis","cols":["Closure pattern","Result"],"rows":[["Upper part closed, distal sac left around testis","Normal tunica vaginalis"],["Stays open, narrow neck","Communicating hydrocele (peritoneal fluid only)"],["Stays open, wide neck","Indirect inguinal hernia (bowel or omentum can enter)"],["Closed above and below an isolated segment","Hydrocele of the spermatic cord (encysted)"],["Closed, fluid trapped or produced within the sac","Noncommunicating hydrocele"]],"caption":"The same patent processus causes a communicating hydrocele or an indirect hernia, depending on the size of its opening."},{"type":"p","text":"In a communicating hydrocele, peritoneal fluid moves in and out through the patent processus. The swelling is smaller in the morning after lying flat and larger after activity, crying, or straining, and it may be reducible. It is common in infants, especially those born preterm, and shares its cause with indirect inguinal hernia."},{"type":"p","text":"A noncommunicating hydrocele has no connection with the peritoneum. Fluid accumulates because the mesothelium of the tunica vaginalis secretes more than its lymphatics reabsorb. In newborns, fluid trapped when the processus closed is usually absorbed during the first year. In adults most cases are idiopathic, but secondary hydroceles follow epididymitis or orchitis (more secretion), trauma, a testicular tumor (reactive fluid), and lymphatic obstruction, including filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti and surgery that interrupts scrotal lymphatics."},{"type":"p","text":"On examination a hydrocele is a smooth, painless, fluctuant swelling that surrounds the testis, so the testis is hard to feel separately. It transilluminates. With a noncommunicating hydrocele the examiner can usually feel a normal cord above the swelling, which separates it from an inguinoscrotal hernia. Because fluid can hide a tumor, a new hydrocele in a young man calls for ultrasound of the testis. In infants, a hernia containing gas-filled bowel can also transilluminate, so light alone does not exclude a hernia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 6-month-old has a scrotal swelling that is small each morning and large by evening. What anatomical defect explains the change?","explain":"A patent processus vaginalis. Lying flat overnight lets fluid drain back into the peritoneal cavity; upright posture and crying push it down again. A noncommunicating hydrocele has no channel and does not change size this way.","check":"The table on the fate of the processus vaginalis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C04","obj":"RE-13-D.4","title":"Spermatocele and simple epididymal cyst","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sperm leave the seminiferous tubules through the rete testis, pass through about 12 to 20 efferent ductules into the head of the epididymis, and then travel through the single coiled duct of the epididymis to the vas deferens. A spermatocele is a retention cyst that forms when part of this outflow, usually an efferent ductule or the rete testis near the epididymal head, becomes obstructed and dilates. Because it is still connected to sperm-producing tubules, it fills with milky or opalescent fluid containing spermatozoa. Its lining is flattened or cuboidal epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"A spermatocele is a smooth, painless, fluctuant mass, usually at the head of the epididymis above and behind the testis. Because it arises from the epididymis rather than filling the space around the testis, the testis can be felt separately below it. This separation is the main bedside distinction from a hydrocele, which surrounds the testis. A spermatocele transilluminates, although its cloudier fluid may glow less brightly than a hydrocele."},{"type":"p","text":"A simple epididymal cyst is a fluid-filled cyst of the epididymis that contains clear fluid without sperm. It can occur anywhere along the epididymis and is often multiple. Clinically and on ultrasound it looks the same as a spermatocele: both are smooth, transilluminating, and separate from the testis. They are told apart only by the contents, sperm or no sperm, if the fluid is aspirated or examined after excision. Both are benign, have no malignant potential, and are common incidental findings in adults."},{"type":"table","title":"Cystic scrotal swellings that transilluminate","cols":["Feature","Spermatocele","Epididymal cyst","Hydrocele"],"rows":[["Origin","Dilated efferent ductule or rete testis","Cyst of the epididymis","Fluid in tunica vaginalis"],["Contents","Milky fluid with sperm","Clear fluid, no sperm","Clear serous fluid"],["Position","Head of epididymis, above and behind testis","Anywhere along epididymis, often multiple","Surrounds the testis"],["Testis felt separately","Yes","Yes","No, often hidden"],["Transilluminates","Yes","Yes","Yes"]],"caption":"Position relative to the testis separates epididymal swellings from a hydrocele; only the contents separate a spermatocele from an epididymal cyst."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C05","obj":"RE-13-D.5","title":"Varicocele: venous anatomy, infertility, and renal cell carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The testicular artery runs down the spermatic cord surrounded by the pampiniform plexus, a network of about 8 to 12 veins that join near the deep inguinal ring to form the testicular vein. Cooler venous blood returning from the testis absorbs heat from the arterial blood beside it. This countercurrent heat exchange, with the thin scrotal skin and the cremaster and dartos muscles, keeps the testis at about 34 to 35 °C (93 to 95 °F), a few degrees below core temperature of 37 °C (98.6 °F). Spermatogenesis depends on that lower temperature."},{"type":"p","text":"A varicocele is dilation and tortuosity of the pampiniform plexus caused by reflux of blood down the testicular vein, usually through absent or incompetent valves. It appears around puberty and is found in about 15% of adult men and a much larger share of men evaluated for infertility. About 90% are left-sided. The left testicular vein is longer, enters the left renal vein at a right angle, and drains into a vein that can be compressed between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta, so its hydrostatic pressure is higher. The right testicular vein enters the inferior vena cava (IVC) directly at an oblique angle."},{"type":"p","text":"With the patient standing, a varicocele feels like a bag of worms above and behind the testis. It enlarges with standing or the Valsalva maneuver and collapses when he lies down, because gravity and abdominal pressure drive the reflux. It does not transilluminate, because it contains blood. It may cause a dull ache after prolonged standing, and the testis on that side may be smaller."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a varicocele impairs spermatogenesis","steps":["Incompetent valves allow reflux down the testicular vein","Pampiniform plexus dilates and warm blood pools","Countercurrent cooling of the testicular artery fails","Testicular temperature, oxidative stress, and hypoxia rise","Germ cell apoptosis and impaired Sertoli and Leydig function","Lower sperm count, motility, and normal morphology"],"caption":"Raised temperature is the main mechanism; reflux of renal and adrenal venous metabolites may add to the injury on the left."},{"type":"p","text":"Not every varicocele causes infertility, but it is a common finding in infertile men, and a unilateral lesion can affect both testes through venous connections across the midline."},{"type":"p","text":"A varicocele can also be the first sign of obstruction higher up. Renal cell carcinoma characteristically grows into the renal vein. A tumor thrombus in the left renal vein blocks drainage of the left testicular vein and produces a new left varicocele, typically in an older man, that may not collapse when he lies down. A right-sided varicocele by itself, or one that persists when supine, suggests compression of the IVC or a retroperitoneal mass."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 58-year-old man has a new right-sided varicocele that stays the same size when he lies flat. Why is this more worrying than a left varicocele in a 17-year-old?","explain":"The right testicular vein drains obliquely into the IVC, so valve failure alone rarely causes a right varicocele, and one that does not empty when supine is held open by obstruction, such as an IVC thrombus or retroperitoneal mass.","check":"The left and right venous drainage and the paragraph on secondary varicocele."},{"type":"example","title":"Sorting four scrotal swellings at the bedside","text":"Ask three questions. Does light pass through? Hydrocele and spermatocele transilluminate; hematocele and varicocele do not. Can the testis be felt separately? A hydrocele hides it, while a spermatocele sits above and behind it. What changes it? A varicocele fills on standing and empties lying down, a communicating hydrocele grows by day and shrinks overnight, and a hematocele follows trauma and is tender. A dark, tender mass after a kick needs the testis checked for rupture; a new left bag of worms in an older man needs the left kidney imaged."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C06","obj":"RE-13-D.6","title":"Testicular torsion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normally the tunica vaginalis covers only the front and sides of the testis, and the back of the testis and epididymis is anchored to the scrotal wall. In the bell-clapper deformity the tunica attaches high on the spermatic cord, so the testis hangs free inside the sac like the clapper of a bell and can rotate. The deformity is often present on both sides, so the opposite testis is also at risk. Rotation of the testis inside the tunica is intravaginal torsion, the type seen in adolescents, with a peak around puberty. Neonatal torsion is usually extravaginal: the whole cord and tunica twist above the testis before the tunica has fixed to the scrotum."},{"type":"flow","title":"Torsion to infarction","steps":["Bell-clapper deformity lets the testis rotate","Spermatic cord twists","Thin-walled, low-pressure veins collapse first","Arterial inflow continues: congestion, edema, hemorrhage","Pressure inside the inelastic tunica albuginea rises","Arterial inflow stops: hemorrhagic infarction"],"caption":"Venous occlusion before arterial occlusion explains why the infarct is hemorrhagic rather than pale."},{"type":"p","text":"Viability depends on the duration of ischemia. Most testes survive if blood flow returns within about 6 hours, and few survive after about 24 hours, so torsion is a surgical emergency."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic presentation is sudden, severe, unilateral scrotal pain, often waking the patient or following exercise. Nausea and vomiting are common, and pain may be felt in the lower abdomen because visceral afferents from the testis follow the testicular vessels to lower thoracic segments around T10, where the testis originated. The twisted cord is shortened, so the testis rides high and may lie horizontally. The cremasteric reflex is normally elicited by stroking the upper medial thigh, with sensory fibers in the ilioinguinal nerve and femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve and motor fibers in the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1 and L2). In torsion it is usually absent; the testis is already drawn up on a twisted, edematous cord. Doppler ultrasound shows reduced or absent flow and sometimes a whirlpool pattern in the cord."},{"type":"table","title":"Acute scrotal pain: three common causes","cols":["Feature","Testicular torsion","Epididymitis","Torsion of the appendix testis"],"rows":[["Age","Adolescents (also newborns)","Sexually active men and older men","Prepubertal boys"],["Onset","Sudden, severe","Gradual over days","Sudden or gradual, milder"],["Testis position","High-riding, horizontal","Normal","Normal"],["Cremasteric reflex","Usually absent","Present","Present"],["Other clues","Nausea, vomiting; reduced Doppler flow","Fever, dysuria, pyuria; increased flow","Tender nodule at upper pole; blue dot sign"]],"caption":"An absent cremasteric reflex and a high-riding testis favor torsion; inflammation increases rather than reduces blood flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C07","obj":"RE-13-D.7","title":"Priapism: ischemic and nonischemic","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Priapism is a full or partial erection lasting more than 4 hours that persists without, or beyond, sexual stimulation. It reflects failure of detumescence, which normally depends on cGMP breakdown and sympathetic alpha-1 contraction of cavernosal smooth muscle to reopen the venous outflow. There are two types with opposite physiology."},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic (low-flow, veno-occlusive) priapism accounts for most cases. Venous outflow from the corpora cavernosa fails, inflow then stops, and the trapped blood becomes hypoxic, hypercapnic, and acidotic, like blood in a closed compartment. The corpora cavernosa are rigid and painful, while the glans and corpus spongiosum usually stay soft. Cavernosal blood is dark, with pO2 below about 30 mmHg, pCO2 above about 60 mmHg, and pH below about 7.25."},{"type":"p","text":"Sickle cell disease is the most common cause in children. Slow, relatively deoxygenated sinusoidal blood promotes polymerization of hemoglobin S, and rigid sickled cells obstruct outflow; disordered NO and PDE5 signaling also contributes, and many patients have recurrent brief (stuttering) episodes. Drug causes include agents injected into the corpora to relax smooth muscle, drugs with alpha-1 blocking activity such as prazosin, trazodone, and some antipsychotics (which remove the sympathetic contraction needed for detumescence), and cocaine. Leukemia with hyperviscosity is another cause."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The self-sustaining cycle of ischemic priapism","steps":["Venous outflow from the sinusoids is blocked","Trapped blood becomes hypoxic and acidotic","Hypoxic smooth muscle cannot contract","Sinusoids stay dilated and keep compressing the venules","Outflow remains blocked"],"caption":"Hypoxia and acidosis prevent the smooth muscle contraction that would end the erection, so the cycle persists until it is broken."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged ischemia injures the cavernosal tissue progressively: edema of the trabeculae within about 12 hours, loss of sinusoidal endothelium and platelet adherence by about 24 hours, and thrombosis with smooth muscle necrosis by about 48 hours. Necrotic smooth muscle is replaced by fibrous tissue, which cannot relax or compress the venules, so persistent ischemic priapism ends in permanent erectile dysfunction from veno-occlusive failure."},{"type":"table","title":"Ischemic compared with nonischemic priapism","cols":["Feature","Ischemic (low-flow)","Nonischemic (high-flow)"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Failed venous outflow","Unregulated arterial inflow through a cavernosal artery to sinusoid fistula"],["Usual cause","Sickle cell disease, drugs, hyperviscosity","Perineal or penile blunt trauma"],["Pain and rigidity","Painful, fully rigid","Usually painless, partly rigid"],["Cavernosal blood gas","Dark; low pO2, high pCO2, acidotic","Bright red; similar to arterial blood"],["Risk of fibrosis","High, time dependent","Low"]],"caption":"Blood gas analysis of cavernosal blood separates the two types."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C08","obj":"RE-13-D.8","title":"Peyronie disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each corpus cavernosum is wrapped in the tunica albuginea, a layer of dense collagen with elastic fibers that must stretch evenly for the erect penis to stay straight. Peyronie disease is a localized fibrous plaque in the tunica albuginea. Robbins groups it with the superficial fibromatoses, together with palmar fibromatosis (Dupuytren contracture), and the two conditions are associated. It mainly affects men aged about 40 to 70."},{"type":"p","text":"The favored mechanism is repeated microtrauma to the tunica during intercourse in a predisposed man. Bleeding and fibrin deposition in the tunica trigger inflammation, transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta) activates fibroblasts to become myofibroblasts, and disorganized collagen is laid down instead of normal elastic tissue. Risk is higher with age, diabetes, and Dupuytren contracture. Histologically the plaque is dense, poorly cellular collagen that replaces the elastic fibers of the tunica, and it may later calcify. The erectile tissue beneath is usually normal at first, which is why curvature, not loss of erection, is the presenting problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the plaque cannot stretch, the erect penis bends toward it. Most plaques are dorsal, giving an upward curve. There is often an early active phase, lasting roughly a year or more, with pain on erection and changing curvature, followed by a stable phase in which pain usually resolves but the deformity and a palpable plaque remain. Other effects are shortening, an indentation or hourglass narrowing, and erectile dysfunction when fibrosis disrupts the veno-occlusive mechanism or prevents rigidity beyond the plaque."},{"type":"table","title":"Penile curvature and tunica disorders","cols":["Condition","Lesion","Key features"],"rows":[["Peyronie disease","Acquired fibrous plaque of the tunica albuginea","Middle age; palpable plaque; curvature toward plaque; painful erection early"],["Congenital penile curvature","Uneven development of the corpora, no plaque","Present since puberty; painless; no palpable lesion"],["Penile fracture","Acute tear of the tunica albuginea during erection","Sudden snap, rapid detumescence, hematoma"],["Ischemic priapism (late)","Diffuse cavernosal fibrosis after ischemia","History of prolonged erection; erectile dysfunction, no focal plaque"]],"caption":"Peyronie disease is the only one of these with a focal acquired plaque in the tunica."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-04-C09","obj":"RE-13-D.9","title":"Male infertility: pretesticular, testicular, and post-testicular","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infertility is failure to conceive after 12 months of regular unprotected intercourse, and a male factor contributes in about half of couples. Spermatogenesis needs pulsatile GnRH, LH acting on Leydig cells to make testosterone, very high intratesticular testosterone, FSH acting on Sertoli cells, a testis kept below core temperature, and open ducts. Sertoli cells secrete inhibin B, which feeds back on FSH. Locating the defect before, within, or after the testis therefore predicts the hormone pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Male infertility by level","cols":["Level","FSH and LH","Testosterone","Testes","Examples"],"rows":[["Pretesticular","Low","Low (serum androgen high with anabolic steroids)","Small","Kallmann syndrome, pituitary disease, hyperprolactinemia, anabolic steroids"],["Testicular","High, FSH especially","Normal or low","Small or soft","Klinefelter syndrome, cryptorchidism, varicocele, mumps orchitis, chemotherapy or radiation"],["Post-testicular","Normal","Normal","Normal size","Obstruction after epididymitis, vasectomy, CBAVD in cystic fibrosis, ejaculatory duct obstruction"]],"caption":"Hormones locate the lesion: low gonadotropins mean the signal is missing, high FSH means the tubules are failing, and normal values with azoospermia mean a blockage."},{"type":"p","text":"Pretesticular causes are hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. In Kallmann syndrome, GnRH neurons fail to migrate from the olfactory placode, so anosmia accompanies hypogonadism. Prolactinomas suppress GnRH pulses. Anabolic steroids suppress GnRH, LH, and FSH by negative feedback; intratesticular testosterone falls and spermatogenesis stops even though serum androgen is high, and the testes shrink."},{"type":"p","text":"Testicular causes damage the tubules directly, so inhibin B falls and FSH rises. In Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) the tubules hyalinize, giving small firm testes, azoospermia, and high FSH and LH. In cryptorchidism, the higher temperature of the abdomen or inguinal canal injures germ cells, and bilateral cases cause the greatest loss. Varicocele works through heat and oxidative stress (C05). Mumps orchitis develops in a minority of men infected after puberty, is usually unilateral, and can leave tubular atrophy; sterility requires bilateral damage and is uncommon."},{"type":"p","text":"Post-testicular causes leave production and hormones normal but block delivery. In cystic fibrosis, CFTR mutations cause congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD) in nearly all affected men. The seminal vesicles, which supply most semen volume and its fructose, are often absent or hypoplastic, so the ejaculate is low in volume, acidic, fructose-poor, and free of sperm. Retrograde ejaculation (diabetic autonomic neuropathy, bladder neck surgery) also lowers volume, but sperm appear in post-ejaculatory urine. Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome) sits outside this scheme: count is normal, but sperm are immotile from defective dynein arms."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading an infertility workup","text":"A 28-year-old man has azoospermia. Case 1: semen volume 0.6 mL, acidic, no fructose; FSH, LH, and testosterone normal; testes normal size. Sperm are made but cannot leave and seminal vesicle fluid is missing, so the lesion is post-testicular: CBAVD, and CFTR testing follows. Case 2: volume normal; FSH and LH high, testosterone low, testes small and firm. The tubules have failed and inhibin B is low, so the lesion is testicular, and a karyotype showing 47,XXY confirms Klinefelter syndrome. Case 3: muscular build, FSH and LH very low, testes small. Exogenous androgen has switched off the axis, a pretesticular cause."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology explain most of the bedside findings. Descent of the testis behind the processus vaginalis accounts for hydrocele and indirect hernia, the shape of the tunica vaginalis accounts for torsion, the asymmetric venous drainage of the testes accounts for left varicocele and its link to renal cell carcinoma, and the countercurrent arrangement of the pampiniform plexus links varicocele and cryptorchidism to infertility through heat. Physics then completes the examination: clear fluid transmits light, while blood, veins, and tissue do not."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology explain the penile disorders as failures of one hemodynamic system. Erection requires NO and cGMP to relax smooth muscle and the tunica albuginea to occlude venous outflow, so ED reflects damage to nerves, endothelium, arteries, or hormones; ischemic priapism is outflow that will not reopen; and Peyronie disease is a tunica that will not stretch. Fibrosis is the common end point of ischemic priapism and Peyronie disease, and both can cause ED. These categories overlap in real patients, and the classic signs (an absent cremasteric reflex, transillumination) are useful but imperfect, which is why imaging confirms what the examination suggests."}],"summary":["Erection follows parasympathetic S2 to S4 release of NO, cGMP-mediated smooth muscle relaxation, sinusoidal filling, and compression of subtunical venules against the tunica albuginea.","ED is vascular, neurogenic, endocrine, drug-induced, or psychogenic; preserved nocturnal and morning erections show intact peripheral machinery and point to a psychogenic cause.","Hydrocele (clear fluid around the testis) and spermatocele (sperm-filled epididymal cyst separate from the testis) transilluminate; hematocele and varicocele, which contain blood, do not.","A patent processus vaginalis gives a communicating hydrocele that changes size with posture; noncommunicating hydroceles follow fluid imbalance from infection, trauma, tumor, or lymphatic obstruction.","A spermatocele and a simple epididymal cyst look the same; only sperm in the fluid separates them, and both are benign.","Varicocele is a refluxing pampiniform plexus, usually left-sided because the left testicular vein drains at a right angle into the left renal vein; it impairs spermatogenesis mainly by heat.","A new left varicocele in an older man, or a right or nondecompressing one, suggests venous obstruction such as renal cell carcinoma in the renal vein or a retroperitoneal mass.","Torsion in a bell-clapper testis blocks veins before arteries, causing hemorrhagic infarction; sudden pain, a high-riding testis, and an absent cremasteric reflex are the clues, and viability falls after about 6 hours.","Ischemic priapism is painful, hypoxic, and acidotic (sickle cell disease, alpha-1 blocking drugs) and ends in fibrosis and ED; nonischemic priapism follows trauma and is painless with arterial blood.","Peyronie disease is a fibrous tunica albuginea plaque that bends the erect penis toward it; infertility is pretesticular (low gonadotropins), testicular (high FSH), or post-testicular (normal hormones, obstruction)."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, sections on the penis, the testis and epididymis, and the tunica vaginalis"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, inguinal region and spermatic cord, scrotum, and testes; Pelvis and Perineum, penis and its innervation"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, descent of the testis and the processus vaginalis"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male, sections on spermatogenesis and the male sexual act"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Male Reproductive System"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the male reproductive tract"}]},"RE-13-E":{"unit":"RE-13-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05","title":"Conditions of the Breast","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers breast disease from benign change and inflammation through benign tumors to carcinoma in women and men. Board questions usually describe a lump, a skin change, or a nipple discharge in a patient of a given age and setting, then ask which lesion it is, whether it raises cancer risk, or what the microscope would show. The unifying ideas are that cancer risk follows epithelial proliferation and atypia, that several benign lesions heal by fibrosis and calcification and so mimic carcinoma, and that the cell of origin (epithelium or stroma) and the adhesion of tumor cells explain how each tumor looks and spreads.","prerequisites":["Breast anatomy: lactiferous ducts, terminal duct lobular unit, suspensory (Cooper) ligaments, and axillary lymphatic drainage","Two-layer duct epithelium: luminal cells over myoepithelial cells on a basement membrane","Estrogen and progesterone effects on breast epithelium across the menstrual cycle, pregnancy, and lactation","Types of necrosis, dystrophic calcification, and chronic and granulomatous inflammation","Tumor suppressor genes, cell adhesion molecules, and the steps of invasion and metastasis"],"connections":[{"to":"Endocrinology (prolactin, estrogen, androgen balance)","how":"Hormone exposure drives fibrocystic change, fibroadenoma growth, carcinoma risk, and gynecomastia."},{"to":"Microbiology (Staphylococcus aureus)","how":"Coagulase and fibrin deposition explain why lactational mastitis tends to localize into an abscess."},{"to":"General pathology (necrosis and calcification)","how":"Traumatic fat necrosis is the classic nonenzymatic fat necrosis, contrasted with enzymatic fat necrosis in acute pancreatitis."},{"to":"Genetics (BRCA1, BRCA2, CDH1, Klinefelter syndrome)","how":"Germline defects in DNA repair or adhesion, and sex chromosome aneuploidy, explain familial and male breast carcinoma."},{"to":"Liver disease (GI)","how":"Cirrhosis raises the estrogen to androgen ratio and produces gynecomastia."}],"terms":[{"term":"Terminal duct lobular unit","def":"The lobule and its terminal duct, the functional unit of the breast and the site where most benign and malignant lesions arise."},{"term":"Myoepithelial cell","def":"A contractile cell between the luminal epithelium and the basement membrane whose presence marks a lesion as benign or in situ rather than invasive."},{"term":"Apocrine metaplasia","def":"Change of cyst lining cells to large cells with abundant pink granular cytoplasm resembling apocrine sweat gland cells, a benign finding."},{"term":"Atypical hyperplasia","def":"A clonal intraductal or intralobular proliferation with some but not all features of carcinoma in situ."},{"term":"Biphasic tumor","def":"A tumor containing both stroma and epithelium, such as fibroadenoma and phyllodes tumor, in which the stroma is the neoplastic component."},{"term":"Desmoplasia","def":"Dense fibrous stroma laid down by fibroblasts in response to invading tumor, making the mass hard."},{"term":"Carcinoma in situ","def":"Malignant epithelial cells confined within ducts or lobules by an intact basement membrane."},{"term":"E-cadherin","def":"A calcium-dependent adhesion protein of adherens junctions, encoded by CDH1, that binds neighboring epithelial cells together."},{"term":"Peau d'orange","def":"Thickened, dimpled skin resembling orange peel, caused by dermal lymphedema."},{"term":"Gynecomastia","def":"Benign enlargement of the male breast from ductal and stromal proliferation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C01","obj":"RE-13-E.1","title":"Fibrocystic change and how benign lesions predict cancer risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fibrocystic change, also called diffuse cystic mastopathy or fibrocystic breast disease, is the most common benign breast condition. It is not a single disease but a set of changes in the terminal duct lobular unit that reflect repeated cycles of estrogen and progesterone stimulation. It is most common from the 20s to menopause and usually regresses afterward. Patients report lumpy, often bilateral breasts with tenderness that worsens before menstruation, sometimes with a discrete cyst that can be felt."},{"type":"p","text":"The organizing principle for board questions is that cancer risk tracks with how much the epithelium proliferates and whether the proliferating cells look atypical. Cysts form when lobules dilate and fill with fluid; large ones can appear blue through the wall and are called blue-dome cysts. Their lining often shows apocrine metaplasia, and ruptured cysts provoke inflammation that heals by fibrosis. These nonproliferative changes involve little epithelial growth and add no meaningful cancer risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Proliferative change without atypia includes usual ductal hyperplasia (extra layers of polyclonal cells filling the duct), sclerosing adenosis (increased acini compressed by fibrosis, which can feel firm and calcify), intraductal papilloma, and radial scar. These carry a slightly raised risk because more cell division gives more chances for mutation. Atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia is a clonal proliferation that shares molecular changes with carcinoma in situ and behaves as a nonobligate precursor and a marker of risk. Its risk applies to both breasts, which shows that it signals a field of susceptible tissue rather than only the spot sampled."},{"type":"table","title":"Benign epithelial changes and relative risk of invasive carcinoma","cols":["Category","Examples","Approximate relative risk"],"rows":[["Nonproliferative","Cysts, apocrine metaplasia, fibrosis","About 1 (no added risk)"],["Proliferative without atypia","Usual ductal hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis, papilloma, radial scar","About 1.5 to 2"],["Atypical hyperplasia","Atypical ductal hyperplasia, atypical lobular hyperplasia","About 4 to 5"],["Carcinoma in situ (comparison)","DCIS, LCIS","About 8 to 10"]],"caption":"Risk rises with epithelial proliferation and then with atypia. Values are relative to women without these lesions and apply to either breast."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A biopsy of a tender lump shows a cyst lined by apocrine metaplasia with surrounding fibrosis. How does this change her cancer risk compared with a biopsy showing atypical ductal hyperplasia?","explain":"The first is nonproliferative and adds no risk; atypical hyperplasia is a clonal precursor lesion with about 4 to 5 times the risk in either breast.","check":"Compare with the risk table and the paragraph on atypia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C02","obj":"RE-13-E.2","title":"Galactocele","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A galactocele is a cyst filled with milk, formed when a lactiferous duct becomes obstructed during lactation or during weaning. The epithelium of the lobule keeps secreting, the outflow is blocked by thickened (inspissated) milk, and the duct behind the block dilates into a smooth, round, usually painless and mobile cyst. It is the most common benign breast mass in lactating women."},{"type":"p","text":"The cyst lining is flattened duct epithelium, and the contents are milky fluid rich in fat and protein. Over time the water is resorbed and the fat concentrates, so older galactoceles become thicker and can appear partly solid on imaging. Aspiration yields milky fluid, which both confirms the diagnosis and distinguishes the lesion from a solid tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"The key prediction is what happens if the cyst ruptures. Milk lipid and protein are irritants once they leave the duct, so their release into the surrounding stroma triggers an acute and then chronic inflammatory reaction with foamy macrophages and giant cells. The breast becomes locally red, tender, and indurated, and healing by fibrosis can leave a firm nodule. Secondary infection can also occur because stagnant milk is a good culture medium, which links galactocele to acute mastitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From blocked duct to inflamed galactocele","steps":["Lactation or weaning with ongoing milk secretion","Inspissated milk obstructs a lactiferous duct","Duct dilates into a milk-filled cyst","Cyst ruptures, releasing milk lipid into stroma","Foreign body inflammation with foamy macrophages","Local redness, tenderness, then fibrotic nodule"],"caption":"Obstruction causes the cyst; leakage of its contents causes the inflammation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C03","obj":"RE-13-E.3","title":"Mammary duct ectasia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mammary duct ectasia is dilation of the large subareolar ducts with stasis of their secretions and chronic inflammation around them. It typically affects women in their 50s and 60s, often multiparous, and, unlike periductal mastitis, it is not specifically linked to smoking. The cause is not certain. The dilated ducts fill with thick, pasty material containing lipid-laden macrophages, and the secretion seeps into the duct wall and surrounding stroma."},{"type":"p","text":"That leakage drives a periductal and interstitial chronic inflammatory reaction, sometimes granulomatous, that is characteristically rich in plasma cells. For this reason the older term plasma cell mastitis is sometimes used. The clinical picture follows from the pathology: a poorly defined, firm periareolar mass, thick white or greenish nipple discharge from several ducts, and, less often, pain and redness. The inspissated secretions can calcify, producing calcifications on mammography."},{"type":"p","text":"The reason duct ectasia mimics carcinoma is fibrosis. Chronic inflammation heals by collagen deposition, and as scar tissue around the subareolar ducts contracts, it shortens the ducts and pulls the nipple inward. A firm irregular mass, nipple retraction, and calcifications are exactly the features that raise concern for invasive carcinoma, in which desmoplastic stroma also contracts and retracts the nipple. Only tissue examination reliably separates them: duct ectasia shows dilated ducts with plasma cell inflammation and no malignant cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Duct ectasia compared with periductal mastitis","cols":["Feature","Mammary duct ectasia","Periductal mastitis"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Older, often multiparous woman","Smoker, often younger"],["Duct change","Dilation with inspissated secretions","Squamous metaplasia with keratin plugs"],["Inflammation","Chronic, plasma cell rich","Acute and granulomatous around keratin"],["Usual presentation","Thick discharge, ill-defined mass, retraction","Painful red subareolar mass or abscess"]],"caption":"Both are subareolar and both can retract the nipple; the duct lining and the patient profile separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C04","obj":"RE-13-E.4","title":"Acute mastitis and breast abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute mastitis is bacterial infection of the breast that occurs almost always during breastfeeding, most often in the first weeks to months. Suckling can crack and fissure the nipple, which breaks the skin barrier and lets bacteria from the mother's skin or the infant's mouth enter the ducts. Milk stasis from incomplete emptying or a blocked duct provides a warm, nutrient-rich medium, so the organisms multiply in the ducts and spread into the lobules and stroma."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus aureus is the usual cause. Its coagulase activates prothrombin to convert fibrinogen to fibrin, and together with tissue destruction by its toxins and the neutrophil response, this tends to wall the infection off into a localized collection of pus, a breast abscess. Streptococci, by contrast, secrete enzymes such as hyaluronidase and streptokinase that favor diffuse spread through tissue, so streptococcal infection tends to cause a spreading cellulitis rather than a single abscess."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from acute inflammation. Vasodilation and increased permeability make the affected segment red, warm, swollen, and painful, and cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF reset the hypothalamic set point to produce fever, often 38.5 °C (101.3 °F) or higher, with malaise and leukocytosis. A fluctuant, tender mass that persists within the inflamed area indicates an abscess. Infection is usually unilateral and wedge-shaped, following the segment drained by one duct system."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of lactational mastitis","steps":["Breastfeeding causes nipple cracks and fissures","Skin or infant oral flora enter the ducts","Milk stasis supports bacterial growth","S. aureus spreads into lobules and stroma","Neutrophils and fibrin localize the infection","Red, warm, painful breast with fever","Possible abscess: fluctuant tender mass"],"caption":"Broken skin, stagnant milk, and the walling-off behavior of S. aureus explain why abscess is the classic complication."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C05","obj":"RE-13-E.5","title":"Periductal mastitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Periductal mastitis, also called recurrent subareolar abscess or squamous metaplasia of lactiferous ducts, is an inflammatory condition that is not related to lactation. More than 90% of affected patients smoke, and it can occur in women of any adult age and occasionally in men. Smoking is thought to alter the differentiation of the ductal epithelium, possibly through toxic components of smoke or relative vitamin A deficiency, although the exact mechanism is uncertain."},{"type":"p","text":"Normally only the most distal portion of each lactiferous duct, at the nipple opening, is lined by keratinizing squamous epithelium. In periductal mastitis, squamous metaplasia extends deeper into the ducts beneath the nipple. The metaplastic cells shed keratin, which accumulates and plugs the duct. The plugged duct dilates and eventually ruptures, spilling keratin into the periductal stroma. Keratin is a powerful irritant outside the epithelium, and it provokes an intense acute and then chronic granulomatous inflammatory response, often with secondary bacterial infection."},{"type":"p","text":"The result is a painful, red subareolar mass that may form an abscess. Because the process can recur and may drain through the skin, a fistulous tract can form that opens at the edge of the areola. Inflammation and fibrosis around the ducts can also invert the nipple. The patient is not breastfeeding, which separates this lesion from acute lactational mastitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Keratin plugging in periductal mastitis","steps":["Smoking alters lactiferous duct epithelium","Squamous metaplasia extends deep into ducts","Shed keratin plugs and dilates the duct","Duct ruptures; keratin enters periductal stroma","Intense granulomatous inflammation","Painful subareolar mass, abscess, possible fistula"],"caption":"Metaplasia creates the keratin, and keratin outside the duct creates the inflammation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman who smokes and is not breastfeeding has a recurrent painful subareolar abscess. What lines the involved ducts, and what material incites the inflammation?","explain":"Keratinizing squamous epithelium from squamous metaplasia; keratin released from ruptured plugged ducts drives granulomatous inflammation.","check":"Compare with the flow on keratin plugging and the contrast with acute mastitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C06","obj":"RE-13-E.6","title":"Traumatic fat necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Traumatic fat necrosis is death of breast adipocytes caused by physical injury, including blunt trauma (such as a seatbelt injury), prior surgery, biopsy, or radiation. A clear history of injury is absent in many patients because the trauma may have been minor and forgotten. It is called nonenzymatic to contrast it with the enzymatic fat necrosis of acute pancreatitis, in which released lipase digests fat. In the breast, adipocytes rupture from mechanical or ischemic injury rather than from enzyme attack."},{"type":"p","text":"The microscopic picture evolves in a predictable order. Early on there is hemorrhage and necrotic fat with neutrophils. The released lipid is then taken up by macrophages, which become foamy (lipid-laden), and multinucleated foreign body giant cells form around lipid droplets and cholesterol clefts. Over weeks, fibroblasts lay down collagen, hemosiderin marks the old hemorrhage, and dystrophic calcification deposits in the necrotic tissue. Liquefied fat may collect into an oil cyst."},{"type":"p","text":"These healing changes explain the clinical problem. The lesion becomes a firm, sometimes irregular, painless mass, and fibrosis can tether it to the skin, causing skin thickening or retraction. On mammography the calcifications and spiculated density can resemble those of carcinoma. Fat necrosis therefore mimics cancer on both physical examination and imaging, and biopsy showing foamy macrophages, giant cells, and fibrosis without malignant cells settles the question."},{"type":"table","title":"Evolution of traumatic fat necrosis","cols":["Stage","Main findings","Clinical correlate"],"rows":[["Early (days)","Hemorrhage, ruptured adipocytes, neutrophils","Bruising, tender swelling"],["Intermediate","Foamy macrophages, giant cells, cholesterol clefts","Firm nodule"],["Late (weeks to months)","Fibrosis, hemosiderin, dystrophic calcification, oil cysts","Firm irregular mass, skin retraction, mammographic calcification"]],"caption":"Fibrosis and calcification in the late stage are what make fat necrosis mimic carcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C07","obj":"RE-13-E.7","title":"Fibroadenoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fibroadenoma is the most common benign tumor of the breast and the most common breast mass in women under about 30. It presents as a small (often 1 to 3 cm), round, rubbery, well-circumscribed mass that moves freely under the fingers because it pushes surrounding tissue aside rather than invading it. Most are solitary, though some women have several, in one or both breasts."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibroadenoma is a biphasic tumor arising from the intralobular stroma of the terminal duct lobular unit. The stromal cells are the neoplastic component, and many carry mutations in MED12, a gene involved in transcriptional regulation. The ducts are not neoplastic; they are carried along and may be compressed into slit-like spaces by the expanding stroma. The duct epithelium keeps its normal two-cell layer of luminal and myoepithelial cells, which helps confirm a benign lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"Both stroma and epithelium respond to hormones. Fibroadenomas may enlarge late in the menstrual cycle, grow during pregnancy under high estrogen and progesterone, and can show lactational change in the epithelium. After menopause, as estrogen falls, they regress, and the stroma often becomes hyalinized and calcified, producing coarse calcifications on mammography in older women. Fibroadenoma adds little or no cancer risk, although complex fibroadenomas containing cysts, sclerosing adenosis, or calcifications sit in the proliferative without atypia group."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C08","obj":"RE-13-E.8","title":"Phyllodes tumor compared with fibroadenoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Phyllodes tumor is also a biphasic tumor of intralobular stroma, so it is a relative of fibroadenoma, but its stroma is more cellular, grows faster, and behaves less predictably. It is much less common than fibroadenoma and usually appears in women in their 40s to 60s, roughly 10 to 20 years later than fibroadenoma. The typical presentation is a palpable mass that is often larger and may grow quickly."},{"type":"p","text":"The name comes from the Greek for leaf. The hypercellular stroma overgrows the epithelium and pushes into epithelium-lined clefts, producing leaf-like projections when the tumor is cut. Under the microscope the stroma shows more cells than a fibroadenoma, and higher-grade tumors add nuclear atypia, frequent mitoses, stromal overgrowth (stroma without epithelium over a wide field), and infiltrative borders. These features are used to grade tumors as benign, borderline, or malignant."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the neoplasm is a stromal (mesenchymal) proliferation, its behavior follows sarcoma rules rather than carcinoma rules. Low-grade tumors may recur locally if incompletely removed, and high-grade tumors can metastasize, chiefly through the blood to the lungs and bone. Spread to axillary lymph nodes is uncommon, which contrasts with breast carcinoma."},{"type":"table","title":"Fibroadenoma versus phyllodes tumor","cols":["Feature","Fibroadenoma","Phyllodes tumor"],"rows":[["Usual age","Under about 30","About 40s to 60s"],["Size and growth","Small, slow, stable","Often larger, may grow rapidly"],["Stroma","Modestly cellular, uniform","Hypercellular, may be atypical with mitoses"],["Architecture","Ducts compressed by stroma","Leaf-like projections into clefts"],["Behavior","Benign, little or no added cancer risk","May recur locally; high grade can metastasize by blood"]],"caption":"Both are stromal biphasic tumors; age, stromal cellularity, and leaf-like architecture separate them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C09","obj":"RE-13-E.9","title":"Intraductal papilloma and nipple discharge","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An intraductal papilloma is a benign papillary growth inside a duct. The common central (large duct) form is usually solitary and lies in a lactiferous sinus or large duct beneath the areola, typically in women of reproductive age through middle age. Each papilla has a branching fibrovascular core covered by the normal two cell layers, luminal epithelium on the inside and myoepithelial cells beneath. The intact myoepithelial layer is the key feature that separates a benign papilloma from papillary carcinoma, in which the myoepithelial layer is absent within the papillae."},{"type":"p","text":"The fronds are delicate and carry small blood vessels. Twisting, infarction, or minor trauma of a frond inside a confined duct bleeds into the lumen, and because the duct opens on the nipple, the blood appears as a serous or bloody discharge from a single duct of one breast. A small subareolar mass may also be felt. Intraductal papilloma is the most common cause of bloody nipple discharge in a woman without a palpable mass. It sits in the proliferative without atypia group, with a slightly raised cancer risk, while multiple peripheral papillomas carry somewhat more risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Nipple discharge is interpreted by its color, laterality, and number of ducts, because each pattern points to a different mechanism, as the table shows. Bloody, spontaneous, single-duct discharge is the pattern that most needs explanation, since carcinoma (often DCIS with a papillary pattern) can also produce it, especially in older women."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of nipple discharge","cols":["Discharge pattern","Typical cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Bloody or serous, one duct, one breast","Intraductal papilloma; less often carcinoma","Bleeding from fronds or tumor inside a large duct"],["Thick, sticky, white to green, several ducts","Mammary duct ectasia","Inspissated secretions in dilated subareolar ducts"],["Milky, both breasts, several ducts","Galactorrhea from hyperprolactinemia","Prolactin excess (prolactinoma, dopamine blockade, hypothyroidism) drives milk secretion"],["Purulent","Mastitis or subareolar abscess","Infection or keratin-driven inflammation of ducts"]],"caption":"Bilateral milky discharge is endocrine; unilateral single-duct bloody discharge is a local duct lesion until proven otherwise."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C10","obj":"RE-13-E.10","title":"Risk factors for breast carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Breast carcinoma is the most common non-skin cancer in women. Most cases arise in the terminal duct lobular unit, and most are hormone dependent. The risk factors make sense once one principle is clear: each menstrual cycle exposes breast epithelium to estrogen and progesterone, which stimulate proliferation, and every round of proliferation gives another chance for DNA replication errors to be copied and kept. More cycles and more total estrogen therefore mean more accumulated mutations."},{"type":"p","text":"Early menarche and late menopause lengthen the span of cycling. Nulliparity and a late first full-term pregnancy increase risk because a full-term pregnancy drives the lobular epithelium to final differentiation, after which it is less responsive to proliferative signals; the earlier this happens, the fewer undifferentiated cycles occur before it. Breastfeeding also lowers risk by suppressing cycling. After menopause the ovary stops making estrogen, and the main source becomes aromatase in adipose tissue, which converts androstenedione to estrone, so postmenopausal obesity raises circulating estrogen and risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Age is the strongest single risk factor after female sex; carcinoma is rare before 25 and incidence rises steadily thereafter. Atypical hyperplasia raises risk about 4 to 5 times. Family history matters most when first-degree relatives were affected young or in both breasts, and a minority of cases arise from germline mutations, chiefly BRCA1 and BRCA2, which impair homologous recombination repair of double-strand DNA breaks. Other factors include chest radiation in youth, dense breast tissue, and alcohol use."},{"type":"p","text":"About half of carcinomas arise in the upper outer quadrant, simply because that quadrant, with the axillary tail, contains the most glandular tissue. This location also places the tumor close to the axillary lymph nodes, the main drainage route and first site of spread."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors grouped by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Risk factors"],"rows":[["Longer or greater estrogen exposure","Early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, late first pregnancy, postmenopausal obesity"],["Accumulated somatic mutation over time","Increasing age"],["Clonal precursor in the tissue","Atypical ductal or lobular hyperplasia"],["Inherited susceptibility","Affected first-degree relatives; BRCA1, BRCA2, and rarer genes (TP53, CDH1)"],["Direct DNA damage","Chest radiation, especially before about age 30"]],"caption":"Most risk factors either add proliferative cycles under estrogen or reduce the ability to repair DNA."},{"type":"example","title":"Ranking estrogen-related risk","text":"Two 55-year-old women are compared. Woman A had menarche at 15, a first full-term pregnancy at 22, breastfed three children, and has a BMI of 23 kg/m2. Woman B had menarche at 11, no pregnancies, and a BMI of 34 kg/m2. Step 1: count cycling time; B began cycling 4 years earlier and never had pregnancy or lactation to interrupt it. Step 2: consider differentiation; A's early full-term pregnancy matured her lobular epithelium decades ago, while B's epithelium remains less differentiated. Step 3: consider current estrogen; after menopause, B's larger adipose mass converts more androstenedione to estrone through aromatase. Every factor points the same way, so B has the higher estrogen-related risk, although family history and biopsy findings could still change the overall ranking."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C11","obj":"RE-13-E.11","title":"Ductal carcinoma in situ and invasive ductal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) is a clonal proliferation of malignant epithelial cells within ducts and lobules that has not crossed the basement membrane. Because the myoepithelial layer and basement membrane remain intact, the cells have no access to stromal lymphatics or blood vessels, so DCIS cannot metastasize. It is usually not palpable and is most often found on screening mammography as microcalcifications. The calcifications form in two ways: in necrotic debris and in secretions within the duct lumens."},{"type":"p","text":"DCIS has several architectural patterns (solid, cribriform, papillary, micropapillary). The comedo type is high grade, with large pleomorphic nuclei; the central cells outgrow their diffusion-dependent blood supply, which lies outside the basement membrane, and die. The necrotic center calcifies, giving linear or branching calcifications on mammography, and on cut section the necrotic material can be squeezed out like a comedone. DCIS is a nonobligate precursor of invasive carcinoma in the same breast, and it can also spread along ducts to the nipple skin as Paget disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Invasive ductal carcinoma, also called invasive carcinoma of no special type, accounts for most invasive breast cancers, roughly 70% to 80%. Tumor cells breach the basement membrane and infiltrate stroma as nests, cords, and glands, and they provoke desmoplasia. This dense reactive collagen makes the tumor rock hard with an irregular stellate outline and gritty cut surface, and its contraction pulls on Cooper ligaments to dimple the skin or retract the nipple. Spread is first to axillary lymph nodes, then by blood to bone, lung, liver, and brain. Size, nodal status, grade, and the expression of estrogen and progesterone receptors and HER2 determine behavior."},{"type":"flow","title":"From in situ to invasive ductal carcinoma","steps":["Atypical ductal hyperplasia: clonal, partly filled duct","DCIS: malignant cells fill duct, basement membrane intact","Central necrosis and microcalcification in comedo DCIS","Loss of myoepithelial cells and basement membrane breach","Stromal invasion with desmoplasia: hard stellate mass","Lymphatic spread to axillary nodes, then hematogenous"],"caption":"Invasion through the basement membrane is the step that allows metastasis; this progression is common but not obligatory."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C12","obj":"RE-13-E.12","title":"Lobular carcinoma and loss of E-cadherin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lobular neoplasia is defined by a single molecular event: loss of E-cadherin, the calcium-dependent adhesion protein of adherens junctions encoded by CDH1. Normally E-cadherin molecules on neighboring epithelial cells bind each other and anchor to the actin cytoskeleton through catenins, holding cells in cohesive sheets. In lobular lesions, one CDH1 allele is lost and the other is inactivated by mutation or silencing, so the cells lose their attachment to one another and become discohesive."},{"type":"p","text":"Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS) is a uniform population of small, round, discohesive cells filling and expanding the acini of a lobule. It rarely forms a mass or calcifications, so it is usually an incidental biopsy finding. It is both a marker of risk and a nonobligate precursor, and the increased risk applies to both breasts. Invasive lobular carcinoma, about 10% of invasive cancers, shows how the lack of adhesion changes the pattern of invasion: cells infiltrate stroma one by one, lined up in single-file strands between collagen bundles, and may encircle normal ducts in a target-like pattern. Some cells contain intracytoplasmic mucin and look like signet ring cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the cells slip singly through tissue with little desmoplasia, invasive lobular carcinoma often forms a vague thickening rather than a discrete hard mass and can be subtle on mammography. Disease is frequently multifocal in one breast and is bilateral more often than ductal carcinoma. Most tumors are estrogen receptor positive and HER2 negative. Lobular carcinoma also spreads to unusual sites, including the peritoneum, retroperitoneum, leptomeninges, gastrointestinal tract, and ovaries. Germline CDH1 mutation causes hereditary diffuse gastric cancer and raises the risk of lobular breast carcinoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a carcinoma loses E-cadherin, how would its cells be arranged in the stroma, and why might it be missed on palpation and mammography?","explain":"Without cell to cell adhesion the cells invade singly in single-file lines with little desmoplasia, so the tumor forms an ill-defined thickening rather than a hard spiculated mass.","check":"Compare with the paragraphs on CDH1 and invasive lobular carcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C13","obj":"RE-13-E.13","title":"Paget disease of the breast","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Paget disease of the breast (Paget disease of the nipple) is an uncommon presentation of breast carcinoma, roughly 1% to 4% of cases, in which malignant cells involve the epidermis of the nipple and areola. It presents as a unilateral red, scaly, crusted, eczema-like rash on the nipple that may itch, ooze, bleed, or ulcerate and then spread to the areola. Because it looks like dermatitis, it can be overlooked, which is why a persistent one-sided nipple rash is significant."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism is intraepithelial spread. Carcinoma cells from an underlying DCIS travel along the lactiferous ducts to the nipple and then move into the squamous epidermis, without crossing the epidermal basement membrane. Their presence separates the keratinocytes and disrupts the epidermal barrier, so fluid exudes and dries into crust. Under the microscope, Paget cells are large, with abundant pale to clear cytoplasm, large nuclei, and prominent nucleoli, scattered singly or in clusters among keratinocytes. They are epithelial cells, so they stain for cytokeratins, and some contain mucin; most overexpress HER2."},{"type":"p","text":"Paget disease is nearly always accompanied by an underlying carcinoma in the same breast, either DCIS alone or invasive carcinoma, and a palpable mass usually means an invasive component. Prognosis depends on that underlying tumor rather than on the skin change itself. Two distinctions are commonly tested: eczema is usually bilateral and lacks Paget cells, and melanoma in situ contains melanocytic cells that stain for melanocytic markers (such as S100 and melan-A) rather than cytokeratins. Extramammary Paget disease of the vulva is related in appearance but, unlike the breast form, usually has no underlying carcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C14","obj":"RE-13-E.14","title":"Inflammatory carcinoma compared with acute mastitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Inflammatory breast carcinoma is a clinical presentation, not a separate histologic type; the underlying tumor is usually a high-grade invasive ductal carcinoma. It accounts for only a few percent of breast cancers but has a poor prognosis, and many patients already have distant metastases at diagnosis. Despite the name, the redness and swelling are not caused primarily by inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"The characteristic lesion is tumor emboli within dermal lymphatics. Clumps of carcinoma cells plug the small lymphatic channels in the skin, lymph cannot drain, and the dermis fills with edema fluid. Dilated superficial vessels and congestion give a red, warm appearance over a third or more of the breast. The swollen skin bulges between points where it is tethered to underlying tissue at hair follicles and gland openings, so those points become pits and the surface looks like orange peel (peau d'orange). The whole breast becomes enlarged, firm, and heavy, and there is often no discrete palpable mass because the tumor diffusely infiltrates rather than forming one lump. Onset is rapid, usually over weeks to a few months."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why inflammatory carcinoma looks inflamed","steps":["High-grade carcinoma invades dermal lymphatics","Tumor emboli block lymph drainage from skin","Dermal edema and vascular congestion","Skin pits at tethered follicles: peau d'orange","Diffuse redness, warmth, and breast enlargement"],"caption":"The redness and swelling come from blocked lymphatics, not from infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Inflammatory carcinoma versus acute mastitis","cols":["Feature","Inflammatory carcinoma","Acute mastitis"],"rows":[["Setting","Usually not lactating, often older","Breastfeeding, early weeks to months"],["Cause of redness","Tumor emboli in dermal lymphatics","Bacterial infection, usually S. aureus"],["Fever and leukocytosis","Usually absent","Common"],["Pain","Often less prominent; heaviness","Marked, localized tenderness"],["Extent and course","Diffuse, progressive over weeks","Segmental, acute onset"],["Skin biopsy","Carcinoma, classically in dermal lymphatics","Neutrophilic inflammation, no tumor"]],"caption":"A red, swollen breast in a nonlactating woman without fever is carcinoma until biopsy shows otherwise."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a red breast","text":"A 52-year-old woman who is not breastfeeding has a red, warm, swollen left breast that has enlarged over six weeks. Her temperature is 36.8 °C (98.2 °F), and the skin is thickened and pitted. Step 1: setting; mastitis is a disease of lactation, and she is not lactating. Step 2: systemic signs; infection severe enough to redden a third of the breast should produce fever and leukocytosis, and she has neither. Step 3: skin change; pitting over a broad area means dermal lymphedema, which follows lymphatic obstruction. Step 4: tie the findings to one lesion; tumor emboli in dermal lymphatics explain the redness, edema, and peau d'orange together. Biopsy is expected to show carcinoma, classically within dermal lymphatic channels."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-05-C15","obj":"RE-13-E.15","title":"Gynecomastia and male breast carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The male breast contains a few ducts embedded in stroma and fat, with no lobules. Estrogen stimulates ductal epithelium and periductal stroma to grow, while androgens oppose this growth, so the balance between the two decides whether the male breast enlarges. Gynecomastia is benign enlargement caused by a raised estrogen to androgen ratio. It forms a firm, rubbery, sometimes tender, disc-like enlargement centered beneath the areola, often bilateral but sometimes one-sided. Histologically, the ducts show epithelial hyperplasia within loose, then later dense, collagenous stroma; lobules usually do not form."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiologic gynecomastia occurs in newborns (maternal estrogen), at puberty (estrogen production rises before testosterone reaches adult levels), and in older men (falling testosterone and more adipose aromatase). Pathologic causes act on the same ratio. In cirrhosis, the liver clears estrogens and androstenedione poorly, and more androstenedione is converted to estrogen in peripheral fat. In Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), damaged testes produce little testosterone, and high LH stimulates aromatase activity. Testicular tumors can secrete estrogen (Leydig cell tumors) or hCG (some germ cell tumors), which drives estrogen production. Drugs act through the same balance, for example spironolactone by blocking androgen receptors."},{"type":"p","text":"Male breast carcinoma is rare, about 1% of breast cancers, and usually occurs in older men. It presents as a firm, painless, usually unilateral mass that is often eccentric rather than symmetric beneath the areola and may cause nipple retraction, discharge, or ulceration. Because the male breast is small, tumors reach the skin and chest wall early. Most are invasive ductal carcinomas that express estrogen receptors. The strongest genetic risk factor is a germline BRCA2 mutation, and Klinefelter syndrome raises risk substantially through long-standing high estrogen relative to androgen. Gynecomastia itself is not considered a precursor."},{"type":"table","title":"Gynecomastia versus male breast carcinoma","cols":["Feature","Gynecomastia","Male breast carcinoma"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Raised estrogen to androgen ratio","Malignant ductal epithelium"],["Age","Newborn, puberty, older age","Usually older men"],["Laterality and position","Often bilateral, centered under areola","Usually unilateral, often eccentric"],["Consistency","Rubbery, may be tender","Hard, painless, may be fixed"],["Skin and nipple","Normal","Retraction, discharge, ulceration possible"],["Key associations","Puberty, cirrhosis, Klinefelter, testicular tumors, drugs","BRCA2, Klinefelter syndrome"]],"caption":"Symmetric soft enlargement suggests hormone imbalance; a hard eccentric fixed mass suggests carcinoma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology set the stage. Nearly all lesions arise in the terminal duct lobular unit or the large subareolar ducts, and location predicts presentation: subareolar disease (duct ectasia, periductal mastitis, papilloma, Paget disease) shows up as nipple discharge, retraction, or skin change, while lobular lesions present as masses or calcifications. Estrogen and progesterone drive normal cyclic proliferation, so the same hormones explain fibrocystic change, fibroadenoma growth, carcinoma risk, and, in men, gynecomastia. Cooper ligaments and axillary drainage explain skin dimpling and the first site of metastasis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology supplies two reasoning rules. First, risk follows proliferation and atypia, and invasion requires breaching the basement membrane, which the myoepithelial layer helps mark. Second, many benign processes (fat necrosis, duct ectasia, sclerosing adenosis, periductal mastitis) heal by fibrosis and calcification and so reproduce the hardness, retraction, and calcification of carcinoma; only histology settles the difference. Microbiology explains the abscess tendency of S. aureus, and genetics explains why BRCA2 and Klinefelter syndrome dominate male disease and why CDH1 loss produces both lobular breast and diffuse gastric carcinoma."}],"summary":["Benign breast lesions raise cancer risk in proportion to epithelial proliferation and atypia: nonproliferative none, proliferative without atypia about 1.5 to 2 times, atypical hyperplasia about 4 to 5 times, in either breast.","Galactocele is a milk-filled duct cyst of lactation or weaning; rupture releases milk lipid and causes local inflammation.","Duct ectasia (older multiparous women) and periductal mastitis (smokers, keratin plugging from squamous metaplasia) are subareolar inflammatory lesions that can retract the nipple.","Acute mastitis is S. aureus infection through cracked nipples during breastfeeding, with a red, warm, painful breast, fever, and possible abscess.","Traumatic fat necrosis heals with foamy macrophages, giant cells, fibrosis, and calcification, so it mimics carcinoma on exam and mammography.","Fibroadenoma (young women, mobile, hormone responsive) and phyllodes tumor (older women, hypercellular leaf-like stroma, can recur or metastasize) are both stromal biphasic tumors.","Intraductal papilloma is the most common cause of bloody single-duct nipple discharge; its preserved myoepithelial layer marks it as benign.","Carcinoma risk rises with age, cumulative estrogen exposure, atypical hyperplasia, and family history; about half arise in the upper outer quadrant.","DCIS is confined by basement membrane and shows calcifications and comedo necrosis; invasive ductal carcinoma is a hard stellate desmoplastic mass; lobular carcinoma loses E-cadherin and invades in single file, often bilaterally.","Paget disease is intraepidermal spread from an underlying carcinoma; inflammatory carcinoma is tumor in dermal lymphatics causing peau d'orange; male carcinoma links to BRCA2 and Klinefelter syndrome, unlike hormone-driven gynecomastia."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Breast: inflammatory disorders, benign epithelial lesions, stromal tumors, carcinoma, and the male breast"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, breasts: suspensory ligaments, quadrants, and lymphatic drainage"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Female Reproductive System, mammary glands"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones; Pregnancy and Lactation"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, breast carcinoma"}]},"RE-13-F":{"unit":"RE-13-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-06","title":"Infectious Diseases and Hyperplasia of the Prostate","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the two common benign disorders of the prostate: benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) and prostatitis. It explains why BPH depends on dihydrotestosterone, why it grows in the zone that wraps the urethra, and how outlet obstruction works its way back through the bladder, ureters, and kidneys. It then sorts prostatitis into acute bacterial, chronic bacterial, and chronic nonbacterial forms by organism, route, and time course. Board questions typically give an older man with urinary symptoms or a febrile man with a tender prostate and ask for the zone, the hormone, the organism, the complication, or the category.","prerequisites":["Zonal anatomy of the prostate and the course of the prostatic urethra","Androgen synthesis and action, including 5-alpha-reductase and the androgen receptor (RE-1 to RE-4)","Micturition reflex and autonomic control of the detrusor and bladder neck","Glomerular filtration and the Starling forces across the glomerulus","Gram-negative rods, Neisseria, and Chlamydia: structure and virulence factors"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal physiology and acute kidney injury","how":"Back pressure from outlet obstruction raises Bowman's space pressure, lowers GFR, and produces postrenal kidney injury."},{"to":"Urinary tract infection and E. coli virulence","how":"The same uropathogenic gram-negative rods that cause cystitis reach the prostate by reflux of infected urine into the prostatic ducts."},{"to":"Sexually transmitted infections (RE-13-B)","how":"N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis cause urethritis, epididymitis, and prostatitis in younger sexually active men."},{"to":"Prostate carcinoma","how":"Zone of origin, histology, and examination findings separate carcinoma from BPH, and both can be present in the same gland."},{"to":"Adaptive cellular responses (general pathology)","how":"BPH is hormone-driven hyperplasia, and detrusor thickening is hypertrophy in response to pressure overload."}],"terms":[{"term":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)","def":"Androgen-dependent nodular proliferation of prostatic glands and fibromuscular stroma, arising in the transition zone of older men."},{"term":"Transition zone","def":"The small region of prostate surrounding the proximal prostatic urethra, where BPH begins."},{"term":"Peripheral zone","def":"The largest prostatic zone, lying posteriorly against the rectum, where most carcinomas arise."},{"term":"Dihydrotestosterone (DHT)","def":"The potent androgen formed from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase, binding the androgen receptor more tightly than testosterone."},{"term":"5-alpha-reductase type 2","def":"The isoenzyme in prostatic stromal and basal cells that converts testosterone to DHT."},{"term":"Lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS)","def":"Voiding symptoms (hesitancy, weak stream) and storage symptoms (frequency, urgency, nocturia) arising from the bladder, outlet, or prostate."},{"term":"Trabeculation","def":"Coarse ridges of hypertrophied detrusor muscle seen on the inner bladder wall in chronic outlet obstruction."},{"term":"Postvoid residual","def":"The volume of urine left in the bladder immediately after voiding, which increases as the bladder fails to overcome obstruction."},{"term":"Hydronephrosis","def":"Dilation of the renal pelvis and calyces from obstructed urine outflow, with progressive atrophy of the renal parenchyma."},{"term":"Acute bacterial prostatitis","def":"Acute suppurative infection of the prostate, usually by gram-negative rods, with fever and a tender, swollen gland."},{"term":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis","def":"Persistent prostatic infection that reseeds the bladder, causing recurrent urinary infections with the same organism."},{"term":"Chronic pelvic pain syndrome","def":"Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis: at least 3 months of pelvic pain with no bacteria found in urine or prostatic secretions."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-06-C01","obj":"RE-13-F.1","title":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia: DHT-driven nodular growth in the transition zone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The prostate is divided into zones with different diseases. The peripheral zone, posterior and next to the rectum, holds most of the glandular tissue. The central zone surrounds the ejaculatory ducts. The transition zone is a small region on either side of the proximal prostatic urethra, together with the periurethral glands. BPH begins in the transition zone and periurethral tissue, which is why it narrows the urethra early, while carcinoma arises mostly in the peripheral zone, where it is palpable on rectal examination but slow to cause urinary symptoms."},{"type":"p","text":"BPH is hyperplasia: an increase in the number of cells, both epithelial and stromal, not an increase in cell size and not a neoplasm. It is extremely common, beginning in some men in their 40s and present histologically in most men by their 80s. Two conditions are required: age and functioning testes producing androgen. Men castrated before puberty and men with inherited 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency do not develop BPH."},{"type":"flow","title":"How androgen drives BPH","steps":["Testes secrete testosterone into the blood","Stromal 5-alpha-reductase type 2 converts testosterone to DHT","DHT binds the androgen receptor in stromal and epithelial nuclei","Stromal cells release growth factors (FGF family members)","Epithelial and stromal proliferation rises; apoptosis falls","Nodules form in the transition zone and compress the urethra"],"caption":"DHT acts mainly through stromal cells, which signal to neighboring epithelium in a paracrine fashion. The net gain in cells comes from both more proliferation and less programmed cell death."},{"type":"p","text":"DHT matters more than testosterone because it binds the androgen receptor with higher affinity and dissociates more slowly, giving a stronger, longer signal. Circulating testosterone falls modestly with age while BPH rises, so other factors contribute; a rising estrogen to testosterone ratio is thought to sensitize prostatic cells to DHT, but this role is less certain."},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly, the gland is firm to rubbery with well-defined nodules, some containing cysts of milky fluid. Microscopically, nodules of proliferating glands with papillary infoldings sit in hyperplastic fibromuscular stroma, and the glands keep two cell layers: inner columnar secretory cells and an outer flattened basal cell layer. Corpora amylacea (laminated concretions of secretion) are common. On rectal examination the gland is symmetrically enlarged, smooth, and rubbery, and the median sulcus may be lost."},{"type":"table","title":"BPH compared with prostate carcinoma","cols":["Feature","BPH","Prostate carcinoma"],"rows":[["Process","Hyperplasia (more normal cells)","Malignant neoplasm"],["Usual zone","Transition zone and periurethral glands","Peripheral zone (most cases)"],["Glands","Two cell layers; basal layer present","Single layer; basal layer absent"],["Rectal examination","Smooth, symmetric, rubbery enlargement","Hard, irregular nodule"],["Early urinary symptoms","Common, because the urethra is compressed","Uncommon, because growth is away from the urethra"],["PSA","Normal or mildly raised (more tissue)","Often raised; can be normal early"]],"caption":"Both diseases are androgen dependent and common in older men, so they often coexist, but one does not turn into the other."},{"type":"p","text":"BPH is not a precursor of carcinoma. The two arise in different zones from different processes, and hyperplastic nodules do not progress through dysplasia to cancer. PSA, a serine protease made by prostatic epithelium, can rise mildly in BPH simply because there is more epithelium, so a raised PSA does not by itself distinguish the two."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 70-year-old man has a hard, irregular nodule on the posterior surface of his prostate but no urinary symptoms. Is this more likely BPH or carcinoma, and why?","explain":"Carcinoma. The posterior gland felt on rectal examination is the peripheral zone, where most carcinomas arise; growth there is away from the urethra, so symptoms appear late. BPH arises around the urethra and gives smooth, rubbery enlargement with early symptoms.","check":"The zonal anatomy paragraph and the BPH compared with prostate carcinoma table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-06-C02","obj":"RE-13-F.2","title":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia: urinary findings and complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"BPH obstructs the bladder outlet in two ways. The static component is the mass of nodules compressing the prostatic urethra; nodules in the median lobe can also project into the bladder neck and act like a ball valve. The dynamic component is tone in prostatic and bladder neck smooth muscle, which contracts through sympathetic alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Gland size correlates poorly with symptoms, because a small gland with a periurethral nodule or high smooth muscle tone can obstruct more than a large gland that expands outward."},{"type":"p","text":"Obstruction produces two groups of symptoms. Voiding symptoms come straight from resistance: the detrusor needs extra time and pressure to open the outlet (hesitancy), flow through a narrowed urethra is weak and interrupted, and terminal dribbling and a sense of incomplete emptying follow. Storage symptoms come from the bladder's adaptation. A detrusor working against pressure overload hypertrophies like the left ventricle in aortic stenosis, and the thickened, partly denervated muscle becomes overactive, contracting at small volumes. Combined with residual urine that reduces the space left to fill, this causes frequency, urgency, and nocturia."},{"type":"flow","title":"From prostatic nodule to kidney injury","steps":["Transition zone nodules and smooth muscle tone narrow the urethra","Detrusor generates higher pressure to void","Detrusor hypertrophies: trabeculation, cellules, diverticula","Detrusor fails to empty fully: postvoid residual rises","Stasis favors infection and bladder stones","Sustained high bladder pressure dilates both ureters","Bilateral hydronephrosis; GFR falls (postrenal kidney injury)"],"caption":"Compensation (hypertrophy) comes first and decompensation (residual urine, retention) follows; upper tract damage is a late complication of long-standing obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure also pushes mucosa outward between the hypertrophied muscle bundles, forming small pits (cellules) and, when larger, diverticula. These are pseudodiverticula, lined by mucosa without a full muscle wall, so they cannot empty and become further sites of stasis. Residual urine is an ideal culture medium, so urinary tract infection and bladder calculi follow; the calculi form because solutes precipitate in stagnant urine."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute urinary retention is a sudden inability to void with a painful, palpable bladder. It occurs when an additional load tips a compensated bladder over: a drug with anticholinergic action weakens detrusor contraction, an alpha-adrenergic agonist such as a decongestant tightens the outlet, and alcohol, immobility, or infection can contribute. Chronic retention is painless; the overdistended bladder leaks small volumes, called overflow incontinence."},{"type":"p","text":"Upper tract damage requires obstruction severe and long enough to keep bladder pressure high. Pressure transmits up both ureters, so BPH causes bilateral hydroureter and hydronephrosis, unlike a stone, which is usually unilateral. Raised tubular and Bowman's space hydrostatic pressure opposes filtration, so GFR falls and blood urea nitrogen and creatinine rise. Prolonged dilation compresses the parenchyma and causes tubular atrophy."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: tracing a rising creatinine to the prostate","text":"A 76-year-old man has had years of weak stream and nocturia. He now has a creatinine of 2.4 mg/dL (reference about 0.7 to 1.3), a palpable bladder, and a postvoid residual of 600 mL. Ultrasound shows bilateral hydronephrosis. Work backward: two dilated kidneys with a full, palpable bladder place the block at the bladder outlet, not in the ureters. A large residual means the detrusor has decompensated. Pressure from the full bladder has risen into both collecting systems, raising Bowman's space pressure, which lowers net filtration pressure and GFR. This is postrenal kidney injury from BPH, and it is potentially reversible if the obstruction is relieved before tubular atrophy becomes permanent."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does BPH cause bilateral hydronephrosis, while an obstructing ureteral stone usually causes hydronephrosis on one side only?","explain":"BPH blocks the single outlet below the bladder, so raised bladder pressure reaches both ureters. A stone blocks one ureter above the bladder, so only that kidney is affected.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on upper tract damage."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-06-C03","obj":"RE-13-F.3","title":"Acute bacterial prostatitis: organisms, routes, and findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute bacterial prostatitis is an acute suppurative infection of the gland. The prostatic ducts open into the prostatic urethra, so the commonest route is intraprostatic reflux of infected urine during voiding. Organisms can also ascend from the urethra or be introduced directly by catheterization, cystoscopy, transurethral surgery, or needle biopsy; blood-borne spread is rare. Because infected urine is the usual source, the organisms are the uropathogens that cause cystitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of acute bacterial prostatitis by setting","cols":["Setting","Usual organisms","Microbiology to know"],"rows":[["Older men, often with BPH, catheter, or recent procedure","E. coli (most common); other gram-negative rods such as Klebsiella, Proteus, Pseudomonas; less often enterococci","E. coli: gram-negative rod, lactose fermenter, fimbriae for adherence, lipopolysaccharide (endotoxin)"],["Younger sexually active men","Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Chlamydia trachomatis","N. gonorrhoeae: gram-negative diplococcus. C. trachomatis: obligate intracellular, not seen on Gram stain"]],"caption":"The age split follows exposure: older men are infected from their own urinary tract, younger men from sexual contacts."},{"type":"p","text":"Older men are predisposed because BPH leaves residual urine and raises voiding pressure, which favors both bacteriuria and reflux into the ducts, and because they undergo more instrumentation. In younger men the same pathogens that cause urethritis extend into the gland, sometimes with epididymitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of the clinical picture","steps":["Infected urine refluxes into prostatic ducts","Bacteria multiply in acini; neutrophils pour in","Edema and microabscesses swell the gland inside its capsule","Capsule stretch and inflammation cause pain and tenderness","Endotoxin and cytokines (IL-1, TNF, IL-6) cause fever and chills","Periurethral swelling causes dysuria, frequency, and possible retention"],"caption":"Each finding traces to one step: local suppuration, capsular tension, systemic cytokines, or swelling around the urethra."},{"type":"p","text":"The onset is abrupt, with fever above 38 °C (100.4 °F), chills, and malaise, together with dysuria, frequency, urgency, and pain in the perineum, low back, or suprapubic region. Fever and rigors occur because lipopolysaccharide on gram-negative rods activates toll-like receptor 4 on macrophages, which release IL-1, TNF, and IL-6; these raise the hypothalamic set point through prostaglandin E2. The swollen gland can compress the urethra enough to cause acute retention."},{"type":"p","text":"On rectal examination the gland is exquisitely tender, warm, swollen, and boggy, meaning soft and edematous from fluid and pus rather than firm. Vigorous palpation can push bacteria into the bloodstream, which is why examination is gentle and massage is avoided. Urinalysis shows pyuria and bacteriuria, and urine culture usually grows the organism. PSA can rise sharply because inflammation disrupts the barrier between acini and capillaries, and it falls as inflammation resolves. Microscopically, there are dense neutrophils in acini and stroma, edema, hyperemia, and in severe cases necrosis and abscesses. Complications include prostatic abscess, bacteremia and sepsis, epididymitis, and progression to chronic bacterial prostatitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-06-C04","obj":"RE-13-F.4","title":"Acute, chronic bacterial, and chronic nonbacterial prostatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Prostatitis is sorted by time course and by whether bacteria can be found. Symptoms lasting 3 months or more are called chronic. The National Institutes of Health classification is widely used: category I is acute bacterial, category II is chronic bacterial, category III is chronic nonbacterial prostatitis (chronic pelvic pain syndrome), and category IV is asymptomatic inflammation found incidentally on biopsy or in semen."},{"type":"table","title":"Three symptomatic forms of prostatitis","cols":["Feature","Acute bacterial","Chronic bacterial","Chronic nonbacterial (pelvic pain syndrome)"],"rows":[["Frequency","Uncommon","Uncommon","Most common (the large majority)"],["Organisms","E. coli and other gram-negative rods; gonococcus or chlamydia in young men","Same gram-negative rods, persisting in the gland","None identified on culture"],["Key history","Abrupt fever and chills","Recurrent UTIs with the same organism","3 months or more of pelvic, perineal, or ejaculatory pain"],["Systemic signs","Present","Usually absent between episodes","Absent"],["Prostate on examination","Tender, warm, boggy","Often normal or mildly tender","Normal or mildly tender"],["Prostatic secretions","Not obtained (massage avoided)","Leukocytes and bacteria","Leukocytes in some cases; no bacteria"]],"caption":"Fever separates the acute form; recurrence with one organism defines chronic bacterial; negative cultures with chronic pain define chronic nonbacterial."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic bacterial prostatitis usually affects older men and may follow an acute episode, although many men never had one. Bacteria persist in the ducts and acini, often in biofilms on small prostatic calculi, where neutrophils cannot clear them and many antimicrobial drugs penetrate poorly across the prostatic epithelium. From this reservoir the gland reseeds the bladder from time to time, so the defining pattern is recurrent urinary tract infection with the same organism. Between episodes the infection is low grade and confined to the gland, so symptoms are limited to low back or perineal discomfort and dysuria, without fever."},{"type":"p","text":"Localization studies show where the bacteria live. In the four-glass (Meares-Stamey) test, the first-voided urine samples the urethra, the midstream urine samples the bladder, and expressed prostatic secretions and the urine voided after prostatic massage sample the prostate. In chronic bacterial prostatitis the prostatic samples contain far more bacteria (classically at least tenfold more) than the urethral and bladder samples. A simplified two-glass version compares urine before and after massage."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: one organism, three infections","text":"A 62-year-old man has had three urinary tract infections in a year, each growing the same E. coli strain, with no fever between episodes and a mildly tender prostate. Recurrent infection with the identical organism means it was never eliminated, and a man without structural kidney or bladder disease most often harbors it in the prostate. Afebrile intervals exclude acute prostatitis. Positive cultures exclude chronic nonbacterial prostatitis. A culture of postmassage urine with many more bacteria than midstream urine would confirm a prostatic reservoir: chronic bacterial prostatitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis, or chronic pelvic pain syndrome, is the most common form. It is defined by at least 3 months of pain in the perineum, pelvis, penis, or testes, often with pain on ejaculation and irritative voiding symptoms, while urine and prostatic secretions grow no bacteria. An inflammatory type has leukocytes in prostatic secretions, semen, or postmassage urine; a noninflammatory type does not. The mechanism is uncertain (pelvic floor muscle dysfunction, altered pain processing, chemical irritation from urine reflux, and autoimmunity are proposed)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 40-year-old man has had 6 months of perineal pain and pain after ejaculation. Urine cultures, including postmassage urine, are repeatedly negative, but expressed prostatic secretions contain many leukocytes. Which category is this, and why is it not chronic bacterial prostatitis?","explain":"Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis (chronic pelvic pain syndrome), inflammatory type. Leukocytes show inflammation, but chronic bacterial prostatitis requires bacteria localized to the prostate and recurrent infection with one organism.","check":"The three forms table and the localization paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and endocrinology explain BPH. The transition zone surrounds the urethra, so a modest amount of DHT-driven hyperplasia there obstructs early, while carcinoma in the peripheral zone grows silently toward the rectum. Physiology then predicts every complication: the detrusor hypertrophies against outlet resistance as any muscle does against pressure overload, residual urine follows when it fails, and raised pressure transmitted to both kidneys lowers GFR by opposing filtration. The same obstruction links BPH to infection, because stagnant urine and high voiding pressure favor bacteriuria and reflux into prostatic ducts."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and immunology explain prostatitis. The organism reflects the source (the man's own urinary tract in older men, sexual contacts in younger men), endotoxin-driven cytokines explain fever, and biofilm on prostatic calculi explains why chronic bacterial infection recurs with one organism. The limits are real: chronic pelvic pain syndrome has no confirmed cause, the prostate examination is often normal in chronic disease, and PSA is raised by hyperplasia and inflammation as well as by cancer, so no single finding settles the diagnosis."}],"summary":["BPH is DHT-dependent hyperplasia of both glands and fibromuscular stroma, beginning in the transition (periurethral) zone of older men; it requires androgen and is absent in prepubertal castrates and in 5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency.","Stromal 5-alpha-reductase type 2 converts testosterone to DHT, which binds the androgen receptor more strongly and drives growth factor release, increased proliferation, and reduced apoptosis.","BPH glands keep their basal cell layer and the gland is smooth and rubbery; carcinoma arises mostly in the peripheral zone, loses the basal layer, and feels hard and irregular. BPH is not a precursor of carcinoma.","Outlet obstruction has a static component (nodule mass) and a dynamic component (alpha-1 adrenergic smooth muscle tone); symptoms correlate poorly with gland size.","Voiding symptoms (hesitancy, weak stream, dribbling) come from resistance; storage symptoms (frequency, urgency, nocturia) come from an overactive hypertrophied detrusor and residual urine.","Complications follow in order: detrusor hypertrophy with trabeculation and pseudodiverticula, rising residual urine, infection and stones, acute or chronic retention, and bilateral hydronephrosis with postrenal kidney injury.","Acute bacterial prostatitis comes from reflux of infected urine or instrumentation; E. coli and other gram-negative rods dominate in older men, N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis in younger sexually active men.","Acute prostatitis presents with fever, chills, dysuria, frequency, perineal pain, and an exquisitely tender, warm, boggy prostate; massage is avoided because it can cause bacteremia.","Chronic bacterial prostatitis causes recurrent urinary infections with the same organism from a prostatic reservoir, often biofilm on calculi, shown by localization cultures.","Chronic nonbacterial prostatitis (chronic pelvic pain syndrome) is the most common form: 3 months or more of pelvic pain with no bacteria and no fever."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System, sections on the prostate: prostatitis and benign prostatic hyperplasia"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the male reproductive tract, benign prostatic hyperplasia"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male; Micturition; Glomerular Filtration"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Male Reproductive System, androgen action and 5-alpha-reductase"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods, the Neisseriae, and Chlamydiae"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, the prostate and prostatic urethra"}]},"RE-13-G":{"unit":"RE-13-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07","title":"Conditions of the Ovary","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the common non-neoplastic conditions of the ovary and adnexa: functional cysts, paraovarian cysts, ovarian torsion, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), and tubo-ovarian cysts and abscesses. Each is explained from the normal ovarian cycle, adnexal anatomy, or the spread of pelvic infection. Board questions usually give an adnexal mass in a particular setting (pregnancy, a molar gestation, fever after PID, sudden pain during exercise) or a woman with irregular cycles and hirsutism, and ask for the mechanism or the expected complication. If you know which hormone drives each cyst, why veins close before arteries in torsion, and how LH and insulin together produce androgen excess, most of these questions follow.","prerequisites":["The ovarian cycle: follicular phase, ovulation, corpus luteum, and luteolysis (RE-7)","The two-cell, two-gonadotropin model of ovarian steroid synthesis","Anatomy of the broad ligament, mesovarium, mesosalpinx, and the ovarian and uterine arteries","Embryology of the mesonephric (Wolffian) and paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts","Insulin signaling and the concept of insulin resistance"],"connections":[{"to":"Gestational trophoblastic disease","how":"The very high hCG of a hydatidiform mole or choriocarcinoma is what produces bilateral theca lutein cysts."},{"to":"Endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma","how":"Chronic anovulation in PCOS leaves the endometrium under estrogen without progesterone, the main driver of endometrioid carcinoma."},{"to":"Endocrinology and metabolism","how":"Insulin resistance links PCOS to type 2 diabetes, dyslipidemia, fatty liver, and acanthosis nigricans."},{"to":"Sexually transmitted infections and PID","how":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis start the ascending infection that ends in a tubo-ovarian abscess and tubal infertility."},{"to":"Testicular torsion (RE-13-D)","how":"Both torsions obstruct low-pressure veins before arteries, so the gonad swells and then undergoes hemorrhagic infarction."}],"terms":[{"term":"Functional cyst","def":"An ovarian cyst formed by an exaggeration of the normal cycle (follicle or corpus luteum) rather than by neoplasia, which usually regresses on its own."},{"term":"Follicular cyst","def":"A fluid-filled cyst formed when a maturing follicle fails to rupture at ovulation and continues to enlarge."},{"term":"Corpus luteum cyst","def":"A cyst formed when the corpus luteum fails to regress and fills with fluid or blood after ovulation."},{"term":"Theca lutein cysts","def":"Multiple, usually bilateral ovarian cysts with luteinized theca cells, caused by very high hCG stimulating the LH receptor."},{"term":"Adnexa","def":"The structures beside the uterus: ovaries, uterine tubes, and the ligaments and vessels that support them."},{"term":"Infundibulopelvic ligament","def":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary, a peritoneal fold from the pelvic sidewall that carries the ovarian artery, vein, lymphatics, and nerves."},{"term":"Mesosalpinx","def":"The part of the broad ligament between the uterine tube and the mesovarium, where paraovarian cysts and embryonic duct remnants lie."},{"term":"Hyperandrogenism","def":"Excess androgen action, shown clinically by hirsutism, acne, or scalp hair loss, or biochemically by raised total or free testosterone."},{"term":"Sex hormone binding globulin (SHBG)","def":"A liver-made plasma protein that binds testosterone and estradiol with high affinity, so its level sets the free, active hormone fraction."},{"term":"Unopposed estrogen","def":"Estrogen acting on the endometrium without the cyclic progesterone that normally converts it to secretory endometrium and leads to orderly shedding."},{"term":"Tubo-ovarian abscess","def":"A walled-off collection of pus involving an inflamed uterine tube adherent to the ovary, usually a complication of pelvic inflammatory disease."},{"term":"Hydrosalpinx","def":"A uterine tube distended with clear fluid after its fimbrial end has been sealed by scarring from earlier infection."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07-C01","obj":"RE-13-G.1","title":"Functional cysts: follicular, corpus luteum, and theca lutein","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Functional cysts are the commonest adnexal masses in women of reproductive age. They are not tumors but exaggerations of normal ovarian events, so each type is best recognized by the hormone that drives it and the phase of the cycle in which it forms. A normal dominant follicle reaches about 2 to 2.5 cm before ovulation; a fluid-filled structure larger than about 3 cm is usually called a cyst. Functional cysts are lined by granulosa or luteinized cells, are usually simple and unilocular on ultrasound, and most resolve within one to three cycles."},{"type":"h","text":"Follicular cyst"},{"type":"p","text":"When the midcycle LH surge fails to trigger rupture, the follicle keeps accumulating fluid. Its granulosa cells may continue to aromatize theca androgen into estradiol, so a follicular cyst can produce estrogen. Persistent estrogen without a following luteal phase can cause irregular bleeding and, if prolonged, endometrial hyperplasia. In girls, an autonomously functioning follicular cyst causes gonadotropin-independent (peripheral) precocious puberty, the mechanism seen in McCune-Albright syndrome."},{"type":"h","text":"Corpus luteum cyst"},{"type":"p","text":"After ovulation, capillaries grow into the former follicle to supply the corpus luteum, which makes progesterone. This vascular, thin-walled structure bleeds easily, and if it fails to regress it becomes a cyst filled with fluid or blood (hemorrhagic corpus luteum cyst). Continued progesterone secretion can delay menses. Rupture, often after intercourse or exercise, releases blood into the peritoneum, causing sudden unilateral pain and hemoperitoneum that mimics a ruptured ectopic pregnancy; anticoagulation raises the risk. A corpus luteum cyst is also normal in early pregnancy, where it is maintained by hCG."},{"type":"h","text":"Theca lutein cysts"},{"type":"p","text":"hCG and LH share an alpha subunit and have closely related beta subunits, so hCG activates the LH receptor on theca cells. When hCG is far above normal, theca cells in many follicles luteinize and the follicles fill with fluid, giving multiple, usually bilateral cysts that can enlarge both ovaries considerably. The causes are complete hydatidiform mole, choriocarcinoma, multiple gestation, and ovarian hyperstimulation with exogenous gonadotropins. Because theca cells make androgen, these cysts occasionally cause maternal virilization. They regress as hCG falls, for example after a mole is evacuated."},{"type":"table","title":"Functional cysts compared","cols":["Feature","Follicular cyst","Corpus luteum cyst","Theca lutein cysts"],"rows":[["Origin","Follicle that did not rupture","Corpus luteum that did not regress","Many follicles luteinized by hCG"],["Phase or setting","Follicular phase, anovulatory cycle","Luteal phase, early pregnancy","Molar or multiple pregnancy, gonadotropin therapy"],["Hormone produced","Estrogen (sometimes)","Progesterone","Androgen (sometimes)"],["Laterality","Usually unilateral, single","Usually unilateral, single","Usually bilateral, multiple"],["Classic complication","Irregular bleeding; torsion","Hemorrhage, rupture, hemoperitoneum","Torsion of the enlarged ovaries"]],"caption":"Each cyst matches the hormone environment in which it forms. Bilateral, multiple cysts point to an hCG source."},{"type":"example","title":"Bilateral cysts in a large-for-dates pregnancy","text":"A woman at 12 weeks has vaginal bleeding, a uterus large for dates, severe vomiting, and bilateral multiloculated adnexal masses. Start with the cysts: bilateral and multiple means hCG-driven, so the question becomes why hCG is so high. Hyperemesis fits very high hCG, and a uterus large for dates with bleeding fits a complete hydatidiform mole (multiple gestation is the other common cause, and ultrasound separates them). The cysts are theca lutein cysts, not tumors, and should regress as hCG falls after the mole is removed. A persistently rising hCG would suggest persistent trophoblastic disease rather than an ovarian problem."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07-C02","obj":"RE-13-G.2","title":"Ovarian torsion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ovary is suspended at two points. Laterally, the infundibulopelvic (suspensory) ligament runs from the pelvic sidewall and carries the ovarian artery, vein, lymphatics, and nerves. Medially, the ovarian ligament (utero-ovarian ligament) joins the ovary to the uterus, and the ovarian branch of the uterine artery runs with it to anastomose with the ovarian artery. Ovarian torsion is rotation of the ovary, usually with the tube (adnexal torsion), around the axis formed by these two attachments, which twists the vascular pedicle."},{"type":"p","text":"A normal adult ovary is small and rarely twists. Torsion usually needs a lever: a cyst or tumor, often about 5 cm or larger, makes the ovary heavy and mobile. Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is a classic lead point, and functional cysts, ovaries enlarged by hyperstimulation, and pregnancy also predispose. Malignant tumors twist less often, probably because invasion and adhesions fix them in place. In prepubertal girls a normal ovary can twist because its ligaments are relatively long. Torsion is more often right-sided, a pattern usually attributed to the sigmoid colon occupying and stabilizing the left pelvis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ovarian torsion: from twist to infarction","steps":["Enlarged ovary or mass acts as a lever","Adnexa rotate around the vascular pedicle","Low-pressure veins and lymphatics compress first","Arterial inflow continues; ovary swells with edema and congestion","Rising tissue pressure finally blocks arterial inflow","Hemorrhagic infarction and necrosis"],"caption":"Outflow fails before inflow, so the ovary enlarges and becomes congested before it infarcts."},{"type":"p","text":"The sequence follows from vessel wall structure. Veins and lymphatics have thin walls and low pressure, so a partial twist collapses them while the thick-walled, high-pressure artery stays open. Blood keeps entering but cannot leave, so the ovary becomes edematous, congested, and enlarged, which further tightens the twist. When interstitial pressure exceeds arterial pressure, inflow stops and hemorrhagic infarction follows."},{"type":"p","text":"The ischemic, stretched capsule produces sudden, severe, unilateral pelvic or lower abdominal pain, often during exertion, with nausea and vomiting from visceral afferent stimulation. The twist may be intermittent, giving episodes of pain that resolve and recur. Low-grade fever and leukocytosis suggest necrosis. Ultrasound shows an enlarged, edematous ovary, often with follicles pushed to the periphery and a twisted pedicle (whirlpool sign). Doppler may show absent flow, but normal flow does not exclude torsion: the dual arterial supply and partial or intermittent twisting can preserve some flow early."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a twisted ovary become larger before it becomes infarcted, and why can Doppler still show arterial flow?","explain":"Thin-walled, low-pressure veins collapse first while the artery keeps delivering blood, so the ovary swells with trapped blood and edema. Arterial flow persists until tissue pressure exceeds arterial pressure, and the ovary also receives blood from the uterine artery through the ovarian ligament.","check":"The opening paragraph on the ligaments, the torsion flow, and the paragraph on vessel wall structure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07-C03","obj":"RE-13-G.3","title":"Paraovarian cysts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Paraovarian (paratubal) cysts lie in the broad ligament, usually in the mesosalpinx between the uterine tube and the ovary, and are separate from the ovary itself. They are thin-walled, unilocular, and filled with clear fluid. They are common, are found at any age, and most are small, benign, and asymptomatic."},{"type":"p","text":"Their origin is embryologic. In the female embryo the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts form the tubes, uterus, and upper vagina, while the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts regress because there is no testosterone to maintain them. Regression is incomplete, and small remnants persist in the broad ligament. Cysts can arise from these mesonephric remnants, from paramesonephric remnants, or from inclusions of peritoneal mesothelium."},{"type":"table","title":"Embryonic remnants near the ovary","cols":["Remnant","Duct of origin","Location"],"rows":[["Epoophoron","Mesonephric (Wolffian) tubules and duct","Mesosalpinx, between ovary and tube"],["Paroophoron","Mesonephric tubules","Broad ligament, nearer the uterus"],["Gartner duct cyst","Mesonephric duct","Lateral wall of the vagina"]],"caption":"Mesonephric remnants persist in the broad ligament and along the lateral vagina, which is why cysts can form beside, rather than within, the ovary."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the cyst arises outside the ovary, imaging shows a normal ovary, with its own follicles, beside the cyst, and the cyst does not change with the menstrual cycle the way a functional cyst does. This is the key distinction from an ovarian cyst. A paraovarian cyst also lacks the hormone-producing cells of a functional cyst, so it causes no endocrine effects."},{"type":"p","text":"Complications come from size and mobility. A large cyst can act as a lever for torsion of the cyst itself, the tube, or the whole adnexa, with the same venous-then-arterial sequence as ovarian torsion and the same sudden pain. Hemorrhage into the cyst or rupture causes acute pain. Large cysts can also press on the bladder or bowel. Neoplasms arising in paraovarian cysts are rare."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07-C04","obj":"RE-13-G.4","title":"PCOS pathogenesis: gonadotropins, insulin, and follicle arrest","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is the most common endocrine disorder of women of reproductive age, affecting roughly 6 to 13% depending on the criteria used. It is a syndrome of chronic anovulation and androgen excess, not a disease of cysts: the cysts are arrested follicles. Its pathogenesis is easiest to follow through the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model."},{"type":"table","title":"The two-cell, two-gonadotropin model and what goes wrong in PCOS","cols":["Cell","Normal control and product","Change in PCOS"],"rows":[["Theca cell","LH (with insulin as cofactor) drives cholesterol to androgens, mainly androstenedione, via CYP17","High LH and high insulin raise CYP17 activity and androgen output"],["Granulosa cell","FSH induces aromatase (CYP19), converting theca androgen to estradiol","Relatively low FSH and high AMH limit aromatase, so androgen accumulates"],["Liver","Makes SHBG, which binds testosterone","Insulin suppresses SHBG synthesis, raising free testosterone"],["Adipose tissue","Aromatizes androgen to estrone","More substrate and more fat give steady estrone"]],"caption":"Androgen production outpaces conversion to estradiol, and low SHBG increases the free fraction that reaches tissues."},{"type":"p","text":"The neuroendocrine defect is a rapid GnRH pulse frequency. Fast pulses favor transcription of the LH beta subunit, while slow pulses favor FSH, so LH rises and FSH stays normal or low; the LH to FSH ratio is often above 2 to 1, although it is not a diagnostic criterion. Pulses stay fast partly because the hypothalamus is less sensitive to progesterone negative feedback and partly because anovulation means little progesterone is made."},{"type":"p","text":"Insulin resistance is present in a large proportion of women with PCOS, including lean women, and obesity worsens it. The resistance is selective: glucose uptake in muscle and fat is impaired, but the ovary and liver still respond to the compensatory hyperinsulinemia. Insulin acts with LH on theca cells to increase androgen synthesis and lowers hepatic SHBG, so free testosterone rises even when total testosterone is only mildly raised."},{"type":"flow","title":"PCOS: from gonadotropin imbalance to anovulation","steps":["Rapid GnRH pulses raise LH relative to FSH","LH and insulin drive theca androgen overproduction","Low FSH limits granulosa aromatase","Insulin lowers SHBG; free testosterone rises","Intraovarian androgen arrests small antral follicles","No dominant follicle, no ovulation, no corpus luteum","No progesterone; estrogen acts unopposed"],"caption":"Two inputs, high LH and high insulin, converge on the theca cell; the androgen-rich follicle then fails to mature."},{"type":"p","text":"Within the ovary, high androgen and high anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) from the many small follicles reduce FSH responsiveness, so no follicle is selected as dominant. Follicles stall at about 2 to 9 mm and line up beneath a thickened, fibrotic cortex, giving the peripheral string of pearls on ultrasound, with hyperplastic theca and stroma. Without ovulation no corpus luteum forms, so progesterone stays low and GnRH pulses stay fast, which closes a self-sustaining loop."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The self-sustaining loop of PCOS","steps":["Fast GnRH pulses raise LH","LH and insulin raise ovarian androgen","Androgen arrests follicles; no ovulation","No corpus luteum, so little progesterone","Weak progesterone feedback keeps GnRH pulses fast"],"caption":"Anovulation removes the progesterone signal that would normally slow GnRH pulses, so the disorder perpetuates itself."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is free testosterone often more abnormal than total testosterone in PCOS?","explain":"Hyperinsulinemia suppresses hepatic SHBG. With less binding protein, a larger fraction of testosterone circulates free and active, so androgen effects appear even when total testosterone is near normal.","check":"The liver row of the two-cell table and the paragraph on insulin resistance."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07-C05","obj":"RE-13-G.5","title":"PCOS: findings and complications explained by mechanism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every finding in PCOS traces back to one of three disturbances: androgen excess, anovulation with unopposed estrogen, or insulin resistance. The commonly used Rotterdam criteria require two of three features (oligo-ovulation or anovulation, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound) after other causes are excluded. Because polycystic-appearing ovaries are common in healthy young women, ultrasound alone never establishes PCOS."},{"type":"table","title":"PCOS findings and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea","Follicle arrest prevents ovulation; bleeding is irregular breakthrough of proliferative endometrium"],["Infertility","Anovulation; the most common cause of anovulatory infertility"],["Hirsutism","Androgen, converted to DHT by 5-alpha reductase in the hair follicle, turns vellus into terminal hair in androgen-dependent areas"],["Acne and male-pattern scalp hair thinning","Androgen enlarges sebaceous glands and raises sebum; DHT shortens the growth phase of scalp follicles"],["Obesity, often central","Common but not universal; visceral fat worsens insulin resistance and adds estrone"],["Acanthosis nigricans","Very high insulin stimulates IGF-1 receptors on keratinocytes and fibroblasts, giving velvety dark skin in folds"],["Type 2 diabetes and impaired glucose tolerance","Insulin resistance until beta cells can no longer compensate"],["Dyslipidemia","Insulin resistance increases lipolysis and fatty acid flow to the liver: high triglycerides and VLDL, low HDL"],["Endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma","Chronic estrogen without progesterone drives unchecked proliferation (endometrioid type)"]],"caption":"Androgen excess explains the skin findings, anovulation explains the cycle and endometrial findings, and insulin resistance explains the metabolic findings."},{"type":"p","text":"The endometrial risk deserves special attention. In a normal cycle, progesterone from the corpus luteum stops estrogen-driven proliferation, converts the endometrium to a secretory pattern, and its withdrawal causes orderly shedding. In PCOS, estrone from adipose aromatization and estradiol from arrested follicles provide steady, moderate estrogen with no luteal progesterone. The endometrium keeps proliferating without regular shedding, progressing to hyperplasia and, in some women, endometrioid adenocarcinoma at a younger age than usual."},{"type":"p","text":"Other associations follow the same mechanisms: nonalcoholic fatty liver disease and obstructive sleep apnea through insulin resistance and obesity, and gestational diabetes when pregnancy adds its own insulin resistance. Laboratory findings typically include raised free testosterone, low SHBG, raised LH with normal or low FSH, and high AMH; estradiol is usually in the normal early follicular range rather than high."},{"type":"p","text":"PCOS is a diagnosis of exclusion because other disorders share its features. Nonclassic 21-hydroxylase deficiency raises 17-hydroxyprogesterone, hyperprolactinemia and thyroid disease disturb ovulation, and Cushing syndrome adds insulin resistance and hirsutism. An androgen-secreting ovarian or adrenal tumor is suggested by rapid onset, very high testosterone or DHEAS, and frank virilization (clitoromegaly, deepened voice), which PCOS rarely causes."},{"type":"example","title":"Linking a PCOS presentation to its mechanisms","text":"A 24-year-old has 5 periods a year, hirsutism, acne, BMI 33, and dark velvety skin on her neck. Free testosterone is high, total testosterone mildly raised, SHBG low, and LH to FSH about 3 to 1. The cycle history shows anovulation. The low SHBG and dark neck skin show hyperinsulinemia, which both suppresses SHBG and stimulates IGF-1 receptors in skin. The high LH explains theca androgen excess, and the free fraction is high because SHBG is low. Because she rarely ovulates, her endometrium is under estrogen without progesterone, so she carries a long-term risk of endometrial hyperplasia, and her insulin resistance puts her at risk of type 2 diabetes and dyslipidemia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-07-C06","obj":"RE-13-G.6","title":"Tubo-ovarian cysts and abscesses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is ascending infection of the upper genital tract: endometritis, salpingitis, oophoritis, and pelvic peritonitis. It usually begins with Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis infecting the endocervix. Cervical mucus and the closed cervix normally keep vaginal bacteria out; once the endocervical barrier is damaged, and especially around menses when the cervix opens and mucus thins, organisms ascend to the endometrium and tubes. The infection then becomes polymicrobial, joined by vaginal anaerobes (Bacteroides, Peptostreptococcus), gram-negative rods such as Escherichia coli, and organisms associated with bacterial vaginosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cervical infection to tubo-ovarian abscess","steps":["Gonococcal or chlamydial endocervicitis","Ascent to endometrium and tubes (salpingitis)","Mixed flora, including anaerobes, join the infection","Inflamed tube and fimbriae adhere to the ovary","Pus collects in a walled-off tubo-ovarian abscess","Rupture spills pus into the peritoneum"],"caption":"Ascending infection becomes polymicrobial and is walled off against the ovary; rupture converts a localized abscess into generalized peritonitis."},{"type":"p","text":"In the tube, gonococci damage ciliated cells (partly through lipooligosaccharide and peptidoglycan fragments that induce TNF), and chlamydial infection provokes a chronic immune response with marked scarring, often with few acute symptoms. Purulent exudate from the fimbriae bathes the ovary, and fibrin binds the inflamed tube and ovary into one mass, the tubo-ovarian complex. When pus collects in a walled-off cavity within this mass, a tubo-ovarian abscess forms; anaerobes thrive in its low-oxygen interior. Women with an intrauterine device have a classic association with Actinomyces israelii abscesses."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow the inflammation: lower abdominal pain, fever above 38.3 °C (101 °F), cervical motion and adnexal tenderness from inflamed peritoneum, purulent cervical discharge, a tender adnexal mass, leukocytosis, and raised C-reactive protein and ESR. Spread along the paracolic gutter to the liver capsule causes perihepatitis with right upper quadrant pain (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome). Rupture of the abscess spills pus into the peritoneal cavity, producing generalized peritonitis, a rigid abdomen, and septic shock."},{"type":"table","title":"Late sequelae of PID and their mechanisms","cols":["Sequela","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Tubal infertility","Scarring blocks the lumen or destroys fimbriae, so the ovum cannot be captured or transported"],["Ectopic pregnancy","Partial damage to cilia and mucosal folds slows transport, so the embryo implants in the tube"],["Hydrosalpinx","Fimbrial end seals; tubal secretions accumulate as clear fluid"],["Tubo-ovarian cyst","After infection resolves, the adherent tube and ovary leave a sterile fluid-filled cavity"],["Chronic pelvic pain","Adhesions bind pelvic organs and stretch on movement"]],"caption":"Healing by fibrosis, not active infection, explains most long-term consequences."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinction between a tubo-ovarian cyst and abscess is clinical and microbiological. An abscess is an active infection with fever and leukocytosis; a tubo-ovarian cyst is the sterile, fluid-filled remnant of an earlier one. Both differ from a hemorrhagic corpus luteum cyst, which gives sudden pain without fever, and from torsion, which gives abrupt pain in a previously well woman."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman treated for PID five years ago now has a positive pregnancy test and unilateral pelvic pain at 7 weeks. Why is her risk of ectopic pregnancy raised?","explain":"PID scars the tubal mucosa and destroys cilia. A partly damaged tube still lets sperm reach the ovum but transports the larger embryo too slowly, so it implants in the tube.","check":"The table of late sequelae of PID."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and anatomy explain most ovarian conditions. Functional cysts are the follicle and corpus luteum of the normal cycle carried too far, and theca lutein cysts follow from hCG acting on the LH receptor, linking the ovary to gestational trophoblastic disease. Adnexal anatomy explains torsion, since the ovary hangs on a vascular pedicle and an enlarged ovary twists it, and embryology explains paraovarian cysts as embryonic duct remnants beside a normal ovary. The same venous-before-arterial obstruction that infarcts a twisted ovary also infarcts a twisted testis."},{"type":"p","text":"PCOS joins reproductive endocrinology to biochemistry and metabolism. The two-cell model, GnRH pulse frequency, SHBG binding, and peripheral aromatization explain its androgen excess and anovulation, while selective insulin resistance explains acanthosis nigricans, diabetes, and dyslipidemia, and unopposed estrogen explains endometrial carcinoma. Microbiology and pathology complete the chapter: gonorrhea and chlamydia start PID, mixed anaerobes form the abscess, and fibrosis produces infertility and ectopic pregnancy. These conditions overlap in practice, since any adnexal mass can twist and several cause sudden pelvic pain, so the setting (pregnancy, fever, metabolic features) is often what separates them."}],"summary":["Follicular cysts are unruptured follicles that may secrete estrogen; corpus luteum cysts may make progesterone and can bleed and rupture into the peritoneum, mimicking ectopic pregnancy.","Bilateral, multiple theca lutein cysts mean very high hCG (mole, choriocarcinoma, multiple gestation, hyperstimulation) acting on theca LH receptors, and they regress as hCG falls.","Ovarian torsion needs a lever, usually a mass about 5 cm or larger such as a mature cystic teratoma; veins close before arteries, so the ovary swells, then undergoes hemorrhagic infarction.","Normal Doppler flow does not exclude torsion because the ovary has a dual supply and twisting may be partial or intermittent.","Paraovarian cysts arise from mesonephric, paramesonephric, or mesothelial remnants in the broad ligament; a separate normal ovary distinguishes them, and torsion or rupture are the complications.","In PCOS, fast GnRH pulses raise LH, LH and insulin drive theca androgen, low FSH limits aromatase, and insulin lowers SHBG, raising free testosterone.","Androgen and AMH arrest small follicles, so no ovulation, no corpus luteum, and no progesterone; the lack of progesterone keeps GnRH pulses fast.","PCOS findings map to androgen excess (hirsutism, acne), anovulation (oligomenorrhea, infertility, endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma), and insulin resistance (acanthosis nigricans, diabetes, dyslipidemia).","Tubo-ovarian abscess is a polymicrobial complication of PID in which the inflamed tube adheres to the ovary; rupture causes peritonitis and sepsis, and scarring causes infertility and ectopic pregnancy."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, sections on the fallopian tubes and the ovaries"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Female Reproductive System"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the female reproductive tract"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, ovaries, uterine tubes, and broad ligament"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, genital ducts in the female"}]},"RE-13-H":{"unit":"RE-13-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08","title":"Diseases of the Placenta","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the diseases that arise from the placenta and its trophoblast: hydatidiform moles, invasive mole, and gestational choriocarcinoma; preeclampsia and its complications; abruption, previa, and accreta; and disorders of amniotic fluid volume. Each is explained from normal placental biology, including genomic imprinting, hCG signaling, trophoblast invasion of the spiral arteries, the decidua basalis, and the balance between fetal urine and fetal swallowing. Board questions turn on a few mechanistic distinctions: paternal versus maternal genomes, villi versus no villi, painful versus painless bleeding, and too little versus too much fluid. Once the mechanism is clear, each finding can be predicted.","prerequisites":["Fertilization, implantation, and formation of the placenta and fetal membranes (RE-1, RE-6)","Cytotrophoblast, syncytiotrophoblast, and the structure of chorionic villi","Structure and actions of hCG and the glycoprotein hormone family (shared alpha subunit)","Endothelial control of vascular tone: nitric oxide, prostacyclin, VEGF","Fetal kidney and gut development (UR-1, GI-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Thyroid disorders (EN)","how":"Very high hCG from a molar pregnancy weakly activates the TSH receptor and can cause hyperthyroidism with suppressed TSH."},{"to":"Hemostasis and DIC (HE)","how":"Abruption, HELLP syndrome, and severe preeclampsia release tissue factor or injure endothelium, consuming platelets and clotting factors."},{"to":"Glomerular disease (UR)","how":"Loss of VEGF signaling in preeclampsia produces glomerular endotheliosis, which explains its proteinuria."},{"to":"Congenital renal and gut anomalies (UR-1, GI-1)","how":"Bilateral renal agenesis and esophageal or duodenal atresia announce themselves before birth as abnormal amniotic fluid volume."},{"to":"Neoplasia and genomic imprinting (general pathology)","how":"Moles show how the parental origin of a genome, not only its content, controls growth."}],"terms":[{"term":"Gestational trophoblastic disease","def":"The group of disorders of abnormal trophoblast proliferation that includes hydatidiform moles, invasive mole, choriocarcinoma, and related tumors."},{"term":"Hydatidiform mole","def":"An abnormal conceptus with swollen (hydropic) chorionic villi and trophoblast proliferation caused by an excess of paternal genome."},{"term":"Androgenetic","def":"Containing chromosomes derived only from the father, as in a complete mole."},{"term":"Genomic imprinting","def":"Parent-of-origin silencing of certain genes, so that only the maternal or only the paternal copy is expressed."},{"term":"p57 (CDKN1C)","def":"A cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor encoded by a paternally imprinted, maternally expressed gene, used as an immunostain to detect maternal DNA in villous tissue."},{"term":"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia","def":"Trophoblastic disease that persists or invades after pregnancy, including invasive mole and choriocarcinoma, recognized clinically by persistent hCG."},{"term":"Spiral artery remodeling","def":"Replacement of the muscle and endothelium of maternal spiral arteries by invading trophoblast, converting them into wide, low-resistance vessels."},{"term":"sFlt-1","def":"Soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1, a circulating fragment of VEGF receptor 1 that binds and neutralizes VEGF and placental growth factor (PlGF)."},{"term":"HELLP syndrome","def":"Hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets, a microangiopathic form of severe preeclampsia."},{"term":"Decidua basalis","def":"The endometrium beneath the implantation site that limits trophoblast invasion and provides the plane along which the placenta separates after birth."},{"term":"Placenta accreta spectrum","def":"Abnormally adherent placenta in which villi attach to (accreta), invade (increta), or penetrate through (percreta) the myometrium."},{"term":"Potter sequence","def":"The chain of fetal deformities and pulmonary hypoplasia that follows severe, prolonged oligohydramnios from any cause."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C01","obj":"RE-13-H.1","title":"Complete versus partial hydatidiform mole","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal development needs one maternal and one paternal genome because of genomic imprinting. Paternally expressed genes generally drive growth of the trophoblast and placenta, while maternally expressed genes restrain trophoblast growth and support the embryo. A hydatidiform mole results when the paternal genome is in excess. The trophoblast proliferates, and the villous stroma fills with fluid largely because a fetal circulation to drain it is absent or inadequate, so the villi swell into grape-like vesicles."},{"type":"p","text":"A complete mole forms when an ovum that has lost its nucleus (an empty ovum) is fertilized. In about 90% of cases one sperm enters and its haploid set duplicates, giving a 46,XX karyotype in which every chromosome is paternal. In the rest, two sperm fertilize the empty ovum, giving 46,XX or 46,XY. A 46,YY conceptus does not survive, so complete moles are never YY. Because there is no maternal genome, no embryo forms, every villus swells, and trophoblast proliferates around the whole villous circumference."},{"type":"p","text":"A partial mole forms when a normal ovum is fertilized by two sperm (or by a diploid sperm), giving a triploid karyotype with one maternal and two paternal sets, most often 69,XXY and less often 69,XXX. The maternal set allows an embryo or fetus to begin developing, so fetal parts, fetal vessels, or nucleated fetal red cells are present, but the fetus is abnormal and does not survive. Only some villi swell, and trophoblast proliferation is focal and mild. Triploidy with two maternal sets and one paternal set does not produce a mole, which confirms that the paternal excess, not triploidy itself, causes molar change."},{"type":"p","text":"The p57 immunostain turns this genetic difference into a slide finding. CDKN1C, the gene for p57, is expressed only from the maternal allele. Villous cytotrophoblast and stromal cells of a complete mole contain no maternal DNA and are therefore p57 negative, while those of a partial mole or a hydropic miscarriage are positive."},{"type":"table","title":"Complete and partial hydatidiform mole","cols":["Feature","Complete mole","Partial mole"],"rows":[["Origin","Empty ovum plus one duplicated sperm (most) or two sperm","Normal ovum plus two sperm"],["Karyotype","46,XX (sometimes 46,XY)","Triploid, usually 69,XXY or 69,XXX"],["Parental genomes","All paternal (androgenetic)","One maternal, two paternal"],["Fetal parts","Absent","Present (abnormal fetus)"],["Villous swelling","Diffuse","Focal, with irregular scalloped villi"],["Trophoblast proliferation","Diffuse and circumferential","Focal and mild"],["p57 stain","Negative","Positive"],["hCG","Very high","Raised, usually less than with complete mole"],["Persistent disease after evacuation","About 15 to 20%","About 1 to 5%"],["Choriocarcinoma risk","About 2 to 3%","Rare"]],"caption":"The parental origin of the genome explains every row: a complete paternal excess gives a larger, more active trophoblast and no embryo."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a karyotype and a stain","text":"Tissue from a failed pregnancy at 11 weeks shows some swollen villi and a few nucleated red cells in villous vessels. Is it a complete mole? Fetal red cells mean a fetal circulation formed, which requires maternal genes, so a complete mole is unlikely. The karyotype is 69,XXY and p57 is positive. Positive p57 proves a maternal genome is present, and triploidy with molar villi means two paternal sets. The diagnosis is partial mole. Had the karyotype been 46,XX with negative p57 and diffuse swelling, the tissue would be a complete mole, even though 46,XX also describes a normal female conceptus; the stain, not the karyotype, shows the parental origin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C02","obj":"RE-13-H.2","title":"Clinical findings of complete hydatidiform mole","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Almost every finding of a complete mole follows from two facts: the uterus is filled with a large mass of swollen villi, and that mass is covered by proliferating syncytiotrophoblast that secretes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). The classic patient presents with vaginal bleeding in the first half of pregnancy, often late in the first trimester or early in the second. The bleeding comes from separation of molar tissue from the decidua, and over time it can cause anemia. The uterus is often larger than expected for the gestational age because of the volume of hydropic villi, and no fetal heart activity is found. Ultrasound shows a heterogeneous intrauterine mass with many small cystic spaces, described as a snowstorm pattern, and no fetus."},{"type":"p","text":"hCG is often above 100,000 mIU/mL. That value overlaps the normal peak at about 8 to 10 weeks, so a single level is suggestive rather than diagnostic; a level far above that expected for the gestational age, or one that keeps rising after the normal peak, is the useful signal. hCG is a glycoprotein hormone whose alpha subunit is shared with LH, FSH, and TSH, and whose beta subunit closely resembles that of LH, so at very high concentration it acts on related receptors."},{"type":"flow","title":"How excess hCG produces the systemic findings","steps":["Diffuse trophoblast proliferation secretes very large amounts of hCG","hCG binds LH/hCG receptors on ovarian theca cells","Bilateral, multilocular theca lutein cysts enlarge the ovaries","hCG weakly activates the structurally related TSH receptor","Thyroid hormone rises and TSH is suppressed (hyperthyroidism)","High hCG with estrogen is associated with hyperemesis gravidarum"],"caption":"Theca lutein cysts and hyperthyroidism regress as hCG falls after the mole is removed. The link between hCG and vomiting is an association; its exact mechanism is not settled."},{"type":"p","text":"Preeclampsia that appears before 20 weeks of gestation strongly suggests a molar pregnancy. Ordinary preeclampsia waits until the second half of pregnancy, but the abnormal, oversized trophoblast mass of a mole releases antiangiogenic factors early, producing maternal endothelial dysfunction ahead of schedule through the mechanism explained in the section on the pathogenesis of preeclampsia."},{"type":"p","text":"The final consequence is malignant potential. Diffuse, unrestrained trophoblast proliferation is the substrate for invasive mole and choriocarcinoma, so after evacuation serial hCG measurements must fall to undetectable levels. A plateau or rise means trophoblast persists. Early ultrasound now detects many complete moles before this classic picture develops."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a partial mole be expected to cause theca lutein cysts and hyperthyroidism as often as a complete mole?","explain":"No. A partial mole has focal, mild trophoblast proliferation and lower hCG, so receptor cross-stimulation is uncommon, and the uterus is more often normal or small for dates.","check":"Compare the hCG and trophoblast rows of the complete versus partial mole table with the flow in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C03","obj":"RE-13-H.3","title":"Invasive mole","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal trophoblast invades the decidua and the inner myometrium during implantation, but that invasion is controlled. An invasive mole is a hydatidiform mole, almost always complete, whose hydropic villi and trophoblast penetrate deep into the myometrium and its blood vessels. It is classified as gestational trophoblastic neoplasia because it persists and invades, yet it is not a carcinoma: it keeps the villous architecture of a mole. That single feature, villi present, separates it histologically from choriocarcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"The consequences follow from where the tissue sits. Suction evacuation empties the uterine cavity but cannot reach villi buried in the wall, so trophoblast remains and continues to secrete hCG. Persistent or rising hCG after evacuation is therefore the usual way an invasive mole is recognized, and tissue diagnosis is uncommon because it requires a sample of the myometrium. Villi eroding through the wall can perforate the uterus and cause intraperitoneal hemorrhage, and those eroding vessels cause continued vaginal bleeding."},{"type":"p","text":"Because villi enter venous channels, they can be carried to the lungs or vagina. These deported villi are not true metastases in the sense of a carcinoma, and they may regress, but they keep producing hCG. Invasive mole follows about 10 to 15% of complete moles and is locally destructive rather than widely metastatic."},{"type":"example","title":"hCG after evacuation of a complete mole","text":"A complete mole is evacuated and hCG is measured weekly: 150,000, then 40,000, 9,000, 8,800, 9,100, and 9,000 mIU/mL. What does the pattern mean? A cleared uterus should give a steady fall to undetectable levels, because hCG has a half-life of roughly a day and a half. A fall that stops at a plateau means living trophoblast remains that suction could not reach. With no new pregnancy, this defines persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia. The most likely lesion after a complete mole is an invasive mole buried in the myometrium; choriocarcinoma is the less common alternative, and only histology (villi present or absent) separates them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C04","obj":"RE-13-H.4","title":"Gestational choriocarcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gestational choriocarcinoma is a malignant tumor of trophoblast. It is composed of sheets of malignant cytotrophoblast mixed with syncytiotrophoblast (a biphasic pattern) and, unlike a mole, contains no chorionic villi. Normal trophoblast is built to invade maternal vessels, and the malignant cells keep that behavior. The tumor therefore grows into and around blood vessels, outgrows its own blood supply, and forms a soft, hemorrhagic, necrotic mass. A biopsy may contain mostly blood and dead tissue with viable tumor only at the edge."},{"type":"p","text":"Choriocarcinoma can follow any pregnancy event. About half arise after a complete hydatidiform mole, about a quarter after a spontaneous or induced abortion, and most of the rest after an apparently normal pregnancy, with a few after ectopic pregnancy. It may present weeks to years later with abnormal uterine bleeding, or first with a metastasis. Syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG in proportion to tumor mass, so hCG is very high and serves as a sensitive tumor marker."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the tumor lives within vessels, it spreads early through the blood rather than through lymphatics. The lungs are the most common site, producing multiple nodules on chest imaging with cough, hemoptysis, or dyspnea. The vagina is next, where metastases appear as dark, hemorrhagic nodules. Brain and liver metastases signal advanced disease and bleed readily, so a young woman with an intracerebral hemorrhage after a recent pregnancy should raise the question. Very high hCG can also cause theca lutein cysts or hyperthyroidism, as it does in a complete mole."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the trophoblastic lesions","cols":["Lesion","Villi","Behavior","Typical hCG","Key clue"],"rows":[["Partial mole","Present, focal swelling","Rarely persists","Raised","Fetal parts, p57 positive"],["Complete mole","Present, diffuse swelling","Precursor to neoplasia","Very high","No fetus, p57 negative"],["Invasive mole","Present, in myometrium","Locally invasive, deported villi","Persistent after evacuation","Perforation, hCG plateau"],["Choriocarcinoma","Absent","Malignant, early blood-borne spread","Very high","Hemorrhagic metastases to lung, vagina, brain"]],"caption":"Presence or absence of villi is the histologic dividing line between molar disease and choriocarcinoma; hCG tracks trophoblast mass in all four."},{"type":"p","text":"Gestational choriocarcinoma must be distinguished from nongestational choriocarcinoma, a germ cell tumor of the ovary or testis that arises from the patient's own cells. The gestational tumor carries paternal DNA from the pregnancy that produced it, while the nongestational tumor does not. The distinction matters because the gestational form has a much better prognosis despite its early spread."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C05","obj":"RE-13-H.5","title":"Pathogenesis of preeclampsia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Maternal blood reaches the placenta through spiral arteries, the terminal branches of the uterine arterial tree (uterine, arcuate, radial, then spiral). In normal pregnancy, extravillous cytotrophoblast migrates into these arteries, replaces their endothelium and smooth muscle, and leaves wide, flaccid channels that no longer constrict. This remodeling, which extends from the decidua into the inner myometrium during the first half of pregnancy, turns a high-resistance, pulsatile supply into a low-resistance, steady flow into the intervillous space."},{"type":"p","text":"In preeclampsia, invasion is shallow. The myometrial segments of the spiral arteries keep their muscle and stay narrow and reactive. The causes are incompletely understood, but they include abnormal maternal immune tolerance of trophoblast (interactions between uterine natural killer cells and fetal HLA molecules) and the inability of trophoblast to adopt the endothelial-like adhesion molecules needed to line vessels. The narrow arteries also develop acute atherosis, fibrinoid necrosis of the wall with lipid-laden macrophages, which narrows them further. The result is placental hypoperfusion, intermittent hypoxia and reoxygenation, and oxidative stress, seen in the placenta as infarcts and small villi."},{"type":"p","text":"This first, placental stage produces the second, maternal stage. The stressed syncytiotrophoblast releases large amounts of sFlt-1, which binds VEGF and placental growth factor (PlGF), and soluble endoglin, which binds TGF-beta. Maternal endothelial cells depend on VEGF and TGF-beta signaling to make nitric oxide and prostacyclin and to maintain the fenestrated endothelium of the glomeruli and hepatic sinusoids. Deprived of these signals, the endothelium shifts toward vasoconstriction and coagulation, with more thromboxane A2 and endothelin-1, increased sensitivity to angiotensin II, and leaky capillaries. In the kidney, glomerular endothelial cells swell and occlude the capillary lumens (glomerular endotheliosis), which reduces filtration and causes proteinuria."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two-stage model of preeclampsia","steps":["Shallow trophoblast invasion; spiral arteries stay narrow and muscular","Placental hypoperfusion, hypoxia and reoxygenation, oxidative stress","Placenta releases sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin into maternal blood","Free VEGF, PlGF, and TGF-beta signaling fall","Less nitric oxide and prostacyclin; more thromboxane and endothelin","Vasoconstriction, capillary leak, platelet activation, glomerular endotheliosis","Hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ injury"],"caption":"Stage 1 (first two steps) occurs in the placenta early in pregnancy and is clinically silent; stage 2 is the maternal syndrome after 20 weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"The model explains why the disease resolves after delivery of the placenta, why it is more frequent with a large placental mass (multiple gestation, molar pregnancy), and why maternal vascular disease (chronic hypertension, diabetes) adds risk. A high sFlt-1 to PlGF ratio in maternal blood reflects this antiangiogenic state and can precede clinical disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a woman with normal placentation were given a drug that blocks VEGF signaling, which features of preeclampsia would you expect?","explain":"Hypertension and proteinuria with glomerular endothelial injury, because the maternal syndrome is driven by loss of VEGF signaling in endothelium; the placental stage is not required for these effects. VEGF inhibitors used in cancer therapy do cause this picture.","check":"Compare with the third paragraph and the last four steps of the flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C06","obj":"RE-13-H.6","title":"Preeclampsia: definition, risk factors, and complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Preeclampsia is new-onset hypertension, a blood pressure of 140/90 mm Hg or higher on two readings at least 4 hours apart, arising after 20 weeks of gestation in a woman previously normotensive, together with proteinuria or evidence of end-organ damage. Proteinuria is 300 mg or more in 24 hours or a urine protein to creatinine ratio of 0.3 mg/mg or more. Proteinuria is no longer required when end-organ injury is present, because the disease is systemic endothelial dysfunction and the kidney is only one of its targets. The 20-week threshold separates it from chronic hypertension, and preeclampsia earlier than that suggests a molar pregnancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy and end-organ criteria","cols":["Category or criterion","Definition","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Chronic hypertension","Hypertension before pregnancy or before 20 weeks","Preexisting maternal vascular disease"],["Gestational hypertension","New hypertension after 20 weeks without proteinuria or end-organ damage","Milder or earlier stage of the same process in some women"],["Preeclampsia","New hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria or end-organ damage","Antiangiogenic endothelial dysfunction"],["Thrombocytopenia","Platelets below 100,000/µL","Platelet activation and consumption on injured endothelium"],["Renal injury","Creatinine above 1.1 mg/dL or doubled","Glomerular endotheliosis reduces filtration"],["Liver injury","Transaminases at least twice normal, right upper quadrant or epigastric pain","Periportal hemorrhage and necrosis stretch the liver capsule"],["Pulmonary edema","Dyspnea, crackles, hypoxemia","Capillary leak and low plasma oncotic pressure"],["Cerebral or visual symptoms","New headache, scotomata, blurred vision","Cerebral vasospasm and vasogenic edema"]],"caption":"Severe features include blood pressure of 160/110 mm Hg or higher or any of the listed end-organ findings. Thresholds follow current US obstetric consensus."},{"type":"p","text":"The strongest risk factor is preeclampsia in a previous pregnancy. Others either enlarge the placental mass or impair the maternal vessels that must support it: nulliparity, multiple gestation, molar pregnancy, chronic hypertension, pregestational diabetes, chronic kidney disease, systemic lupus erythematosus or antiphospholipid syndrome, obesity, and maternal age over about 35 years. Nulliparity is thought to matter because maternal immune tolerance of paternal antigens is not yet established."},{"type":"p","text":"Each complication is endothelial injury in a particular organ. Eclampsia is new generalized tonic-clonic seizures in a woman with preeclampsia that have no other cause. Sudden hypertension overwhelms cerebral autoregulation, and endothelial leak produces vasogenic edema, mainly in the parieto-occipital white matter (posterior reversible encephalopathy), which explains the headache and visual symptoms that often come first. HELLP syndrome is the microangiopathic form: red cells are sheared on fibrin strands in damaged small vessels (schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin), hepatic sinusoidal injury and periportal necrosis raise transaminases and can cause subcapsular hematoma or rupture, and platelets are consumed. HELLP may occur with only mild hypertension. Severe disease can progress to DIC. The same narrowed spiral arteries that start the disease limit placental flow, causing fetal growth restriction, and arterial disease in the decidua predisposes to placental abruption. Stroke, mainly intracerebral hemorrhage, is a leading cause of maternal death in preeclampsia."},{"type":"flow","title":"From endothelial injury to HELLP syndrome","steps":["Injured endothelium of small vessels in liver and elsewhere","Platelets adhere and fibrin is deposited in small vessels","Red cells shear: schistocytes, high LDH, low haptoglobin","Hepatic sinusoidal obstruction and periportal hemorrhagic necrosis","Transaminases rise; capsule stretch causes epigastric pain","Platelet consumption: count falls below 100,000/µL"],"caption":"HELLP is a thrombotic microangiopathy of pregnancy; the letters name the three laboratory consequences of one process."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C07","obj":"RE-13-H.7","title":"Placental abruption versus placenta previa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bleeding in the second half of pregnancy is classified by where the placenta sits and whether it is still attached. Placental abruption is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta from the uterine wall before delivery of the fetus. It begins with rupture of maternal vessels in the decidua basalis, and blood collecting between the placenta and the uterus (a retroplacental hematoma) dissects the placenta further away. Anything that damages decidual arteries or shears the placenta from the wall predisposes to it: hypertension and preeclampsia, cocaine use (intense vasoconstriction and surges in blood pressure), smoking, abdominal trauma, sudden uterine decompression after membrane rupture with polyhydramnios, and a prior abruption."},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding of abruption has a mechanism. Blood dissecting into the myometrium irritates it, causing pain, a firm or rigid tender uterus, and frequent contractions; severe extravasation into the muscle produces a purple, boggy Couvelaire uterus. Bleeding may be visible, or concealed behind the placenta, so vaginal blood loss can underestimate maternal hemorrhage. The separated area no longer exchanges gas, causing fetal distress or death. Damaged decidua and placenta are rich in tissue factor, which enters the maternal circulation and triggers disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), consuming fibrinogen and platelets."},{"type":"p","text":"Placenta previa is implantation of the placenta over or near the internal cervical os in the lower uterine segment. Risk factors are those that scar the upper uterus or make it less receptive: prior cesarean delivery or other uterine surgery, multiparity, multiple gestation, advanced maternal age, smoking, and a previous previa. In the third trimester the lower uterine segment forms and thins and the cervix begins to efface. The placenta cannot stretch with it, so its attachment shears and maternal vessels bleed. The blood escapes directly through the os rather than dissecting into muscle, so bleeding is painless and bright red, and the uterus is soft and nontender. The first episode is often small and stops on its own, with later bleeds heavier."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of bleeding in the second half of pregnancy","cols":["Condition","Mechanism","Pain and uterus","Distinguishing clue"],"rows":[["Placental abruption","Decidual hemorrhage separates a normally sited placenta","Painful; firm, tender, contracting uterus","Hypertension, cocaine, trauma; fetal distress; DIC"],["Placenta previa","Placenta over the os shears as the lower segment thins","Painless; soft, nontender uterus","Bright red bleeding; prior cesarean or multiparity"],["Vasa previa","Unprotected fetal vessels cross the membranes over the os and tear","Painless; soft uterus","Bleeding at membrane rupture with rapid fetal bradycardia; the blood is fetal"],["Uterine rupture","Tear through a scarred myometrium during labor","Sudden severe pain; contractions stop","Prior uterine scar; fetal parts palpable outside the uterus"]],"caption":"Pain means blood is dissecting into or tearing the myometrium; painless bleeding means blood is escaping through the cervix without muscle injury."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman at 33 weeks with preeclampsia develops abdominal pain, a rigid uterus, and only a small amount of vaginal bleeding, yet her blood pressure falls and she oozes from her IV site. Why?","explain":"This is abruption with a concealed retroplacental hematoma, so true blood loss is much greater than what appears vaginally, and tissue factor release has started DIC, consuming clotting factors and platelets.","check":"Compare with the second paragraph and the abruption row of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C08","obj":"RE-13-H.8","title":"Placenta accreta spectrum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"At implantation the trophoblast invades the endometrium, which has transformed into decidua under progesterone. The decidua basalis under the placenta does two jobs. It restrains invasion, and it contains a layer of fibrinoid material (the Nitabuch layer) where trophoblast meets decidua. After delivery, the placenta separates through the spongy layer of the decidua basalis, and contraction of the myometrium then compresses the open maternal vessels to stop bleeding."},{"type":"p","text":"In the placenta accreta spectrum, the decidua basalis is absent or deficient, so villi anchor directly onto or into the myometrium. Without decidua there is no cleavage plane, and the placenta does not separate after birth. The problem is a defect in the maternal bed rather than overly aggressive trophoblast, which is why the main risk factors are conditions that scar or thin the endometrium: prior cesarean delivery, myomectomy, repeated curettage, and intrauterine adhesions. Placenta previa adds risk because the lower uterine segment has thin, poorly formed decidua and is where cesarean scars lie. The combination of a previa lying over a prior cesarean scar carries the highest risk, which rises with each additional cesarean."},{"type":"table","title":"Depth of attachment in the accreta spectrum","cols":["Form","Depth","Added consequence"],"rows":[["Accreta","Villi attach to the myometrial surface without intervening decidua","Placenta fails to separate; postpartum hemorrhage"],["Increta","Villi invade into the myometrium","Deeper invasion; heavier bleeding with attempted removal"],["Percreta","Villi penetrate the full wall and serosa, sometimes into the bladder or other organs","Hematuria if the bladder is invaded; intraperitoneal hemorrhage or uterine rupture"]],"caption":"All three share the same cause, a missing decidua basalis; they differ only in how far villi extend."},{"type":"p","text":"Before delivery the disorder is usually silent, except for bleeding from an accompanying previa or hematuria from percreta. The dramatic manifestation comes in the third stage of labor: the placenta, or part of it, cannot be delivered. Attempts at separation tear open maternal vessels that the myometrium cannot compress because placental tissue remains embedded in it, causing massive postpartum hemorrhage, hemorrhagic shock, and consumptive coagulopathy. The rising cesarean rate has made the accreta spectrum much more common over recent decades."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-08-C09","obj":"RE-13-H.9","title":"Disorders of amniotic fluid volume","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Early in pregnancy amniotic fluid is mainly a filtrate of maternal and fetal plasma that crosses the membranes and the still unkeratinized fetal skin. The fetal kidneys begin producing urine at about 10 to 12 weeks, and from about the second trimester onward fetal urine is the main source of fluid, with a smaller contribution from fluid secreted by the fetal lungs. Fluid is removed mainly by fetal swallowing, with absorption in the fetal gut, and partly by absorption across the fetal surface of the placenta into fetal vessels. Volume peaks at about 800 mL in the mid third trimester and falls slightly toward term. Abnormal volume therefore points to a problem with fetal urine production or outflow, fetal swallowing, or the membranes."},{"type":"p","text":"Oligohydramnios is too little fluid, defined on ultrasound as an amniotic fluid index below 5 cm or a deepest vertical pocket below 2 cm. Fetal causes are failure to make urine (bilateral renal agenesis, bilateral cystic renal disease) or failure to pass it (posterior urethral valves in males, urethral atresia). Placental insufficiency also reduces urine output, because the hypoxic fetus redistributes blood to the brain, heart, and adrenals and away from the kidneys. Rupture of the membranes lets fluid leak away. Drugs that lower fetal renal perfusion, such as ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs, act through the same mechanism."},{"type":"flow","title":"Potter sequence","steps":["Fetal kidneys make little or no urine, or urine cannot leave","Severe oligohydramnios from early in the second trimester","Uterine wall compresses the fetus","Flattened face, recessed chin, low-set ears, clubfeet, joint contractures","Chest compression and loss of lung fluid limit lung growth","Pulmonary hypoplasia causes respiratory failure at birth"],"caption":"A sequence is a cascade from one primary defect. Any cause of prolonged early oligohydramnios produces it; pulmonary hypoplasia is the usual cause of death."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal lung growth requires retained lung fluid and room for fetal breathing movements, so the lungs are the organ most damaged by long-standing oligohydramnios. The characteristic face and limb deformities are mechanical, not primary malformations, which is why they occur whatever the original cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Polyhydramnios is excess fluid, an amniotic fluid index of about 24 to 25 cm or more or a deepest pocket of 8 cm or more. The fetus either cannot remove fluid or makes too much. Swallowing or upper gut absorption fails with esophageal atresia (often with a tracheoesophageal fistula), duodenal atresia (associated with Down syndrome), and anencephaly or other neurologic and neuromuscular disorders that impair the swallowing reflex. In maternal diabetes, glucose crosses the placenta, fetal hyperglycemia exceeds the renal threshold, and the resulting osmotic diuresis increases urine output. Many cases are mild and idiopathic. An overdistended uterus predisposes to preterm labor and, after delivery, to poor contraction and postpartum hemorrhage."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of abnormal amniotic fluid volume","cols":["Disorder","Mechanism","Examples"],"rows":[["Oligohydramnios","No fetal urine produced","Bilateral renal agenesis, bilateral cystic kidney disease"],["Oligohydramnios","Urine cannot reach the amniotic cavity","Posterior urethral valves, urethral atresia"],["Oligohydramnios","Reduced renal perfusion","Placental insufficiency, ACE inhibitors, NSAIDs"],["Oligohydramnios","Fluid leaks out","Rupture of membranes"],["Polyhydramnios","Impaired swallowing or upper gut absorption","Esophageal atresia, duodenal atresia, anencephaly"],["Polyhydramnios","Excess fetal urine","Maternal diabetes (osmotic diuresis), recipient twin in twin to twin transfusion"]],"caption":"Amniotic fluid volume is the balance between fetal urine in and fetal swallowing out."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The trophoblast links every disorder in this chapter. Its growth is set by genomic imprinting, so an excess of paternal genome produces moles, and its malignant transformation produces a tumor that keeps the vessel-invading habit of normal trophoblast. Its depth of invasion is regulated from both sides: too shallow an invasion of the spiral arteries begins preeclampsia, while a missing decidua basalis lets villi invade too deeply, as in accreta. Its products act on the mother through ordinary endocrine and vascular physiology, hCG through the LH and TSH receptors and sFlt-1 by removing the VEGF that maternal endothelium needs."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanisms also explain the limits of single findings. hCG is high in molar disease, choriocarcinoma, multiple gestation, and a normal pregnancy near its peak, so its trend and the gestational age matter more than one value. Painful versus painless bleeding is a good guide but not absolute, since a posterior abruption may cause back pain alone and a previa can bleed during labor. Amniotic fluid abnormalities are clues to fetal renal and gut anomalies that the embryology and urinary chapters explain in detail."}],"summary":["A complete mole is androgenetic (usually 46,XX from an empty ovum and a duplicated sperm), has no fetus, diffuse villous swelling, and is p57 negative; a partial mole is triploid with one maternal set, fetal parts, focal swelling, and is p57 positive.","Complete mole findings come from mass and hCG: bleeding, uterus large for dates, snowstorm ultrasound, very high hCG, theca lutein cysts, hyperthyroidism through TSH receptor cross-reaction, hyperemesis, and preeclampsia before 20 weeks.","After evacuation hCG must fall to undetectable; a plateau or rise means persistent trophoblastic neoplasia, most often an invasive mole after a complete mole.","An invasive mole keeps villi but invades the myometrium, so it causes perforation, hemorrhage, and persistent hCG; choriocarcinoma has no villi.","Gestational choriocarcinoma is malignant cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast that follows a mole, abortion, or normal pregnancy, makes very high hCG, and spreads early through blood to lung, vagina, brain, and liver.","Preeclampsia begins with failed spiral artery remodeling and placental ischemia; sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin then neutralize VEGF, PlGF, and TGF-beta, causing systemic maternal endothelial dysfunction.","Preeclampsia is new blood pressure of 140/90 mm Hg or higher after 20 weeks with proteinuria or end-organ damage; complications are eclampsia, HELLP syndrome, abruption, fetal growth restriction, DIC, and stroke.","Abruption is painful bleeding with a tender firm uterus from decidual hemorrhage, linked to hypertension, cocaine, and trauma, and can trigger DIC through tissue factor; previa is painless bright red bleeding from a placenta over the os.","Placenta accreta spectrum results from a deficient decidua basalis, so villi attach to or invade myometrium; previa over a prior cesarean scar is the major risk, and failed separation causes massive postpartum hemorrhage.","Oligohydramnios reflects too little fetal urine or a leak and leads to Potter sequence with pulmonary hypoplasia; polyhydramnios reflects impaired swallowing (esophageal or duodenal atresia, anencephaly) or fetal osmotic diuresis in maternal diabetes."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, sections on gestational and placental disorders, including gestational trophoblastic disease and preeclampsia"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Month to Birth: The Fetus and Placenta, sections on the placenta and amniotic fluid"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Fertilization, Pregnancy, and Lactation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pregnancy and Lactation, sections on the placenta, hormonal factors in pregnancy, and abnormal responses of the mother"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the female reproductive tract, section on preeclampsia"}]},"RE-13-I":{"unit":"RE-13-I","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-09","title":"Benign Conditions of the Vagina and Vulva","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers two groups of benign lower genital tract conditions: the Bartholin duct cyst and abscess, and pelvic organ prolapse of the vaginal walls (cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele). Both are explained from normal anatomy. The Bartholin gland keeps secreting into a blocked duct, so the duct swells. The pelvic organs are held up by a muscular floor and a connective tissue suspension, so damage to either lets an organ push the vaginal wall ahead of it. Board questions turn on location (posterolateral vestibule, anterior or posterior wall, upper or lower anterior wall) and on the symptom each location produces.","prerequisites":["Anatomy of the vulva, vestibule, and perineal pouches (RE-4)","Pelvic diaphragm: levator ani parts and their innervation","Development of the urogenital sinus and its glandular derivatives (RE-1)","Effects of estrogen on vaginal epithelium and connective tissue (RE-7)","Normal micturition and continence mechanisms (UR)"],"connections":[{"to":"Sexually transmitted infections (RE, MI)","how":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis can infect a Bartholin duct, although most abscesses are polymicrobial."},{"to":"Urinary tract physiology (UR)","how":"Stress incontinence from a hypermobile urethra and residual urine from a cystocele follow from how abdominal pressure is shared between bladder and urethra."},{"to":"Connective tissue disorders (MS)","how":"Abnormal collagen (Ehlers-Danlos) or fibrillin (Marfan) weakens the pelvic suspension and raises prolapse risk."},{"to":"Defecation and anorectal anatomy (GI)","how":"A rectocele creates a pocket in front of the rectum that traps stool during straining, causing obstructed defecation."},{"to":"Menopause (RE)","how":"Loss of estrogen thins the vaginal epithelium and reduces collagen in the supporting tissues."}],"terms":[{"term":"Bartholin gland","def":"Greater vestibular gland, a paired mucus-secreting gland in the superficial perineal pouch whose duct opens into the posterolateral vestibule."},{"term":"Vestibule","def":"The space between the labia minora into which the urethra, vagina, and vestibular gland ducts open."},{"term":"Bartholin duct cyst","def":"Dilation of a blocked Bartholin duct by retained mucus."},{"term":"Bartholin abscess","def":"A collection of pus that forms when a blocked duct or gland becomes infected."},{"term":"Pelvic organ prolapse","def":"Descent of a pelvic organ, with the vaginal wall or apex ahead of it, because its support has failed."},{"term":"Levator ani","def":"The muscular pelvic floor (pubococcygeus, puborectalis, iliococcygeus) that keeps the urogenital hiatus closed."},{"term":"Urogenital hiatus","def":"The midline gap in the levator ani through which the urethra and vagina pass."},{"term":"Endopelvic fascia","def":"The fibromuscular connective tissue that suspends the pelvic organs and attaches the vagina to the pelvic side walls."},{"term":"Cystocele","def":"Herniation of the bladder into the upper anterior vaginal wall."},{"term":"Urethrocele","def":"Herniation of the urethra into the distal anterior vaginal wall."},{"term":"Rectocele","def":"Herniation of the rectum into the posterior vaginal wall through a weak rectovaginal septum."},{"term":"Stress urinary incontinence","def":"Involuntary leakage of urine when abdominal pressure rises suddenly, as with coughing, laughing, or lifting."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-09-C01","obj":"RE-13-I.1","title":"Bartholin duct cyst and abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Bartholin glands, or greater vestibular glands, are paired pea-sized glands lying in the superficial perineal pouch at the posterior ends of the bulbs of the vestibule. Each drains through a duct about 2 cm long that opens into the vestibule lateral to the hymen, at about the 4 and 8 o'clock positions of the vaginal opening. The glands secrete mucus that lubricates the vestibule, especially during sexual arousal. They develop from the urogenital sinus, as do their male homologs, the bulbourethral (Cowper) glands."},{"type":"p","text":"A Bartholin duct cyst forms when the duct opening is blocked, most often by inflammation, edema, trauma, or thick mucus. The gland keeps secreting, and because the fluid cannot escape, the duct dilates behind the obstruction. The result is a usually unilateral, round, often painless mass in the posterolateral vestibule, commonly 1 to 3 cm across. Small cysts may cause no symptoms, while larger ones cause pressure, discomfort with sitting or walking, and dyspareunia."},{"type":"flow","title":"From blocked duct to abscess","steps":["Duct opening blocked by inflammation, edema, or mucus","Gland continues to secrete mucus","Duct dilates into a cyst in the posterolateral vestibule","Retained mucus becomes infected","Neutrophils and pus raise pressure in a closed space","Hot, tender, fluctuant abscess"],"caption":"A cyst is retained secretion; an abscess is the same closed space after infection."},{"type":"p","text":"An abscess forms when the retained secretion becomes infected, either in a pre-existing cyst or in the gland itself. Most abscesses are polymicrobial, with skin, vaginal, and bowel flora such as Escherichia coli and anaerobes; Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis are found less often. Pus accumulates under pressure in a closed space, so the swelling becomes red, hot, fluctuant, and very tender, and it develops quickly over 2 to 4 days. Pain is often severe enough to prevent sitting or walking, and surrounding cellulitis or fever may accompany it."},{"type":"p","text":"Age is an important clue. These lesions occur mainly in reproductive-age women, when the glands are active. The glands shrink after menopause, so a new Bartholin mass in an older woman is less likely to be a simple cyst, and Bartholin gland carcinoma, though rare, must be excluded by biopsy. Location also separates the Bartholin cyst from look-alikes: a Skene (paraurethral) duct cyst sits beside the urethral opening, and an epidermal inclusion cyst usually lies in the labia."},{"type":"table","title":"Bartholin cyst and Bartholin abscess","cols":["Feature","Cyst","Abscess"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Duct blockage with retained mucus","Infection of the retained secretion or gland"],["Onset","Slow, often found incidentally","Rapid, over a few days"],["Pain","None or mild pressure","Severe; sitting and walking are painful"],["Examination","Soft, nontender, cystic mass","Red, hot, tender, fluctuant mass, sometimes cellulitis"],["Organisms","None required","Usually polymicrobial; occasionally gonococcal or chlamydial"]],"caption":"Both sit in the posterolateral vestibule; infection and pressure account for every difference."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a Bartholin cyst be expected to arise in a 70-year-old woman as often as in a 25-year-old?","explain":"No. The gland atrophies after menopause and secretes little, so obstruction rarely produces a cyst; a new mass at that age raises concern for carcinoma.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on age and the differential."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-09-C02","obj":"RE-13-I.2","title":"Why the pelvic floor fails","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pelvic organs are supported by two systems that work together. The levator ani muscles form a muscular floor with constant resting tone that keeps the urogenital hiatus nearly closed and holds the pelvic organs on a shelf. Above this floor, the endopelvic fascia and its thickenings suspend the organs: the cardinal and uterosacral ligaments hold the cervix and upper vagina, the pubocervical fascia supports the anterior vaginal wall and bladder, and the rectovaginal septum supports the posterior wall. Distally the vagina fuses with the perineal body and perineal membrane."},{"type":"table","title":"Levels of vaginal support","cols":["Level","Structures","Failure produces"],"rows":[["I, apical suspension","Cardinal and uterosacral ligaments","Uterine or vaginal vault prolapse, enterocele"],["II, lateral attachment","Pubocervical fascia and rectovaginal septum attached to the pelvic side walls","Cystocele (anterior) and rectocele (posterior)"],["III, distal fusion","Perineal body, perineal membrane, levator ani","Urethrocele and urethral hypermobility, low rectocele, perineal descent"]],"caption":"The three-level description of vaginal support (DeLancey) predicts which defect produces which prolapse."},{"type":"p","text":"While the levator ani is intact and the hiatus is closed, the connective tissue carries little load. When the muscle is torn or denervated, the hiatus widens and the organs rest on the fascia and ligaments alone. Connective tissue under constant load slowly stretches and does not recover, so the vaginal walls descend and the organ behind each wall follows it. This explains why prolapse often appears years after the injury that began it."},{"type":"flow","title":"How vaginal childbirth leads to prolapse","steps":["Fetal head distends the levator ani and pelvic floor","Muscle tears or avulses from the pubic bone","Stretch injures pudendal and levator nerves","Weak floor lets the urogenital hiatus widen","Fascia and ligaments bear the load alone","Connective tissue stretches and the vaginal walls descend"],"caption":"The floor fails first; the suspension then fails gradually under constant load."},{"type":"p","text":"Vaginal childbirth is the strongest risk factor, and risk rises with the number of vaginal deliveries, a large baby, and a long second stage of labor. Aging adds loss of muscle mass and weaker collagen. Estrogen loss after menopause matters because the vaginal epithelium, endopelvic fascia, and levator ani contain estrogen receptors, and low estrogen reduces collagen content and tissue strength. Obesity, chronic cough, chronic constipation with straining, and repeated heavy lifting all raise intra-abdominal pressure again and again, pushing the organs against weakened support. Inherited connective tissue disorders (Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan) and prior hysterectomy, which disrupts apical support, add risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms come from the bulge itself and from the organ involved. Women describe pelvic pressure, heaviness, or a sensation of something falling out, and may see or feel a bulge at the vaginal opening. Because gravity and abdominal pressure drive the descent, symptoms worsen with standing, straining, and at the end of the day, and they ease when lying down. Exposed vaginal mucosa can become dry, ulcerated, and bleed, particularly when estrogen is low."},{"type":"example","title":"Weighing the risk factors","text":"A 62-year-old woman with three vaginal deliveries, a body mass index of 34 kg/m2, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease reports a bulge that is worse in the evening. Vaginal delivery was the initiating injury: it tore or denervated the levator ani and widened the hiatus. Menopause then reduced collagen strength in the fascia carrying the load, and obesity and chronic cough supply repeated rises in abdominal pressure that stretch it further. The evening worsening reflects hours upright. A nulliparous woman with the same weight and cough is at much lower risk, because her intact muscular floor still closes the hiatus and protects the fascia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why might a woman with a large pelvic floor tear at delivery have no prolapse until her late fifties?","explain":"The intact endopelvic fascia can carry the load for years; it stretches gradually, and menopause then weakens its collagen.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on load transfer from muscle to connective tissue."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-09-C03","obj":"RE-13-I.3","title":"Cystocele, urethrocele, and rectocele compared","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The site of the support defect determines which organ herniates, and the organ determines the symptoms. The bladder and urethra lie in front of the vagina, so their prolapse bulges the anterior vaginal wall and causes urinary symptoms. The rectum lies behind the vagina, so its prolapse bulges the posterior wall and causes bowel symptoms. Along the anterior wall, the bladder sits higher and the urethra lower, so a cystocele bulges the upper anterior wall and a urethrocele bulges the distal anterior wall, just inside the vaginal opening."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing anterior and posterior wall prolapse","cols":["Feature","Cystocele","Urethrocele","Rectocele"],"rows":[["Vaginal wall","Upper anterior","Distal anterior","Posterior"],["Support defect","Pubocervical fascia","Distal anterior support and urethral attachments","Rectovaginal septum"],["Key symptom","Incomplete emptying, frequency, recurrent urinary tract infection","Stress urinary incontinence","Difficult or incomplete defecation"],["Typical clue","Pressing the bulge up helps the bladder empty","Leakage with cough, sneeze, or laughter","Pressing on the posterior wall or perineum helps evacuation"]],"caption":"Anterior wall prolapse gives urinary symptoms and posterior wall prolapse gives bowel symptoms."},{"type":"p","text":"In a cystocele the bladder base sags below the level of the bladder neck. Urine collects in this dependent pouch and is not expelled during voiding, so the post-void residual volume rises. Retained urine allows bacteria to multiply, which explains recurrent urinary tract infections, and frequency follows from the reduced functional capacity. A large cystocele can kink the urethra and obstruct outflow, causing retention and a weak stream; reducing the bulge by hand straightens the outlet and helps the bladder empty."},{"type":"p","text":"In a urethrocele the urethra has lost its support and moves downward when abdominal pressure rises. Normally the proximal urethra rests on a firm layer of anterior vaginal wall and fascia, so a cough presses the urethra closed against this support at the same moment it presses on the bladder. When that support is lost, the urethra descends instead of being compressed, bladder pressure exceeds urethral closure pressure, and urine leaks. This is stress urinary incontinence: small leaks with coughing, sneezing, laughing, or lifting, without urgency. It differs from urge incontinence, which results from involuntary detrusor contraction and is preceded by a sudden need to void."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stress incontinence from a hypermobile urethra","steps":["Cough raises intra-abdominal pressure","Pressure reaches the bladder fully","Unsupported urethra descends instead of being compressed","Bladder pressure exceeds urethral closure pressure","Small leak of urine without urgency"],"caption":"Leakage reflects lost compression of the urethra, not an overactive bladder."},{"type":"p","text":"In a rectocele the rectovaginal septum is thin or torn, so the anterior rectal wall bulges into the posterior vagina. During straining, stool is pushed forward into this pocket rather than down through the anal canal. Women describe incomplete evacuation, the need to strain, and splinting, meaning pressure with fingers on the posterior vaginal wall or perineum to redirect stool. A high posterior bulge in a woman who has had a hysterectomy may be an enterocele instead, a herniation of the peritoneal cul-de-sac behind the vagina, usually containing small bowel, which reflects apical rather than mid-posterior failure."},{"type":"example","title":"Matching the symptom to the wall","text":"Three women have anterior or posterior wall bulges. The first leaks small amounts of urine when she sneezes and has a bulge just inside the vaginal opening: a urethrocele. The second has recurrent cystitis, incomplete voiding, and a higher anterior bulge she pushes up to void: a cystocele. The third presses on the back wall of the vagina to finish a bowel movement: a rectocele."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can a woman with a large cystocele have no leakage at first, then develop leakage with cough once the bulge is held up?","explain":"The sagging bladder kinks the urethra, which obstructs outflow and masks stress incontinence; reducing the bulge removes the kink and reveals the lost urethral support.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on cystocele."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Both topics in this chapter are anatomy problems before they are disease problems. The Bartholin cyst follows from a gland that secretes into a duct with a single small opening, and its abscess follows from infection in a closed space. Prolapse follows from a two-part support system in which the levator ani protects the connective tissue; once the muscle fails, the fascia stretches, and the position of each fascial defect predicts which organ herniates and whether urinary or bowel symptoms result. Physiology links each symptom to its cause: residual urine and infection in cystocele, lost pressure transmission and compression in urethral hypermobility, and trapped stool in rectocele."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits matter on examinations. Defects are often multiple, so one woman may have a cystourethrocele and a rectocele together. Stress incontinence can also come from intrinsic weakness of the urethral sphincter without hypermobility, and a large cystocele can hide stress incontinence. A vulvar mass that is not in the posterolateral vestibule, or that appears after menopause, should not be assumed to be a Bartholin cyst."}],"summary":["The Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands lie posterolateral to the vaginal opening, drain into the vestibule, develop from the urogenital sinus, and are homologous to the bulbourethral glands.","A Bartholin duct cyst forms because the gland keeps secreting into a blocked duct; it is usually a unilateral, painless mass in the posterolateral vestibule of a reproductive-age woman.","Infection of the retained secretion produces a hot, tender, fluctuant abscess, usually polymicrobial and sometimes gonococcal or chlamydial.","A new Bartholin mass after menopause requires biopsy because the gland has atrophied and carcinoma must be excluded.","Pelvic organ support depends on the levator ani closing the urogenital hiatus and on the endopelvic fascia and ligaments suspending the organs; muscle failure transfers the load to connective tissue, which stretches.","Vaginal childbirth is the main risk factor for prolapse; aging, estrogen loss, obesity, chronic cough, chronic straining, connective tissue disorders, and hysterectomy add to it.","A cystocele bulges the upper anterior wall and causes incomplete emptying, frequency, and recurrent urinary tract infection.","A urethrocele bulges the distal anterior wall; the hypermobile urethra loses compression when abdominal pressure rises, causing stress urinary incontinence.","A rectocele bulges the posterior wall through a weak rectovaginal septum and causes difficult defecation relieved by splinting.","Anterior wall prolapse produces urinary symptoms, and posterior wall prolapse produces bowel symptoms."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, sections on the pelvic floor, female perineum, and vestibular glands"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, section on the vulva"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital System, sections on the urogenital sinus and external genitalia"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the female reproductive tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Micturition, section on control of bladder emptying"}]},"RE-13-J":{"unit":"RE-13-J","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10","title":"Reproductive Dysplasia and Neoplasms","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the precancers and tumors of the cervix, endometrium, myometrium, ovary, vagina, vulva, prostate, penis, and testis. Rather than memorizing each tumor separately, learn the small number of drivers that explain most of them: high-risk HPV acting through the E6 and E7 oncoproteins, unopposed estrogen acting on the endometrium, inherited or acquired loss of tumor suppressors such as TP53, PTEN, and BRCA, and the rule that a tumor makes the products of the cell it imitates. Board cases usually give an age, a risk setting, a histologic clue, or a tumor marker and ask you to name the lesion, its precursor, or its route of spread.","prerequisites":["Cell cycle control by p53 and RB, and how oncogenes and tumor suppressors cause cancer","Histology of the cervix, endometrium, ovary, testis, and prostate","Ovarian and menstrual cycle hormones: estrogen, progesterone, LH, FSH, and aromatase","Embryology of the gonads, Mullerian ducts, and testicular descent","Lymphatic and venous drainage of the pelvis and perineum"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive pathogens and sexually transmitted infections (RE-12, RE-13-K)","how":"HPV types 16 and 18 cause most cervical, vaginal, vulvar, and penile squamous precancers, while types 6 and 11 cause benign condylomas."},{"to":"Endocrinology","how":"Estrogen excess, androgen excess, hCG, and thyroid hormone from tumors explain precocious puberty, virilization, gynecomastia, and hyperthyroidism."},{"to":"General pathology: neoplasia","how":"Dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, invasion, grade, and stage are all illustrated by the cervix, endometrium, and vulva."},{"to":"Reproductive genetics (RE-10)","how":"BRCA1, BRCA2, and Lynch syndrome link ovarian, endometrial, prostate, breast, and colon cancer within families."},{"to":"Renal and musculoskeletal pathology","how":"Cervical cancer causes postrenal failure through ureteral obstruction, and prostate cancer produces osteoblastic bone metastases."}],"terms":[{"term":"Dysplasia","def":"Disordered growth of an epithelium with atypical cells and loss of normal maturation, which may regress or progress to cancer."},{"term":"Carcinoma in situ","def":"Full-thickness malignant-appearing change of an epithelium that has not yet crossed the basement membrane."},{"term":"Transformation zone","def":"The band of the cervix between the original and the current squamocolumnar junction, where columnar epithelium is replaced by squamous metaplasia."},{"term":"Koilocyte","def":"An HPV-infected squamous cell with an enlarged, dark, wrinkled nucleus and a clear perinuclear halo."},{"term":"Unopposed estrogen","def":"Estrogen stimulation of the endometrium without the progesterone that normally converts it to secretory endometrium and ends the cycle with shedding."},{"term":"Germ cell neoplasia in situ (GCNIS)","def":"Malignant germ cells confined within seminiferous tubules, the precursor of most adult testicular germ cell tumors."},{"term":"Tumor marker","def":"A substance made by a tumor, or in response to it, that can be measured in blood to suggest its type or follow its course."},{"term":"Psammoma body","def":"A small round calcification with concentric layers, formed by dystrophic calcification of degenerating tumor cells in papillary tumors."},{"term":"Gleason score","def":"The sum of the two most common gland patterns in a prostate carcinoma, each graded 1 to 5, used to express its grade."},{"term":"Batson plexus","def":"The valveless vertebral venous plexus that links pelvic veins with veins around the spine, allowing tumor spread to vertebrae."},{"term":"Signet ring cell","def":"A tumor cell whose cytoplasm is filled with a mucin vacuole that pushes the nucleus to one side."},{"term":"Lichen sclerosus","def":"A chronic inflammatory skin disease of the vulva with thin, white, scarred epidermis that predisposes to HPV-independent squamous cell carcinoma."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C01","obj":"RE-13-J.1","title":"Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia: HPV, the transformation zone, and grading","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ectocervix is covered by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium and the endocervical canal by mucus-secreting columnar epithelium. At puberty and in pregnancy, estrogen everts the columnar epithelium onto the ectocervix, where the acidic vaginal environment drives squamous metaplasia. The area of metaplasia is the transformation zone. Its immature basal and reserve cells are actively dividing and easily reached through small abrasions, which is why nearly all cervical precancers and carcinomas begin there."},{"type":"p","text":"Human papillomavirus (HPV) infects these basal cells. Low-risk types (6 and 11) stay episomal and cause condylomas. High-risk types (16 and 18, with 31, 33, 45, and others) express two oncoproteins: E6 binds p53 and targets it for ubiquitin-mediated degradation, removing the G1 checkpoint and apoptosis, and E7 binds RB, releasing E2F and pushing the cell into S phase. Integration of high-risk viral DNA into the host genome often disrupts the viral E2 gene, which normally represses E6 and E7, so the oncoproteins are overexpressed. Inactivated RB leads to overexpression of the cell cycle inhibitor p16, so strong diffuse p16 staining is used as a tissue marker of high-risk HPV activity."},{"type":"p","text":"In a productive infection the virus replicates in maturing upper cells, producing koilocytes: cells with enlarged, hyperchromatic, wrinkled (raisin-like) nuclei and a clear perinuclear halo. Koilocytes therefore mark infection, not grade. Grade is set by how much of the epithelial thickness is replaced by atypical immature basal-type cells that fail to mature as they move toward the surface."},{"type":"table","title":"Grading of cervical intraepithelial neoplasia","cols":["Grade","Atypical immature cells occupy","Two-tier term","Biology"],"rows":[["CIN 1","Lower third","Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL)","Mostly productive viral infection with koilocytes; usually regresses"],["CIN 2","Lower two-thirds","High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (HSIL)","True precancer; may regress, especially in young women"],["CIN 3","More than two-thirds to full thickness (includes carcinoma in situ)","HSIL","Highest risk of invasion; basement membrane still intact"]],"caption":"The same LSIL and HSIL terms are used for Pap cytology, so a cytology report and a biopsy grade can be compared directly."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the basement membrane is intact in all grades, CIN cannot metastasize. Cytology (the Pap test) samples cells shed from the transformation zone, and the abnormal nuclear to cytoplasmic ratio of immature atypical cells is what the cytologist recognizes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C02","obj":"RE-13-J.2","title":"Risk factors for cervical dysplasia and the natural history of CIN","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"HPV infection is very common in young sexually active people, and most infections are cleared by cell-mediated immunity within about 1 to 2 years. Dysplasia develops when a high-risk type persists. Every major risk factor works in one of two ways: it increases exposure to high-risk HPV, or it weakens the local immune response that would clear it."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for cervical dysplasia and how they act","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Persistent HPV 16 or 18","Continued E6 and E7 expression; HPV 16 and 18 together cause about 70% of cervical cancers, and HPV 18 is relatively more common in adenocarcinoma"],["Early first intercourse","Exposure while the transformation zone is large and metaplasia is most active"],["Many sexual partners, or a partner with many partners","More exposures to high-risk types"],["Smoking","Tobacco carcinogens concentrate in cervical mucus, and smoking reduces local immune cells such as Langerhans cells"],["Immunodeficiency (HIV, transplant immunosuppression)","Poor cell-mediated clearance; persistent, multifocal, faster-progressing lesions"],["Long-term oral contraceptive use and high parity (secondary factors)","Associated with higher risk among HPV-positive women; mechanism not fully settled"]],"caption":"HPV is necessary but not sufficient. The other factors determine whether infection persists and progresses."},{"type":"p","text":"Most CIN 1 regresses: in about 60% the lesion disappears, about 30% persist, and only about 10% progress to HSIL. CIN 1 is largely a sign of a productive infection that the immune system usually removes. HSIL regresses less often (roughly a third) and progresses to invasive carcinoma in a minority over years. Progression from infection to invasive cancer usually takes a decade or more, which is why periodic screening works: there is a long window in which an intraepithelial lesion can be found."},{"type":"p","text":"In HIV infection, lesions are more often high grade, multifocal, and extend to the vagina, vulva, and anus, because the same virus infects all of these squamous surfaces and is not cleared. Invasive cervical carcinoma is an AIDS-defining illness."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 23-year-old with CIN 1 on biopsy and a 45-year-old with HIV and CIN 1 on biopsy are followed for two years. Which lesion is more likely to persist or progress?","explain":"The woman with HIV. Clearance of HPV depends on cell-mediated immunity, so poor T cell function lets high-risk HPV persist and keeps E6 and E7 active, whereas the immunocompetent young woman will usually clear the infection and the lesion will regress.","check":"Compare with the risk factor table and the paragraph on regression."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C03","obj":"RE-13-J.3","title":"Invasive cervical carcinoma and ureteral obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Invasive carcinoma begins when atypical cells break through the basement membrane into the cervical stroma. About 75 to 80% are squamous cell carcinomas arising from HSIL in the transformation zone. About 15 to 20% are adenocarcinomas arising from adenocarcinoma in situ of the endocervical glands; their share has grown because glandular precursors high in the canal are sampled less reliably by cytology. Adenosquamous and neuroendocrine (small cell) carcinomas are uncommon. Both main types are driven by high-risk HPV."},{"type":"p","text":"Early invasive cancer is often silent. As the tumor grows into a friable exophytic or ulcerated mass, contact during intercourse makes it bleed, so postcoital bleeding is the classic symptom, along with irregular or postmenopausal bleeding and a watery or foul discharge from necrotic tumor."},{"type":"flow","title":"High-risk HPV to invasive cervical carcinoma and renal failure","steps":["High-risk HPV infects basal cells of the transformation zone","Viral DNA integrates; E6 and E7 are overexpressed","E6 degrades p53; E7 inactivates RB","Atypical immature cells expand from CIN 1 to CIN 3","Cells breach the basement membrane and invade stroma","Lateral spread into the parametrium encases the ureters","Bilateral hydronephrosis causes postrenal failure and uremia"],"caption":"Lateral spread, not distant metastasis, is a leading cause of death in advanced cervical cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"The anatomy explains the complication. The parametrium is the connective tissue beside the cervix within the base of the broad ligament (the cardinal ligament region). Each ureter runs through it about 1.5 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix, passing beneath the uterine artery. Tumor extending laterally compresses and encases the ureters. Obstruction raises pressure above the block, dilating the ureter and renal pelvis (hydroureter and hydronephrosis); sustained back pressure lowers the glomerular filtration rate and causes pressure atrophy of the renal parenchyma. When both ureters are blocked, blood urea nitrogen and creatinine rise as postrenal azotemia progresses to uremia, a leading cause of death in advanced disease. Hydronephrosis from tumor is a criterion for advanced (stage III) disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Other routes follow the same local logic: anterior growth into the bladder and posterior growth into the rectum cause hematuria, fistulas, and rectal bleeding; lymphatic spread goes to paracervical, obturator, and internal and external iliac nodes, then common iliac and para-aortic nodes. Pelvic sidewall involvement causes back or sciatic pain and leg edema from lymphatic and venous obstruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C04","obj":"RE-13-J.4","title":"Endometrial hyperplasia: unopposed estrogen and PTEN loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Estrogen, acting through estrogen receptor alpha, drives proliferation of endometrial glands and stroma in the first half of the cycle. After ovulation, progesterone from the corpus luteum opposes this: it stops proliferation, converts the glands to secretory endometrium, reduces estrogen receptor levels, and increases the enzyme that converts estradiol to the weaker estrone. When the corpus luteum fails, progesterone withdrawal causes orderly shedding. Endometrial hyperplasia is an increase in the ratio of glands to stroma caused by estrogen stimulation that is not opposed by progesterone."},{"type":"table","title":"Sources of unopposed estrogen","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Obesity","Aromatase in adipose stroma converts adrenal androstenedione to estrone; insulin resistance lowers sex hormone binding globulin, raising free estrogen"],["Anovulation (perimenopause, PCOS)","No ovulation means no corpus luteum and no progesterone, while follicles still make estrogen; PCOS adds high androgen for peripheral aromatization"],["Estrogen-only therapy","Exogenous estrogen given without a progestin to a woman with a uterus"],["Granulosa cell tumor or thecoma","Tumor aromatizes androgen to estrogen"],["Tamoxifen (mechanistic example)","Antagonist at breast estrogen receptors but partial agonist at endometrial receptors"]],"caption":"Each cause raises estrogen action or removes the progesterone that normally limits it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Unopposed estrogen to endometrioid carcinoma","steps":["Estrogen acts without progesterone","Glands proliferate continuously without secretory change or shedding","Dividing cells accumulate mutations, most often PTEN loss","PI3K/AKT signaling increases growth and survival","Hyperplasia without atypia","Atypical hyperplasia (endometrioid intraepithelial neoplasia)","Endometrioid adenocarcinoma"],"caption":"A continuum from a reversible hormonal response to a clonal precancer to carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"PTEN is a lipid phosphatase that removes a phosphate from PIP3, opposing PI3K. When PTEN is lost, PIP3 accumulates and AKT is activated, promoting proliferation and inhibiting apoptosis. PTEN-null glands are already found in many hyperplasias, which shows that hyperplasia is not purely hormonal but is often an early clonal step."},{"type":"p","text":"Current classification divides hyperplasia into two groups. Hyperplasia without atypia is a diffuse, polyclonal response to estrogen with crowded but cytologically normal glands; it carries a low risk of carcinoma (about 1 to 3%) and often regresses when the estrogen excess is removed. Atypical hyperplasia, also called endometrioid intraepithelial neoplasia, is clonal, with crowded back-to-back glands and nuclear atypia; a large fraction of women with it already have, or will develop, endometrioid carcinoma. Hyperplasia presents with heavy, prolonged, or irregular bleeding, or with postmenopausal bleeding. Bleeding occurs because the thick proliferative lining outgrows its blood supply and breaks down irregularly instead of shedding in an organized way."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C05","obj":"RE-13-J.5","title":"Endometrioid versus serous endometrial carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Endometrial carcinoma is the most common invasive gynecologic cancer in the United States. It follows two main pathways that differ in cause, precursor, genetics, and behavior, and the distinction is one of the most tested ideas in gynecologic pathology."},{"type":"table","title":"Two pathways of endometrial carcinoma","cols":["Feature","Endometrioid (type I)","Serous (type II)"],"rows":[["Share of cases","About 80%","About 10 to 15%"],["Typical patient","Perimenopausal or early postmenopausal, about 55 to 65","Older postmenopausal, about 65 to 75"],["Hormonal setting","Estrogen excess: obesity, diabetes, anovulation, nulliparity","Not estrogen-related"],["Background endometrium","Hyperplasia","Atrophic"],["Precursor","Atypical hyperplasia","Serous endometrial intraepithelial carcinoma"],["Key mutations","PTEN (most common), PIK3CA, KRAS, ARID1A; mismatch repair defects","TP53 in nearly all"],["Histology","Glands resembling normal endometrium, often well differentiated","Papillae with marked nuclear atypia; psammoma bodies possible"],["Behavior","Usually confined to uterus at diagnosis; good prognosis","Spreads early through tubes and peritoneum even when small; poor prognosis"]],"caption":"Both types present with postmenopausal or abnormal bleeding, so the bleeding does not tell them apart; the setting and histology do."},{"type":"p","text":"Mismatch repair defects matter in endometrioid carcinoma. Mismatch repair proteins (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) correct base mispairs made during DNA replication. When they fail, errors accumulate in short repeated sequences (microsatellite instability) and in growth genes. In sporadic tumors the usual cause is silencing of MLH1 by promoter hypermethylation. In Lynch syndrome, a germline mutation in one mismatch repair gene is inherited, and loss of the second allele in the endometrium starts the tumor. Women with Lynch syndrome have a lifetime endometrial cancer risk that equals or exceeds their colon cancer risk, so endometrial carcinoma can be the sentinel cancer in a family."},{"type":"p","text":"Serous carcinoma explains a classic exam trap: a thin, atrophic endometrium does not protect an older woman. Because TP53-mutant cells grow without needing estrogen, the tumor can arise on an atrophic surface, exfoliate through the tubes, and seed the peritoneum, behaving much like high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma, with which it shares TP53 mutation and papillary architecture."},{"type":"p","text":"Postmenopausal bleeding is never normal, because a postmenopausal endometrium should be thin and inactive. It is explained by atrophy in most women, but endometrial carcinoma is the diagnosis that must be excluded. Endometrial carcinoma spreads by direct myometrial invasion, then to the cervix, pelvic and para-aortic nodes, and in serous type to the peritoneum and omentum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C06","obj":"RE-13-J.6","title":"Endometrial and endocervical polyps","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A polyp is a localized, benign overgrowth that projects from a mucosal surface. Endometrial and endocervical polyps both contain glands and stroma, but they differ in origin and setting."},{"type":"p","text":"Endometrial polyps are sessile or pedunculated masses, usually 0.5 to 3 cm, made of endometrial glands, dense fibrous stroma, and characteristic thick-walled blood vessels. The stromal cells are clonal and often carry chromosomal rearrangements, so the stroma is the true neoplastic component and the glands are drawn in. Polyps respond to estrogen and are more common around and after menopause, with obesity, and with tamoxifen, whose partial agonist action stimulates the endometrium. Their glands often do not respond to the cycle normally and may be hyperplastic."},{"type":"p","text":"Endocervical polyps are the most common cervical growths. They are smooth, soft, often red masses that protrude through the external os, composed of loose fibromyxomatous stroma covered by mucus-secreting endocervical columnar epithelium, often with chronic inflammation. They are thought to arise from inflammatory hyperplasia of the endocervix and have essentially no malignant potential."},{"type":"table","title":"Endometrial and endocervical polyps compared","cols":["Feature","Endometrial polyp","Endocervical polyp"],"rows":[["Location","Uterine cavity","Endocervical canal, may protrude through the os"],["Composition","Endometrial glands, fibrous stroma, thick-walled vessels","Endocervical mucinous glands, loose inflamed stroma"],["Driver","Clonal stromal proliferation, estrogen-sensitive","Inflammatory hyperplasia"],["Typical bleeding","Intermenstrual, heavy, or postmenopausal bleeding","Postcoital or intermenstrual spotting"],["Malignant potential","Low; a small risk of carcinoma within a polyp, higher after menopause","Essentially none"]],"caption":"Both are benign, but a polyp found with postmenopausal bleeding still requires tissue diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The bleeding mechanism is the same in both. The surface of a polyp is fragile; its tip may become congested as the stalk twists or is compressed, and the surface erodes or ulcerates. Contact (intercourse or examination) or ischemic breakdown then causes bleeding that is unrelated to the timing of menstruation, which is why polyps classically cause intermenstrual or postcoital spotting. An endometrial polyp can also enlarge the bleeding surface of the cavity and add to menstrual flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C07","obj":"RE-13-J.7","title":"Leiomyoma (fibroid)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A leiomyoma is a benign tumor of myometrial smooth muscle and the most common benign tumor in women; by age 50 most women have at least one, though many cause no symptoms. Each fibroid is monoclonal, arising from a single smooth muscle cell. The most common driver is a mutation in MED12, a subunit of the transcriptional Mediator complex; others carry HMGA2 rearrangements, and inherited fumarate hydratase mutations cause multiple leiomyomas with renal cell carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Growth depends on ovarian hormones. Estrogen increases progesterone receptors and growth factor signaling, and progesterone itself promotes fibroid cell proliferation and extracellular matrix production. This explains the natural history: fibroids appear after menarche, can enlarge in pregnancy, and shrink after menopause when ovarian hormone production falls. They are diagnosed at a younger age, are larger and more numerous, and cause more symptoms in Black women, in whom the incidence is about two to three times higher."},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly, leiomyomas are firm, sharply circumscribed, gray-white masses that bulge from the cut surface with a whorled pattern from interlacing smooth muscle bundles. They lack a true capsule but are surrounded by compressed myometrium (a pseudocapsule), so they shell out easily. Microscopically, uniform spindle cells with cigar-shaped nuclei form fascicles with few mitoses and no necrosis. Large fibroids outgrow their blood supply and degenerate: hyaline or cystic change, red (hemorrhagic) degeneration in pregnancy with acute pain, and calcification after menopause."},{"type":"table","title":"Fibroid location predicts symptoms","cols":["Location","Main effects","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Submucosal (beneath endometrium)","Heavy menstrual bleeding, iron deficiency anemia, infertility, miscarriage","Distorts and enlarges the cavity, increasing the bleeding endometrial surface and impairing implantation"],["Intramural (within the wall)","Heavy bleeding, enlarged irregular uterus, pelvic pressure","Enlarges the uterus and interferes with myometrial contraction that limits bleeding"],["Subserosal or pedunculated","Pressure on bladder (frequency) or rectum (constipation), torsion pain","Mass effect on neighboring organs; a pedunculated fibroid can twist on its stalk"]],"caption":"Most women have fibroids in more than one location."},{"type":"p","text":"Heavy menstrual bleeding is the most important symptom because chronic blood loss depletes iron stores, producing microcytic, hypochromic anemia with low ferritin. In pregnancy, fibroids are linked to miscarriage, malpresentation, obstructed labor, and postpartum hemorrhage, the last because they prevent the uterus from contracting uniformly after delivery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C08","obj":"RE-13-J.8","title":"Leiomyosarcoma versus leiomyoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Leiomyosarcoma is a rare malignant smooth muscle tumor of the uterus, typically in women in their 40s to 60s, often postmenopausal. It arises de novo from myometrium or its precursor cells. Leiomyomas do not transform into leiomyosarcomas: the two tumors have different genetic profiles (leiomyosarcomas have complex karyotypes with frequent TP53 and RB pathway disruption, whereas leiomyomas have simple changes such as MED12 mutation). A woman with many fibroids is therefore not at increased risk of sarcoma because of those fibroids."},{"type":"table","title":"Leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma compared","cols":["Feature","Leiomyoma","Leiomyosarcoma"],"rows":[["Age","Reproductive years","Usually older, often postmenopausal"],["Number","Often multiple","Usually single"],["Gross appearance","Firm, whorled, sharply circumscribed","Large, soft, fleshy, with hemorrhage and necrosis; infiltrative borders"],["Cytology","Uniform spindle cells","Moderate to severe nuclear atypia"],["Mitoses","Few","Many (often 10 or more per 10 high-power fields)"],["Necrosis","Infarct-type only, from degeneration","Coagulative tumor cell necrosis"],["Hormone behavior","Shrinks after menopause","Growth independent of menopause"],["Spread","None","Hematogenous, most often to the lungs; frequent recurrence"]],"caption":"The diagnosis rests on combining atypia, mitotic rate, and tumor cell necrosis; usually at least two of the three are required."},{"type":"p","text":"Necrosis must be read carefully. A degenerating fibroid can show infarct-type necrosis with a rim of granulation tissue, because a benign tumor outgrew its supply. Coagulative tumor cell necrosis in a sarcoma shows an abrupt transition from viable atypical cells to dead cells, because a fast-growing malignant clone outruns its vessels throughout the tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically, leiomyosarcoma cannot be reliably separated from a fibroid before surgery. A uterine mass that grows rapidly after menopause, when fibroids should shrink, is the classic warning sign, because a hormone-dependent benign tumor should not enlarge once estrogen and progesterone fall. Spread is hematogenous, like most sarcomas, rather than lymphatic, which explains early lung metastases. Prognosis is poor, with roughly 40% five-year survival overall and much better outcomes when the tumor is confined to the uterus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C09","obj":"RE-13-J.9","title":"Prostate adenocarcinoma: risk, zonal origin, grading, and PSA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Prostatic adenocarcinoma is the most common noncutaneous cancer in men and a leading cause of cancer death in men. Incidence rises steeply with age, and many older men have small, clinically silent tumors at autopsy. Incidence and mortality are highest in Black men. Risk is increased by a family history in first-degree relatives and by germline mutations, most importantly BRCA2, which is associated with earlier and more aggressive disease. The tumor depends on androgen signaling: testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone, which drives growth through the androgen receptor. A common early genetic event is fusion of the androgen-regulated TMPRSS2 promoter to an ETS family transcription factor gene such as ERG, so androgen directly switches on an oncogene."},{"type":"p","text":"About 70 to 80% of carcinomas arise in the peripheral zone, the posterior and lateral part of the gland that lies against the rectum. That location explains two findings: the tumor can be felt as a hard, irregular nodule on digital rectal examination, and it rarely causes early urinary obstruction because it is away from the urethra. Benign prostatic hyperplasia, by contrast, arises in the periurethral transition zone and obstructs early. The precursor is high-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"Microscopically, carcinoma forms small, crowded glands lined by a single layer of cells with enlarged nuclei and prominent nucleoli. The key diagnostic point is the absence of the basal cell layer that surrounds benign glands; stains for basal cells (p63, high molecular weight keratin) are negative, and the tumor cells express AMACR. Perineural invasion is common."},{"type":"p","text":"Gleason grading is based on gland architecture, not nuclear detail. Each pattern is graded from 1 (well formed, uniform glands) to 5 (no glands, sheets or single cells). The score adds the most common (primary) and second most common pattern, so 3 + 4 = 7. In practice scores now range from 6 to 10, and they are grouped into Grade Groups 1 (score 6) to 5 (score 9 or 10). Higher scores predict spread and death."},{"type":"table","title":"Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) and what raises it","cols":["Point","Explanation"],"rows":[["What PSA is","A serine protease (a kallikrein) made by normal and malignant prostatic epithelium that liquefies semen"],["Why it enters blood","Disrupted gland architecture and basement membrane let it leak into the circulation; cancer releases more per gram of tissue"],["Traditional cutoff","About 4 ng/mL, with age-adjusted ranges; no value fully separates cancer from benign disease"],["Benign causes of a raised PSA","Benign prostatic hyperplasia, prostatitis, infarction, urinary retention, instrumentation, and recent ejaculation"],["Lowers PSA","5-alpha-reductase inhibition shrinks the gland and lowers PSA by about half"],["Free PSA","Cancer releases relatively more bound PSA, so a low free PSA fraction favors cancer"]],"caption":"PSA is organ-specific, not cancer-specific."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C10","obj":"RE-13-J.10","title":"Osteoblastic bone metastases of prostate carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone is the most common site of distant spread from prostate cancer, and the lumbar spine and pelvis are the bones most often involved, followed by the proximal femur and ribs. Anatomy explains the pattern. The prostatic venous plexus drains into the internal iliac veins, but it also connects freely with the vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus), a network of valveless veins around the spinal column. When intra-abdominal pressure rises with coughing or straining, blood in the pelvic veins can flow backward into this plexus, carrying tumor cells straight to the lumbar vertebrae and pelvis without passing through the lungs."},{"type":"flow","title":"Prostate carcinoma to osteoblastic bone metastases","steps":["Peripheral zone carcinoma invades the prostatic venous plexus","Valveless vertebral veins carry cells to the lumbar spine and pelvis","Tumor cells settle in red marrow","Tumor secretes factors such as endothelin-1 and BMPs","Osteoblasts lay down new, disorganized woven bone","Sclerotic lesions, bone pain, raised alkaline phosphatase"],"caption":"Prostate cancer is the classic cause of osteoblastic (sclerotic) metastases in men."},{"type":"p","text":"Most carcinomas that metastasize to bone, such as lung, kidney, and thyroid, are osteolytic: they stimulate osteoclasts, often through RANKL and PTHrP, and destroy bone. Prostate cancer is the classic exception. Its cells secrete osteoblast-stimulating factors, including endothelin-1 and bone morphogenetic proteins, so new bone is laid down around the tumor. The new bone is woven and poorly organized, so it is dense on imaging but mechanically weak. Even sclerotic lesions have an underlying increase in resorption, which is why fractures still occur. Breast cancer can produce lytic, blastic, or mixed lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Each laboratory and clinical finding follows from osteoblast activity. Osteoblasts express alkaline phosphatase, so serum alkaline phosphatase (bone isoenzyme) rises with active bone formation. Radionuclide bone scans show intense uptake because the tracer binds to sites of new mineralization, and plain films show dense vertebrae. Serum calcium is usually normal and can even fall, because calcium is taken up into the new bone, in contrast to the hypercalcemia typical of lytic metastases. Pain arises from periosteal stretch and marrow infiltration. Collapse of an involved vertebra or epidural tumor can compress the spinal cord or cauda equina, causing back pain, leg weakness, and bladder or bowel dysfunction."},{"type":"example","title":"Telling blastic from lytic metastases","text":"A 72-year-old man has low back pain. Imaging shows several dense lumbar vertebrae, alkaline phosphatase is high, and calcium is 8.9 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.2). Work through it: dense bone means osteoblasts are being driven, not osteoclasts. Raised alkaline phosphatase confirms osteoblast activity. Normal calcium fits, because new bone takes calcium in rather than releasing it. Spine plus pelvis in an older man points to Batson plexus spread from the prostate. A lytic pattern with hypercalcemia would instead suggest myeloma, lung, kidney, or breast primary."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C11","obj":"RE-13-J.11","title":"Classification of ovarian tumors by cell of origin","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ovary contains three tissues that can give rise to tumors, and each tumor resembles the tissue it comes from. Surface epithelial tumors arise from Mullerian-type epithelium (the ovarian surface, inclusion cysts, endometriosis, or the fallopian tube). Germ cell tumors arise from oocytes and copy embryonic or extraembryonic tissues. Sex cord-stromal tumors arise from the cells of the follicle and ovarian stroma that make hormones. Knowing the origin predicts age, behavior, and what the tumor secretes."},{"type":"table","title":"Ovarian tumors by origin","cols":["Group","Share of ovarian tumors","Typical patient","Members","Products"],"rows":[["Surface epithelial","About two-thirds of all, about 90% of malignant","Adults, mostly over 40","Serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, Brenner","CA-125 in serous carcinoma"],["Germ cell","About 15 to 20%","Children, adolescents, and young women","Teratoma (mature, immature, monodermal), dysgerminoma, yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma, embryonal carcinoma","AFP, hCG, LDH by type; thyroid hormone in struma ovarii"],["Sex cord-stromal","About 5 to 10%","Any age","Granulosa cell, Sertoli-Leydig cell, fibroma, thecoma","Estrogen, androgen, or none"],["Metastatic","About 5%","Adults","Krukenberg tumor and others","None characteristic"]],"caption":"Most ovarian tumors are benign; the malignant ones are mostly epithelial."},{"type":"p","text":"Surface epithelial tumors are further graded as benign (cystadenoma), borderline (low malignant potential, with atypia but no destructive stromal invasion), or malignant (carcinoma). The subtypes differ in their lining. Serous tumors have tubal-type ciliated or papillary epithelium; mucinous tumors form large multiloculated cysts lined by mucin-secreting cells resembling endocervix or intestine, often unilateral and sometimes very large; endometrioid and clear cell carcinomas often arise from endometriosis and share mutations such as ARID1A; Brenner tumors contain nests of urothelium-like cells with grooved coffee-bean nuclei in a fibrous stroma and are usually benign."},{"type":"p","text":"Epithelial cancer risk rises with nulliparity, family history, BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation, Lynch syndrome, and endometriosis (for endometrioid and clear cell types), and falls with oral contraceptive use, multiparity, breastfeeding, and tubal ligation. The protective factors share a theme: fewer ovulations, or less exposure of tube and ovary to repeated injury and repair, means fewer chances for mutation in Mullerian epithelium."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C12","obj":"RE-13-J.12","title":"High-grade serous carcinoma: genetics, tubal origin, and spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"High-grade serous carcinoma is the most common and most lethal ovarian cancer. TP53 is mutated in nearly every case, and germline or somatic BRCA1 or BRCA2 loss is present in a substantial fraction. BRCA1 and BRCA2 are needed for homologous recombination, the error-free repair of double-strand DNA breaks. Without them, cells repair breaks by error-prone pathways and accumulate chromosomal damage, and loss of p53 lets these damaged cells survive instead of undergoing apoptosis. The result is a genetically unstable, high-grade tumor. Women with a BRCA1 mutation have a lifetime ovarian cancer risk of about 40%, and with BRCA2 about 10 to 20%."},{"type":"p","text":"Many of these cancers begin in the fallopian tube, not the ovary. Serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma, a TP53-mutant intraepithelial lesion of the fimbria, is found in a large share of tubes removed from BRCA carriers and of women with pelvic serous carcinoma. The fimbria lies on the ovary, so cells shed from the tube implant on the ovarian surface and peritoneum. By contrast, low-grade serous carcinoma is a different disease that arises from serous borderline tumors, carries KRAS or BRAF mutations, and grows slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"Histologically the tumor forms papillae and solid sheets with marked nuclear atypia. Psammoma bodies, laminated calcifications formed when single tumor cells or papillary tips die and calcify, are a classic clue to serous papillary tumors, and they also appear in papillary thyroid carcinoma and meningioma."},{"type":"flow","title":"How serous carcinoma spreads through the peritoneum","steps":["Carcinoma arises in the tubal fimbria or ovarian surface","Tumor cells exfoliate into peritoneal fluid","Fluid circulation carries cells to omentum, diaphragm, and bowel","Implants grow into an omental cake and peritoneal nodules","Blocked diaphragmatic lymphatics and leaky vessels cause ascites","Abdominal distension, bloating, early satiety, bowel obstruction"],"caption":"Spread is mainly transcoelomic, which is why most patients have peritoneal disease at diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the peritoneal cavity offers no barrier, symptoms are late and vague: bloating, abdominal fullness, early satiety, and urinary frequency. Most women are diagnosed with disease beyond the pelvis. CA-125 (MUC16) is a glycoprotein expressed by coelomic and Mullerian epithelium; it is raised (above about 35 U/mL) in most women with advanced serous carcinoma but in only about half with early disease, and it also rises in endometriosis, pelvic inflammatory disease, pregnancy, menstruation, cirrhosis with ascites, and any peritoneal irritation. It is therefore a marker for following known disease, not a screening test."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 46-year-old woman with a BRCA1 mutation has bloating, ascites, and a CA-125 of 600 U/mL, but both ovaries are only slightly enlarged at surgery while the omentum is full of tumor. How can a small ovary produce such widespread disease?","explain":"High-grade serous carcinoma often begins in the tubal fimbria and spreads by exfoliation into peritoneal fluid, so it seeds the omentum and peritoneum early without forming a large ovarian mass. BRCA1 loss with TP53 mutation makes the tumor genetically unstable and aggressive from the start.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on tubal origin and the peritoneal spread flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C13","obj":"RE-13-J.13","title":"Ovarian germ cell tumors: teratoma, struma ovarii, dysgerminoma, yolk sac tumor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Germ cell tumors arise from oocytes and mimic the tissues an early embryo can make. Tissues of the embryo proper form teratoma; undifferentiated germ cells form dysgerminoma; extraembryonic yolk sac forms yolk sac tumor, and trophoblast forms choriocarcinoma. Each tumor type therefore secretes the product of the tissue it copies, and that is the basis of the tumor markers."},{"type":"p","text":"Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is the most common ovarian germ cell tumor and one of the most common ovarian tumors of young women. It is benign. It arises from a germ cell after the first meiotic division, so it is usually 46,XX. The cyst is lined by skin with hair follicles and sebaceous glands and is filled with sebum and hair; teeth, cartilage, bone, respiratory epithelium, and neural tissue are common, usually concentrated in a nodule on the wall. About 10 to 15% are bilateral. Complications follow from its weight and contents: torsion with sudden severe pain, rupture with chemical peritonitis from sebum, rare malignant transformation (usually squamous cell carcinoma, in older women), and anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis when the neural tissue provokes antibodies that cross-react with brain receptors. Immature teratoma, which contains primitive embryonic tissue (especially immature neuroepithelium), is malignant."},{"type":"p","text":"Struma ovarii is a monodermal teratoma composed mainly or entirely of mature thyroid tissue. That tissue makes thyroid hormone independently of TSH, so a minority of patients become hyperthyroid with low TSH and a normal-sized thyroid."},{"type":"table","title":"Ovarian germ cell tumors and their markers","cols":["Tumor","Patient","Histology","Marker","Behavior"],"rows":[["Mature cystic teratoma","Young women","Skin, hair, sebum, teeth, neural tissue","None","Benign"],["Struma ovarii","Adults","Thyroid follicles with colloid","Thyroid hormone","Usually benign"],["Dysgerminoma","Adolescents and young women; gonadal dysgenesis","Sheets of large clear cells, fibrous septa with lymphocytes","LDH; placental alkaline phosphatase; KIT","Malignant, very radiosensitive, good prognosis"],["Yolk sac tumor","Children and young women","Schiller-Duval bodies, hyaline globules","AFP","Malignant, aggressive"],["Choriocarcinoma (nongestational)","Young women","Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast, no villi","hCG","Malignant, early blood-borne spread"]],"caption":"Dysgerminoma is the ovarian counterpart of testicular seminoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumor. Its uniform clear cells are rich in glycogen, and lymphocytes infiltrate the fibrous septa, exactly as in seminoma. It is linked to gonadal dysgenesis with Y chromosome material, and LDH is the usual serum marker. Yolk sac tumor is identified by Schiller-Duval bodies, glomerulus-like structures in which a central blood vessel is wrapped by tumor cells within a space also lined by tumor cells. Like fetal yolk sac, it secretes alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), visible as hyaline globules."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C14","obj":"RE-13-J.14","title":"Hormonally active ovarian stromal tumors and Meigs syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sex cord-stromal tumors copy the hormone-producing cells of the gonad, so their clinical effects are predicted by the hormone they make and by the age of the patient. In the normal follicle, theca cells make androgen under LH, and granulosa cells convert it to estradiol with aromatase under FSH. Leydig cells make testosterone, and Sertoli cells support germ cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Granulosa cell tumors produce estrogen. The adult type, which carries a FOXL2 mutation in nearly all cases, usually occurs in peri- and postmenopausal women; a juvenile type occurs in children. Microscopically the cells have grooved coffee-bean nuclei and may form Call-Exner bodies, small follicle-like spaces containing eosinophilic material. They secrete inhibin, a useful marker. They are low-grade malignancies that can recur many years later."},{"type":"table","title":"Effects of a granulosa cell tumor depend on age","cols":["Age","Effect of excess estrogen","Explanation"],"rows":[["Prepubertal girl","Precocious pseudopuberty: breast development, vaginal bleeding, advanced bone age","Estrogen comes from the tumor, so it is GnRH-independent and LH and FSH are suppressed"],["Reproductive age","Irregular or heavy bleeding","Excess estrogen disrupts the cycle and stimulates the endometrium"],["Postmenopausal woman","Endometrial hyperplasia, endometrial carcinoma, breast tenderness, postmenopausal bleeding","Unopposed estrogen acts on an endometrium with no progesterone"]],"caption":"Thecomas, made of lipid-laden theca-like cells in postmenopausal women, can also produce estrogen with the same endometrial effects."},{"type":"p","text":"Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors occur mainly in young women and secrete androgens. The effects come in sequence: first defeminization (amenorrhea, breast atrophy, loss of female fat distribution) as androgen suppresses the cycle, then virilization (hirsutism, clitoromegaly, deepening voice, temporal balding). Some are associated with DICER1 mutations."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibroma is a benign, hormonally inactive tumor of ovarian stromal fibroblasts that forms a hard, white, solid mass. Meigs syndrome is the combination of an ovarian fibroma (or another benign solid ovarian tumor), ascites, and a pleural effusion, usually right-sided. The fluid is thought to leak from the tumor surface into the peritoneum and then pass to the pleural space through diaphragmatic lymphatics and small diaphragmatic defects, which are more common on the right. The ascites and effusion resolve after the tumor is removed. Meigs syndrome matters because it mimics disseminated ovarian cancer: a pelvic mass with ascites and a pleural effusion that turns out to be benign."},{"type":"example","title":"Matching a hormone to a tumor","text":"A 7-year-old girl has breast development, vaginal bleeding, and a right adnexal mass; LH and FSH are low. Reason it through: puberty-like changes with low gonadotropins mean the estrogen is not coming from activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis (that would be central precocious puberty with high LH and FSH). The source must be autonomous, and an adnexal mass that makes estrogen is a granulosa cell tumor, most likely the juvenile type. If the same mass made androgen, she would show virilization instead, pointing to a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C15","obj":"RE-13-J.15","title":"Krukenberg tumor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A Krukenberg tumor is a metastatic carcinoma to the ovaries composed of mucin-producing signet ring cells in a reactive, cellular ovarian stroma. It is usually bilateral. The most common primary site is the stomach, specifically diffuse-type gastric carcinoma; other sources include the colon, appendix, and breast (lobular carcinoma). A Krukenberg tumor is sometimes found before the primary tumor is known."},{"type":"p","text":"Signet ring cells form because the tumor cells fill with intracellular mucin, which pushes the nucleus to the edge. In diffuse gastric carcinoma, loss of E-cadherin (CDH1) prevents cells from sticking to one another, so they infiltrate as single cells rather than forming glands, a pattern that persists in the ovarian metastasis. Mucin stains such as PAS and mucicarmine highlight the vacuoles. The ovaries are enlarged and solid, often keeping their outline, and the stroma around the tumor cells can become luteinized and occasionally produce androgen or estrogen."},{"type":"p","text":"The route of spread is not fully settled; lymphatic, hematogenous, and transcoelomic routes have all been proposed. The bilateral, often symmetric involvement and frequent peritoneal disease support spread through lymphatics and peritoneal fluid."},{"type":"table","title":"Bilateral mucinous ovarian masses: metastatic or primary?","cols":["Feature","Krukenberg (metastatic)","Primary mucinous ovarian tumor"],"rows":[["Laterality","Usually bilateral","Usually unilateral"],["Size and form","Solid, moderately enlarged ovaries","Often a very large multiloculated cyst"],["Cells","Single signet ring cells infiltrating stroma","Cysts and glands lined by mucinous epithelium"],["Primary site","Stomach most often; colon, appendix, breast","Ovary itself"]],"caption":"Bilateral mucinous tumors should prompt a search for a gastrointestinal primary."},{"type":"p","text":"The general lesson extends beyond Krukenberg tumor: bilateral solid ovarian masses in a woman with gastrointestinal symptoms, weight loss, or a known carcinoma should be considered metastatic until shown otherwise. Mucinous tumors of the appendix that spread through the peritoneum cause pseudomyxoma peritonei, a different pattern of gelatinous ascites."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C16","obj":"RE-13-J.16","title":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the penis and its in situ forms","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the penis is uncommon in the United States and more common where circumcision is rare. It usually affects men over 50 and arises on the glans or inner surface of the foreskin. Two pathways mirror those of the vulva. In the HPV-related pathway, high-risk HPV, most often type 16, drives the tumor through E6 and E7 exactly as in the cervix; these tumors tend to be basaloid or warty. In the HPV-independent pathway, chronic inflammation and lichen sclerosus produce keratinizing carcinomas."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for penile squamous cell carcinoma","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["HPV 16 (and 18)","E6 degrades p53 and E7 inactivates RB in infected keratinocytes"],["Lack of circumcision","The foreskin retains smegma and moisture and harbors HPV; neonatal circumcision is protective"],["Phimosis (nonretractile foreskin)","Trapped smegma, poor hygiene, and chronic balanoposthitis cause ongoing inflammation and epithelial injury"],["Smoking","Tobacco carcinogens and local immune suppression"],["Lichen sclerosus","Chronic inflammatory scarring that favors HPV-independent carcinoma"]],"caption":"Like the cervix, HPV is one road to cancer; chronic inflammation is the other."},{"type":"p","text":"Three lesions represent squamous carcinoma in situ (penile intraepithelial neoplasia), and boards distinguish them by appearance and patient. All show full-thickness atypia of the epidermis with an intact basement membrane, and all are associated with HPV, mainly type 16."},{"type":"table","title":"Carcinoma in situ of the penis","cols":["Lesion","Patient and site","Appearance","Behavior"],"rows":[["Bowen disease","Older men; shaft and scrotum","Solitary, sharply marked gray-white or scaly plaque","Can progress to invasive carcinoma in a minority"],["Erythroplasia of Queyrat","Older men; glans and inner foreskin","Single or multiple bright red, velvety plaques","Can progress to invasive carcinoma"],["Bowenoid papulosis","Younger sexually active men","Multiple small reddish-brown papules, often mistaken for warts","Rarely if ever progresses; often regresses"]],"caption":"Bowenoid papulosis looks like carcinoma in situ under the microscope but behaves benignly, so age and lesion number matter."},{"type":"p","text":"Invasive carcinoma presents as a painless ulcer or an exophytic, warty mass, often hidden under a phimotic foreskin and so found late. It spreads first by lymphatics to the superficial and then deep inguinal nodes, following the drainage of penile skin, and only later to pelvic nodes and distant sites. Enlarged inguinal nodes may be metastatic or reactive to infection of the ulcerated tumor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C17","obj":"RE-13-J.17","title":"Testicular tumors: classification, origin, and presentation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Testicular tumors are the most common solid cancers in men aged about 15 to 35. About 95% are germ cell tumors; the rest are mainly sex cord-stromal tumors (Leydig and Sertoli cell tumors) and, in older men, lymphoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Most adult germ cell tumors develop from germ cell neoplasia in situ (GCNIS), malignant germ cells lying within seminiferous tubules along the basement membrane. GCNIS is thought to arise from fetal gonocytes that failed to mature, which is why the risk is set early in life and linked to abnormal testicular development. Nearly all invasive germ cell tumors carry extra copies of the short arm of chromosome 12, most often as isochromosome 12p, a chromosome made of two 12p arms. Genes on 12p that maintain pluripotency are thought to drive the tumor. GCNIS-derived tumors are divided into seminoma and nonseminomatous germ cell tumors (embryonal carcinoma, postpubertal yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma, teratoma, and mixed tumors). Prepubertal teratoma and yolk sac tumor, and spermatocytic tumor of older men, do not arise from GCNIS."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for testicular germ cell tumors","cols":["Risk factor","Explanation"],"rows":[["Cryptorchidism","Risk is several-fold higher in the undescended testis and modestly higher in the descended one, pointing to an underlying developmental defect"],["Gonadal dysgenesis and androgen insensitivity","Abnormal gonads retain immature germ cells"],["Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)","Increased germ cell tumor risk, especially mediastinal germ cell tumors"],["Prior testicular germ cell tumor","Risk in the opposite testis is increased"],["Family history","Brothers and sons of affected men have higher risk"]],"caption":"Infertility and other signs of testicular dysgenesis also travel with these tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic presentation is a painless, firm testicular mass or heaviness in a young man. Transillumination separates solid from fluid: light passes through a fluid-filled hydrocele or spermatocele, but a solid tumor blocks it. Pain or a sudden enlargement can occur with hemorrhage into the tumor."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymphatic spread follows embryology. The testis develops on the posterior abdominal wall and descends, carrying its vessels and lymphatics with it, so testicular lymph drains to the para-aortic (retroperitoneal) nodes near the renal veins, not to the inguinal nodes. Scrotal skin drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. Seminoma tends to spread by lymphatics; nonseminomatous tumors, especially choriocarcinoma, spread early through blood to the lungs, liver, and brain."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C18","obj":"RE-13-J.18","title":"Seminoma and nonseminomatous germ cell tumors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Seminoma is the most common pure testicular germ cell tumor, with a peak in the 30s, slightly later than nonseminomatous tumors. It forms a homogeneous, gray-white, lobulated mass with little hemorrhage or necrosis. Microscopically it consists of sheets of uniform large cells with clear, glycogen-rich cytoplasm, central nuclei with prominent nucleoli, and fibrous septa infiltrated by lymphocytes, sometimes with granulomas. The cells express placental alkaline phosphatase (PLAP), KIT (CD117), and OCT3/4. Seminoma is very sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy, and its prognosis is excellent because it grows slowly and spreads in an orderly way through lymphatics."},{"type":"p","text":"Tumor markers follow the tissue each component imitates. Yolk sac elements make AFP, as fetal yolk sac does, and syncytiotrophoblast makes hCG, as placenta does. Pure seminoma never raises AFP. A minority of seminomas contain scattered syncytiotrophoblastic giant cells and raise hCG modestly. An elevated AFP in a tumor called seminoma therefore means a nonseminomatous component is present. Many germ cell tumors are mixed, and the most aggressive component determines behavior."},{"type":"table","title":"Testicular germ cell tumors compared","cols":["Tumor","Typical patient","Gross and histology","Marker","Behavior"],"rows":[["Seminoma","Men in their 30s","Homogeneous mass; sheets of clear cells, lymphocytes in septa","PLAP; hCG sometimes mildly raised; AFP normal","Radiosensitive, lymphatic spread, excellent prognosis"],["Embryonal carcinoma","Men in their 20s","Hemorrhagic, necrotic mass; primitive glands and sheets","CD30 positive; hCG or AFP when mixed","Aggressive, metastasizes early"],["Yolk sac tumor","Boys under about 3 (pure form); common component of adult mixed tumors","Schiller-Duval bodies","AFP","Malignant; most common testicular tumor in young children"],["Choriocarcinoma","Young men","Small hemorrhagic tumor; cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast","Very high hCG","Early blood-borne spread to lungs and brain"],["Teratoma","Children (usually benign) and men","Tissues from two or more germ layers","Usually none when pure","In postpubertal men, malignant potential regardless of maturity"]],"caption":"Seminoma and ovarian dysgerminoma are histologic twins."},{"type":"p","text":"Very high hCG produces systemic effects because hCG shares its alpha subunit with LH, FSH, and TSH and its beta subunit resembles that of LH. Acting like LH, hCG stimulates Leydig cells and testicular aromatase, raising estradiol and causing gynecomastia; at very high levels it weakly activates TSH receptors and can cause hyperthyroidism. A small primary with widespread lung metastases and a huge hCG is the classic choriocarcinoma picture."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 26-year-old man has a testicular mass called seminoma on biopsy, but his serum AFP is clearly elevated. What does the AFP tell you?","explain":"Pure seminoma does not make AFP. An elevated AFP means yolk sac tumor elements are present, so this is a mixed or nonseminomatous germ cell tumor, which behaves more aggressively than pure seminoma.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on tumor markers and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C19","obj":"RE-13-J.19","title":"Leydig cell tumor and testicular lymphoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Leydig cell tumor is the most common sex cord-stromal tumor of the testis. It occurs in boys and in men of any adult age. It forms a well-circumscribed, golden-brown nodule; the color comes from lipid and lipofuscin in the abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm. Reinke crystals, rod-shaped eosinophilic cytoplasmic crystalloids, are characteristic but present in only a minority of tumors. Most are benign; roughly 10% behave in a malignant way, usually in older men."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical picture depends on the hormone and the patient's age. Leydig cells normally make testosterone, and testosterone can be aromatized to estradiol, so a Leydig cell tumor can produce androgen, estrogen, or both."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormonal effects of a Leydig cell tumor","cols":["Patient","Dominant effect","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Prepubertal boy","Precocious pseudopuberty: penile growth, pubic hair, deep voice, rapid growth","Autonomous testosterone; LH and FSH are suppressed, so it is GnRH-independent"],["Adult man","Gynecomastia, reduced libido, erectile dysfunction","Tumor estrogen (direct secretion and aromatization) stimulates breast tissue and suppresses gonadotropins and normal testosterone"]],"caption":"In an adult the extra testosterone is masked by normal levels, so estrogen effects are what is noticed."},{"type":"p","text":"Testicular lymphoma is the most common testicular tumor in men over 60. Germ cell tumors are rare at that age, so a testicular mass in an older man should suggest lymphoma first. Most cases are diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. The tumor infiltrates between seminiferous tubules and often replaces the testis diffusely. It is frequently bilateral at diagnosis or later, and it has a tendency to spread to the central nervous system and other extranodal sites such as skin and Waldeyer ring; one proposed explanation is that the testis and brain are both immune-privileged sites. Its prognosis is worse than that of germ cell tumors."},{"type":"table","title":"Testicular mass by age","cols":["Age","Most likely tumor"],"rows":[["Under about 3 years","Yolk sac tumor, prepubertal teratoma"],["About 15 to 35 years","Germ cell tumors: nonseminomatous in the 20s, seminoma in the 30s"],["Over about 60 years","Lymphoma; spermatocytic tumor is a rare alternative"]],"caption":"Age is the first clue in any testicular mass question."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C20","obj":"RE-13-J.20","title":"Vaginal tumors: squamous cell carcinoma, clear cell adenocarcinoma, and sarcoma botryoides","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Primary vaginal cancer is rare; most malignant tumors in the vagina are extensions or metastases from the cervix, endometrium, or vulva. The three primary tumors that boards test each occur in a distinct age group, and each age group is explained by a different cause."},{"type":"table","title":"Primary vaginal malignancies","cols":["Tumor","Patient","Cause or precursor","Key findings"],"rows":[["Squamous cell carcinoma","Older women, usually over 60","High-risk HPV; vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia (VAIN); prior cervical neoplasia","Bleeding or discharge; mass usually in upper posterior vagina"],["Clear cell adenocarcinoma","Young women, typically late teens to 20s","In utero diethylstilbestrol (DES) exposure; vaginal adenosis","Glandular tumor with clear, glycogen-rich cells and hobnail cells"],["Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, botryoid type (sarcoma botryoides)","Girls usually under 5","Malignant tumor of primitive skeletal muscle cells","Soft, polypoid, grape-like mass protruding from the vagina; desmin and myogenin positive"]],"caption":"Age separates the three almost by itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Vaginal squamous cell carcinoma uses the same HPV mechanism as cervical cancer and arises from VAIN, which is graded like CIN. Women with previous cervical HSIL or carcinoma are at higher risk because the whole lower genital tract shares the infection. Lymphatic spread depends on level: the upper vagina drains to the iliac nodes like the cervix, and the lower vagina drains to the inguinal nodes like the vulva."},{"type":"p","text":"DES is a synthetic nonsteroidal estrogen that was given to pregnant women from the 1940s to 1971. In the exposed female fetus it disrupted the normal replacement of Mullerian glandular epithelium by squamous epithelium in the vagina, leaving persistent glands called vaginal adenosis. A small fraction of exposed daughters, roughly 1 in 1,000, developed clear cell adenocarcinoma in this tissue. DES daughters also have structural changes such as a T-shaped uterus and cervical ridges. The disease has become rare as this cohort has aged."},{"type":"p","text":"Sarcoma botryoides is a botryoid embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. It grows beneath the vaginal mucosa, pushing it out into soft polypoid clusters that resemble grapes. Under the epithelium the tumor cells condense into a dense cambium layer. Because the cells are primitive skeletal muscle (rhabdomyoblasts, sometimes with cross-striations), they express the muscle markers desmin, myogenin, and MyoD1. It presents with vaginal bleeding or a mass protruding from the introitus in a very young girl."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C21","obj":"RE-13-J.21","title":"HPV-related and HPV-independent vulvar carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"About 90% of vulvar cancers are squamous cell carcinomas. Like cervical and penile carcinoma, vulvar carcinoma follows two separate pathways. They differ in patient age, background skin, precursor, and genetics, and recognizing which pathway fits a case is the main exam skill."},{"type":"table","title":"Two pathways to vulvar squamous cell carcinoma","cols":["Feature","HPV-related","HPV-independent"],"rows":[["Share","Minority (about a third)","Majority"],["Patient","Younger, often 40s to 50s; smokers; immunosuppressed","Older, often over 60 or 70"],["Background","Other HPV lesions of cervix, vagina, or anus; often multifocal","Lichen sclerosus or chronic lichen simplex"],["Precursor","Usual (HPV-related) VIN, also called vulvar HSIL","Differentiated VIN"],["Molecular driver","HPV 16 E6 and E7; diffuse p16 staining","TP53 mutation; aberrant p53 staining; HPV negative"],["Histology","Basaloid or warty, poorly keratinizing","Well differentiated, keratinizing with keratin pearls"]],"caption":"The same split appears in penile and some head and neck squamous carcinomas."},{"type":"p","text":"The HPV-related pathway is the familiar one: high-risk HPV infects the vulvar squamous epithelium, E6 and E7 disable p53 and RB, and full-thickness dysplasia develops before invasion. Smoking and immunodeficiency promote persistence, which is why these patients are younger and often have lesions at other lower genital sites."},{"type":"p","text":"The HPV-independent pathway arises from chronically inflamed skin. Lichen sclerosus produces thin, white, parchment-like skin with epidermal atrophy and a band of dermal sclerosis, causing itching and scarring. Years of inflammation and repair select keratinocytes with TP53 mutations, which form differentiated VIN and then invasive carcinoma. Only a small percentage of women with lichen sclerosus develop carcinoma, but most HPV-independent carcinomas arise on such a background."},{"type":"p","text":"Vulvar carcinoma presents with itching, a persistent plaque, an ulcer, or a mass, sometimes with bleeding. Because itching from lichen sclerosus is common, a new nodule or nonhealing ulcer is the change that matters. Spread is to the superficial inguinal nodes, then the deep femoral nodes, then pelvic nodes; risk of nodal spread increases with depth of invasion. Midline tumors can drain to both groins."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-10-C22","obj":"RE-13-J.22","title":"Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia and extramammary Paget disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN) is the precursor of vulvar squamous carcinoma, and its two forms mirror the two carcinoma pathways. They look different under the microscope, which is why differentiated VIN is easy to miss."},{"type":"table","title":"Usual VIN and differentiated VIN","cols":["Feature","Usual VIN (vulvar HSIL)","Differentiated VIN"],"rows":[["Cause","High-risk HPV, mainly type 16","HPV-independent; TP53 mutation"],["Patient","Younger women, smokers, immunosuppressed","Older women with lichen sclerosus"],["Gross","Often multifocal white, red, or pigmented plaques","Usually a single thickened white or gray plaque on lichen sclerosus"],["Microscopy","Full-thickness atypia of basaloid cells, like CIN 3; koilocytes may be present","Atypia limited to the basal layers; upper cells look prematurely mature with abundant pink cytoplasm"],["Stains","Diffuse, block-like p16 positivity","Aberrant p53 staining; p16 negative"],["Risk","Lower and slower progression; may regress","Higher and faster progression to carcinoma"]],"caption":"Differentiated VIN looks deceptively mature but carries the greater cancer risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Extramammary Paget disease of the vulva is an intraepithelial adenocarcinoma. Large pale tumor cells with abundant cytoplasm containing mucin spread through the epidermis singly and in clusters. They are thought to arise from glandular or apocrine-type progenitor cells within the epidermis and adnexa. The lesion is a red, moist, crusted, eczema-like plaque, often with a map-like border, that itches and is frequently treated for months as dermatitis. Because the cells spread within the epidermis well beyond the visible edge, recurrence is common."},{"type":"table","title":"Paget disease of the vulva and of the breast","cols":["Feature","Extramammary Paget disease (vulva)","Paget disease of the nipple (breast)"],"rows":[["Origin of cells","Usually arises within the epidermis or adnexa","Extension of an underlying ductal carcinoma into nipple skin"],["Underlying carcinoma","Usually absent; a minority have an adnexal carcinoma or an anorectal or urothelial primary","Nearly always present (ductal carcinoma in situ or invasive carcinoma)"],["Appearance","Red, crusted, map-like plaque on vulva","Eczema-like scaling, crusting, or ulceration of the nipple and areola"]],"caption":"Similar cells, different meaning: in the breast, Paget cells signal a carcinoma beneath."},{"type":"p","text":"Paget cells must be distinguished from melanoma and from pagetoid spread of squamous neoplasia. Paget cells contain mucin (PAS-positive, diastase-resistant) and express CK7 and CEA, whereas melanoma cells lack mucin and express S100 and SOX10."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Most tumors in this chapter can be predicted from a few mechanisms. High-risk HPV links microbiology to pathology across the cervix, vagina, vulva, penis, and anus: E6 removes p53, E7 removes RB, and persistence depends on cell-mediated immunity, which is why smoking and HIV matter. Where HPV is absent, chronic inflammation (lichen sclerosus, phimosis) and TP53 mutation take its place. In the endometrium, the balance of estrogen and progesterone, together with PTEN and mismatch repair genes, decides between orderly cycling, hyperplasia, and carcinoma, and the same endocrine logic explains why granulosa cell tumors cause hyperplasia and why fibroids shrink after menopause."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology predict spread and markers. The parametrial course of the ureters explains death from renal failure in cervical cancer; valveless vertebral veins explain prostate metastases to the lumbar spine; descent of the testis from the posterior abdominal wall explains para-aortic nodal spread; and the open peritoneal cavity explains why serous carcinoma presents late. Germ cell and sex cord-stromal tumors make the products of the tissues they copy, so AFP, hCG, LDH, estrogen, androgen, and thyroid hormone each point to a cell type. The limits are real: markers such as PSA and CA-125 are organ- or tissue-related, not cancer-specific, and a definite diagnosis always rests on histology."}],"summary":["High-risk HPV causes CIN by E6 degradation of p53 and E7 inactivation of RB in the transformation zone; koilocytes mark infection, and grade depends on the thickness of atypical immature cells.","Most CIN 1 regresses; persistence of HPV 16 or 18, smoking, and immunodeficiency drive progression to HSIL and invasive carcinoma.","Cervical carcinoma presents with postcoital bleeding and kills through lateral parametrial spread that obstructs the ureters and causes postrenal failure.","Unopposed estrogen and PTEN loss lead to endometrial hyperplasia; atypical hyperplasia is the precursor of endometrioid carcinoma, whereas serous carcinoma arises in atrophic endometrium with TP53 mutation.","Leiomyomas are common, estrogen-sensitive, monoclonal, and benign; leiomyosarcomas arise de novo and are recognized by atypia, mitoses, and tumor cell necrosis.","Prostate carcinoma arises in the peripheral zone, is graded by gland pattern (Gleason), raises PSA, and spreads through the vertebral venous plexus to make osteoblastic metastases with high alkaline phosphatase.","Ovarian tumors are classified as surface epithelial, germ cell, or sex cord-stromal; high-grade serous carcinoma carries TP53 and often BRCA mutations, starts in the tubal fimbria, and spreads through the peritoneum with ascites and raised CA-125.","Germ cell and stromal tumors make what their cell of origin makes: AFP (yolk sac), hCG (choriocarcinoma), LDH (dysgerminoma), thyroid hormone (struma ovarii), estrogen (granulosa cell), and androgen (Sertoli-Leydig, Leydig).","Testicular germ cell tumors arise from GCNIS with isochromosome 12p, present as painless solid masses that do not transilluminate, and spread to para-aortic nodes; lymphoma is the most common testicular tumor after 60.","Lower genital tract squamous carcinomas follow an HPV-related pathway in younger patients and an HPV-independent, TP53-driven pathway on lichen sclerosus in older patients; vulvar Paget disease, unlike breast Paget disease, usually lacks an underlying carcinoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, sections on oncogenic viruses and tumor suppressor genes"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the female reproductive tract, and disorders of the male reproductive tract"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Papillomaviruses and the human papillomavirus oncoproteins"},{"book":"Moore Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and perineum, sections on the ureters, uterus, and lymphatic drainage of the testis"}]},"RE-13-K":{"unit":"RE-13-K","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11","title":"Infectious Diseases of the Genitourinary System","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the infections that board questions build around a genital ulcer, an abnormal discharge, a syphilis serology result, or a patient in shock with a tampon in place. It explains why chancroid hurts and a syphilitic chancre does not, how loss of lactobacilli changes vaginal chemistry, how Chlamydia and Neisseria reach and scar the upper tract, how herpes simplex virus hides in sacral ganglia, how HPV type predicts a wart or a cancer, how syphilis progresses and how its two kinds of antibody test behave, and how a superantigen causes toxic shock. Most questions hinge on one or two discriminating findings, such as pain, pH, a wet mount, or a Gram stain.","prerequisites":["Gram stain, bacterial cell envelope, and endotoxin (lipooligosaccharide) structure","Vaginal and cervical epithelium, the transformation zone, and the effect of estrogen on glycogen content","Complement pathways and the membrane attack complex (C5b to C9)","MHC class II antigen presentation and T cell receptor activation","Cell cycle control by p53 and RB, and viral latency"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive dysplasia and neoplasms (RE-13-J)","how":"High-risk HPV drives the cervical, vulvar, vaginal, penile, and anal squamous lesions taught there."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Superantigens, terminal complement deficiency, and HLA-B27-associated reactive arthritis are immune mechanisms that explain toxic shock, recurrent Neisseria infection, and postinfectious arthritis."},{"to":"Cardiovascular pathology","how":"Syphilitic aortitis is a classic cause of ascending aortic aneurysm and aortic regurgitation."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy","how":"Tabes dorsalis destroys dorsal roots and dorsal columns, so its sensory signs follow the proprioceptive pathway; its pupil sign reflects a separate midbrain light reflex lesion."},{"to":"Pregnancy and the newborn","how":"Syphilis crosses the placenta, while gonorrhea, Chlamydia, and HSV infect the newborn during delivery, which predicts the timing and form of neonatal disease."}],"terms":[{"term":"Chancre","def":"The painless, indurated, clean-based ulcer of primary syphilis that forms at the site of inoculation."},{"term":"Bubo","def":"A greatly enlarged, often fluctuant inflamed lymph node, seen in chancroid and lymphogranuloma venereum."},{"term":"Clue cell","def":"A vaginal squamous cell so covered with adherent coccobacilli that its borders look stippled and blurred, the microscopic marker of bacterial vaginosis."},{"term":"Whiff (amine) test","def":"Release of a fishy odor when 10% potassium hydroxide is added to vaginal discharge, caused by volatilized amines from anaerobic bacteria."},{"term":"Elementary body","def":"The small, metabolically inactive, infectious extracellular form of Chlamydia."},{"term":"Reticulate body","def":"The larger, metabolically active intracellular form of Chlamydia that divides by binary fission inside an inclusion."},{"term":"Koilocyte","def":"An HPV-infected squamous cell with an enlarged, dark, wrinkled nucleus surrounded by a clear perinuclear halo."},{"term":"Condylomata lata","def":"Flat, moist, gray-white plaques of secondary syphilis in warm intertriginous skin, teeming with spirochetes."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A microbial protein that binds MHC class II and the T cell receptor V beta region outside the peptide groove, activating a large fraction of T cells at once."},{"term":"Nontreponemal test","def":"A syphilis screening test (VDRL, RPR) that measures antibody to cardiolipin antigen; its titer reflects disease activity."},{"term":"Treponemal test","def":"A syphilis test (FTA-ABS, TP-PA, enzyme immunoassay) that measures antibody to Treponema pallidum itself and usually stays positive for life."},{"term":"Obliterative endarteritis","def":"Concentric endothelial and fibroblastic proliferation narrowing small arteries, with a surrounding plasma cell rich infiltrate, the basic vascular lesion of syphilis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C01","obj":"RE-13-K.1","title":"Chancroid versus the chancre of primary syphilis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A genital ulcer question turns on three features: whether the ulcer hurts, the texture of its edge and base, and the behavior of the regional lymph nodes. Each feature follows from the kind of inflammation the organism provokes."},{"type":"p","text":"Haemophilus ducreyi is a small gram-negative coccobacillus that enters through microabrasions during intercourse. Within about a week a tender papule becomes a pustule and then breaks down into an ulcer. Bacterial toxins (including cytolethal distending toxin) and a dense neutrophil infiltrate cause necrosis, so the ulcer is painful, soft rather than indurated, with ragged undermined edges and a friable base covered by gray to yellow exudate that bleeds easily. Ulcers are often multiple because organisms spread to opposing skin."},{"type":"p","text":"About half of patients develop painful inguinal lymphadenitis, usually on one side, and the nodes can liquefy into buboes that rupture through the skin. On Gram stain the organisms may line up in parallel chains (described as a school of fish), and culture requires special media containing hemin (X factor)."},{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum, a thin spirochete, produces a chancre at the inoculation site after about 3 weeks (range about 10 to 90 days). Lymphocytes and plasma cells gather around small vessels whose endothelium swells and proliferates (obliterative endarteritis). Because there is little neutrophilic necrosis, the lesion is painless; because cells and fibrosis fill the base, it is firm and indurated with a smooth raised border and a clean base."},{"type":"p","text":"It is usually single, and the inguinal nodes enlarge on both sides but stay firm, rubbery, and nontender. The spirochete is too thin to see on Gram stain and is shown in ulcer fluid by dark-field microscopy or immunofluorescence. Serology may still be negative in the first days of the chancre (C11)."},{"type":"table","title":"Genital ulcers compared","cols":["Feature","Chancroid","Primary syphilis","Genital herpes","LGV"],"rows":[["Organism","Haemophilus ducreyi","Treponema pallidum","HSV-2, increasingly HSV-1","C. trachomatis L1 to L3"],["Ulcer pain","Painful","Painless","Painful","Painless, often unnoticed"],["Ulcer character","Soft, ragged undermined edge, gray exudate","Firm, indurated, clean base","Grouped vesicles that become shallow erosions","Small transient papule or erosion"],["Number","Often multiple","Usually single","Multiple","Single"],["Nodes","Painful, often unilateral, may suppurate","Painless, rubbery, bilateral","Tender, bilateral in primary infection","Painful buboes weeks later"],["Laboratory clue","Gram-negative coccobacilli in chains","Spirochetes on dark-field; serology","Multinucleated giant cells; PCR","Chlamydial NAAT of ulcer or node aspirate"]],"caption":"Pain separates chancroid and herpes from syphilis and LGV; ulcer texture and node behavior separate the rest."},{"type":"p","text":"Pain alone does not settle the diagnosis, because herpes is also painful, but herpes begins as grouped vesicles and leaves shallow erosions without a dirty necrotic base (C07). All genital ulcers break the mucosal barrier and recruit CD4 T cells and macrophages to the surface, which is why ulcerative infections raise both acquisition and transmission of HIV. Coinfection also occurs, so one negative test does not exclude another cause."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C02","obj":"RE-13-K.2","title":"Bacterial vaginosis: loss of lactobacilli and its chemical signature","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In reproductive-age women, estrogen thickens the vaginal squamous epithelium and fills its cells with glycogen. As cells shed, glycogen is broken down to small sugars that lactobacilli ferment to lactic acid, holding vaginal pH at about 3.8 to 4.5. This acid environment, together with a stable lactobacillus population that occupies the epithelial surface, suppresses most anaerobes and gram-negative rods."},{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial vaginosis is a shift in the whole bacterial community rather than invasion by one pathogen. Lactobacilli decline, and Gardnerella vaginalis, a small gram-variable rod, forms a biofilm on the epithelium together with anaerobes such as Prevotella, Mobiluncus, and Atopobium vaginae. With less lactic acid, pH rises above 4.5. The anaerobes decarboxylate amino acids into volatile amines (trimethylamine, putrescine, cadaverine) that cause the fishy odor. The odor is stronger after intercourse or menses because semen and blood are alkaline, and alkali turns the amines into their volatile form. Adding 10% KOH to the discharge does the same thing on a slide, which is the positive whiff test."},{"type":"p","text":"Clue cells are squamous cells coated with adherent Gardnerella and anaerobes, the biofilm seen under the microscope. Because the bacteria stay on the surface and do not invade, there is little tissue injury and few neutrophils; this is why the condition is called a vaginosis rather than a vaginitis, and why itching, redness, and dysuria are usually absent. The discharge is thin, homogeneous, and gray-white, coating the vaginal walls."},{"type":"table","title":"Clinical (Amsel) criteria for bacterial vaginosis","cols":["Finding","What it reflects"],"rows":[["Thin, homogeneous gray-white discharge","Overgrowth without inflammation"],["pH above 4.5","Loss of lactic acid production by lactobacilli"],["Positive whiff test","Volatile amines from anaerobic metabolism"],["Clue cells (at least about 20% of epithelial cells)","Bacterial biofilm on squamous cells"]],"caption":"Three of the four findings support the diagnosis; a Gram stain scoring system (Nugent score) is the laboratory reference."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk rises with new or multiple sexual partners and douching. The condition is associated with sexual activity but is not classed as a classic sexually transmitted infection, and Gardnerella is found in many healthy women, so culturing it is not diagnostic. Enzymes from the altered flora, such as sialidases and proteases, degrade the protective mucus layer, which helps explain the associations with preterm birth, chorioamnionitis, PID, infection after gynecologic procedures, and greater susceptibility to HIV and other STIs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C03","obj":"RE-13-K.3","title":"Separating the three common vaginal infections at the bedside","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three simple tests separate the common causes of abnormal discharge, and each measures something specific. Vaginal pH shows how much lactic acid lactobacilli are still making. The whiff test detects volatile amines produced by anaerobes. Microscopy shows the cells: a saline wet mount reveals clue cells, motile trichomonads, and neutrophils, while 10% KOH dissolves epithelial cells and leaves fungal elements intact."},{"type":"p","text":"Candida albicans is part of the normal flora in many women and tolerates acid, so candidal vaginitis occurs at a normal pH of 4.5 or below and produces no amines. Overgrowth follows conditions that remove competing bacteria or raise glycogen and glucose in the epithelium: broad-spectrum antibiotics, poorly controlled diabetes, pregnancy and other high-estrogen states, corticosteroids, and impaired cell-mediated immunity. Hyphae invade the superficial epithelium and provoke inflammation, so vulvar itching, burning, redness, and a thick white curdy discharge dominate."},{"type":"table","title":"Bedside findings in vaginal discharge","cols":["Finding","Bacterial vaginosis","Trichomoniasis","Candidal vaginitis"],"rows":[["Discharge","Thin, gray-white, homogeneous","Frothy, yellow-green, malodorous","Thick, white, curdy"],["pH (normal about 3.8 to 4.5)","Above 4.5","Above 4.5","4.5 or below (normal)"],["Whiff test with KOH","Positive","Often positive","Negative"],["Saline wet mount","Clue cells, few neutrophils","Motile flagellated trophozoites, many neutrophils","Some neutrophils; yeast may be seen"],["KOH preparation","No fungal elements","No fungal elements","Pseudohyphae and budding yeast"],["Inflammation","Minimal","Marked; strawberry cervix in some","Marked vulvar itching and redness"]],"caption":"pH divides Candida from the other two; neutrophils and motile organisms divide trichomoniasis from bacterial vaginosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a discharge workup","text":"A woman has a malodorous discharge. pH is 5.5, so lactobacilli are no longer acidifying the vagina, which rules against Candida alone. The whiff test is positive, so anaerobes are producing amines; that fits bacterial vaginosis and is also common in trichomoniasis. The saline mount is the decider. Many squamous cells with stippled, blurred borders and very few neutrophils indicate a surface biofilm without inflammation: bacterial vaginosis. If the same slide instead showed sheets of neutrophils and pear-shaped organisms twitching with flagella, the diagnosis would be trichomoniasis. If pH had been 4.2 with a negative whiff test and itching, a KOH mount showing pseudohyphae would point to Candida."},{"type":"p","text":"A raised pH is not specific. Semen, menstrual blood, and atrophic vaginitis after menopause (low estrogen, little glycogen, few lactobacilli, thin inflamed epithelium) all raise it. Mixed infections occur, and a negative wet mount does not exclude trichomoniasis (C14)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C04","obj":"RE-13-K.4","title":"Genital Chlamydia trachomatis: a silent intracellular infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium: it depends on host ATP and nutrients and cannot grow on artificial media. Its envelope resembles a gram-negative wall but contains very little peptidoglycan, so it stains poorly on Gram stain. Serovars A to C cause trachoma, D to K cause genital and neonatal infection, and L1 to L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum (C05)."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Chlamydial developmental cycle","steps":["Elementary body attaches to columnar epithelial cell","Endocytosed into an inclusion that avoids lysosomal fusion","Elementary body converts to reticulate body","Reticulate body divides by binary fission using host ATP","Reticulate bodies reorganize into elementary bodies","Cell lysis or extrusion releases elementary bodies to infect neighbors"],"caption":"The cycle takes about 2 to 3 days. Only the elementary body is infectious; only the reticulate body replicates."},{"type":"p","text":"The organism infects columnar epithelium, which explains its sites: urethra, endocervix, endometrium and fallopian tubes, epididymis, rectum, and in the newborn the conjunctiva and respiratory tract. Early infection provokes little acute inflammation, so most infected women and many men have no symptoms and continue to transmit. Urethritis causes dysuria with a scant watery or mucoid discharge. Cervicitis causes a mucopurulent discharge and a friable endocervix that bleeds on contact."},{"type":"p","text":"Tissue damage comes mostly from the host response. Persistent or repeated infection drives lymphocyte-rich chronic inflammation, and cytokines plus immune responses to chlamydial heat shock protein 60 cause fibrosis of the tubal mucosa. Ascent causes PID (endometritis and salpingitis), with tubal-factor infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain as sequelae. Spread to the liver capsule causes perihepatitis with violin-string adhesions (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome). In men, ascent along the vas deferens causes epididymitis, the leading cause in sexually active men under about 35. Reactive arthritis is discussed with nongonococcal urethritis (C09)."},{"type":"p","text":"A newborn delivered through an infected cervix can develop conjunctivitis 5 to 14 days after birth, with watery to mucopurulent discharge. Pneumonia appears at about 1 to 3 months: the infant is afebrile and tachypneic, with a repetitive staccato cough and often peripheral eosinophilia. Gonococcal conjunctivitis, by contrast, appears within 2 to 5 days (C06). Diagnosis in adults is by nucleic acid amplification testing; Giemsa staining of infected cells shows cytoplasmic inclusions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C05","obj":"RE-13-K.5","title":"Lymphogranuloma venereum: a lymphatic chlamydial infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serovars L1, L2, and L3 differ from serovars D to K in where they grow. They infect mononuclear phagocytes and travel through lymphatics to regional nodes, so the disease is centered on lymph nodes and the tissues they drain rather than on the mucosal surface. The result is a small, brief primary lesion followed by dramatic nodal disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of lymphogranuloma venereum","cols":["Stage","Timing","Findings","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Primary","About 3 to 30 days after exposure","Small painless papule, pustule, or shallow erosion that heals quickly and is often unnoticed","Local replication at the inoculation site"],["Secondary (inguinal)","About 2 to 6 weeks later","Painful, usually unilateral inguinal and femoral nodes with fever and malaise; nodes mat together, become fluctuant buboes, and may drain through sinuses","Spread to nodes; granulomas with central stellate abscesses"],["Secondary (anorectal)","After rectal exposure","Proctocolitis: rectal pain, tenesmus, bloody mucopurulent discharge","Infection of rectal mucosa and perirectal lymphatics"],["Late","Months to years if untreated","Rectal strictures, fistulas, genital elephantiasis","Chronic lymphatic obstruction and fibrosis"]],"caption":"LGV is a disease of lymphatics: the ulcer is trivial and the nodes and their drainage beds carry the damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Inguinal nodes drain the external genitalia, so penile or vulvar inoculation produces inguinal buboes. When nodes enlarge both above and below the inguinal ligament, the ligament creates a crease between them (the groove sign). The node histology, granulomas with central stellate abscesses full of neutrophils, is shared with cat scratch disease, so it is suggestive rather than specific."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymph from the rectum and upper vagina drains to perirectal and deep pelvic nodes rather than inguinal nodes. After receptive anal exposure, which accounts for recent outbreaks among men who have sex with men, inguinal buboes may be absent and the presentation is proctocolitis that can mimic inflammatory bowel disease. Untreated, chronic inflammation and fibrosis of lymphatics produce strictures, fistulas, and lymphedema of the genitalia."},{"type":"p","text":"The contrast with chancroid is the order of events. In chancroid the painful ulcer and painful nodes are present together; in LGV the painless lesion has usually healed by the time painful buboes appear, so the patient often reports no ulcer at all. Diagnosis is by chlamydial nucleic acid testing of the lesion, rectal swab, or node aspirate, with genotyping to identify L serovars where available."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C06","obj":"RE-13-K.6","title":"Gonorrhea: purulent local disease, dissemination, and complement","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus whose paired cocci have flattened adjacent sides. It is oxidase positive, ferments glucose but not maltose (unlike N. meningitidis), lacks a polysaccharide capsule, and is grown on selective chocolate agar (Thayer-Martin) that suppresses normal flora. Pili attach it to columnar epithelium, Opa proteins tighten that attachment, and an IgA1 protease cleaves mucosal IgA. Pili and Opa proteins change their antigens continually, so infection leaves no protective immunity and reinfection is common."},{"type":"p","text":"Lipooligosaccharide and peptidoglycan fragments released by the organism trigger a brisk neutrophil response. After about 2 to 7 days, men typically develop dysuria and a copious purulent urethral discharge; a Gram stain of the discharge showing gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils is highly sensitive in symptomatic men. Women are more often asymptomatic or have mild purulent cervicitis, and Gram stain of cervical secretions is far less sensitive, so nucleic acid amplification testing is used. Ascent causes PID, which tends to be more acute and febrile than chlamydial PID, and pharyngeal and rectal infection follow oral and anal exposure."},{"type":"table","title":"Gonorrhea and genital Chlamydia compared","cols":["Feature","N. gonorrhoeae","C. trachomatis (D to K)"],"rows":[["Organism","Extracellular gram-negative diplococcus","Obligate intracellular, poorly Gram staining"],["Urethral discharge","Copious, purulent","Scant, watery or mucoid"],["Gram stain of discharge","Intracellular diplococci in neutrophils","Neutrophils without organisms"],["Asymptomatic infection","Common in women","Common in both sexes"],["Neonatal conjunctivitis onset","About 2 to 5 days, purulent, can perforate cornea","About 5 to 14 days, milder"],["Distinctive complication","Disseminated infection with arthritis and pustules","Afebrile infant pneumonia; reactive arthritis"]],"caption":"Both cause urethritis, cervicitis, epididymitis, and PID and frequently coexist."},{"type":"p","text":"A newborn exposed during delivery develops hyperacute purulent conjunctivitis within the first few days. Because the organism can penetrate intact corneal epithelium, untreated infection can ulcerate and perforate the cornea and cause blindness."},{"type":"p","text":"In a small percentage of infections, usually in women and often during menses or pregnancy, serum-resistant strains enter the blood and cause disseminated gonococcal infection. One pattern is the arthritis-dermatitis syndrome: migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis of the wrists, hands, or ankles, and a few painless pustules or vesiculopustules on an erythematous base on the extremities. The other is purulent septic monoarthritis, most often of the knee. Gonococci are the most common cause of septic arthritis in sexually active young adults."},{"type":"p","text":"Killing of Neisseria in blood depends heavily on the membrane attack complex (C5b to C9), which inserts into the outer membrane and lyses the cell. People with deficiency of any terminal component (C5 to C9) suffer recurrent, often disseminated Neisseria infection, most famously meningococcal but also gonococcal. Pharmacologic blockade of C5 creates the same susceptibility, which shows that the defect is in lysis rather than in opsonization or phagocytosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with C8 deficiency has had two episodes of disseminated gonococcal infection. Why is he not also prone to recurrent Staphylococcus aureus infection?","explain":"Gram-positive bacteria have a thick peptidoglycan wall that the membrane attack complex cannot penetrate; they are cleared by C3b opsonization and phagocytosis, which work normally in terminal complement deficiency. Neisseria have a thin outer membrane that is directly lysed by C5b to C9, so only they depend on the missing step.","check":"The final paragraph on the membrane attack complex."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C07","obj":"RE-13-K.7","title":"Genital herpes: lytic infection, latency, and neonatal risk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Herpes simplex virus is an enveloped double-stranded DNA virus. HSV-2 causes most recurrent genital herpes, while HSV-1, acquired through oral-genital contact, now causes a large share of first genital episodes but recurs genitally less often. The virus replicates in squamous epithelial cells, which swell, fuse into multinucleated giant cells, and develop intranuclear inclusions; loss of cell cohesion produces intraepithelial vesicles that rupture into shallow, painful erosions on a red base."},{"type":"p","text":"A primary infection in a person with no prior HSV antibody is the most severe episode. After about 2 to 12 days, extensive bilateral grouped vesicles appear with fever, headache, malaise, myalgia, and tender bilateral inguinal nodes. Dysuria is common, and inflammation of sacral nerve roots can cause urinary retention; some patients develop aseptic meningitis. Lesions take about 2 to 3 weeks to heal. A first episode in someone already carrying HSV-1 antibody is milder because cross-reactive immunity limits spread."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Herpes simplex latency and reactivation","steps":["Lytic replication in genital epithelium forms vesicles","Virus enters sensory nerve endings","Retrograde axonal transport to sacral dorsal root ganglia","Latent genome persists in neurons with minimal gene expression","Stress, menses, or immunosuppression trigger reactivation","Anterograde transport returns virus to the skin"],"caption":"Latency in sacral ganglia explains lifelong recurrence in the same dermatome and asymptomatic shedding between outbreaks."},{"type":"p","text":"Recurrences are milder because memory T cells contain them: lesions are fewer, often on one side, and heal in about a week. A prodrome of tingling or burning in the buttock, thigh, or genitals often precedes them, reflecting viral activity in the sensory neuron. Virus is also shed from normal-appearing skin, and this asymptomatic shedding accounts for much transmission. A Tzanck smear from the base of an unroofed vesicle shows multinucleated giant cells, but it cannot separate HSV-1, HSV-2, and varicella-zoster virus and is insensitive; PCR is the preferred test."},{"type":"p","text":"Most neonatal herpes is acquired during delivery by contact with infected genital secretions; a minority is acquired in utero or after birth. The risk is highest when the mother has a primary infection near term, because she sheds large amounts of virus, often from the cervix, and has not yet made IgG to pass across the placenta. With recurrent maternal infection the risk is low, because viral load is lower and the baby is protected by maternal antibody. Neonatal disease appears in the first weeks as skin, eye, and mouth lesions, encephalitis, or disseminated infection of the liver, lungs, and adrenals with high mortality."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C08","obj":"RE-13-K.8","title":"HPV: condylomata acuminata, condylomata lata, and HPV-driven carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Human papillomavirus is a nonenveloped double-stranded DNA virus that reaches basal keratinocytes through microabrasions. Its life cycle is tied to squamous differentiation: the genome is maintained at low copy number in basal cells, and new virions are assembled only in the maturing upper layers, which is why the cytopathic changes are seen near the surface."},{"type":"p","text":"Low-risk types, mainly HPV 6 and 11, cause most anogenital warts (condylomata acuminata). These are soft, flesh-colored, papillary or cauliflower-like growths on the vulva, perineum, perianal skin, vagina, cervix, and penis. Microscopically they show papillary fronds, thickened squamous epithelium, and koilocytes: superficial cells with enlarged, dark, wrinkled nuclei and a clear perinuclear halo. The same types acquired during birth can cause recurrent respiratory papillomatosis of the larynx in children."},{"type":"table","title":"Condylomata acuminata and condylomata lata","cols":["Feature","Condylomata acuminata","Condylomata lata"],"rows":[["Cause","HPV 6 and 11","Secondary syphilis"],["Appearance","Raised, papillary, cauliflower-like, often keratotic on dry skin","Flat-topped, broad-based, moist, gray-white plaques"],["Microscopy","Koilocytes, papillomatosis","Plasma cell infiltrate, spirochetes on silver stain or immunostain"],["Associated findings","Usually isolated","Rash on palms and soles, lymphadenopathy, mucous patches"],["Serology","Syphilis tests negative","Nontreponemal titers high"]],"caption":"Both occur in warm moist anogenital skin; flat, moist lesions with a rash point to syphilis."},{"type":"p","text":"High-risk types, above all HPV 16 and 18 (also 31, 33, 45, and others), cause cancer because of two viral oncoproteins. E6 recruits a host ubiquitin ligase that degrades p53, so damaged cells neither arrest nor die. E7 binds RB and releases E2F transcription factors, pushing cells into S phase. In low-risk types these proteins bind weakly and the genome usually stays episomal. In high-risk infections the genome often integrates into host DNA and disrupts the viral E2 gene that normally restrains E6 and E7, so their expression rises. Most infections are cleared by cell-mediated immunity within about 1 to 2 years; persistence is the key risk."},{"type":"flow","title":"From high-risk HPV infection to carcinoma","steps":["High-risk HPV infects basal cells at a transformation zone","Persistent infection escapes immune clearance","Integration disrupts E2, increasing E6 and E7","E6 degrades p53; E7 inactivates RB","Unchecked proliferation accumulates host mutations","Intraepithelial neoplasia progresses to invasive squamous carcinoma"],"caption":"Overexpression of the host protein p16 is a tissue marker of this E7-driven pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"High-risk HPV causes nearly all cervical carcinomas, most anal carcinomas, a large share of vaginal carcinomas, the HPV-related subset of vulvar and penile carcinomas in younger patients, and a growing share of oropharyngeal carcinomas of the tonsil and base of tongue, mostly HPV 16. The squamocolumnar junctions of the cervix and anus are vulnerable because their immature metaplastic cells are easily infected and actively dividing (RE-13-J)."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C09","obj":"RE-13-K.9","title":"Nongonococcal urethritis and reactive arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Urethritis is inflammation of the urethra, causing dysuria and urethral discharge or itching. It is called nongonococcal when urethral secretions contain neutrophils but no intracellular gram-negative diplococci and gonococcal testing is negative. Compared with gonorrhea, onset is slower and discharge is scant, clear, or mucoid, because these organisms provoke a milder neutrophil response."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of nongonococcal urethritis","cols":["Organism","Key biology","Notes"],"rows":[["Chlamydia trachomatis (D to K)","Obligate intracellular bacterium","Most common identified cause; also epididymitis and reactive arthritis"],["Mycoplasma genitalium","Tiny bacterium with no cell wall, invisible on Gram stain","Important cause of persistent or recurrent urethritis"],["Ureaplasma urealyticum","Wall-less, urease-producing mollicute","Often found in healthy people; causal role less certain"],["Trichomonas vaginalis","Flagellated protozoan","Often asymptomatic in men (C14)"],["Others","HSV, adenovirus","No organism is found in many cases"]],"caption":"Because Mycoplasma and Ureaplasma lack a cell wall, they are not seen on Gram stain and are unaffected by agents that act on peptidoglycan."},{"type":"p","text":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile inflammatory arthritis that begins about 1 to 4 weeks after an infection elsewhere, either genitourinary (Chlamydia most often) or enteric (Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, Campylobacter). The classic triad is arthritis, conjunctivitis (sometimes anterior uveitis), and urethritis, although many patients do not show all three. The arthritis is asymmetric and involves a few large joints of the legs, often with enthesitis of the Achilles tendon or plantar fascia, dactylitis (sausage digits), and sometimes sacroiliitis. Skin and mucosal signs include thick hyperkeratotic plaques on the palms and soles (keratoderma blennorrhagicum), circinate balanitis, and painless oral ulcers. Post-urethritis cases occur mainly in young men."},{"type":"p","text":"Joint fluid is inflammatory but culture is sterile, which separates reactive arthritis from disseminated gonococcal infection, where organisms reach the joint and pustules appear on the skin. The mechanism involves bacterial antigens, and in Chlamydia even viable but nonculturable organisms, carried to the synovium in monocytes, together with an abnormal host response. HLA-B27, an MHC class I allele, increases the risk and the likelihood of severe, chronic, or axial disease, possibly by presenting bacterial peptides that resemble self peptides to CD8 T cells or by misfolding in a way that triggers IL-23 and IL-17 driven inflammation. Rheumatoid factor is negative, which places reactive arthritis among the seronegative spondyloarthropathies with ankylosing spondylitis and psoriatic arthritis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C10","obj":"RE-13-K.10","title":"Stages of syphilis and the endarteritis behind them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum is a thin, tightly coiled spirochete that moves by corkscrew motion and cannot be grown on routine media. Its outer membrane has few exposed proteins, which slows antibody recognition and lets it persist for decades. It spreads by sexual contact and across the placenta. In every stage, the basic lesion is obliterative endarteritis with a plasma cell rich infiltrate, and where the organism has spread determines what that lesion damages."},{"type":"flow","title":"Natural history of untreated syphilis","steps":["Inoculation through mucosa or broken skin","Primary: painless chancre after about 3 weeks, heals in weeks","Spirochetemia spreads organisms throughout the body","Secondary: rash, condylomata lata, lymphadenopathy, weeks to months later","Latent: positive serology without signs","Tertiary in a minority: gummas, aortitis, neurosyphilis after years"],"caption":"Early stages are rich in organisms and infectious; tertiary lesions have few organisms and reflect the host response."},{"type":"p","text":"The chancre heals in about 3 to 6 weeks even without treatment, because organisms have already disseminated. Secondary syphilis appears about 2 to 10 weeks after the chancre, when blood-borne spread seeds the skin and mucosa. It produces a symmetric, nonpruritic, reddish-brown maculopapular rash on the trunk and extremities that characteristically includes the palms and soles, condylomata lata in moist skin, gray mucous patches in the mouth, generalized painless lymphadenopathy, fever, malaise, and patchy hair loss. Less often there is hepatitis, immune complex glomerulonephritis, or early neurosyphilis such as meningitis or ocular involvement. These lesions are full of spirochetes, so secondary syphilis is the most infectious stage."},{"type":"p","text":"Latent syphilis has positive serology but no signs. Early latent disease (infection within about the past year) can still relapse into secondary lesions. Untreated, a minority of patients, historically about one third, develop tertiary disease years to decades later."},{"type":"table","title":"Tertiary syphilis lesions","cols":["Lesion","Mechanism","Consequences"],"rows":[["Gumma","Delayed hypersensitivity granuloma with central necrosis and few organisms","Firm, rubbery destructive masses in skin, bone, liver, testis"],["Aortitis","Endarteritis of the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta and arch, ischemic loss of medial elastic tissue","Wrinkled tree-bark intima, ascending aortic aneurysm, aortic root dilation with aortic regurgitation, coronary ostial narrowing"],["Tabes dorsalis","Degeneration of lumbosacral dorsal roots and dorsal columns","Loss of proprioception and vibration, sensory ataxia, positive Romberg sign, lightning pains, areflexia, Charcot joints, Argyll Robertson pupils"],["General paresis","Chronic meningoencephalitis of the cortex","Dementia, personality change, psychosis"]],"caption":"The thoracic aorta is affected because its wall depends on a dense network of vasa vasorum; the abdominal aorta has few and is usually spared."},{"type":"p","text":"Tabes dorsalis shows how anatomy predicts signs. Large-fiber proprioceptive axons in the dorsal roots and dorsal columns are lost, so the patient cannot sense joint position, walks with a broad-based stamping gait, and falls when the eyes close (Romberg sign). Loss of the afferent limb abolishes deep tendon reflexes, and unfelt joint trauma destroys joints. Argyll Robertson pupils are small and irregular, constrict to accommodation, but do not react to light, a sign attributed to a lesion of the light reflex pathway near the pretectal midbrain."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C11","obj":"RE-13-K.11","title":"Syphilis serology: nontreponemal and treponemal tests","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Because T. pallidum cannot be cultured routinely, syphilis is diagnosed mostly by two families of antibody test that measure different things. Nontreponemal tests, the VDRL and the RPR, detect IgM and IgG against a cardiolipin, lecithin, and cholesterol antigen. Cardiolipin is a phospholipid of mitochondrial membranes released from damaged host cells and also present in treponemal membranes, so these antibodies are a marker of tissue injury rather than of the organism itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Results are titers from twofold dilutions, and a fourfold change is significant. Titers become positive about 1 to 4 weeks after the chancre appears, so they can be negative in early primary syphilis. They peak in secondary syphilis, when sensitivity approaches 100%, fall after successful treatment and may become nonreactive, and decline slowly in untreated late disease. In secondary syphilis a very high antibody concentration can prevent the antigen lattice from forming in undiluted serum, giving a false negative that disappears on dilution (the prozone phenomenon)."},{"type":"p","text":"Because anticardiolipin antibodies arise whenever tissue is injured or autoimmunity is active, nontreponemal tests have biologic false positives: pregnancy, systemic lupus erythematosus and antiphospholipid syndrome, acute viral infections such as infectious mononucleosis, hepatitis, varicella, and HIV, as well as malaria, leprosy, injection drug use, and older age."},{"type":"p","text":"Treponemal tests, including the fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test (FTA-ABS), the T. pallidum particle agglutination assay (TP-PA), and automated enzyme or chemiluminescence immunoassays, detect antibody to T. pallidum antigens. They are more specific and turn positive at about the same time or slightly earlier, but in most patients they remain positive for life even after cure. They therefore confirm that infection has occurred but cannot show whether it is active or follow treatment. Cross-reactions occur with other treponemes (yaws, pinta, bejel) and sometimes with Lyme disease."},{"type":"table","title":"The two families of syphilis tests","cols":["Feature","Nontreponemal (VDRL, RPR)","Treponemal (FTA-ABS, TP-PA, EIA)"],"rows":[["Antigen","Cardiolipin, lecithin, cholesterol","T. pallidum proteins"],["Result","Quantitative titer","Reactive or nonreactive"],["Specificity","Lower; biologic false positives","Higher"],["After treatment","Titer falls, may become nonreactive","Usually stays positive for life"],["Main use","Screening and following activity","Confirming that infection occurred"]],"caption":"One family tracks disease activity; the other records lifetime exposure."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting paired syphilis results","text":"Pattern 1: RPR reactive at 1:64, treponemal test reactive. Both antibodies are present and the high titer signals activity, so this is untreated or recently treated syphilis. Pattern 2: RPR reactive at 1:2, treponemal test nonreactive. Anticardiolipin antibody without antibody to the organism is a biologic false positive; in a pregnant woman or a patient with lupus this is expected. Pattern 3: RPR nonreactive, treponemal test reactive. The person has been infected at some point; the usual explanation is past treated syphilis, but very early primary infection and untreated late disease, when nontreponemal titers wane, are alternatives. A painless chancre with both tests negative is still possible syphilis, because antibodies lag behind the lesion; dark-field examination of the ulcer can show spirochetes."},{"type":"p","text":"In the cerebrospinal fluid, a reactive VDRL is highly specific for neurosyphilis but often negative in true disease, so a negative result does not exclude it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C12","obj":"RE-13-K.12","title":"Congenital syphilis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Treponema pallidum crosses the placenta at any stage of pregnancy, and the risk of transmission is highest when the mother has primary, secondary, or early latent syphilis, the stages with the most spirochetes in her blood. Infection can cause miscarriage, stillbirth, hydrops fetalis, prematurity, or neonatal death. Many infected infants look normal at birth and develop signs over the following weeks. The placenta is often large and pale with villitis and endarteritis of villous vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings divide by timing. Early congenital syphilis, appearing in the first 2 years, reflects active infection with abundant organisms. Late congenital syphilis, appearing after 2 years, reflects scarring and maldevelopment of tissues injured during infancy and persistent low-grade inflammation, and its signs are therefore permanent."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in congenital syphilis","cols":["Finding","Timing","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Rhinitis (snuffles)","Early","Infected nasal mucosa; discharge full of spirochetes and infectious"],["Rash, often on palms and soles, with peeling or bullae","Early","Disseminated infection of skin"],["Hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, anemia, lymphadenopathy","Early","Hematogenous spread to liver, spleen, and nodes"],["Painful limbs with reluctance to move","Early","Osteochondritis and periostitis of long bones"],["Saddle nose","Late","Destruction of nasal cartilage and bone after rhinitis"],["Hutchinson teeth","Late","Damaged tooth buds; notched, widely spaced, peg-shaped upper central incisors"],["Mulberry molars","Late","Damaged first molar buds with many small poorly formed cusps"],["Saber shins","Late","Chronic periostitis causing anterior bowing of the tibia"],["Sensorineural deafness","Late","Damage to the eighth cranial nerve and inner ear"],["Interstitial keratitis","Late","Immune-mediated corneal inflammation and clouding"]],"caption":"Hutchinson teeth, interstitial keratitis, and eighth nerve deafness together form the Hutchinson triad; other late signs include frontal bossing, perioral scars (rhagades), and painless knee effusions."},{"type":"p","text":"The dental findings show the importance of timing. The crowns of the permanent incisors and first molars calcify during infancy, the period when early congenital infection is active, so inflammation around the developing tooth buds leaves deformities that are seen only years later when these teeth erupt."},{"type":"p","text":"Serologic diagnosis in the newborn is complicated by maternal IgG, which crosses the placenta, so a positive test in the infant may only reflect the mother. A nontreponemal titer fourfold or more above the mother's, or IgM antibody, which cannot cross the placenta, indicates that the infant itself is infected. Passively acquired antibody falls over the first months of life."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C13","obj":"RE-13-K.13","title":"Toxic shock syndrome: a superantigen disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A conventional protein antigen activates only the rare T cell clones whose receptors fit one processed peptide in the MHC groove, well under 0.01% of T cells. A superantigen skips processing. Toxic shock syndrome toxin 1 (TSST-1) from Staphylococcus aureus binds the outside of MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells and the V beta region of the T cell receptor directly, so every T cell with a matching V beta family is activated, up to about 20% of all T cells."},{"type":"flow","title":"From superantigen to shock","steps":["S. aureus grows locally and secretes TSST-1","Toxin crosses mucosa into blood","TSST-1 bridges MHC class II and T cell receptor V beta","Polyclonal T cell activation releases IL-2 and IFN-gamma","Macrophages release TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6","Vasodilation and capillary leak cause fever, hypotension, organ injury"],"caption":"The organism stays local; the toxin and the cytokine storm it triggers are systemic."},{"type":"p","text":"Menstrual toxic shock syndrome is linked to high-absorbency tampons, especially when left in place, which introduce oxygen and create a near-neutral, protein-rich environment that favors toxin production. Nonmenstrual cases follow nasal packing after surgery, surgical and burn wounds, postpartum and post-abortion infection, and skin and soft tissue infections. TSST-1 causes nearly all menstrual cases, while staphylococcal enterotoxins, especially B, cause many nonmenstrual cases. Most adults have neutralizing antibody to TSST-1, so disease occurs mainly in people who lack it."},{"type":"table","title":"Features of staphylococcal toxic shock syndrome","cols":["Feature","Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Fever","38.9 °C (102 °F) or higher","IL-1 and TNF reset the hypothalamic set point"],["Rash","Diffuse macular erythroderma like sunburn","Cytokine-driven cutaneous vasodilation"],["Desquamation","Palms and soles, about 1 to 2 weeks after onset","Late consequence of epidermal injury"],["Hypotension","Systolic pressure 90 mmHg or lower in adults","Vasodilation and capillary leak"],["Multiorgan involvement (3 or more systems)","Vomiting or diarrhea, myalgia with high creatine kinase, mucosal hyperemia, raised creatinine, raised bilirubin or transaminases, platelets below 100,000 per microliter, confusion","Hypoperfusion and direct cytokine injury"],["Blood cultures","Usually negative","Toxin, not organism, disseminates"]],"caption":"Thresholds follow the standard surveillance case definition."},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcal toxic shock syndrome, caused by Streptococcus pyogenes pyrogenic exotoxins that also act as superantigens, differs mainly in source and invasiveness. It usually arises from deep soft tissue infection such as necrotizing fasciitis, often with severe local pain, and blood cultures are frequently positive."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with menstrual toxic shock has profound hypotension, yet her blood cultures grow nothing. How can a local infection produce this?","explain":"The S. aureus remains in the vagina on the tampon. Only TSST-1 enters the blood, and because it activates a large fraction of all T cells without antigen processing, a small amount of toxin triggers a systemic release of IL-2, IFN-gamma, TNF, and IL-1 that dilates vessels and causes capillary leak.","check":"The superantigen paragraph and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-13-11-C14","obj":"RE-13-K.14","title":"Trichomoniasis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Trichomonas vaginalis is an anaerobic, pear-shaped flagellated protozoan with four anterior flagella and an undulating membrane. It exists only as a trophozoite and forms no cyst, so it cannot survive long outside the body and is transmitted almost entirely by sexual contact. It infects the squamous epithelium of the vagina and ectocervix, and the urethra and occasionally the prostate in men. It is the most common nonviral sexually transmitted infection worldwide."},{"type":"p","text":"The organism attaches to epithelial cells with surface adhesins and damages them by contact-dependent cytotoxicity and secreted cysteine proteases. The injured epithelium releases chemoattractants, and neutrophils flood the vagina. Inflammation of the ectocervix produces punctate subepithelial hemorrhages, the strawberry cervix; it is seen with the naked eye in only a small fraction of patients and more often on colposcopy. The infection disturbs the lactobacillus population, so pH rises above 4.5 and the whiff test is often positive."},{"type":"p","text":"Women develop a frothy, yellow-green, malodorous discharge with vulvovaginal itching and irritation, dysuria, and pain with intercourse, although many are asymptomatic. Most infected men have no symptoms and act as carriers; some have nongonococcal urethritis (C09). Infection does not produce lasting immunity, so reinfection from an untreated partner is common."},{"type":"table","title":"Trichomoniasis and bacterial vaginosis, both with pH above 4.5","cols":["Feature","Trichomoniasis","Bacterial vaginosis"],"rows":[["Cause","Sexually transmitted protozoan","Shift in vaginal bacterial flora"],["Discharge","Frothy, yellow-green","Thin, gray-white"],["Neutrophils","Many","Few"],["Wet mount","Motile flagellated trophozoites","Clue cells"],["Cervix","Strawberry cervix in some","Normal"],["Symptoms of inflammation","Itching, irritation, dysuria common","Usually absent"]],"caption":"Neutrophils and motile organisms mark the inflammatory infection; clue cells without neutrophils mark the flora shift."},{"type":"p","text":"On a saline wet mount, trophozoites are about the size of a neutrophil or slightly larger and move with a jerky, twitching motion. Motility fades within minutes as the specimen cools and dries, and the wet mount detects only about half to two thirds of infections, so nucleic acid amplification tests are preferred when available. Organisms may also be reported on a Pap smear. Infection in pregnancy is associated with preterm rupture of membranes, preterm birth, and low birth weight, and the mucosal inflammation and microulceration increase both acquisition and transmission of HIV."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and microbiology explain where each infection settles. Gonococci and Chlamydia attach to columnar epithelium, so they target the endocervix, urethra, and tubes and ascend to cause PID; Trichomonas and Candida favor stratified squamous epithelium of the vagina; HPV needs dividing basal cells at a transformation zone; LGV serovars and H. ducreyi reach regional nodes, whose drainage pattern predicts inguinal buboes or rectal disease. Lymphatic and venous drainage, placental transfer, and passage through the birth canal decide whether neonatal infection is congenital (syphilis) or acquired at delivery (gonorrhea, Chlamydia, HSV)."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and pathology explain most of the clinical picture. The difference between a painful neutrophilic ulcer and a painless plasma cell rich chancre, between a vaginosis with few neutrophils and a vaginitis with many, and between local infection and toxic shock is the host response. Antigenic variation explains gonococcal reinfection, terminal complement deficiency explains disseminated Neisseria, HLA-B27 shapes reactive arthritis, and the persistence of treponemal antibodies explains why only nontreponemal titers track activity. Bedside tests narrow the diagnosis, but each has limits: wet mounts miss trichomonads, serology lags behind early chancres, and coinfection is common, so one positive finding should prompt a search for others."}],"summary":["Chancroid (H. ducreyi) gives a painful, soft, ragged ulcer with gray exudate and suppurative nodes; primary syphilis gives a painless, indurated, clean chancre with rubbery nontender nodes.","Bacterial vaginosis is replacement of lactobacilli by Gardnerella and anaerobes: thin gray discharge, pH above 4.5, positive whiff test from amines, clue cells, few neutrophils.","pH separates Candida (normal pH, pseudohyphae on KOH) from bacterial vaginosis and trichomoniasis; neutrophils and motile trophozoites separate trichomoniasis from bacterial vaginosis.","C. trachomatis cycles between infectious elementary bodies and replicating reticulate bodies, is often silent, and scars the tubes through the immune response; neonatal conjunctivitis precedes afebrile staccato pneumonia.","LGV (L1 to L3) is lymphatic: a trivial painless lesion, then painful buboes or proctocolitis, and late strictures or elephantiasis.","Gonorrhea causes purulent urethritis with intracellular gram-negative diplococci, PID, hyperacute neonatal conjunctivitis, and disseminated arthritis with tenosynovitis and pustules; C5 to C9 deficiency predisposes to recurrent Neisseria infection.","HSV forms painful grouped vesicles with multinucleated giant cells, hides in sacral ganglia, and is most dangerous to the newborn when the mother acquires primary infection near delivery.","HPV 6 and 11 cause condylomata acuminata with koilocytes; high-risk HPV 16 and 18 use E6 against p53 and E7 against RB to cause anogenital and oropharyngeal carcinoma.","Syphilis progresses from chancre to a palm and sole rash with condylomata lata, to latency, and in a minority to gummas, ascending aortitis, and tabes dorsalis; VDRL and RPR titers track activity, while treponemal tests stay positive for life.","Toxic shock syndrome is TSST-1 bridging MHC class II and T cell receptor V beta, causing a cytokine storm with fever, hypotension, erythroderma, later palm and sole desquamation, and usually negative blood cultures."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, section on infections"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases, sections on sexually transmitted infections and syphilis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Sections on Neisseria, Chlamydia, spirochetes, Haemophilus, herpesviruses, papillomaviruses, and Staphylococcus"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes; complement system; superantigens"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases, section on sexually transmitted infections"}]},"RE-2":{"unit":"RE-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01","title":"Reproductive Gross and Microscopic Anatomy","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the cells and tissue zones of the testis, male ducts, female tract, ovary, breast, and prostate that board questions most often show on a slide or describe in a case. For each site you will learn what the cells look like, what they make, which hormone controls them, and which disease begins there. The organizing idea is that structure predicts disease: the blood-testis barrier explains antisperm antibodies, the transformation zone explains where cervical dysplasia starts, the two-layer breast duct separates in situ from invasive carcinoma, and prostate zone position explains why hyperplasia obstructs urine flow early while carcinoma is often silent.","prerequisites":["Epithelial classification (simple, stratified, pseudostratified; squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and apical specializations such as cilia and stereocilia","Cell junctions, especially tight junctions and gap junctions","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis: GnRH, LH, FSH, and negative feedback","Steroid synthesis from cholesterol, including aromatase and 5-alpha-reductase (RE-8)","Meiosis and the timing of meiotic arrest in oocytes and spermatocytes (RE-3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive embryology (RE-1)","how":"Sertoli cell AMH and Leydig cell testosterone direct male duct development, and the Wolffian and Mullerian ducts become the male and female tracts described here."},{"to":"Reproductive hormones and biochemistry (RE-7, RE-8)","how":"The two-cell models of the testis and ovary are the cellular basis of gonadotropin control and steroid output."},{"to":"Reproductive pathogens and immunology (RE-11, RE-12)","how":"High-risk HPV infects metaplastic cervical cells, and breach of the blood-testis barrier exposes sperm antigens to the immune system."},{"to":"Breast and prostate conditions (RE-13-E, RE-13-F, RE-13-J)","how":"Fibroadenoma, breast carcinoma, BPH, and prostate carcinoma are predicted by the cell layer or zone in which they begin."},{"to":"Pelvic anatomy (RE-4, RE-5)","how":"The course of the ductus deferens and the position of prostate zones relative to the urethra and rectum explain clinical findings."}],"terms":[{"term":"Blood-testis barrier","def":"Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells that separate the basal compartment of the seminiferous epithelium from the adluminal compartment."},{"term":"Androgen-binding protein (ABP)","def":"A Sertoli cell protein, induced by FSH, that binds testosterone and keeps its concentration high inside the seminiferous tubule and epididymis."},{"term":"Inhibin B","def":"A peptide hormone from Sertoli cells in men and granulosa cells in women that selectively suppresses FSH secretion by the pituitary."},{"term":"Stereocilia","def":"Long, nonmotile microvilli with an actin core that increase absorptive surface, found in the epididymis and ductus deferens."},{"term":"Transformation zone","def":"The area of the cervix between the original and current squamocolumnar junctions, where columnar epithelium is replaced by metaplastic squamous epithelium."},{"term":"Squamous metaplasia","def":"Replacement of one mature epithelium by stratified squamous epithelium, arising from reserve cells, as an adaptation to an irritating environment."},{"term":"Granulosa cells","def":"Follicular epithelial cells inside the follicle basement membrane that convert androgens to estradiol under FSH and later become granulosa lutein cells."},{"term":"Theca interna","def":"Vascular layer of steroid-secreting stromal cells outside the follicle basement membrane that produces androgens under LH."},{"term":"Terminal duct lobular unit (TDLU)","def":"A terminal duct with its lobule of acini and specialized intralobular stroma; the functional and disease-generating unit of the breast."},{"term":"Myoepithelial cell","def":"A contractile epithelial cell between luminal cells and the basement membrane of breast ducts and acini that ejects milk in response to oxytocin."},{"term":"Transition zone","def":"The small prostatic zone surrounding the proximal prostatic urethra, the site of benign prostatic hyperplasia."},{"term":"Peripheral zone","def":"The posterior and lateral prostatic zone, holding most of the glandular tissue, palpable on digital rectal exam, and the site of most carcinomas."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01-C01","obj":"RE-2.1","title":"Sertoli cells and Leydig cells of the testis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The testis has two working compartments. The seminiferous tubules make sperm, and the interstitium between them makes testosterone. Each tubule is lined by seminiferous epithelium (germ cells plus supporting Sertoli cells) on a basement membrane, ringed by contractile peritubular myoid cells that move immotile sperm toward the rete testis."},{"type":"h","text":"Sertoli cells: support, barrier, and FSH response"},{"type":"p","text":"Sertoli cells are tall columnar cells that span the full height of the epithelium, from basement membrane to lumen. On a slide they are recognized by a pale, oval or triangular, often folded nucleus with a prominent nucleolus, sitting near the basement membrane, and by indistinct cell borders. Tight junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells, just above the spermatogonia, form the blood-testis barrier. It divides the epithelium into a basal compartment, open to substances from the interstitial capillaries, and an adluminal compartment that only material crossing Sertoli cells can enter. Primary spermatocytes move across the barrier early in meiosis, so every meiotic and haploid cell, which carries antigens that appeared long after immune tolerance was established, is sequestered from immune cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Sertoli cells carry the FSH receptors and the androgen receptors that drive spermatogenesis; germ cells themselves lack androgen receptors and depend on Sertoli cells to relay the testosterone signal. In response, Sertoli cells secrete androgen-binding protein, which holds intratubular testosterone far above the serum level, and inhibin B, which feeds back on the pituitary to lower FSH. They also secrete tubular fluid, phagocytose the residual cytoplasm shed by maturing spermatids, aromatize some testosterone to estradiol, and in the fetus secrete anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH)."},{"type":"h","text":"Leydig cells: testosterone under LH"},{"type":"p","text":"Leydig (interstitial) cells lie in clusters in the loose connective tissue between tubules, beside capillaries into which they secrete. They are polygonal with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, lipid droplets, round nuclei, and, in humans, rod-shaped Reinke crystals. Their abundant smooth endoplasmic reticulum and mitochondria with tubular cristae mark them as steroid-producing cells. LH binds Leydig cell receptors, raises cAMP, and increases cholesterol transport into mitochondria by steroidogenic acute regulatory protein (StAR), the rate-limiting step in testosterone synthesis."},{"type":"table","title":"Sertoli versus Leydig cells","cols":["Feature","Sertoli cell","Leydig cell"],"rows":[["Location","Inside seminiferous tubules, basement membrane to lumen","Interstitium between tubules, near capillaries"],["Histology","Pale folded nucleus with prominent nucleolus; indistinct borders","Eosinophilic polygonal cells, lipid droplets, Reinke crystals"],["Trophic hormone","FSH (and testosterone)","LH"],["Main products","Androgen-binding protein, inhibin B, tubular fluid, AMH (fetal)","Testosterone"],["Feedback target","Inhibin B lowers FSH","Testosterone lowers LH and GnRH"],["Heat sensitivity","High, with germ cells","Relatively resistant"],["Tumor clue","Sertoli cell tumor","Leydig cell tumor with Reinke crystals; androgen or estrogen excess"]],"caption":"The two cells divide spermatogenic support from endocrine output, and each has its own gonadotropin and feedback signal."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Split feedback of the testis","steps":["Hypothalamic GnRH pulses","Pituitary releases LH and FSH","LH drives Leydig testosterone; FSH drives Sertoli function","Testosterone suppresses GnRH and LH","Inhibin B selectively suppresses FSH","Gonadotropin release adjusts"],"caption":"Leydig cell output controls LH and Sertoli cell output controls FSH, so a lab pattern can localize damage to one cell type."},{"type":"p","text":"This split explains classic patterns. Spermatogenesis needs a scrotal temperature about 2 to 3 °C (about 4 to 5 °F) below core. In an undescended testis, heat damages germ and Sertoli cells, so sperm count and inhibin B fall and FSH rises, while Leydig cells keep testosterone near normal. When the barrier is breached by trauma, orchitis, or vasectomy, exposed sperm antigens can provoke antisperm antibodies."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man has a selective rise in serum FSH with normal LH and testosterone. Which testicular cell is failing?","explain":"The Sertoli cell and its germ cells. Less inhibin B removes the brake on FSH, while intact Leydig cells keep testosterone, and therefore LH, normal.","check":"Compare with the Sertoli versus Leydig table and the split feedback cycle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01-C02","obj":"RE-2.2","title":"The path of sperm from tubule to urethra","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sperm released into the tubule lumen are immotile and cannot yet fertilize. The duct system carries them through sites of maturation, storage, and propulsion, and each segment's lining fits its job. The sequence is fixed, so obstruction at any point stops sperm from reaching the ejaculate even though the testis keeps working."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of sperm","steps":["Seminiferous tubules","Straight tubules (tubuli recti)","Rete testis in the mediastinum testis","Efferent ductules","Epididymis: head, body, tail","Ductus deferens and its ampulla","Ejaculatory duct through the prostate","Prostatic, membranous, then spongy urethra"],"caption":"Sperm pass from the testis through the excurrent ducts and exit by the three parts of the male urethra."},{"type":"table","title":"Lining and role of each segment","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Main role"],"rows":[["Straight tubules","Sertoli cells, then simple cuboidal","Connect tubules to rete testis"],["Rete testis","Simple cuboidal to low columnar","Anastomosing channels in the mediastinum"],["Efferent ductules","Alternating ciliated columnar and nonciliated cuboidal cells (scalloped lumen)","Absorb most tubular fluid; cilia move sperm to the epididymal head"],["Epididymis","Pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia","Absorption, maturation, storage in the tail"],["Ductus deferens","Pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia; thick three-layer smooth muscle","Rapid propulsion during emission"],["Prostatic urethra","Urothelium (transitional)","Receives ejaculatory and prostatic ducts"],["Membranous urethra","Pseudostratified or stratified columnar","Passes through the external urethral sphincter"],["Spongy urethra","Pseudostratified or stratified columnar; stratified squamous at the navicular fossa","Receives bulbourethral gland ducts"]],"caption":"Epithelium changes from absorptive and ciliated in the testis outflow to muscular conduits and then urethral linings."},{"type":"p","text":"The epididymis is a single, tightly coiled duct several meters long. Its principal cells absorb fluid through stereocilia, which concentrates sperm, and secrete proteins that remodel the sperm membrane. During several days of transit sperm acquire forward motility and the ability to bind the zona pellucida, and they are stored in the tail. Final activation, called capacitation, occurs later in the female tract."},{"type":"p","text":"The ductus deferens begins at the epididymal tail, ascends in the spermatic cord, passes through the inguinal canal from the superficial to the deep inguinal ring, and descends on the lateral pelvic wall. It crosses superior to the ureter, widens into the ampulla behind the bladder, and joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct. Each ejaculatory duct traverses the prostate and opens on the seminal colliculus in the posterior wall of the prostatic urethra. During emission, sympathetic fibers contract the ductal smooth muscle; during ejaculation, rhythmic contraction of the bulbospongiosus expels semen."},{"type":"p","text":"Most semen volume does not come from the testis. Seminal vesicles contribute the largest share, an alkaline, fructose-rich fluid, and the prostate adds a thin, slightly acidic fluid with citrate, zinc, and PSA. Ductal obstruction therefore produces a characteristic pattern: azoospermia with normal testosterone, LH, and FSH. In congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, associated with CFTR mutations, the seminal vesicles are usually also abnormal, so the ejaculate is low in volume, acidic, and lacks fructose."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: obstructive or testicular azoospermia?","text":"Two men have no sperm in the ejaculate. Man A has normal testosterone, LH, and FSH, normal testis size, and low semen volume with low fructose. Man B has small testes, high FSH, low inhibin B, and normal semen volume. In A the testes are working, because normal FSH means inhibin B is normal, so sperm are made but blocked; low volume and fructose place the block at the ejaculatory ducts or at absent vasa and seminal vesicles. In B the Sertoli and germ cell compartment has failed, so inhibin B falls and FSH rises; the ducts are patent, but nothing is entering them. The hormone pattern localizes the problem."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01-C03","obj":"RE-2.3","title":"Epithelia of the female tract and the cervical transformation zone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The female tract changes its lining along its length to match local demands. Upper segments transport gametes and support implantation, so they are lined by simple columnar epithelium with cilia or glands. Lower segments face friction during intercourse and childbirth, so they are lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. The meeting point of these two epithelia on the cervix is the most important site of epithelial disease in the tract."},{"type":"table","title":"Female tract epithelia","cols":["Segment","Epithelium","Functional point"],"rows":[["Uterine tube","Simple columnar: ciliated cells and nonciliated secretory (peg) cells on highly folded mucosa","Cilia beat toward the uterus; peg cells nourish gametes and early embryo"],["Endometrium","Simple columnar surface with simple tubular glands in cellular stroma","Functional layer sheds monthly; basal layer regenerates it"],["Endocervix","Simple columnar mucus-secreting cells lining branched crypts","Mucus changes with the cycle; blocked crypts form Nabothian cysts"],["Ectocervix","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Continuous with vaginal lining"],["Vagina","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous, glycogen-rich; no glands","Lactobacilli ferment glycogen breakdown products to lactic acid (pH about 3.8 to 4.5)"]],"caption":"Columnar linings serve transport and secretion; squamous linings resist abrasion."},{"type":"p","text":"The uterine tube has a muscular wall and a mucosa that is most elaborately folded in the ampulla, the usual site of fertilization. Estrogen increases ciliation and secretory activity. Because ciliary beating, aided by peristalsis, moves the embryo toward the uterus, scarring and loss of cilia after salpingitis delay transport and raise the risk of tubal ectopic pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"The endometrium has a functional layer, supplied by spiral arteries and shed at menstruation, and a basal layer, supplied by straight arteries and retained to regenerate the next functional layer. In the proliferative phase, estrogen produces straight, narrow glands with mitoses; in the secretory phase, progesterone produces coiled glands with glycogen vacuoles and an edematous stroma. Destruction of the basal layer, as by aggressive curettage, prevents regeneration."},{"type":"h","text":"The transformation zone"},{"type":"p","text":"The squamocolumnar junction is where endocervical columnar epithelium meets ectocervical squamous epithelium. Its position moves. At puberty, in pregnancy, and with estrogen exposure, the cervix enlarges and everts, exposing columnar epithelium on the ectocervix (ectropion). In the acidic vagina, subcolumnar reserve cells undergo squamous metaplasia, and a new junction forms closer to the os. The band between the original and the new junction is the transformation zone. After menopause the junction recedes into the endocervical canal, which is why sampling must reach into the canal in older women."},{"type":"flow","title":"From transformation zone to cervical dysplasia","steps":["Eversion exposes columnar epithelium to acidic vaginal pH","Reserve cells undergo squamous metaplasia","High-risk HPV enters immature metaplastic basal cells","E6 promotes p53 degradation; E7 inactivates Rb","Cell-cycle checkpoints are lost; mutations accumulate","Dysplasia (CIN) begins in the transformation zone"],"caption":"Actively dividing metaplastic cells are the most vulnerable target for high-risk HPV types such as 16 and 18."},{"type":"p","text":"Immature metaplastic cells divide actively, and HPV reaches the basal layer through microabrasions, so this zone is where nearly all cervical squamous dysplasia and carcinoma arise. Endocervical adenocarcinoma also arises near the junction from glandular cells. Cytologic and HPV screening sample the transformation zone for this reason."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why might a cervical sample from a 65-year-old miss dysplasia if it scrapes only the ectocervix?","explain":"After menopause the squamocolumnar junction and transformation zone retract into the endocervical canal, so an ectocervical sample may contain only mature squamous cells.","check":"Reread the paragraph on how the squamocolumnar junction moves with age."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01-C04","obj":"RE-2.4","title":"Ovarian follicles and the corpus luteum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The ovary has a cortex of follicles in cellular stroma and a vascular medulla, covered by simple cuboidal surface epithelium. Every follicle surrounds a primary oocyte arrested in the diplotene stage of prophase I, an arrest that can last from fetal life to menopause. Follicle stages are named by changes in the layers around that oocyte: granulosa cells inside the follicular basement membrane, and theca cells that condense from stroma outside it."},{"type":"table","title":"Follicle stages","cols":["Stage","Oocyte","Granulosa cells","Theca and other features"],"rows":[["Primordial","Primary oocyte in prophase I","One layer of flat (squamous) cells","No theca; gonadotropin independent"],["Primary","Primary oocyte enlarges; zona pellucida forms","Cuboidal, first one layer then several","Stromal cells begin to organize as theca"],["Secondary (antral)","Primary oocyte","Many layers with fluid spaces merging into an antrum","Theca interna (vascular, steroid-making) and theca externa (fibrous)"],["Mature (Graafian)","Completes meiosis I after the LH surge; secondary oocyte arrests in metaphase II","Oocyte on the cumulus oophorus, ringed by corona radiata","Large antrum; follicle about 2 cm, bulging at the surface"],["Corpus luteum","Released at ovulation","Large, pale granulosa lutein cells","Smaller, darker theca lutein cells at the periphery; progesterone and estradiol"]],"caption":"Each stage adds granulosa layers, then fluid, then a theca; luteinization converts both layers into a progesterone-secreting gland."},{"type":"p","text":"In the primary follicle the oocyte and granulosa cells secrete the zona pellucida, a glycoprotein coat that later binds sperm. Granulosa cell processes cross the zona and connect to the oocyte by gap junctions, which deliver nutrients and keep the oocyte arrested. Granulosa cells have no blood vessels, because capillaries stop at the basement membrane in the theca interna. Most follicles never ovulate; they undergo atresia, with apoptosis of granulosa cells and collapse of the oocyte."},{"type":"p","text":"Estradiol synthesis requires both cell types, because each lacks an enzyme the other has. Theca interna cells have LH receptors and 17-alpha-hydroxylase activity (CYP17) and make androgens, but they lack aromatase. Granulosa cells lack CYP17 and cannot make androgens, but FSH induces aromatase in them."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two-cell, two-gonadotropin estradiol synthesis","steps":["LH binds theca interna receptors","Theca cells convert cholesterol to androstenedione","Androgen diffuses across the basement membrane","FSH induces aromatase in granulosa cells","Granulosa cells aromatize the androgen to estrogen","Estradiol enters follicular fluid and blood"],"caption":"Theca supplies the androgen substrate and granulosa supplies aromatase; neither alone makes much estradiol."},{"type":"p","text":"After ovulation the basement membrane breaks down, capillaries invade the former granulosa layer, and LH luteinizes both cell types. The new blood supply delivers LDL cholesterol, which allows high-volume progesterone output. Granulosa lutein cells, the larger and paler majority, make progesterone and, with aromatase, estradiol; theca lutein cells make androgens and progesterone. Without hCG the corpus luteum regresses after about 14 days into the corpus albicans, a white collagen scar. In pregnancy, hCG from the trophoblast maintains it until placental progesterone takes over at about 7 to 9 weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"Recognizing these cells explains several ovarian lesions. Granulosa cell tumors secrete estrogen and may show small rosettes around eosinophilic material (Call-Exner bodies). In polycystic ovary syndrome, multiple small antral follicles and hyperplastic theca produce excess androgen. A follicular cyst is an unruptured follicle; a corpus luteum cyst can bleed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01-C05","obj":"RE-2.5","title":"The terminal duct lobular unit of the breast","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The adult breast is a modified apocrine sweat gland made of about 15 to 20 lobes embedded in fat and dense fibrous stroma. Each lobe drains through a lactiferous duct that widens into a lactiferous sinus beneath the nipple. Moving away from the nipple, ducts branch into segmental and subsegmental ducts and end in terminal ducts, each of which feeds a lobule of small blind-ended ductules called acini."},{"type":"p","text":"The terminal duct and its lobule, together with the loose, hormonally responsive intralobular stroma around the acini, form the terminal duct lobular unit (TDLU). This is the secretory unit of the breast and the unit that responds to each menstrual cycle, to pregnancy, and to menopause. The surrounding interlobular stroma is denser and less cellular."},{"type":"h","text":"Two cell layers"},{"type":"p","text":"Every duct and acinus, from the large ducts to the acini, is lined by two cell layers on a basement membrane. The inner luminal cells are cuboidal to columnar; a subset expresses estrogen and progesterone receptors, and in lactation these cells secrete milk proteins and lipid. The outer myoepithelial cells are contractile and squeeze milk toward the nipple in response to oxytocin. They express smooth muscle actin, calponin, and the nuclear protein p63, markers pathologists stain for, and they help maintain the basement membrane."},{"type":"table","title":"Where breast lesions begin","cols":["Lesion","Cell of origin","Myoepithelial layer"],"rows":[["Ductal and lobular carcinoma in situ","TDLU luminal epithelium","Present around involved spaces"],["Invasive ductal (no special type) and lobular carcinoma","TDLU luminal epithelium","Absent around invading nests"],["Fibroadenoma","Intralobular stroma of the TDLU, with entrapped ducts","Present in the compressed ducts"],["Phyllodes tumor","Intralobular stroma, more cellular","Present in the epithelial clefts"],["Intraductal papilloma","Usually large ducts beneath the nipple (solitary papilloma)","Present along the papillary cores"],["Sclerosing adenosis","Proliferating acini and stroma of the TDLU","Present, which separates it from carcinoma"]],"caption":"The cell layer of origin and the fate of the myoepithelial layer classify most breast lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Most breast carcinomas, both ductal and lobular, arise from luminal cells of the TDLU. The names ductal and lobular describe the growth pattern and cell biology (lobular carcinoma lacks E-cadherin), not the anatomic site. Fibroadenoma arises from the specialized intralobular stroma, so it is biphasic: proliferating stroma with compressed but normal two-layered ducts. It is common in young women because the intralobular stroma is hormone responsive. The main exception is solitary intraductal papilloma, which usually arises in the large ducts beneath the nipple."},{"type":"p","text":"The myoepithelial layer is the key to invasion. While neoplastic luminal cells stay inside the basement membrane, myoepithelial cells still surround them and the lesion is in situ. Invasive carcinoma has crossed the basement membrane into stroma with no myoepithelial rim. Benign mimics such as sclerosing adenosis keep the layer even when the glands look crowded and distorted."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the myoepithelial stain","text":"A breast biopsy shows crowded, angulated glands in dense stroma. To decide whether they are invasive, the pathologist stains for p63 and smooth muscle myosin, which mark myoepithelial cells. If each gland is ringed by brown p63-positive nuclei, the two-layer structure is intact and the basement membrane has not been breached, which fits sclerosing adenosis or an in situ lesion. If the glands have no outer ring of p63-positive nuclei, epithelial cells have escaped the basement membrane into stroma, which is invasive carcinoma. The same structural logic applies to prostate basal cells."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-02-01-C06","obj":"RE-2.6","title":"Prostate zones, hyperplasia, and carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The prostate surrounds the first part of the urethra below the bladder neck. Its glands are tubuloalveolar, with luminal secretory cells over a layer of flat basal cells, embedded in fibromuscular stroma. The secretory cells make the prostatic component of semen, including PSA and acid phosphatase, and their lumens may contain lamellated corpora amylacea. Lobes such as the median lobe are still used clinically, but pathology and imaging use zones, because each zone has a characteristic disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Prostate zones","cols":["Zone","Position","Share of glandular tissue (young adult)","Disease link"],"rows":[["Peripheral","Posterior, lateral, and apical; lies against the rectum","About 70%","Most carcinomas (about 70%)"],["Central","Cone around the ejaculatory ducts at the base","About 25%","Seldom diseased; few carcinomas"],["Transition","Two small lobes around the proximal prostatic urethra","About 5%","Benign prostatic hyperplasia; some carcinomas"],["Anterior fibromuscular stroma","Anterior surface","No glands","Rarely involved"]],"caption":"Zone position relative to the urethra and rectum determines what a lesion does and how it is found."},{"type":"p","text":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is nodular hyperplasia of glands and stroma arising in the transition zone and the periurethral tissue. Its driver is dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Stromal cells express type 2 5-alpha-reductase, convert testosterone to DHT, and DHT acting on androgen receptors in stromal and epithelial cells increases growth factor production. Because the transition zone wraps the urethra, even modest growth compresses it. Increased smooth muscle tone in the hyperplastic stroma, under alpha-1 adrenergic control, adds a dynamic component to obstruction."},{"type":"flow","title":"From DHT to urinary obstruction in BPH","steps":["Testosterone enters prostatic stromal cells","Type 2 5-alpha-reductase forms DHT","DHT increases growth factor signaling","Transition zone nodules of stroma and glands enlarge","Prostatic urethra is compressed; stromal smooth muscle tone adds","Hesitancy, weak stream, retention; bladder wall hypertrophies"],"caption":"Hyperplasia in the transition zone narrows the urethra, and the bladder works harder to empty against it."},{"type":"p","text":"Obstruction explains the manifestations. The bladder detrusor hypertrophies and becomes trabeculated, diverticula form, residual urine predisposes to infection and stones, and sustained retention can cause hydronephrosis. Gland size on rectal exam correlates poorly with symptoms, because a small amount of periurethral growth can obstruct. BPH arises in a different zone from most carcinomas and is not considered a precursor of cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Prostatic adenocarcinoma arises mostly in the peripheral zone, often multifocally, with high-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia as its precursor. Because the peripheral zone lies against the rectum and away from the urethra, carcinoma is often found as a firm or hard nodule on rectal exam and causes urinary symptoms only when advanced. Histologically, malignant glands lack the basal cell layer, which pathologists confirm with basal cell stains such as p63 and high-molecular-weight cytokeratin, and they often overexpress AMACR. PSA rises in both BPH and carcinoma, so it reflects prostate tissue rather than cancer specifically."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can a large peripheral zone carcinoma be silent while a small transition zone nodule causes urinary retention?","explain":"The transition zone surrounds the urethra, so small growth compresses it; the peripheral zone is posterior and lateral, so a tumor there must grow large or invade before it narrows the urethra.","check":"Compare with the prostate zones table and the BPH flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Across this chapter, a single structural fact explains a disease pattern. Tight junctions between Sertoli cells sequester antigens that arose after tolerance was established, so trauma, mumps orchitis, or vasectomy can produce antisperm antibodies. Embryology sets the plan: Sertoli cell AMH removes the Mullerian ducts and Leydig cell testosterone preserves the Wolffian ducts that become the epididymis, ductus deferens, and seminal vesicles. Biochemistry divides labor between cells, with theca androgens and granulosa aromatase in the ovary and stromal 5-alpha-reductase driving prostatic growth."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows the cell layer. Metaplastic cells at the cervical transformation zone are the target of high-risk HPV, and the oncoproteins E6 and E7 link microbiology to loss of p53 and Rb control. In breast and prostate, the outer cell layer (myoepithelial or basal) separates benign and in situ lesions from invasive carcinoma. Zone position explains symptoms in the prostate. These rules have limits: some carcinomas arise outside the expected zone, epithelial boundaries shift with age and hormones, and immunostains support but do not replace the whole histologic picture."}],"summary":["Sertoli cells span the seminiferous epithelium, form the blood-testis barrier with tight junctions, respond to FSH, and secrete androgen-binding protein, inhibin B, and fetal AMH.","Leydig cells in the interstitium respond to LH and make testosterone; they are eosinophilic, lipid-rich, and may contain Reinke crystals.","Inhibin B selectively suppresses FSH and testosterone suppresses LH, so a selective FSH rise points to Sertoli and germ cell failure.","Sperm travel through seminiferous tubules, straight tubules, rete testis, efferent ductules, epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, and the prostatic, membranous, and spongy urethra.","Ductal obstruction gives azoospermia with normal hormones; absent vasa and seminal vesicles give low-volume, acidic, fructose-negative semen.","The uterine tube is ciliated simple columnar, the endometrium and endocervix are simple columnar, and the ectocervix and vagina are nonkeratinized stratified squamous.","The transformation zone is metaplastic epithelium between the old and new squamocolumnar junctions, where HPV-driven dysplasia begins; it moves into the canal after menopause.","Follicles progress from flat to cuboidal to multilayered granulosa, then antrum formation; theca interna makes androgens under LH, and granulosa aromatizes them under FSH.","Most breast carcinomas and fibroadenomas arise in the TDLU, carcinoma from luminal epithelium and fibroadenoma from intralobular stroma; loss of myoepithelial cells marks invasion.","BPH arises in the transition zone around the urethra and obstructs early; most prostate carcinomas arise in the peripheral zone, are palpable, and lack basal cells."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"The Male Reproductive System; The Female Reproductive System"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System; The Female Genital Tract; The Breast"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male; Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The Male Reproductive System; The Female Reproductive System"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum, male and female internal genital organs"}]},"RE-3":{"unit":"RE-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01","title":"Gametogenesis, Fertilization, Implantation, and Early Embryogenesis","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows human development from the germ cell to the end of the embryonic period. It explains how sperm and oocytes are made and why their meiotic schedules differ, how a sperm is remodeled and activated, how fertilization blocks polyspermy and restores diploidy, and how the blastocyst implants and rescues the corpus luteum with hCG. It then applies these events to ectopic pregnancy and placenta previa, and ends with the bilaminar and trilaminar discs and the timing of teratogen sensitivity. Board questions usually name an arrest point, a cell layer, a day, or a risk factor and ask you to reason to the mechanism or the consequence.","prerequisites":["Phases of mitosis and meiosis, crossing over, and cyclin and CDK control of the cell cycle","Hormonal control of the ovarian and menstrual cycles, including the LH surge and the corpus luteum (RE-6, RE-7)","Histology of the seminiferous tubule, Sertoli and Leydig cells, and ovarian follicles (RE-2)","Gross anatomy of the uterine tube, uterus, cervix, and rectouterine pouch (RE-2, RE-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive, placental, and breast embryology (RE-1)","how":"The trophoblast layers that implant in week 1 and 2 go on to build the chorionic villi and the placenta."},{"to":"Reproductive hormones (RE-7)","how":"The LH surge releases the first oocyte arrest, and hCG acting on the LH receptor keeps the corpus luteum alive in early pregnancy."},{"to":"Reproductive genetics (RE-10)","how":"Nondisjunction during the long oocyte arrest explains the rise in trisomy with maternal age."},{"to":"Diseases of the placenta (RE-13-H)","how":"Errors at fertilization produce complete and partial hydatidiform moles, and abnormal implantation sites produce previa and accreta."},{"to":"Reproductive pathogens and inflammatory disease (RE-12, RE-13-B)","how":"Chlamydial and gonococcal salpingitis damage tubal cilia and are the classic setup for tubal ectopic pregnancy."}],"terms":[{"term":"Polar body","def":"A small cell produced by the unequal division of an oocyte that receives a chromosome set but almost no cytoplasm and then degenerates."},{"term":"Spermiogenesis","def":"The remodeling of a round spermatid into a spermatozoon, without cell division."},{"term":"Capacitation","def":"The conditioning of sperm in the female tract, by removal of membrane cholesterol and surface glycoproteins, that makes them able to undergo the acrosome reaction."},{"term":"Acrosome reaction","def":"Calcium-triggered exocytosis of the acrosome after the sperm binds the zona pellucida, releasing enzymes and exposing proteins needed for fusion."},{"term":"Cortical reaction","def":"Exocytosis of oocyte cortical granules after sperm fusion, which modifies the zona pellucida so no further sperm can bind or enter."},{"term":"Morula","def":"A solid ball of about 16 or more blastomeres formed by cleavage, about day 3 after fertilization."},{"term":"Blastocyst","def":"The fluid-filled conceptus of days 4 to 6 with an inner cell mass (embryoblast) and an outer trophoblast."},{"term":"Syncytiotrophoblast","def":"The outer, multinucleated, nondividing trophoblast layer that invades the endometrium and secretes hCG."},{"term":"Decidua","def":"Endometrium of pregnancy, whose stromal cells enlarge and store glycogen and lipid in response to progesterone (decidual reaction)."},{"term":"Epiblast","def":"The dorsal layer of the bilaminar disc, which gives rise to all three germ layers of the embryo."},{"term":"Gastrulation","def":"The process in week 3 by which epiblast cells move through the primitive streak to form ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm."},{"term":"Embryonic period","def":"Weeks 3 to 8 after fertilization, when organ primordia form and structural malformations are most easily induced."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01-C01","obj":"RE-3.1","title":"Spermatogenesis and oogenesis compared","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both sexes use meiosis to halve the chromosome number and recombine alleles by crossing over, but the programs differ in when they start, whether they pause, and how they divide the cytoplasm."},{"type":"p","text":"Spermatogenesis begins at puberty in the seminiferous tubules. Type A spermatogonia on the basement membrane divide by mitosis to renew themselves and to produce type B spermatogonia, which become primary spermatocytes. These move past the Sertoli cell tight junctions (the blood-testis barrier) into the adluminal compartment, finish meiosis I to form two secondary spermatocytes, and finish meiosis II almost at once to form four spermatids. Incomplete cytokinesis leaves each clone joined by cytoplasmic bridges, so it matures in step. The sequence takes about 64 to 74 days and depends on FSH acting on Sertoli cells and on a high local testosterone made by Leydig cells under LH."},{"type":"p","text":"Oogenesis is front-loaded into fetal life. Oogonia multiply by mitosis to a peak of about 7 million by month 5 of gestation, then enter meiosis I and stop in the diplotene stage of prophase I. Each primary oocyte, wrapped in flat follicle cells, forms a primordial follicle. Atresia leaves about 1 to 2 million at birth and about 400,000 at puberty; only about 400 to 500 are ovulated, and none are made after birth."},{"type":"p","text":"The prophase I arrest is held by the follicle. Granulosa cells pass cGMP to the oocyte through gap junctions; cGMP inhibits the oocyte phosphodiesterase, so cAMP stays high and maturation promoting factor (cyclin B with CDK1) stays inactive. The LH surge closes these junctions and lowers cGMP, cAMP falls, and the oocyte finishes meiosis I in the hours before ovulation. The division is unequal: a large secondary oocyte and a tiny first polar body. The secondary oocyte then stops at metaphase II, held by cytostatic factor (Mos and MAPK signaling, with Emi2 inhibiting the anaphase promoting complex), and finishes meiosis II only if a sperm fuses with it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Oocyte from fetal ovary to zygote","steps":["Oogonia divide by mitosis in the fetal ovary","Primary oocytes arrest in prophase I before birth","LH surge: meiosis I completes; first polar body forms","Secondary oocyte arrests in metaphase II and is ovulated","Sperm fusion: meiosis II completes; second polar body forms","Parental chromosomes join as a diploid zygote"],"caption":"The first arrest can last about five decades; the second lasts only until fertilization or until the oocyte degenerates about a day later."},{"type":"table","title":"Spermatogenesis versus oogenesis","cols":["Feature","Spermatogenesis","Oogenesis"],"rows":[["Onset","Puberty, then continuous","Fetal life; completed cyclically after puberty"],["Stem cell pool","Spermatogonia self-renew for life","None after birth; fixed, declining pool"],["Meiosis I","No pause","Arrested in prophase I (diplotene) until the LH surge"],["Meiosis II","No pause","Arrested in metaphase II until sperm fusion"],["Cytokinesis","Equal, with cytoplasmic bridges","Unequal; cytoplasm kept by one cell"],["Functional gametes per meiosis","Four spermatozoa","One ovum plus two or three polar bodies"],["Age-related error","De novo point mutations","Nondisjunction"]],"caption":"The first polar body may or may not divide again."},{"type":"p","text":"Unequal division keeps mitochondria, stored mRNAs, and nutrients in one cell to support the embryo until its own genome switches on. The long arrest has a cost: cohesin holding homologs together erodes over decades, so meiosis I nondisjunction and trisomy rise with maternal age. Continuous spermatogonial division instead raises de novo point mutations with paternal age (sporadic achondroplasia)."},{"type":"example","title":"Meiosis I or meiosis II nondisjunction","text":"A child with trisomy 21 carries two different maternal chromosome 21 homologs (distinguishable near the centromere). Homologs separate in meiosis I, so failure there sends both different homologs into the gamete; sister chromatids separate in meiosis II, so failure there gives two identical copies. Two different maternal homologs therefore mean a maternal meiosis I error, the most common origin of trisomy 21, because female meiosis I spans the decades-long arrest while male meiosis I lasts weeks."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01-C02","obj":"RE-3.2","title":"Spermiogenesis, capacitation, and the acrosome reaction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Spermiogenesis converts a round haploid spermatid into a streamlined spermatozoon with no further cell division. Each new structure comes from an existing organelle, so a defect in that organelle predicts a specific sperm defect. The process occurs while spermatids are embedded in Sertoli cells, which then release the mature cells into the lumen (spermiation)."},{"type":"table","title":"Structures built during spermiogenesis","cols":["Structure","Origin","Function","Defect"],"rows":[["Acrosome","Golgi vesicles that fuse into a cap over the nucleus","Lysosome-like store of hyaluronidase and acrosin for crossing the zona","Absent acrosome (round-headed sperm) cannot fertilize"],["Flagellum (axoneme, 9 + 2)","Distal centriole","Dynein-driven beating for motility","Missing dynein arms (primary ciliary dyskinesia) give immotile sperm"],["Midpiece","Mitochondria gathered in a helical sheath","ATP for flagellar beating","Low motility"],["Condensed nucleus","Histones replaced by protamines","Small, protected, transcriptionally silent DNA","DNA fragmentation"],["Residual body","Excess cytoplasm shed","Streamlining; phagocytosed by Sertoli cells","Retained cytoplasmic droplet"]],"caption":"Golgi makes the cap, the centriole makes the tail, and mitochondria power it."},{"type":"p","text":"Testicular sperm are not yet able to swim forward or fertilize. They gain progressive motility during transit through the epididymis, where glycoproteins and cholesterol from epididymal and seminal fluid also coat the membrane and stabilize it. That stabilization protects sperm from releasing the acrosome too early, but it means freshly ejaculated sperm cannot fertilize an oocyte."},{"type":"p","text":"Capacitation is the removal of this restraint in the female tract, mainly in the uterus and uterine tube, over several hours (about 7 hours in humans). Albumin in tubal fluid extracts cholesterol, raising membrane fluidity, and surface glycoproteins are stripped. Bicarbonate entry activates soluble adenylyl cyclase, so cAMP and protein kinase A rise and many proteins become tyrosine phosphorylated. Intracellular alkalinization opens the sperm-specific CatSper Ca2+ channel, and the Ca2+ rise produces hyperactivated motility, a vigorous asymmetric beat that drives the sperm through the cumulus."},{"type":"p","text":"The acrosome reaction occurs only in a capacitated sperm. It is classically triggered by binding to the zona pellucida (ZP3 in teaching texts), and progesterone from cumulus cells can also trigger it. Ca2+ influx fuses the outer acrosomal membrane with the plasma membrane at many points, releasing acrosin and other enzymes that digest a narrow path through the zona. The reaction also exposes Izumo1 on the equatorial segment, which binds Juno on the oocyte membrane and is required for gamete fusion. A sperm that reacts too early, before reaching the zona, loses its enzymes and cannot fertilize."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ejaculate to zona penetration","steps":["Epididymal transit: forward motility; membrane coated and stabilized","Female tract: cholesterol and glycoproteins removed (capacitation)","HCO3-, cAMP, and CatSper Ca2+ produce hyperactivated motility","Sperm passes the corona radiata and binds the zona","Ca2+ influx triggers acrosomal exocytosis","Enzymes digest a path; Izumo1 is exposed for fusion"],"caption":"Capacitation happens in the female tract; the acrosome reaction happens at the zona pellucida."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01-C03","obj":"RE-3.3","title":"Fertilization, the block to polyspermy, and completion of meiosis II","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla of the uterine tube, its widest and longest segment, where the tubal fluid and the ovulated cumulus-oocyte complex meet ascending sperm. The secondary oocyte remains fertilizable for only about 12 to 24 hours after ovulation, whereas sperm survive in the female tract for a few days, so most conceptions follow intercourse in the days just before ovulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Fertilization proceeds in three barriers. Hyperactivated sperm push through the corona radiata, aided by surface hyaluronidase that loosens the cumulus matrix. Zona binding triggers the acrosome reaction, and acrosomal enzymes cut a path through the zona pellucida. Finally Izumo1 binds Juno, and the sperm plasma membrane fuses with the oocyte membrane, so the whole sperm head and tail enter the oocyte cytoplasm."},{"type":"p","text":"Fusion delivers sperm phospholipase C zeta into the oocyte. It generates IP3, which releases Ca2+ from the oocyte endoplasmic reticulum in repeated oscillations. This single Ca2+ signal activates the egg in three ways. First, cortical granules beneath the membrane undergo exocytosis (the cortical reaction). Their protease, ovastacin, cleaves ZP2 so the zona can no longer bind sperm (the zona reaction), and Juno is shed from the oocyte membrane, adding a membrane block. Second, Ca2+ activates CaMKII, which removes Emi2; the anaphase promoting complex then degrades cyclin B and securin, so the oocyte finishes meiosis II and extrudes the second polar body. Third, metabolism and protein synthesis are switched on for cleavage. Unlike the sea urchin, mammals do not rely on a fast electrical block; the cortical reaction is the main defense."},{"type":"flow","title":"Egg activation by the sperm calcium signal","steps":["Sperm and oocyte membranes fuse","Sperm PLC zeta makes IP3","IP3 releases Ca2+ from oocyte ER in oscillations","Cortical granules exocytose; ZP2 cleaved; zona stops binding sperm","CaMKII removes Emi2; APC degrades cyclin B","Meiosis II completes; second polar body extruded","Pronuclei form and chromosomes meet on the first spindle"],"caption":"One Ca2+ signal both blocks polyspermy and releases the metaphase II arrest."},{"type":"p","text":"The sperm nucleus swells as protamines are replaced by maternal histones, forming the male pronucleus beside the female pronucleus. Each replicates its DNA; then their envelopes break down and both chromosome sets align on the first cleavage spindle, organized in humans by the sperm-derived centriole. The pronuclei therefore do not fuse as nuclei. The results are restoration of the diploid number, determination of genetic sex by the X- or Y-bearing sperm, and the start of cleavage. Sperm mitochondria are destroyed after entry, which is why mitochondrial DNA is inherited from the mother."},{"type":"table","title":"Errors at fertilization and their karyotypes","cols":["Event","Karyotype","Result"],"rows":[["Normal monospermic fertilization","46,XX or 46,XY","Normal conceptus"],["Two sperm enter (failed polyspermy block)","69,XXY, 69,XXX, or 69,XYY","Triploidy; often a partial hydatidiform mole with a malformed fetus"],["Sperm fertilizes an oocyte lacking maternal chromosomes","Usually 46,XX, all paternal","Complete hydatidiform mole with no embryo"]],"caption":"An excess of paternal genome drives trophoblast overgrowth, linking fertilization errors to the molar diseases of RE-13-H."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If an oocyte were injected with a drug that blocked the rise in cytoplasmic Ca2+ just before sperm fusion, what two things would fail?","explain":"Cortical granules would not release ovastacin, so the zona would keep binding sperm and polyspermy could occur. The anaphase promoting complex would stay inhibited, so meiosis II would remain stuck at metaphase II and no second polar body or female pronucleus would form.","check":"The three effects of the Ca2+ signal and the egg activation flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01-C04","obj":"RE-3.4","title":"Zygote to blastocyst, implantation, and hCG","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cleavage is a series of rapid mitoses without growth, so the blastomeres shrink with each division. Tubal cilia and muscle move it toward the uterus inside the zona pellucida, which keeps it from sticking to the tubal wall. At about the 8-cell stage the blastomeres compact, sealing to one another through E-cadherin and tight junctions, and by about day 3 a solid morula of 16 or more cells has formed. Inner cells become the inner cell mass (embryoblast), and outer cells become trophoblast."},{"type":"p","text":"The morula enters the uterine cavity about day 3 to 4. Trophoblast cells pump Na+ inward with Na+/K+ ATPase, water follows, and a fluid cavity (blastocele) forms, turning the morula into a blastocyst. The inner cell mass sits at one pole (the embryonic pole) and will form the embryo; the trophoblast will form the fetal part of the placenta. About day 5 the blastocyst hatches from the zona pellucida, which frees it to attach."},{"type":"table","title":"Week 1 timeline after fertilization","cols":["Time","Stage","Location","Key event"],"rows":[["Day 0","Zygote","Ampulla","Meiosis II complete; pronuclei meet"],["About 30 hours","2-cell","Uterine tube","First cleavage division"],["About day 3","Morula","Tube to uterus","Compaction; inner and outer cells separate"],["Days 4 to 5","Blastocyst","Uterine cavity","Blastocele forms; hatching from the zona"],["About day 6","Implanting blastocyst","Endometrium, usually posterior or anterior wall of the body","Trophoblast attaches at the embryonic pole"],["Days 8 to 10","Embedded conceptus","Within the stroma","Syncytiotrophoblast invades; hCG enters maternal blood"]],"caption":"Days count from fertilization, not from the last menstrual period."},{"type":"p","text":"Implantation needs a receptive endometrium. Progesterone from the corpus luteum makes the secretory endometrium thick, vascular, and full of glandular secretions, and receptivity is limited to a short window in the mid-secretory phase (about days 20 to 24 of a 28-day cycle). At the embryonic pole the trophoblast differentiates into two layers. The inner cytotrophoblast is mitotically active and supplies new cells. The outer syncytiotrophoblast forms by cell fusion, has many nuclei and no cell boundaries, does not divide, and erodes the epithelium and stroma with proteases. The conceptus is fully embedded by about day 10, and endometrial stromal cells around it swell with glycogen and lipid (the decidual reaction), forming decidua."},{"type":"p","text":"Without pregnancy the corpus luteum regresses about 14 days after ovulation, progesterone falls, and the functional endometrium is shed as menses. The syncytiotrophoblast prevents this by secreting human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). hCG shares its alpha subunit with LH, FSH, and TSH, and its beta subunit closely resembles that of LH, so it binds the LH receptor on luteal cells and keeps them secreting progesterone and estrogen. The corpus luteum of pregnancy is required until the placenta makes enough progesterone itself, at about 7 to 9 weeks of gestational age. Serum hCG becomes detectable about 8 to 10 days after fertilization, roughly doubles every 2 days in early pregnancy, peaks near 8 to 10 weeks of gestational age, and then falls. Pregnancy tests detect the beta subunit because it is the part unique to hCG."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the conceptus prevents menstruation","steps":["Syncytiotrophoblast invades the endometrium","Syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG into maternal blood","hCG binds LH receptors on the corpus luteum","Corpus luteum survives past day 14 after ovulation","Progesterone stays high; decidua is maintained","Placenta takes over progesterone at about 7 to 9 weeks"],"caption":"Loss of the corpus luteum before the luteal to placental shift ends the pregnancy."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a pregnancy at 5 weeks of gestational age, what would happen if the corpus luteum were removed? What about at 12 weeks?","explain":"At 5 weeks the corpus luteum is still the main progesterone source, so progesterone would fall, the decidua would break down, and the pregnancy would be lost. By 12 weeks the syncytiotrophoblast makes enough progesterone itself.","check":"The hCG paragraph and the luteal to placental shift."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01-C05","obj":"RE-3.5","title":"Abnormal implantation: tubal ectopic pregnancy and placenta previa","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The blastocyst implants wherever it is when it hatches and becomes adhesive, about day 5 to 6. Normal implantation depends on two things: transport to the uterine cavity on time, and attachment to the upper uterine body. Delay produces an ectopic pregnancy; attachment too low in the uterus produces placenta previa."},{"type":"p","text":"An ectopic pregnancy implants outside the uterine cavity, and over 95% are tubal, most often in the ampulla. Anything that slows tubal transport raises the risk. The classic cause is prior salpingitis (pelvic inflammatory disease from Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae), which destroys ciliated cells and leaves intraluminal adhesions and partial obstruction. Other risk factors are a previous ectopic pregnancy, tubal surgery including sterilization, pregnancy with an intrauterine device in place, assisted reproduction, and smoking, which impairs ciliary function."},{"type":"flow","title":"From salpingitis to tubal rupture","steps":["Salpingitis destroys cilia and scars the tubal lumen","Conceptus moves slowly and is still in the tube at day 6","Trophoblast invades the thin tubal wall without proper decidua","Tube distends; trophoblast erodes tubal vessels","Rupture, often about 6 to 8 weeks of gestational age","Hemoperitoneum pools in the rectouterine pouch"],"caption":"Ampullary ectopics usually rupture in the first trimester; interstitial (cornual) ectopics, surrounded by myometrium, rupture later and bleed more."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow from this mechanism. The tube cannot support normal trophoblast growth, so serum hCG is lower than expected for dates and rises more slowly than the usual doubling. Because hCG still maintains the corpus luteum and progesterone, the endometrium undergoes decidual change, but curettings show decidua without chorionic villi, sometimes with the Arias-Stella reaction of enlarged, hyperchromatic glandular cells. The typical picture is amenorrhea, then lower abdominal pain and vaginal spotting as the stretched tube bleeds and decidua is shed. Rupture causes hemoperitoneum in the rectouterine pouch, the lowest part of the female peritoneal cavity in the upright position; blood under the diaphragm can cause shoulder pain referred through the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5); and large blood loss produces hypotension and tachycardia."},{"type":"p","text":"Placenta previa occurs when the blastocyst implants in the lower uterine segment, so the placenta covers the internal cervical os or lies close to it. Risk factors are those that damage or scar the upper endometrium or enlarge the placental area: prior cesarean delivery or uterine surgery, multiparity, advanced maternal age, multiple gestation, and smoking. In the third trimester the lower segment thins and lengthens and the cervix begins to efface, which shears the inelastic placenta from its base and opens maternal sinuses. The result is painless, bright red bleeding with a soft, nontender uterus. When previa lies over a cesarean scar, the decidua basalis is often deficient, so villi attach directly to myometrium (placenta accreta spectrum)."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing causes of bleeding from abnormal placentation","cols":["Feature","Placenta previa","Placental abruption","Vasa previa"],"rows":[["Lesion","Placenta over or near internal os","Premature separation of a normally sited placenta","Fetal vessels cross the membranes over the os"],["Pain","Painless","Painful; tender, firm uterus","Painless"],["Blood lost","Maternal","Maternal, often partly concealed behind placenta","Fetal"],["Main risks","Prior cesarean, multiparity, maternal age","Hypertension, preeclampsia, cocaine, trauma","Velamentous cord insertion, low placenta"],["Key consequence","Hemorrhage; accreta if over a scar","Fetal hypoxia; coagulopathy","Fetal exsanguination at membrane rupture"]],"caption":"Pain and the source of the blood separate the three."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-03-01-C06","obj":"RE-3.6","title":"Bilaminar disc, gastrulation, and the period of teratogen sensitivity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In week 2 the implanted conceptus organizes into two layers. The inner cell mass splits into a dorsal layer of tall columnar epiblast cells and a ventral layer of small cuboidal hypoblast cells, forming the bilaminar disc. The amniotic cavity forms above the epiblast and the yolk sac below the hypoblast. Extraembryonic mesoderm lines the trophoblast, and the chorionic cavity opens within it, leaving the connecting stalk as the future umbilical cord. Syncytiotrophoblast lacunae tap maternal sinusoids about days 11 to 12, starting the uteroplacental circulation. Week 2 thus has two germ layers, two cavities, and two trophoblast layers."},{"type":"p","text":"Gastrulation in week 3 converts the bilaminar disc into a trilaminar disc and sets the body axes. The primitive streak appears about day 15 on the caudal epiblast, with the primitive node at its cranial end. Epiblast cells detach and migrate through the streak, driven by FGF8 signaling and the transcription factor brachyury. The first cells to pass displace the hypoblast and form definitive endoderm, the next spread between the layers as intraembryonic mesoderm, and the epiblast left behind becomes ectoderm. All three germ layers therefore come from epiblast. Nodal cells form the notochord, which induces the neural plate and later contributes the nucleus pulposus. Beating cilia at the node move fluid leftward and establish left-right asymmetry, which is why dynein defects can cause situs inversus. The streak normally regresses; a persistent remnant can give a sacrococcygeal teratoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Gastrulation in week 3","steps":["Primitive streak and node form on caudal epiblast","Epiblast cells detach and migrate through the streak","First cells replace hypoblast as definitive endoderm","Next cells spread between layers as mesoderm","Remaining epiblast becomes ectoderm","Nodal cells form the notochord, which induces the neural plate"],"caption":"Epiblast cells displace the hypoblast; hypoblast does not become endoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"Sensitivity to a teratogen depends on the developmental stage at exposure. In weeks 1 and 2 the blastomeres and epiblast cells are still undifferentiated and can replace lost neighbors, so an injury either kills the conceptus or is repaired completely (the all-or-none period). From week 3 through week 8, each organ primordium is laid down in a narrow window of rapid proliferation, migration, and induction. Damage then removes cells or inductive signals that cannot be restored, causing major structural malformations. From week 9 (fetal period) organs mainly grow and mature, so exposures more often cause growth restriction or functional deficits, though the brain remains vulnerable."},{"type":"table","title":"Stage at exposure and typical outcome","cols":["Period after fertilization","Gestational age (from LMP)","Main events","Typical effect of a teratogen"],"rows":[["Weeks 1 to 2","Weeks 3 to 4","Cleavage, implantation, bilaminar disc","All or none: loss or normal development"],["Weeks 3 to 8","Weeks 5 to 10","Gastrulation, neurulation, organogenesis","Major structural malformations"],["Week 9 to birth","Week 11 to birth","Growth and maturation","Growth restriction, functional deficits"]],"caption":"Gestational age runs about 2 weeks ahead of age from fertilization."},{"type":"example","title":"Dating an exposure","text":"A woman with regular 28-day cycles is exposed to a teratogen on day 24 after her last menstrual period (LMP). Fertilization occurs about day 14, so exposure fell about 10 days after fertilization, in week 2, before gastrulation. The expected outcome is all or none: early loss or normal development. The same exposure on day 42 after the LMP falls about 4 weeks after fertilization, when the neural tube, heart, and limb buds are forming, so structural defects become likely, often before the pregnancy is recognized."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a toxic insult on day 10 after fertilization rarely cause a cleft lip or a limb defect, while the same insult on day 30 can?","explain":"On day 10 no organ primordia exist and surviving cells can replace lost ones, so the embryo dies or recovers. By day 30 the face and limb buds are forming in fixed windows, and lost cells or signals cannot be replaced.","check":"The teratogen paragraph and the stage table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The chapter rests on cell biology. The same cyclin B and CDK1 machinery that drives mitosis is held in check at two points in the oocyte, by cAMP in prophase I and by cytostatic factor in metaphase II, and a single Ca2+ wave at fertilization both releases the second arrest and blocks polyspermy. Errors at these checkpoints link directly to genetics and pathology: nondisjunction during the long prophase arrest produces maternal age-related trisomy, and failure of the polyspermy block or loss of the maternal genome produces triploidy and molar pregnancy. Organelles explain sperm defects, from the Golgi-derived acrosome to the centriole-derived axoneme whose dynein defects cause both infertility and situs inversus."},{"type":"p","text":"Endocrinology and microbiology meet at implantation. Progesterone from the corpus luteum prepares the endometrium, and hCG, acting through the LH receptor, keeps that support in place until the placenta takes over, which is also why hCG levels reflect trophoblast health in both normal and ectopic pregnancy. Sexually transmitted Chlamydia and gonococci damage tubal cilia and convert a timing problem into a tubal ectopic pregnancy, while surgical scarring of the uterus shifts implantation toward previa and accreta. Finally, gastrulation sets the timing of teratogen sensitivity; that timing, not the agent alone, decides whether exposure leads to loss, normal development, or a malformation."}],"summary":["Spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty and gives four sperm per meiosis; oogenesis starts before birth and gives one ovum plus polar bodies.","Primary oocytes arrest in prophase I until the LH surge; secondary oocytes arrest in metaphase II until sperm fusion.","The long oocyte arrest favors meiosis I nondisjunction with maternal age; paternal age raises de novo point mutations.","Spermiogenesis builds the acrosome from the Golgi and the flagellum from a centriole and sheds cytoplasm as a residual body.","Capacitation in the female tract removes cholesterol and glycoproteins; the acrosome reaction occurs only after zona binding.","Fertilization in the ampulla triggers a Ca2+ wave that drives the cortical reaction (block to polyspermy) and completes meiosis II.","The blastocyst implants about day 6; syncytiotrophoblast hCG acts on LH receptors to keep the corpus luteum making progesterone.","Slow tubal transport after salpingitis causes tubal ectopic pregnancy, usually ampullary, with low hCG and risk of rupture.","Placenta previa lies over the internal os and bleeds painlessly in the third trimester as the lower segment stretches.","All three germ layers come from epiblast in week 3; weeks 3 to 8 are the period of greatest sensitivity to structural teratogens."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Gametogenesis: Conversion of Germ Cells Into Male and Female Gametes"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"First Week of Development: Ovulation to Implantation; Second Week of Development: Bilaminar Germ Disc"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Week of Development: Trilaminar Germ Disc; Birth Defects and Prenatal Diagnosis"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Fertilization, Pregnancy, and Lactation"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male; Pregnancy and Lactation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract, diseases of pregnancy (ectopic pregnancy and placental disorders)"}]},"RE-4":{"unit":"RE-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01","title":"Reproductive Organ Location and Boundaries","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the reproductive organs in the pelvis, perineum, and chest wall and explains what holds each one in position. It covers the broad ligament and pelvic peritoneal pouches, the named ligaments of the uterus and ovary and the vessels they carry, pelvic floor support and prolapse, the coverings of the testis and spermatic cord, the position and fibrous framework of the breast, the erectile bodies of the penis, and the boundaries of the perineum with the prostate behind them. Board questions link a structure to its contents, its blood supply, its embryological origin, or the sign that appears when it tears, stretches, or is invaded by tumor. Knowing the layer or ligament involved predicts the finding.","prerequisites":["Layers of the anterior abdominal wall and the inguinal canal","Parietal and visceral peritoneum, mesenteries, and intraperitoneal versus retroperitoneal organs","Branches of the abdominal aorta and internal iliac artery","Bony pelvis: pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrum, and coccyx","Descent of the gonads and the gubernaculum (reproductive embryology)"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive embryology","how":"The gubernaculum and processus vaginalis explain why the round ligament and the spermatic cord both pass through the inguinal canal."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal anatomy (inguinal region and peritoneum)","how":"Indirect inguinal hernias and hydroceles follow a patent processus vaginalis inside the coverings of the cord."},{"to":"Renal and urinary anatomy","how":"The ureter runs beside the suspensory ligament at the pelvic brim and beneath the uterine artery near the cervix, so pelvic disease and surgery can involve it."},{"to":"Reproductive pathology","how":"Ectopic pregnancy, endometriosis, breast carcinoma, and prostate carcinoma present through the anatomy of the pouches, ligaments, and zones described here."},{"to":"Lymphatics and oncology","how":"Gonadal lymph drains to para-aortic nodes while scrotal and vulvar skin drains to superficial inguinal nodes, which predicts where tumors spread."}],"terms":[{"term":"Broad ligament","def":"A double fold of peritoneum that runs from the lateral uterus to the lateral pelvic wall and floor and encloses the uterine tube, ovarian attachments, and uterine vessels."},{"term":"Rectouterine pouch","def":"The peritoneal recess between the uterus and rectum that forms the lowest part of the female peritoneal cavity in the upright position; also called the pouch of Douglas or cul-de-sac."},{"term":"Suspensory ligament of the ovary","def":"A peritoneal fold from the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall that carries the ovarian vessels, lymphatics, and nerves; also called the infundibulopelvic ligament."},{"term":"Gubernaculum","def":"The embryonic fibrous cord that guides gonadal descent; in the female it persists as the ovarian ligament and the round ligament."},{"term":"Levator ani","def":"The broad muscle sheet of the pelvic floor, formed by puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus, whose tone supports the pelvic organs."},{"term":"Perineal body","def":"A fibromuscular mass between the vagina (or bulb of the penis) and the anal canal where several perineal muscles and the external anal sphincter converge."},{"term":"Pelvic organ prolapse","def":"Descent of the uterus, vaginal apex, bladder, rectum, or small bowel into or through the vaginal canal because its supports have failed."},{"term":"Processus vaginalis","def":"An outpouching of peritoneum that precedes the testis through the inguinal canal; its distal part becomes the tunica vaginalis."},{"term":"Pampiniform plexus","def":"The network of testicular veins in the spermatic cord that surrounds the testicular artery and cools arterial blood by countercurrent heat exchange."},{"term":"Suspensory ligaments of the breast","def":"Fibrous bands (Cooper ligaments) that run from the dermis through the breast to the deep fascia and support the breast tissue."},{"term":"Tunica albuginea of the penis","def":"The dense collagenous sheath around each erectile body, thick around the corpora cavernosa and thinner around the corpus spongiosum."},{"term":"Perineum","def":"The diamond-shaped region below the pelvic diaphragm bounded by the pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrotuberous ligaments, and coccyx."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C01","obj":"RE-4.1","title":"The broad ligament and the pelvic peritoneal pouches","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The uterus rises from the pelvic floor into the peritoneal cavity, and the peritoneum drapes over it like a sheet over a clothesline. Anteriorly the sheet covers the bladder and uterine body; posteriorly it covers the uterus, the posterior fornix of the vagina, and the front of the rectum. Lateral to the uterus the anterior and posterior layers meet as a double fold, the broad ligament, which runs to the lateral pelvic wall and floor. Like a mesentery, it carries vessels, nerves, and lymphatics between its layers."},{"type":"p","text":"The broad ligament has three named parts, each named for the organ it suspends. The mesometrium is the largest part, lying beside the uterus and below the other two. The mesosalpinx is the thin upper portion between the uterine tube, which runs in the free upper edge, and the attachment of the ovary. The mesovarium is a short fold projecting from the posterior layer that attaches the ovary. The ovary itself is not covered by peritoneum; its surface epithelium replaces it, so ovulation releases the oocyte directly into the peritoneal cavity near the fimbriae."},{"type":"table","title":"Parts and contents of the broad ligament","cols":["Part","Position","What it holds"],"rows":[["Mesometrium","Largest part, beside the uterus, below the other two","Uterine vessels at its base, round ligament, ovarian ligament, ureter passing just below its base"],["Mesosalpinx","Between the uterine tube and the mesovarium","Uterine tube in its free edge, tubal branches of the uterine and ovarian vessels, mesonephric remnants"],["Mesovarium","Fold from the posterior layer","Attaches the ovary; carries ovarian vessels into the hilum"]],"caption":"The broad ligament is a mesentery for the uterus, tubes, and ovaries; each part is named for what it suspends (metra, salpinx, ovarium)."},{"type":"p","text":"Where the peritoneum dips between organs it forms pouches. The vesicouterine pouch between bladder and uterus is shallow. The rectouterine pouch between uterus and rectum is deep, extending down behind the cervix over the posterior vaginal fornix, and it is bounded laterally by the rectouterine folds that contain the uterosacral ligaments. Because it is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in a standing or sitting woman, gravity carries free blood, pus, ascites, and shed tumor cells there. In the male the equivalent lowest recess is the rectovesical pouch. In a supine patient fluid also collects in the hepatorenal recess, the most dependent space of the upper abdomen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ruptured tubal pregnancy to the rectouterine pouch","steps":["Tubal pregnancy erodes the tubal wall and vessels","Tube ruptures and bleeds into the peritoneal cavity","Blood runs down to the rectouterine pouch","Ultrasound shows fluid behind the uterus","Thin posterior fornix allows needle aspiration (culdocentesis)"],"caption":"Only the vaginal wall and peritoneum separate the posterior fornix from the pouch, which is why the pouch can be imaged and sampled through the vagina."},{"type":"p","text":"The same dependency explains other findings. Blood in the pouch irritates the peritoneum over the rectum, so it can cause a sense of rectal pressure or painful defecation. Pelvic abscesses from salpingitis or a ruptured pelvic appendix collect there and can be felt as a tender, fluctuant mass on vaginal or rectal examination. Endometriosis commonly implants in the pouch and on the uterosacral ligaments, and the resulting scarring can obliterate the pouch and fix the uterus in retroversion."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with a ruptured ovarian cyst is examined lying flat, then sitting upright. Where does the free fluid collect in each position?","explain":"Lying flat, fluid collects in the most dependent spaces, the rectouterine pouch and the hepatorenal recess. Upright, it drains to the rectouterine pouch, the lowest point of the female peritoneal cavity, because the pouch extends down to the posterior vaginal fornix.","check":"The paragraph on peritoneal pouches and the culdocentesis flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C02","obj":"RE-4.2","title":"Named ligaments of the uterus and ovary and the vessels they carry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The named ligaments make sense once you ask where the blood supply comes from. The ovary develops high on the posterior abdominal wall and descends into the pelvis, bringing its vessels with it, so its artery arises from the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries and reaches it from above. The uterus develops in the pelvis, so its artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and reaches it from the side, at the level of the cervix."},{"type":"p","text":"The suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament is the lateral upper corner of the broad ligament, running from the ovary and tubal infundibulum to the lateral pelvic wall where it crosses the external iliac vessels. It carries the ovarian vessels, lymphatics, and nerves; ovarian lymph follows them to para-aortic (lumbar) nodes, which matters for ovarian cancer spread. The ureter crosses the pelvic brim just behind this ligament."},{"type":"p","text":"Two ligaments are remnants of the female gubernaculum, which attached the gonad to the labioscrotal swelling and became anchored to the side of the uterus near the uterine tube. The part above that attachment is the ovarian ligament (proper ovarian ligament), from the uterine pole of the ovary to the uterus just below the tube; it carries no major vessels. The part below is the round ligament, which leaves the uterus just in front of and below the tube, crosses the pelvic wall, enters the deep inguinal ring, traverses the inguinal canal, and ends in the labium majus, the female counterpart of the scrotum. Stretching of the round ligament in pregnancy causes groin pain."},{"type":"p","text":"The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament is a condensation of fibrous tissue and smooth muscle at the base of the broad ligament, running from the cervix and lateral vaginal fornix to the lateral pelvic wall. The uterine artery and vein run in and just above it toward the cervix. About 2 cm lateral to the cervix the uterine artery passes over the ureter, which runs forward beneath it toward the bladder. The uterosacral ligaments run from the posterior cervix around the rectum to the sacrum within the rectouterine folds; they hold the cervix back, which keeps the uterus anteverted over the bladder."},{"type":"table","title":"Female pelvic ligaments","cols":["Ligament","Runs from and to","Contents","Clinical link"],"rows":[["Suspensory (infundibulopelvic)","Ovary and infundibulum to lateral pelvic wall","Ovarian artery and vein, lymphatics, nerves","Ureter lies close behind it at the pelvic brim; ovarian torsion twists these vessels"],["Ovarian (proper ovarian)","Ovary to side of uterus below the tube","No major vessels","Upper remnant of the gubernaculum"],["Round","Uterine horn through the inguinal canal to the labium majus","Small artery, lymphatics","Lower remnant of the gubernaculum; lymph to superficial inguinal nodes"],["Cardinal (transverse cervical)","Cervix and lateral fornix to lateral pelvic wall","Uterine artery and vein run in and just above it","Ureter passes beneath the uterine artery about 2 cm lateral to the cervix"],["Uterosacral","Posterior cervix to sacrum","Fibrous tissue, smooth muscle, autonomic nerves","Holds the cervix back; tender nodules in endometriosis"]],"caption":"Ovarian vessels enter from above in the suspensory ligament; uterine vessels enter from the side at the base of the broad ligament."},{"type":"example","title":"Which ligament is involved?","text":"A woman has sudden severe pelvic pain with a large ovarian mass, and Doppler ultrasound shows absent ovarian venous flow. The ovary swings on two attachments: the ovarian ligament medially and the suspensory ligament laterally. Only the suspensory ligament carries the ovarian artery and vein, so twisting of the ovary around these attachments (torsion) kinks the ovarian vessels. The thin-walled veins collapse first, the ovary swells, and arterial inflow then fails, causing ischemia. Because the uterine artery also supplies the ovary through the broad ligament, some arterial flow on Doppler does not exclude torsion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C03","obj":"RE-4.3","title":"Support of the uterus and vagina and pelvic organ prolapse","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every rise in intra-abdominal pressure from coughing, lifting, or straining pushes the pelvic organs downward, so they need support at several levels. Two systems share the load: an active muscular floor below and passive fibrous suspension above. Prolapse occurs when one system fails and the other is overloaded."},{"type":"p","text":"The levator ani forms most of the pelvic diaphragm. Its parts, the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus, run from the pubis, the tendinous arch of the obturator internus fascia, and the ischial spine to the perineal body, anococcygeal ligament, and coccyx. It is supplied by the nerve to levator ani (S3 to S4) and branches of the pudendal nerve. The urethra, vagina, and rectum pass through a gap in front, the urogenital hiatus. Constant resting tone narrows this hiatus and lifts the floor into a firm shelf, so in a standing woman the upper vagina lies almost horizontally on the levator plate and abdominal pressure presses the organs against muscle rather than out through the opening."},{"type":"p","text":"The perineal body anchors the lower end of this system. It is a fibromuscular knot between the vagina and anal canal where the bulbospongiosus, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, external anal sphincter, and fibers of the levator ani converge. Above, the cardinal and uterosacral ligaments suspend the cervix and upper vagina from the pelvic walls and sacrum, while the middle vagina is attached laterally to the pelvic wall fascia. These fibrous supports work well only while the muscle carries most of the load."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of pelvic organ prolapse","steps":["Vaginal delivery stretches or tears levator ani and pudendal nerve","Levator tone falls; urogenital hiatus widens","Vagina becomes more vertical; load shifts to ligaments","Low estrogen, aging, and straining weaken connective tissue","Ligaments stretch; uterus, bladder, or rectum descends"],"caption":"When levator support fails, ligaments suited to brief loads carry a constant load and stretch."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk factors follow the mechanism. Vaginal childbirth, especially large infants, prolonged second stage, or forceps delivery, can avulse the pubococcygeus from the pubis and stretch the pudendal nerve. Estrogen loss after menopause reduces collagen content and strength in the pelvic connective tissue and vaginal wall. Obesity, chronic cough, constipation with straining, and heavy lifting raise abdominal pressure, and inherited collagen disorders such as Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan syndromes weaken the fibrous supports from the start."},{"type":"table","title":"Types of pelvic relaxation","cols":["Defect","What descends","Where it bulges","Symptoms explained by mechanism"],"rows":[["Uterine prolapse","Cervix and uterus (failed cardinal and uterosacral support)","Down the vaginal canal, to or beyond the introitus","Heavy dragging sensation; exposed cervix may ulcerate from friction and drying"],["Cystocele","Bladder base","Anterior vaginal wall","Incomplete emptying and stress incontinence from a hypermobile urethra"],["Rectocele","Rectum","Posterior vaginal wall","Stool trapped in the pouch; incomplete evacuation"],["Enterocele","Small bowel in the rectouterine pouch","Upper posterior vaginal wall","Pelvic pressure, worse standing"]],"caption":"The organ behind a weakened vaginal wall bulges into the canal; symptoms worsen with standing and straining and ease when lying down."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolapse is graded by how far the leading edge descends relative to the hymen, up to complete eversion of the uterus outside the body (procidentia). Stress urinary incontinence often accompanies a cystocele because the urethra loses its firm backing of anterior vaginal wall, so a cough no longer compresses it closed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C04","obj":"RE-4.4","title":"Layers of the scrotum and contents of the spermatic cord","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The testis develops on the posterior abdominal wall and, guided by the gubernaculum, descends through the inguinal canal into the scrotum, usually by birth. A finger of peritoneum, the processus vaginalis, goes ahead of it. As the testis passes through the anterior abdominal wall, each layer of the wall wraps it in a sleeve, so the coverings of the testis and spermatic cord are a map of the abdominal wall."},{"type":"flow","title":"Coverings collected during testicular descent","steps":["Peritoneum: processus vaginalis becomes tunica vaginalis","Deep inguinal ring: transversalis fascia becomes internal spermatic fascia","Internal oblique: cremaster muscle and fascia","Superficial ring: external oblique aponeurosis becomes external spermatic fascia","Scrotum: dartos fascia and skin from superficial fascia and skin"],"caption":"Read from deep to superficial. The transversus abdominis arches over the cord and contributes no layer."},{"type":"table","title":"Scrotal layers from outside in","cols":["Layer","Origin","Function or clinical point"],"rows":[["Skin","Abdominal skin","Thin, pigmented, without fat; lymph to superficial inguinal nodes"],["Dartos fascia and muscle","Superficial fascia, continuous with Colles and Scarpa fascia","Smooth muscle wrinkles the skin in cold, reducing heat loss"],["External spermatic fascia","External oblique aponeurosis","Outer covering of the cord"],["Cremaster muscle and fascia","Internal oblique","Skeletal muscle that raises the testis; supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve"],["Internal spermatic fascia","Transversalis fascia","Inner covering of the cord"],["Tunica vaginalis (parietal and visceral)","Processus vaginalis of peritoneum","Serous sac around the front and sides of the testis; fluid here is a hydrocele"]],"caption":"The cord has the three spermatic coverings; the tunica vaginalis surrounds only the testis. The tunica albuginea of the testis lies deep to its visceral layer."},{"type":"p","text":"The spermatic cord runs from the deep inguinal ring to the posterior border of the testis. Its contents are the ductus deferens, which feels like a firm cord at the back of the bundle; the testicular artery from the abdominal aorta; the artery to the ductus from a vesical artery; the cremasteric artery from the inferior epigastric artery; the pampiniform venous plexus; the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve; sympathetic and visceral afferent fibers on the arteries; lymphatics that drain the testis to para-aortic nodes; and any remnant of the processus vaginalis. The ilioinguinal nerve runs on the cord, outside its coverings."},{"type":"p","text":"Spermatogenesis works best a few degrees below core temperature, about 2 to 3 °C (3.6 to 5.4 °F) lower. The pampiniform plexus surrounds the testicular artery so that cooler venous blood draws heat from arterial blood before it reaches the testis, a countercurrent exchange. The dartos and cremaster tighten the scrotum and raise the testis in cold. The cremasteric reflex (stroking the medial thigh raises the testis) uses the L1 to L2 segments through the genitofemoral and ilioinguinal nerves, and it is usually absent in testicular torsion."},{"type":"p","text":"The plexus converges into the testicular vein near the deep ring. The left vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle and the right enters the inferior vena cava obliquely, so dilation of the plexus (varicocele) is far more common on the left. A varicocele feels like a bag of worms, fills on standing, and impairs cooling. A new right-sided varicocele, or one that does not empty when the patient lies down, suggests obstruction of venous outflow by a retroperitoneal mass. A processus vaginalis that stays open allows peritoneal fluid into the tunica vaginalis (communicating hydrocele) or bowel into the cord coverings (indirect inguinal hernia)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 3-cm testicular tumor spreads by lymphatics. Which nodes enlarge first, and would the answer change for a carcinoma of the scrotal skin?","explain":"Testicular lymph follows the testicular vessels back to their origin, so it drains to para-aortic (lumbar) nodes near the renal vessels. Scrotal skin drains like the rest of the lower abdominal and perineal skin, to superficial inguinal nodes.","check":"The cord contents paragraph and the skin row of the scrotal layers table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C05","obj":"RE-4.5","title":"Position of the breast and the suspensory ligaments","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The breast is a modified sweat gland that lies within the superficial fascia of the anterior chest wall. Its base extends vertically from about the second to the sixth rib and transversely from the lateral border of the sternum to about the midaxillary line. About two thirds of the base rests on the pectoral fascia over the pectoralis major and the rest on the fascia over the serratus anterior. A small extension, the axillary tail, passes along the inferolateral edge of the pectoralis major toward the axilla, so breast tissue and its tumors can appear as an axillary mass."},{"type":"p","text":"Between the breast and the pectoral fascia lies the retromammary space, a layer of loose connective tissue that lets the normal breast glide over the chest wall. The gland consists of about 15 to 20 lobes, each drained by a lactiferous duct that opens on the nipple. Fibrous septa between the lobes condense into the suspensory ligaments (Cooper ligaments), which run from the dermis of the skin through the breast to the deep fascia. They support the breast, and their gradual stretching with age, pregnancy, and weight change allows the breast to sag."},{"type":"p","text":"These attachments explain the classic signs of carcinoma. Invasive carcinoma provokes a desmoplastic reaction, a dense fibrous stroma that contracts as it matures. When the tumor infiltrates suspensory ligaments, their shortening pulls the overlying skin inward, causing dimpling (skin retraction) that becomes obvious when the patient raises her arms or presses her hands on her hips. Involvement of the lactiferous ducts behind the nipple shortens them and causes nipple retraction. Tumor that invades through the retromammary space into the pectoral fascia fixes the mass, so it no longer moves when the pectoralis major contracts."},{"type":"table","title":"Breast signs and the structure responsible","cols":["Sign","Structure involved","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Skin dimpling","Suspensory ligaments","Tumor and desmoplastic fibrosis shorten the ligaments and pull the skin inward"],["Nipple retraction","Lactiferous ducts","Fibrosis shortens the ducts behind the nipple"],["Fixation to the chest wall","Retromammary space and pectoral fascia","Invasion obliterates the gliding layer"],["Peau d'orange","Dermal lymphatics","Tumor emboli block lymph flow; edematous skin is tethered at hair follicles and pores"],["Axillary mass","Axillary tail or axillary nodes","Breast tissue extends toward the axilla, and most breast lymph drains to axillary nodes"]],"caption":"Each sign localizes the tumor to a particular part of the breast framework; acquired nipple retraction differs from a lifelong inverted nipple."},{"type":"p","text":"Most lymph from the breast, about three quarters, drains to the axillary nodes, and most of the rest drains to parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels, so medial tumors may spread inside the chest. The upper outer quadrant contains the most glandular tissue, which is why most carcinomas arise there. Accessory nipples or breast tissue can appear anywhere along the embryonic mammary ridge from the axilla to the groin."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two women have a firm breast mass. In one the skin over it dimples when she raises her arms; in the other the skin is thick and pitted over a red, warm breast. Which structure is involved in each?","explain":"Dimpling means the tumor has shortened the suspensory ligaments that tether the skin. Thick pitted skin (peau d'orange) means tumor emboli are blocking dermal lymphatics, so the skin is edematous except where hair follicles tether it, as in inflammatory carcinoma.","check":"The breast signs table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C06","obj":"RE-4.6","title":"Erectile bodies of the penis and penile fracture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The penis has a fixed root in the urogenital triangle of the perineum and a free body. The root contains three erectile masses: two crura, attached to the ischiopubic rami and covered by the ischiocavernosus muscles, and the midline bulb, attached to the perineal membrane and covered by the bulbospongiosus. In the body, the crura continue as the paired corpora cavernosa on the dorsal side, and the bulb continues as the single corpus spongiosum on the ventral side. The corpus spongiosum surrounds the spongy urethra and expands distally into the glans."},{"type":"p","text":"Each erectile body is wrapped in a tunica albuginea of dense collagen with some elastic fibers. Around the corpora cavernosa it is thick and forms a midline septum that is incomplete, so the two corpora fill as one unit. Around the corpus spongiosum it is thinner and more elastic. All three bodies are then enclosed by the deep fascia of the penis (Buck fascia), and outside that lie the loose superficial (dartos) fascia and thin skin."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the tunica albuginea produces rigidity","steps":["Pelvic splanchnic (S2 to S4) fibers release nitric oxide","cGMP rises; arterial and trabecular smooth muscle relaxes","Blood fills the cavernous sinusoids","Expanding sinusoids compress venules against the tunica","Venous outflow falls; pressure rises toward arterial levels"],"caption":"Veno-occlusion depends on an intact, inextensible tunica albuginea around the corpora cavernosa."},{"type":"p","text":"The thick tunica around the corpora cavernosa does not stretch, so intracavernous pressure rises high enough for rigidity. The thinner tunica around the corpus spongiosum keeps that body at lower pressure during erection, so the urethra stays open for ejaculation and the glans stays relatively soft."},{"type":"p","text":"Erection stretches the tunica and makes it much thinner and stiffer, so a sudden bending force on the erect penis, usually during intercourse, can tear it. This is penile fracture: a rupture of the tunica albuginea of one or both corpora cavernosa. The patient typically reports a cracking sound, sharp pain, and immediate loss of erection as the pressurized blood escapes. Blood collects under the skin, and the shaft swells, discolors, and often deviates away from the side of the tear."},{"type":"p","text":"The distribution of the hematoma depends on the fascia. While Buck fascia is intact, blood is confined to the shaft. If Buck fascia also tears, blood spreads in the plane beneath the membranous superficial fascia into the scrotum, perineum, and lower abdominal wall. Because the corpus spongiosum lies against the corpora cavernosa, a minority of fractures also injure the urethra, signaled by blood at the meatus, hematuria, or inability to void. A localized fibrous plaque in the tunica albuginea (Peyronie disease) curves the erect penis toward the plaque, because the scarred tunica cannot lengthen with the rest."},{"type":"example","title":"Tracing a hematoma by fascial planes","text":"A man has a perineal straddle injury, followed by swelling and bruising of the scrotum, penis, and lower abdominal wall, but not the thighs. Ask which plane the blood is in. The membranous superficial fascia of the perineum (Colles fascia) attaches posteriorly to the perineal membrane and laterally to the ischiopubic rami, then continues into the scrotum as dartos and onto the abdomen as the membranous layer (Scarpa fascia), which fuses with the fascia lata just below the inguinal ligament. Blood or urine from a ruptured bulbar urethra fills this space, so it cannot pass into the thighs or the anal triangle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-04-01-C07","obj":"RE-4.7","title":"Boundaries of the perineum and the position of the prostate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The perineum is the region below the pelvic diaphragm (levator ani and coccygeus) and between the thighs. Its outline is the inferior pelvic outlet, a diamond whose corners and sides are bony and ligamentous landmarks that can be felt or pictured on a skeleton. A transverse line between the ischial tuberosities divides it into two triangles that lie in different planes, the urogenital triangle facing downward and forward and the anal triangle facing downward and backward."},{"type":"table","title":"Boundaries and divisions of the perineum","cols":["Position","Boundary or division","Notes"],"rows":[["Anterior","Pubic symphysis (inferior margin)","Apex of the urogenital triangle"],["Anterolateral","Ischiopubic rami","Attachment of the perineal membrane, crura, and Colles fascia"],["Lateral","Ischial tuberosities","A line between them separates the two triangles"],["Posterolateral","Sacrotuberous ligaments","Covered by the lower border of gluteus maximus"],["Posterior","Tip of the coccyx","Apex of the anal triangle"],["Urogenital triangle","Anterior half","Perineal membrane, superficial and deep perineal pouches, external genitalia, urethra, and vagina in the female"],["Anal triangle","Posterior half","Anal canal, external anal sphincter, fat-filled ischioanal fossae, pudendal canal on their lateral walls"]],"caption":"The perineal body lies at the center of the diamond, where the two triangles meet."},{"type":"p","text":"The structures in each triangle explain several clinical patterns. The pudendal nerve (S2 to S4), which supplies perineal skin, the external anal and urethral sphincters, and the perineal muscles, passes around the ischial spine and then runs in the pudendal canal in the lateral wall of the ischioanal fossa. The ischioanal fossae communicate behind the anal canal, which allows an abscess on one side to spread around to the other."},{"type":"p","text":"The prostate is a fibromuscular gland that surrounds the first part of the male urethra. Its base lies against the neck of the bladder, its apex rests on the external urethral sphincter in the deep perineal pouch, and its posterior surface faces the rectum, separated from it only by the thin rectoprostatic fascia (Denonvilliers fascia). Because the anterior rectal wall is thin, a finger in the rectum feels the posterior surface: two lateral lobes separated by a shallow median sulcus. A normal gland feels smooth and rubbery, about the size of a walnut. The seminal vesicles lie above it, behind the bladder, and are normally out of reach."},{"type":"table","title":"Prostate zones and what the rectal examination detects","cols":["Zone","Location","Typical disease","Finding on digital rectal exam"],"rows":[["Peripheral","Posterior and lateral, against the rectum; most of the glandular tissue","Most carcinomas","Hard, irregular nodule; median sulcus may be lost"],["Transition","Around the proximal urethra","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Smooth, symmetric, rubbery enlargement; may feel nearly normal while still compressing the urethra"],["Central","Around the ejaculatory ducts at the base","Fewer cancers","Often beyond reach"],["Whole gland","Diffuse inflammation","Acute bacterial prostatitis","Warm, boggy, very tender"]],"caption":"Rectal examination samples the posterior gland, which favors detection of peripheral zone cancer over transition zone hyperplasia."},{"type":"p","text":"This explains why benign hyperplasia and carcinoma behave differently. Hyperplastic nodules grow in the transition zone around the urethra, so they cause obstructive urinary symptoms early, even when the palpable surface feels only moderately enlarged. Carcinoma arises mainly in the peripheral zone, far from the urethra, so it often causes no urinary symptoms until it is advanced, but it is the zone the examining finger reaches first."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy explain most of this chapter. The gubernaculum and processus vaginalis account for the inguinal course of both the round ligament and the spermatic cord, the abdominal wall origin of each scrotal layer, and the hernias and hydroceles that follow a patent processus. Gonads keep their abdominal blood supply and lymph drainage after descent, so ovarian and testicular cancers spread first to para-aortic nodes while the skin of the scrotum and vulva drains to inguinal nodes. The peritoneum explains the broad ligament and its pouches, and fascial continuities, such as Colles fascia with the dartos and Scarpa fascia, predict how blood and urine spread."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology act on this framework. Gravity sends blood, pus, and tumor cells to the rectouterine pouch; collagen loss with low estrogen and muscle injury from childbirth explain prolapse; desmoplastic fibrosis in breast carcinoma turns the suspensory ligaments into a visible sign; the inextensible tunica albuginea is both the basis of rigidity and the structure that fractures; and the zonal anatomy of the prostate decides whether disease obstructs the urethra or is felt on rectal examination. These findings localize disease but do not establish its nature, which requires imaging or tissue diagnosis."}],"summary":["The broad ligament is a double peritoneal fold divided into mesometrium (uterus), mesosalpinx (tube), and mesovarium (ovary).","The rectouterine pouch is the lowest point of the female peritoneal cavity when upright; blood or pus collects there and is reached through the posterior vaginal fornix.","Ovarian vessels run in the suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament from above; uterine vessels run at the base of the broad ligament with the cardinal ligament and cross over the ureter near the cervix.","The ovarian ligament and the round ligament are remnants of the gubernaculum; the round ligament traverses the inguinal canal to the labium majus.","Levator ani tone keeps the urogenital hiatus narrow; when it fails, the cardinal and uterosacral ligaments stretch and the uterus, bladder, or rectum prolapses into the vagina.","Scrotal layers mirror the abdominal wall: external oblique to external spermatic fascia, internal oblique to cremaster, transversalis fascia to internal spermatic fascia, peritoneum to tunica vaginalis.","The spermatic cord carries the ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve; the plexus cools arterial blood, and left varicoceles predominate because the left testicular vein drains at a right angle into the left renal vein.","The breast lies on pectoralis major and serratus anterior from about rib 2 to rib 6 with an axillary tail; carcinoma that shortens the suspensory ligaments dimples the skin.","Two dorsal corpora cavernosa and a ventral corpus spongiosum around the urethra are wrapped in tunica albuginea; tearing it in the erect penis is a penile fracture.","The perineum is a diamond divided into urogenital and anal triangles; the prostate lies below the bladder and in front of the rectum, so its peripheral zone is felt on rectal examination."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and perineum: peritoneum, ligaments of the uterus and ovary, pelvic floor, perineum, and male internal genital organs"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: inguinal region, spermatic cord, scrotum, and testis"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: breasts"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital system: descent of the testes and ovaries"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Breast; The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male, male sexual act"}]},"RE-5":{"unit":"RE-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01","title":"Blood Supply, Lymph Drainage, and Innervation of the Reproductive Organs and Breast","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the arteries, veins, lymphatics, and nerves of the gonads, uterus, external genitalia, perineum, and breast. Most of the anatomy is explained by two rules: the gonads keep the vessels they had high on the posterior abdominal wall before they descended, and lymph tends to follow the arteries back to their origin. Board questions turn these rules into predictions: which side gets a varicocele, which structure a pelvic clamp can catch, where a tumor spreads first, which nerve fails after a pelvic operation, and where to find the pudendal nerve. Learn the route of each vessel and nerve and the clinical findings follow from it.","prerequisites":["Branches of the abdominal aorta and tributaries of the inferior vena cava and renal veins","Branches of the internal iliac artery and the course of the pelvic ureter","Pelvic organ position, broad ligament, and perineal boundaries (RE-2, RE-4)","Organization of the autonomic nervous system: sympathetic thoracolumbar and parasympathetic craniosacral outflow","Gonadal descent and the formation of the inguinal canal (RE-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive embryology (RE-1)","how":"Gonadal descent from the upper lumbar region explains why gonadal vessels and lymph run to the aorta and para-aortic nodes."},{"to":"Reproductive processes (RE-6)","how":"The nerves described here carry out the male sexual response, and the uterine spiral arteries drive menstruation."},{"to":"Renal and urinary anatomy","how":"The ureter's course under the uterine artery and the left renal vein's role in varicocele link the pelvis to the kidney."},{"to":"Reproductive neoplasms (RE-13-J) and breast conditions (RE-13-E)","how":"First nodal sites for testicular, ovarian, cervical, vulvar, and breast cancers follow the lymph routes taught here."},{"to":"Autonomic pharmacology","how":"Alpha-1 receptors in the bladder neck and cGMP in cavernosal smooth muscle explain drug effects on emission and erection."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pampiniform plexus","def":"A network of about 8 to 12 veins in the spermatic cord that surrounds the testicular artery, cools arterial blood, and drains into the testicular vein."},{"term":"Varicocele","def":"Dilation and tortuosity of the pampiniform plexus caused by raised venous pressure or valve failure in the testicular vein."},{"term":"Cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament","def":"Condensed connective tissue at the base of the broad ligament that carries the uterine vessels from the pelvic wall to the cervix."},{"term":"Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","def":"Lymph nodes around the abdominal aorta and inferior vena cava that receive lymph from the gonads, kidneys, and pelvic node chains."},{"term":"Superficial inguinal nodes","def":"Nodes in the subcutaneous tissue below the inguinal ligament that drain the skin of the lower trunk, perineum, and lower limb."},{"term":"Sentinel node","def":"The first lymph node to receive lymph from a tumor site, and therefore the first expected to contain metastasis."},{"term":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","def":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers from the ventral rami of S2 to S4 that supply the pelvic organs and erectile tissue."},{"term":"Hypogastric nerves","def":"Sympathetic nerves that connect the superior hypogastric plexus to the inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus on each side."},{"term":"Emission","def":"Sympathetically driven movement of sperm and glandular secretions into the prostatic urethra with closure of the bladder neck."},{"term":"Pudendal nerve","def":"Somatic nerve from S2 to S4 that supplies the skin and striated muscles of the perineum, including the external urethral and anal sphincters."},{"term":"Pudendal (Alcock) canal","def":"A fascial tunnel on the medial surface of the obturator internus that carries the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels to the perineum."},{"term":"Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes","def":"Nodes along the internal thoracic vessels beside the sternum that drain the medial breast and anterior chest wall."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01-C01","obj":"RE-5.1","title":"Gonadal arteries and veins, and why varicocele favors the left","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The testes and ovaries develop on the posterior abdominal wall at about the level of the upper lumbar vertebrae and take their blood vessels with them as they descend. Both gonadal arteries therefore arise from the anterolateral abdominal aorta at about L2, just below the renal arteries. Each runs down and laterally behind the peritoneum, crossing anterior to the ureter."},{"type":"p","text":"The testicular artery enters the deep inguinal ring and runs in the spermatic cord to the testis. The testis also receives the artery to the ductus deferens (from a vesical branch of the internal iliac) and the cremasteric artery (from the inferior epigastric), which anastomose with it. The ovarian artery crosses the pelvic brim in the suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament and anastomoses with the uterine artery in the broad ligament, so the ovary and uterus share a dual supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Venous drainage begins in the pampiniform plexus, which wraps around the testicular artery in the cord. Cooler venous blood draws heat from the arterial blood (countercurrent heat exchange), helping keep the testis about 2 to 3 °C (about 4 to 5 °F) below core temperature, which spermatogenesis requires. The plexus narrows to a single testicular vein near the deep ring. The two veins then end differently: the right vein enters the inferior vena cava (IVC) directly at an acute, oblique angle, while the longer left vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle. The ovarian veins follow the same pattern."},{"type":"table","title":"Right versus left gonadal veins","cols":["Feature","Right gonadal vein","Left gonadal vein"],"rows":[["Termination","Inferior vena cava","Left renal vein"],["Angle of entry","Oblique, in line with flow","Right angle"],["Length of column","Shorter","Longer"],["Upstream compression","Rare","Left renal vein can be compressed between the superior mesenteric artery and aorta"],["Varicocele","Uncommon; isolated or nonreducing cases suggest obstruction","About 90% of varicoceles"]],"caption":"Anatomical differences that raise venous pressure on the left."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why a left varicocele impairs fertility","steps":["Long left vein enters left renal vein at right angle","Higher hydrostatic pressure and absent or incompetent valves","Reflux dilates the pampiniform plexus","Countercurrent cooling fails and testicular temperature rises","Germ cell damage lowers sperm count and motility"],"caption":"Causal chain from left-sided venous anatomy to impaired spermatogenesis."},{"type":"p","text":"A varicocele feels like a bag of worms above the testis, enlarges with standing or a Valsalva maneuver, and collapses when the patient lies down because gravity no longer opposes drainage. It is found in about 15% of adult men and is more common in men with infertility, where warming of the testis, local hypoxia, and oxidative stress are thought to damage germ cells; the affected testis may be smaller. A varicocele that appears suddenly, does not empty when supine, or is isolated on the right points to obstruction: a left renal tumor with renal vein thrombus blocks left gonadal outflow, and a retroperitoneal mass or IVC obstruction can do the same on the right. The female counterpart of venous reflux is pelvic congestion from dilated ovarian veins."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the side of a varicocele","text":"A 62-year-old man develops a new left scrotal swelling over a few weeks, with hematuria and flank pain. The veins stay full when he lies down. A varicocele in an older man that does not empty supine is not a valve problem, because a valve-failure varicocele drains when gravity is removed. The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein, so a left renal cell carcinoma growing into the renal vein raises pressure in the gonadal vein and backs blood into the pampiniform plexus. The same finding on the right would point to IVC obstruction, because the right vein drains directly into the IVC."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01-C02","obj":"RE-5.2","title":"The uterine artery and its relation to the ureter","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, often near the umbilical artery. It runs medially across the pelvic floor in the base of the broad ligament, within the cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament, toward the cervix. Near the supravaginal cervix, at about the level of the internal os, it gives a cervicovaginal branch and turns sharply upward. It then climbs, tortuous, along the lateral margin of the uterus between the layers of the broad ligament and ends by anastomosing with the ovarian artery near the uterine horn, sending tubal and ovarian branches."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the myometrium the uterine artery gives arcuate arteries, then radial arteries, which become straight arteries to the basal endometrium and coiled spiral arteries to the functional layer. Only the spiral arteries respond to ovarian hormones, which is why the functional layer is shed at menstruation while the basal layer survives (RE-6). Uterine veins form a plexus beside the uterus and vagina that drains to the internal iliac veins."},{"type":"h","text":"Where the ureter is at risk"},{"type":"p","text":"The ureter enters the pelvis by crossing the bifurcation of the common iliac artery, descends on the lateral pelvic wall in front of the internal iliac artery and behind the ovary, then turns medially through the base of the broad ligament toward the bladder. In that medial course it passes beneath the uterine artery about 1.5 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix, just above the lateral vaginal fornix. A clamp or ligature placed on the uterine vessels at this point can include or cut the ureter. A second site of risk is the pelvic brim, where the ureter lies just behind the ovarian vessels in the suspensory ligament."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of ureteric injury near the uterine artery","cols":["Injury","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Ligation","Lumen closed by suture or clamp","Hydronephrosis, flank pain; bilateral ligation gives anuria and rising creatinine"],["Transection","Ureter cut with the vessels","Urine leak into the pelvis (urinoma), fever, ileus, rising serum creatinine as leaked urine is reabsorbed"],["Devascularization","Adventitial vessels stripped","Delayed necrosis after days to weeks, then a ureterovaginal fistula"]],"caption":"Each pattern follows from what was done to the ureter at the crossing."},{"type":"p","text":"Devascularization deserves emphasis because it is delayed. The ureter has no single artery; it is supplied segmentally by small branches that run longitudinally in its adventitia. The abdominal ureter receives branches from the medial side (renal, gonadal, aortic), but the pelvic ureter receives them from the lateral side (internal iliac, vesical, and uterine branches). Stripping the pelvic ureter from its lateral attachments can leave a segment ischemic, and a fistula to the vagina presents as continuous leakage of urine with otherwise normal voiding. Unilateral obstruction may be clinically silent at first because the other kidney keeps total glomerular filtration, and serum creatinine, near normal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01-C03","obj":"RE-5.3","title":"Lymph drainage of the reproductive organs and predicting nodal spread","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lymphatic vessels usually travel with the arteries that supply an organ and drain back toward the origin of those arteries. Applying this rule predicts the first nodal station for most reproductive tumors. The gonads draw their arteries from the aorta at about L2, so testicular and ovarian lymph runs up the gonadal vessels to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes near the renal veins, skipping the pelvis and groin. Pelvic organs supplied by internal iliac branches drain to the iliac nodes on the pelvic wall. Skin and surface mucosa of the perineum below the hymen drain, like most skin of the lower trunk and perineum, to the superficial inguinal nodes."},{"type":"table","title":"First nodal stations of reproductive structures","cols":["Structure","First nodes","Basis"],"rows":[["Testis and epididymis","Para-aortic (lumbar), at about L1 to L2","Follow testicular vessels to the aorta"],["Ovary and uterine tube","Para-aortic","Follow ovarian vessels in the suspensory ligament"],["Uterine body","Mainly external iliac; fundus also para-aortic","Follow uterine and ovarian vessels"],["Uterus near the horn","Some to superficial inguinal","Lymph runs along the round ligament"],["Cervix","Internal and external iliac (including obturator) and sacral","Follow uterine vessels in the cardinal ligament"],["Upper vagina","Internal and external iliac","Pelvic organ"],["Lower vagina below the hymen, vulva","Superficial inguinal","Perineal skin and mucosa"],["Scrotum and penile skin","Superficial inguinal","Perineal skin"],["Glans penis and clitoris","Deep inguinal, some to external iliac","Deeper erectile tissue drains below the fascia lata"]],"caption":"Pelvic and para-aortic stations drain upward to the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct; inguinal nodes drain to the external iliac nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"The most tested contrast is the testis versus the scrotum. A testicular germ cell tumor spreads first to retroperitoneal para-aortic nodes, the right testis mainly to interaortocaval nodes and the left mainly to left para-aortic nodes just below the left renal vein. An enlarged inguinal node is not expected from a testicular tumor unless the tumor has invaded the scrotal skin or the scrotum has been surgically breached, which opens a route into skin lymphatics. In contrast, squamous cell carcinoma of the scrotal skin spreads first to superficial inguinal nodes. Because retroperitoneal nodes drain into the thoracic duct, advanced testicular cancer can enlarge the left supraclavicular node."},{"type":"p","text":"In women, ovarian cancer reaches para-aortic nodes directly, although its dominant spread is across the peritoneal surface. Cervical cancer spreads laterally through the cardinal ligament to pelvic nodes before para-aortic nodes, so involvement of para-aortic nodes indicates more advanced disease. Vulvar cancer goes first to superficial inguinal nodes, and lesions near the midline or clitoris can drain to both groins. Vaginal tumors drain according to their level: upper third to iliac nodes and lower third to inguinal nodes."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: matching the node to the tumor","text":"A 30-year-old man has a firm, painless testicular mass and a palpable inguinal node. The testis drains along its vessels to para-aortic nodes, so the inguinal node does not fit testicular spread unless the scrotal skin is involved. The next question is whether another skin or perineal source drains to that groin: scrotal skin, penile skin, the lower anal canal, or the lower limb. For the testicular mass itself, the first station to examine is the retroperitoneum at about the level of the renal veins."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the first nodal station for a tumor of the lower third of the vagina and for one of the cervix.","explain":"The lower vagina is surface tissue below the hymen and drains with perineal skin; the cervix drains with the uterine vessels to the pelvic wall.","check":"Compare with the table of first nodal stations."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01-C04","obj":"RE-5.4","title":"Autonomic and somatic nerves of the male sexual response","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The male sexual response runs in three phases, each controlled by a different division of the nervous system: erection by parasympathetic fibers, emission by sympathetic fibers, and ejaculation by the somatic pudendal nerve acting on striated perineal muscle. Knowing which pathway runs each phase lets you predict which phase fails after a given injury."},{"type":"table","title":"Three phases, three divisions","cols":["Phase","Division","Pathway","Effector and action"],"rows":[["Erection","Parasympathetic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4), inferior hypogastric plexus, cavernous nerves","Nitric oxide relaxes helicine arteries and trabecular smooth muscle"],["Emission","Sympathetic","Lower thoracic and upper lumbar cord (about T11 to L2), lumbar splanchnic nerves, superior hypogastric plexus, hypogastric nerves","Alpha-1 contraction of epididymis, ductus deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate; bladder neck closes"],["Ejaculation","Somatic","Pudendal nerve (S2 to S4), perineal branch","Rhythmic contraction of bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus expels semen"]],"caption":"Detumescence after ejaculation is sympathetic: norepinephrine contracts cavernosal smooth muscle through alpha-1 receptors."},{"type":"flow","title":"Molecular mechanism of erection","steps":["Cavernous nerves and endothelium release nitric oxide","Nitric oxide activates soluble guanylyl cyclase","cGMP rises and activates protein kinase G","Cytosolic Ca2+ falls; smooth muscle relaxes","Arterial inflow fills cavernous sinusoids","Expanding sinusoids compress subtunical veins against tunica albuginea","Venous outflow is trapped; the penis becomes rigid"],"caption":"Phosphodiesterase type 5 ends the signal by degrading cGMP, which is why blocking it prolongs smooth muscle relaxation."},{"type":"p","text":"Erection begins with nitric oxide from nonadrenergic noncholinergic nerve terminals and is sustained by endothelial nitric oxide; acetylcholine contributes mainly by stimulating endothelial release. Arterial blood reaches the corpora through the deep arteries of the penis, branches of the internal pudendal artery. Erection is primarily a vascular event, because rigidity depends on high inflow and closure of venous outflow (veno-occlusion), and either arterial disease or a leaky venous mechanism can defeat it. Erection can be reflexogenic, through a sacral reflex arc carried by the dorsal nerve of the penis to S2 to S4, or psychogenic, from the brain. Men with spinal cord injury above the sacral segments often keep reflex erections, while sacral cord or cavernous nerve damage abolishes them."},{"type":"p","text":"Emission loads semen into the prostatic urethra. At the same time sympathetic tone through alpha-1 receptors closes the bladder neck (internal urethral sphincter), so that when the pudendal nerve fires the bulbospongiosus, semen can only go forward through the spongy urethra. The external urethral sphincter relaxes during expulsion. The whole sequence is coordinated by spinal reflex centers in the lumbosacral cord under brain modulation."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting failure from the injured pathway","cols":["Injury or condition","Pathway lost","Result"],"rows":[["Cavernous nerve damage in radical pelvic surgery (nerves run posterolateral to the prostate)","Parasympathetic","Erectile dysfunction"],["Atherosclerosis of internal pudendal or penile arteries","Arterial inflow","Erectile dysfunction, often an early sign of systemic vascular disease"],["Diabetes mellitus","Autonomic nerves and endothelial nitric oxide","Erectile dysfunction and retrograde ejaculation"],["Retroperitoneal node dissection injuring sympathetic chain or hypogastric plexus","Sympathetic","Absent emission or retrograde ejaculation"],["Alpha-1 blockade or surgery at the bladder neck","Bladder neck closure","Retrograde ejaculation into the bladder"]],"caption":"Retrograde ejaculation is shown by sperm in urine collected after orgasm."},{"type":"p","text":"The female response follows the same plan: parasympathetic S2 to S4 fibers dilate vessels of the clitoris and vestibular bulbs and increase vaginal transudation (lubrication), while orgasmic contractions of the perineal muscles are carried by the pudendal nerve."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the sexual function deficit after injury to the superior hypogastric plexus during retroperitoneal lymph node dissection.","explain":"Erection (sacral parasympathetic) and the pudendal nerve are spared, but sympathetic emission and bladder neck closure are lost.","check":"Compare with the table on predicting failure from the injured pathway."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01-C05","obj":"RE-5.5","title":"Course of the pudendal nerve and the ischial spine landmark","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pudendal nerve is the main somatic nerve of the perineum. It forms from the ventral rami of S2 to S4 in the sacral plexus and carries sensation from the perineal skin, motor fibers to the striated perineal muscles and external sphincters, and sympathetic fibers to perineal skin. Its route is indirect: it leaves the pelvis, hooks around the ischial spine, and re-enters below the pelvic floor, because the perineum lies beneath the levator ani and the nerve cannot reach it by passing straight through."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of the pudendal nerve","steps":["Ventral rami of S2 to S4 join in the sacral plexus","Exits pelvis through greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis","Crosses the sacrospinous ligament at the ischial spine","Re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen","Runs in the pudendal canal on obturator internus fascia","Divides into inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal nerves"],"caption":"The internal pudendal artery and vein take the same route, lying alongside the nerve at the ischial spine."},{"type":"p","text":"At the ischial spine the nerve lies on the posterior surface of the sacrospinous ligament, near its attachment to the spine, with the internal pudendal vessels beside it. The spine can be felt through the lateral vaginal wall, which makes it the landmark for a transvaginal pudendal nerve block during the second stage of labor. Anesthetic placed just medial and posterior to the spine bathes the nerve before it enters the pudendal canal. The canal itself is a split in the obturator fascia on the lateral wall of the ischioanal fossa."},{"type":"table","title":"Branches of the pudendal nerve","cols":["Branch","Motor supply","Sensory supply"],"rows":[["Inferior rectal (anal) nerve","External anal sphincter","Perianal skin and anal canal below the pectinate line"],["Perineal nerve, superficial branches","None","Posterior scrotum or labia"],["Perineal nerve, deep branch","Bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, transverse perineal muscles, external urethral sphincter","None"],["Dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris","None","Skin of the penis or clitoris, especially the glans"]],"caption":"The pudendal nerve carries both sensation and continence, so its injury causes both numbness and sphincter weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"A pudendal block numbs most of the perineum but has clear limits that follow from other nerves. Pain from uterine contractions travels with sympathetic fibers to about T10 to L1 and is untouched. The anterior labia are supplied by the ilioinguinal nerve and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, and part of the posterior perineum by the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve, so sensation may persist there."},{"type":"p","text":"Injury follows the course. Prolonged or difficult vaginal delivery stretches the nerve where it is fixed at the ischial spine and in the pudendal canal, and long periods of cycling compress it in the canal or between the sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments. The result is perineal numbness or burning pain, worse when sitting, with possible weakness of the external anal and urethral sphincters that contributes to fecal and stress urinary incontinence."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-05-01-C06","obj":"RE-5.6","title":"Blood supply and lymph drainage of the breast","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The breast lies in the subcutaneous tissue over the pectoralis major and part of the serratus anterior, extending as an axillary tail toward the armpit. It has no single named artery and is supplied by branches of the vessels that run beside it. Medially, perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery (from the subclavian) emerge through the second to fourth intercostal spaces. Laterally, the lateral thoracic artery and pectoral branches of the thoracoacromial artery, both from the axillary artery, enter the gland. Lateral branches of the posterior intercostal arteries in the second to fourth spaces add to the supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Veins follow the arteries to the axillary vein, internal thoracic veins, and intercostal veins. The intercostal veins connect with the valveless vertebral venous plexus, which offers tumor cells a route to the vertebrae that bypasses the lungs."},{"type":"table","title":"Breast arteries","cols":["Artery","Parent vessel","Region supplied"],"rows":[["Perforating (medial mammary) branches of internal thoracic","Subclavian artery","Medial breast"],["Lateral thoracic (lateral mammary branches)","Axillary artery","Lateral breast"],["Pectoral branches of thoracoacromial","Axillary artery","Deep and upper breast"],["Lateral branches of posterior intercostals (spaces 2 to 4)","Thoracic aorta","Lateral and deep breast"]],"caption":"Arteries enter from the medial and lateral sides, so the gland has a rich collateral supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Lymph collects in a plexus under the areola and in the gland and then follows the arteries. More than about 75% drains, mainly from the lateral quadrants, to the axillary nodes, usually first to the pectoral (anterior) group. From there lymph passes to central and apical nodes, then to the subclavian lymphatic trunk and supraclavicular nodes. Most of the remainder, mainly from the medial quadrants, runs with the perforating vessels to the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic artery. Smaller routes reach the opposite breast and, from the inferior quadrants, the abdominal wall below the diaphragm."},{"type":"flow","title":"Main lymph route from the lateral breast","steps":["Subareolar and glandular lymphatic plexus","Pectoral (anterior) axillary nodes","Central axillary nodes","Apical axillary nodes","Subclavian lymphatic trunk and supraclavicular nodes","Venous angle at the junction of the internal jugular and subclavian veins"],"caption":"This stepwise route is why axillary nodes are usually the first site of breast cancer spread and why a single sentinel node can represent the axilla."},{"type":"p","text":"These routes predict spread and its signs. A lateral tumor usually reaches axillary nodes first, while a medial tumor can reach parasternal nodes that lie inside the chest wall, beyond the axilla. When tumor cells block dermal lymphatics, the skin swells around tethered hair follicles and looks pitted like orange peel (peau d'orange); this is a typical sign of inflammatory carcinoma. Tumor involving the suspensory ligaments shortens them and dimples the skin. Removing axillary nodes interrupts lymph return from the arm and can cause lymphedema, and the long thoracic nerve on the serratus anterior lies in the same field, so its injury produces a winged scapula."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains the vessels: the gonads arise near the kidneys and descend, so their arteries, veins, and lymph still run to the aorta, IVC, left renal vein, and para-aortic nodes, while pelvic organs that never moved are supplied from the internal iliac system. Physiology explains why vessel anatomy matters: the pampiniform plexus is a heat exchanger, so venous congestion becomes a fertility problem, and erection is a nitric oxide and cGMP event that depends on both nerves and endothelium, linking sexual function to diabetes and atherosclerosis. Autonomic organization explains the male sexual response, and somatic sacral segments explain continence and perineal sensation."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology applies these routes to spread. Following the artery back to its source predicts the first nodal station for testicular, ovarian, cervical, vulvar, scrotal, and breast tumors, but the rule has limits: the uterus drains partly along the round ligament to the groin, midline vulvar lesions can drain to both sides, ovarian cancer spreads mainly across the peritoneum, and prior surgery or skin invasion can open new lymphatic routes. Individual variation in arterial origins and nodal anatomy is common, so these patterns predict the usual case rather than every patient."}],"summary":["Gonadal arteries arise from the aorta at about L2 because the gonads descend from the upper lumbar region; the ovarian artery anastomoses with the uterine artery.","The right gonadal vein enters the IVC obliquely; the longer left vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle, so about 90% of varicoceles are left-sided.","A varicocele that does not empty when supine, or appears suddenly or only on the right, suggests venous obstruction such as a renal tumor or IVC obstruction.","The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac and crosses above the ureter about 1.5 to 2 cm lateral to the cervix, where clamping can ligate, cut, or devascularize the ureter.","Testis and ovary drain to para-aortic nodes; uterus, cervix, and upper vagina to iliac nodes; scrotum, vulva, and lower vagina to superficial inguinal nodes.","Erection is parasympathetic (S2 to S4, nitric oxide, cGMP, veno-occlusion), emission is sympathetic (about T11 to L2, alpha-1, bladder neck closure), and ejaculation is somatic (pudendal, bulbospongiosus).","Loss of sympathetic bladder neck closure causes retrograde ejaculation; cavernous nerve or arterial injury causes erectile dysfunction.","The pudendal nerve (S2 to S4) leaves through the greater sciatic foramen, crosses the sacrospinous ligament at the ischial spine, and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen to the pudendal canal.","The ischial spine is the landmark for pudendal block, which numbs the perineum but not uterine contraction pain (T10 to L1).","The breast is supplied by internal thoracic, lateral thoracic, thoracoacromial, and posterior intercostal arteries; over about 75% of its lymph goes to axillary nodes, with medial quadrants also draining to parasternal nodes."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Pelvis and Perineum: pelvic vessels, lymphatic drainage, nerves, and the ureter"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: posterior abdominal wall vessels and the spermatic cord"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax: the breasts, their vasculature and lymphatic drainage"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male, section on the male sexual act"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"The male reproductive system, section on erection and ejaculation"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System; The Breast"}]},"RE-6":{"unit":"RE-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01","title":"Reproductive Processes, Pregnancy, and Lactation","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the reproductive processes that board questions test as cause and effect: the linked ovarian and endometrial cycles, the vascular mechanism of erection, the endocrine partnership of corpus luteum, placenta, and fetus, the maternal adaptations to pregnancy, the insulin resistance behind gestational diabetes, lactation, and the positive feedback of labor. Questions usually give a hormone level, a laboratory value from a pregnant patient, or one step of a reflex and ask what comes next or whether a finding is normal. If you can explain why the luteal phase is fixed, why a PaCO2 of 40 mm Hg is abnormal in late pregnancy, and why progesterone withdrawal starts both menses and milk secretion, you can reason through most items.","prerequisites":["The hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis: GnRH pulses, LH, FSH, inhibin, and steroid feedback (RE-7)","Steroid synthesis from cholesterol, including 17-alpha-hydroxylase and aromatase (RE-8)","Histology of the ovary, endometrium, penis, and breast (RE-2)","Implantation and formation of the placenta (RE-1, RE-3)","Smooth muscle signaling through Gq and IP3, cAMP, and cGMP"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive hormones (RE-7)","how":"The switch of estradiol from negative to positive feedback that times ovulation is a property of the axis described in that chapter."},{"to":"Benign conditions of the penis and scrotum (RE-13-D)","how":"Erectile dysfunction and ischemic priapism are failures of the nitric oxide, cGMP, and veno-occlusion sequence explained here."},{"to":"Diseases of the placenta (RE-13-H)","how":"Molar pregnancy and preeclampsia distort the hCG pattern and the vascular adaptations that are normal in pregnancy."},{"to":"Metabolic endocrine disease (EN-12-D)","how":"Gestational diabetes follows the same model as type 2 diabetes: insulin resistance that the beta cells cannot overcome."},{"to":"Renal, respiratory, and cardiovascular physiology","how":"Pregnancy resets the normal ranges for creatinine, PaCO2, bicarbonate, hemoglobin, and blood pressure, so nonpregnant cutoffs mislead."}],"terms":[{"term":"Dominant follicle","def":"The antral follicle with the most FSH receptors and aromatase, which keeps growing while falling FSH causes the rest of its cohort to undergo atresia."},{"term":"LH surge","def":"The large midcycle rise in LH, triggered by sustained high estradiol, that completes oocyte meiosis I, ruptures the follicle, and luteinizes its cells."},{"term":"Corpus luteum","def":"The luteinized remnant of the ovulated follicle that secretes progesterone, estradiol, and inhibin A for about 14 days unless rescued by hCG."},{"term":"Luteal-placental shift","def":"The transfer of progesterone production from the corpus luteum to the placenta at about 7 to 9 weeks of gestation."},{"term":"Fetoplacental unit","def":"The cooperation of fetal adrenal, fetal liver, and placenta needed to make estriol, because no one of them has every required enzyme."},{"term":"Veno-occlusion","def":"Compression of the small veins draining the corpora cavernosa against the tunica albuginea as the sinusoids fill, which traps blood during erection."},{"term":"Dilutional anemia of pregnancy","def":"The fall in hemoglobin concentration that occurs because plasma volume expands proportionally more than red cell mass."},{"term":"Human placental lactogen (hPL)","def":"A syncytiotrophoblast hormone of the growth hormone and prolactin family, also called human chorionic somatomammotropin, that raises maternal insulin resistance and lipolysis."},{"term":"Secretory activation","def":"The onset of copious milk secretion about 2 to 3 days after delivery, triggered by the fall in progesterone once the placenta is delivered (also called lactogenesis II)."},{"term":"Milk ejection reflex","def":"Suckling-induced oxytocin release that contracts myoepithelial cells around the alveoli and pushes milk into the ducts (let-down)."},{"term":"Ferguson reflex","def":"The neuroendocrine reflex in which stretch of the cervix and upper vagina increases oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary."},{"term":"Functional progesterone withdrawal","def":"Loss of progesterone action on the term myometrium through receptor and local changes, even though maternal serum progesterone stays high."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C01","obj":"RE-6.1","title":"Ovarian and endometrial cycles and the fixed luteal phase","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The menstrual cycle is two linked cycles. The ovarian cycle (follicular phase, ovulation, luteal phase) produces the hormones, and the endometrium responds to them. Day 1 is the first day of menses, and normal adult cycles last about 21 to 35 days."},{"type":"h","text":"Follicular phase and the proliferative endometrium"},{"type":"p","text":"As the previous corpus luteum fails, falling estradiol, progesterone, and inhibin A release FSH, which recruits a cohort of antral follicles. Under the two-cell, two-gonadotropin arrangement, LH drives theca cells to make androstenedione, and FSH induces aromatase in granulosa cells, which converts it to estradiol. Rising estradiol and inhibin B then lower FSH, so only the follicle with the most FSH receptors and aromatase keeps growing; the others become atretic. Estradiol makes the functional layer of the endometrium regrow from the basal layer, with straight tubular glands, stromal mitosis, and new progesterone receptors, and it thins cervical mucus so sperm can pass."},{"type":"h","text":"Ovulation and the luteal phase"},{"type":"p","text":"When estradiol stays high (roughly 200 pg/mL or more for about 2 days), its feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary becomes positive and produces the LH surge. The surge restarts meiosis in the oocyte, which completes meiosis I and arrests in metaphase II, triggers proteases and prostaglandins that rupture the follicle about 34 to 36 hours after the surge begins, and luteinizes the granulosa and theca cells."},{"type":"p","text":"The corpus luteum secretes progesterone, estradiol, and inhibin A, peaking about 7 days after ovulation and suppressing new follicle growth. Progesterone converts the endometrium to the secretory pattern: coiled glands with glycogen vacuoles and then luminal secretion, edematous stroma, and coiled spiral arteries, ready for implantation about days 20 to 24 of a 28-day cycle. Progesterone also thickens cervical mucus and raises the hypothalamic temperature set point, so basal body temperature rises about 0.3 to 0.5 °C (0.5 to 0.9 °F) after ovulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Menses and why the luteal phase is fixed"},{"type":"p","text":"The corpus luteum has a programmed lifespan. With only baseline LH pulses it regresses after about 14 days unless hCG from an implanted embryo rescues it. Progesterone withdrawal releases endometrial prostaglandins, mainly PGF2-alpha, which constrict the spiral arteries; the ischemic functional layer breaks down through matrix metalloproteinases and sheds, while the basal layer, supplied by straight arteries, survives to regenerate it."},{"type":"p","text":"Because luteal lifespan is nearly constant, differences in cycle length come from the follicular phase, the time a follicle needs to be recruited and reach preovulatory size. Ovulation therefore occurs about 14 days before the next menses, not on day 14 of every cycle. A cycle without ovulation has no corpus luteum, so the endometrium sees unopposed estrogen and bleeds irregularly when it outgrows its support (RE-13-A)."},{"type":"table","title":"Ovarian and endometrial phases in a 28-day cycle","cols":["Days","Ovarian phase","Dominant hormones","Endometrial phase","Endometrial features"],"rows":[["1 to 5","Early follicular","Estradiol and progesterone low; FSH rising","Menstrual","Spiral artery constriction; functional layer sheds"],["6 to 13","Follicular","Estradiol rising; FSH falling","Proliferative","Straight glands; stromal mitosis"],["About 14","Ovulation","LH surge","Late proliferative","Longest, slightly tortuous glands; full proliferative thickness"],["15 to 28","Luteal (about 14 days)","Progesterone, estradiol, inhibin A","Secretory","Coiled glands with glycogen; edematous stroma; coiled spiral arteries"]],"caption":"Only the follicular phase and the matching proliferative phase change length when cycle length varies."},{"type":"example","title":"Timing ovulation in a 35-day cycle","text":"A woman has regular 35-day cycles. The luteal phase lasts about 14 days, so ovulation is near day 21 (35 minus 14) and the LH surge begins on about day 19 or 20. A serum progesterone drawn on day 21, the usual time for a 28-day cycle, falls at ovulation, when progesterone has barely begun to rise, and could wrongly suggest anovulation. The mid-luteal sample should be drawn about 7 days after ovulation, near day 28. Her basal body temperature would rise after day 21, and her extra 7 days are all follicular."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C02","obj":"RE-6.2","title":"Erection: nitric oxide, cGMP, and veno-occlusion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Erection is a hemodynamic event controlled by smooth muscle tone. In the flaccid state, sympathetic norepinephrine acting on alpha-1 receptors and intrinsic myogenic tone keep the cavernosal arteries, helicine arterioles, and trabecular smooth muscle around the lacunar spaces (sinusoids) of the corpora cavernosa contracted, so inflow is small."},{"type":"h","text":"Molecular mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Sexual stimulation increases parasympathetic outflow from S2 to S4 through the pelvic splanchnic and cavernous nerves. Nonadrenergic, noncholinergic (nitrergic) nerve terminals release nitric oxide (NO) made by neuronal NO synthase, and acetylcholine and shear stress activate endothelial NO synthase to add more. NO diffuses into the smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, which raises cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers cytosolic Ca2+ (less entry through L-type channels, K+ channel opening and hyperpolarization, Ca2+ sequestration) and activates myosin light chain phosphatase, so the muscle relaxes."},{"type":"flow","title":"From nerve signal to rigid erection","steps":["Parasympathetic (S2 to S4) and endothelial release of NO","NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase; cGMP rises","Protein kinase G lowers Ca2+; smooth muscle relaxes","Arterial inflow fills and expands the sinusoids","Sinusoids compress subtunical veins against tunica albuginea","Outflow falls; intracavernosal pressure nears arterial pressure","Ischiocavernosus contraction briefly pushes pressure above systolic"],"caption":"Erection needs both increased inflow and reduced outflow; relaxation of smooth muscle produces both."},{"type":"h","text":"Veno-occlusion and its limits"},{"type":"p","text":"As the sinusoids fill, they press the subtunical venular plexus against the inelastic tunica albuginea and stretch the tunica, which narrows the emissary veins passing through it. Outflow falls as inflow rises, so pressure climbs toward mean arterial pressure. The corpus spongiosum has a thin tunica and does not occlude, so the urethra stays open for ejaculation and the glans stays softer."},{"type":"p","text":"Erection ends when phosphodiesterase 5 (PDE5) hydrolyzes cGMP and sympathetic discharge, especially after ejaculation, contracts the smooth muscle again; the sinusoids collapse and the veins reopen. Emission is sympathetic, and expulsion uses rhythmic bulbospongiosus contraction through the pudendal nerve."},{"type":"h","text":"Failure explained by mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"Each step predicts a form of erectile dysfunction. Arteriogenic dysfunction comes from atherosclerosis and from endothelial dysfunction in diabetes, smoking, and hypertension, which reduce NO. Neurogenic dysfunction follows cavernous nerve injury in pelvic surgery, diabetic autonomic neuropathy, or sacral cord damage; reflex erections survive cord lesions above S2 because the sacral arc is intact. Veno-occlusive dysfunction occurs when a fibrotic or damaged tunica cannot compress the veins. Ischemic priapism is the opposite failure: sickled cells or other causes block outflow, and trapped deoxygenated blood becomes acidotic and damages the smooth muscle."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After bilateral cavernous nerve resection, would blocking PDE5 restore erections?","explain":"Little, if at all. PDE5 inhibition only slows breakdown of cGMP; it does not make cGMP. Without nitrergic nerve release of NO, guanylyl cyclase is not activated, so there is little cGMP to preserve.","check":"The molecular mechanism and the termination paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C03","obj":"RE-6.3","title":"hCG, the luteal-placental shift, and estriol from the fetoplacental unit","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast once implantation begins. It is a glycoprotein whose alpha subunit is identical to that of LH, FSH, and TSH; its beta subunit closely resembles LH beta but carries an extra glycosylated C-terminal peptide, which gives it a much longer half-life. hCG is detectable in maternal serum about 8 to 10 days after fertilization, roughly doubles every 2 days in early viable pregnancy, peaks at about 8 to 10 weeks, and then falls to a lower plateau."},{"type":"h","text":"Rescue of the corpus luteum"},{"type":"p","text":"hCG binds the LH/hCG receptor on luteal cells and keeps the corpus luteum secreting progesterone and estradiol beyond its usual 14 days. Progesterone maintains the decidualized endometrium and keeps the myometrium quiet, which is why menses do not occur. The placenta meanwhile makes progesterone from maternal LDL cholesterol, using side-chain cleavage enzyme and 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase. By about 7 to 9 weeks placental output is enough on its own (the luteal-placental shift). Losing the corpus luteum before the shift drops progesterone and ends the pregnancy; after it, the fall in hCG after week 10 does no harm."},{"type":"p","text":"hCG has other effects that board items use. It drives fetal Leydig cell testosterone in the first trimester, before fetal LH takes over, and at high levels it weakly stimulates the TSH receptor. Very high hCG suggests multiple gestation or molar pregnancy (RE-13-H); a slow rise suggests ectopic or failing pregnancy."},{"type":"h","text":"Why estriol needs the fetus"},{"type":"p","text":"The placenta lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase (CYP17), so it cannot turn pregnenolone or progesterone into androgens, yet it has abundant sulfatase and aromatase. It must therefore be supplied with C19 androgen precursors. The fetal zone of the fetal adrenal cortex, driven by fetal ACTH and low in 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, secretes large amounts of DHEA sulfate (DHEA-S). Estradiol and estrone come from DHEA-S of both maternal and fetal adrenals, but estriol requires 16-alpha-hydroxylation, which occurs in the fetal liver, so about 90% of estriol depends on the fetus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Estriol synthesis in the fetoplacental unit","steps":["Fetal adrenal fetal zone makes DHEA-S from cholesterol","Fetal liver 16-alpha-hydroxylates DHEA-S","Placental sulfatase removes the sulfate group","Placental 3-beta-HSD, aromatase, and 17-beta-HSD form estriol","Estriol enters maternal blood and urine"],"caption":"Each organ supplies an enzyme the others lack; maternal estriol reflects fetal adrenal, fetal liver, and placental function together."},{"type":"p","text":"Low maternal estriol therefore points to a problem anywhere in the unit: anencephaly (no hypothalamic-pituitary ACTH drive, so the fetal adrenal atrophies), fetal adrenal hypoplasia, fetal death, or placental sulfatase deficiency. Placental aromatase deficiency also lowers estrogen, and the accumulating androgens can virilize the mother and a female fetus. On second-trimester screening, low unconjugated estriol is one marker associated with trisomy 21 and 18."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a pregnancy with an anencephalic fetus at 30 weeks, what happens to maternal progesterone and to estriol?","explain":"Progesterone stays near normal because the placenta makes it from maternal cholesterol without fetal help. Estriol is very low because, without fetal ACTH, the fetal adrenal makes little DHEA-S.","check":"The two placental pathways and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C04","obj":"RE-6.4","title":"Maternal cardiovascular, renal, respiratory, and hemostatic adaptations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most maternal adaptations follow from early systemic vasodilation. Progesterone, relaxin, nitric oxide, and prostacyclin relax vascular smooth muscle, and the low-resistance uteroplacental circulation is added in parallel, so systemic vascular resistance (SVR) falls by about 20 to 30%. The resulting arterial underfilling activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (estrogen also raises hepatic angiotensinogen), and the osmotic thresholds for thirst and ADH reset lower. Sodium and water are retained, and plasma osmolality falls about 10 mOsm/kg."},{"type":"h","text":"Blood volume and cardiac output"},{"type":"p","text":"Plasma volume rises about 40 to 50%, while erythropoietin raises red cell mass only about 20 to 30%, so hemoglobin concentration falls (dilutional anemia) even though total hemoglobin rises. The extra volume buffers blood loss at delivery. Cardiac output rises 30 to 50%, first through stroke volume and later through heart rate. Because SVR falls proportionally more, blood pressure dips, diastolic more than systolic, and returns near baseline by term. After about 20 weeks, lying supine lets the uterus compress the inferior vena cava and reduce venous return (supine hypotension)."},{"type":"h","text":"Kidney and lungs"},{"type":"p","text":"Renal vasodilation, mediated largely by relaxin and NO, raises renal plasma flow and GFR, so serum creatinine and BUN fall, and the higher filtered glucose load can cause glucosuria at normal blood glucose. Progesterone relaxes ureteral smooth muscle and the uterus compresses the ureters, so mild dilation (more on the right) is normal and favors ascending infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Progesterone makes the respiratory center more sensitive to CO2. Tidal volume rises while respiratory rate changes little, so minute ventilation rises and PaCO2 falls; the kidney excretes bicarbonate to compensate, leaving a mild, chronic respiratory alkalosis. The lower maternal PaCO2 steepens the gradient for fetal CO2 to cross the placenta. The rising diaphragm lowers functional residual capacity by about 20% while oxygen consumption rises, so a pregnant patient desaturates quickly during apnea."},{"type":"h","text":"Hypercoagulability"},{"type":"p","text":"Estrogen increases hepatic synthesis of fibrinogen, factors VII, VIII, and X, and von Willebrand factor; protein S falls, and plasminogen activator inhibitors 1 and 2 (PAI-2 is placental) damp fibrinolysis. This protects against hemorrhage when the placenta separates but completes Virchow's triad with venous stasis and vessel injury at delivery. Deep vein thrombosis favors the left leg, where the right common iliac artery crosses the left common iliac vein, and risk peaks after delivery."},{"type":"table","title":"Maternal adaptations and the values that are normal in late pregnancy","cols":["System","Change","Main cause","Expected finding"],"rows":[["Blood","Plasma volume up more than red cell mass","RAAS activation; reset ADH and thirst thresholds","Hemoglobin lower limit about 11 g/dL (about 10.5 g/dL in the second trimester)"],["Heart","Cardiac output up 30 to 50%","Higher stroke volume, then heart rate","Heart rate 10 to 20 per minute higher; flow murmur and S3 common"],["Vessels","SVR down about 20 to 30%","Progesterone, relaxin, NO, prostacyclin; placental circuit","Lowest blood pressure in the second trimester"],["Kidney","GFR up about 50%","Relaxin- and NO-mediated renal vasodilation","Creatinine about 0.4 to 0.8 mg/dL; lower BUN; possible glucosuria"],["Lungs","Minute ventilation up 30 to 50%; FRC down","Progesterone sensitizes the respiratory center","PaCO2 about 28 to 32 mm Hg; HCO3- about 18 to 22 mEq/L; pH about 7.40 to 7.45"],["Clotting","More fibrinogen and factors; less protein S; less fibrinolysis","Estrogen; placental PAI-2","Higher fibrinogen; higher risk of DVT and PE, greatest postpartum"]],"caption":"Nonpregnant reference ranges misclassify these values."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading an arterial blood gas at 34 weeks","text":"A woman at 34 weeks has pH 7.45, PaCO2 30 mm Hg, and HCO3- 20 mEq/L. This is the expected compensated respiratory alkalosis: progesterone has lowered PaCO2 and the kidney has lowered bicarbonate. Weeks later, during an asthma attack, she has pH 7.38, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, and HCO3- 23 mEq/L. Each value is normal outside pregnancy, but against her baseline of 30 mm Hg, a PaCO2 of 40 means ventilation is failing to keep up and CO2 is being retained."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C05","obj":"RE-6.5","title":"Placental hormones, insulin resistance, and gestational diabetes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Maternal fuel metabolism changes in two stages. In the first half of pregnancy, estrogen and progesterone promote beta cell hyperplasia and insulin secretion while insulin sensitivity stays normal, so the mother stores fat. In the second half, as the placenta grows, insulin sensitivity falls by roughly half by the third trimester, and the mother shifts toward burning fat so that glucose and amino acids are left for the fetus. Glucose crosses the placenta by facilitated diffusion through GLUT1, down its concentration gradient; insulin does not cross."},{"type":"h","text":"How placental hormones cause insulin resistance"},{"type":"p","text":"Human placental lactogen (hPL), also called human chorionic somatomammotropin, is made by the syncytiotrophoblast and belongs to the growth hormone and prolactin family. Its secretion is proportional to placental mass, so it rises steadily and is highest in the third trimester. hPL impairs insulin signaling after the receptor in muscle and fat, reducing glucose uptake, and it stimulates lipolysis, so free fatty acids become a larger share of maternal fuel. Placental growth hormone (which replaces pituitary growth hormone during pregnancy and raises IGF-1), progesterone, a rise in free cortisol, prolactin, and inflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha add to the resistance."},{"type":"p","text":"The fetus draws glucose continuously, so fasting maternal glucose is lower than outside pregnancy and ketosis develops quickly during a fast. After meals, resistance makes glucose rise higher and stay up longer. A normal pancreas meets this by increasing insulin secretion about two- to threefold."},{"type":"h","text":"Gestational diabetes"},{"type":"p","text":"Gestational diabetes develops when the beta cells cannot raise insulin output enough to match the resistance, usually in women whose beta cell reserve was already limited by obesity, family history, prior gestational diabetes, or polycystic ovary syndrome. It appears in the second half of pregnancy, when hPL is rising, which is why screening is commonly done at 24 to 28 weeks. It usually resolves within days of delivery because hPL has a short half-life and disappears with the placenta, but it reveals limited beta cell reserve and a high later risk of type 2 diabetes."},{"type":"flow","title":"From placental hormones to neonatal hypoglycemia","steps":["Placental mass grows; hPL and other placental hormones rise","Maternal insulin resistance increases","Beta cells cannot raise insulin enough; maternal hyperglycemia","Excess glucose crosses the placenta through GLUT1","Fetal beta cell hyperplasia and fetal hyperinsulinemia","Insulin acts as a fetal growth factor: macrosomia","Cord clamped: glucose supply stops, insulin stays high, neonatal hypoglycemia"],"caption":"Maternal glucose reaches the fetus but maternal insulin does not, so the fetus responds with its own insulin."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal hyperinsulinemia explains the other findings. Insulin drives fat deposition in the trunk and shoulders (risk of shoulder dystocia) and organ growth, it opposes the cortisol-driven maturation of surfactant production, and fetal hyperglycemia causes osmotic diuresis and polyhydramnios. Distinguish pregestational diabetes: hyperglycemia during organogenesis (weeks 3 to 8) raises the risk of malformations such as cardiac defects, neural tube defects, and caudal regression. Gestational diabetes begins after organogenesis, so it causes growth and metabolic problems rather than malformations."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C06","obj":"RE-6.6","title":"Lactation: preparation, secretion, ejection, and suppression of ovulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"During pregnancy, estrogen elongates and branches the ducts and progesterone builds the lobules and alveoli, with prolactin, hPL, cortisol, insulin, and growth hormone supporting both. Estrogen also causes lactotroph hyperplasia, so prolactin rises roughly tenfold by term. By mid-pregnancy the alveolar cells can secrete small amounts of colostrum, which is rich in protein and secretory IgA."},{"type":"h","text":"Why milk comes in after delivery"},{"type":"p","text":"Despite high prolactin, copious milk is not secreted in pregnancy because high placental progesterone blocks the secretory response of the alveolar cells, including synthesis of alpha-lactalbumin, the subunit that makes lactose synthase. Lactose is the main osmotic determinant of milk volume. Delivery of the placenta removes progesterone, and with prolactin, insulin, and cortisol present, secretory activation follows in about 2 to 3 days. Retained placental tissue keeps progesterone up and delays it."},{"type":"h","text":"Prolactin for synthesis, oxytocin for ejection"},{"type":"p","text":"Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone under dominant tonic inhibition: dopamine from arcuate (tuberoinfundibular) neurons reaches the lactotrophs through the portal vessels and acts on D2 receptors. Suckling sends afferent signals from the nipple to the hypothalamus that reduce dopamine release, producing a prolactin surge that drives milk synthesis for the next feed. Milk removal also matters; milk left in the alveoli locally suppresses further secretion."},{"type":"p","text":"The same afferent input excites oxytocin neurons in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei. Oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary acts on Gq-coupled receptors of myoepithelial cells around the alveoli, which contract and push milk into the ducts within about a minute. This let-down can become conditioned to an infant's cry and is inhibited by pain and stress. Oxytocin also contracts the uterus, causing cramping during feeds and speeding involution."},{"type":"flow","title":"Suckling reflexes after delivery","steps":["Infant suckles; nipple afferents reach the hypothalamus","Dopamine release falls; anterior pituitary prolactin surges","Prolactin drives milk synthesis for the next feed","Oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary","Myoepithelial cells contract; milk ejected","GnRH pulses suppressed; low LH and FSH; no ovulation"],"caption":"One afferent signal drives three outputs: synthesis, ejection, and suppression of ovulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Suckling and high prolactin suppress the GnRH pulse generator, partly by inhibiting kisspeptin neurons, so LH pulses are too infrequent to mature a follicle or trigger a surge, and estradiol stays low (lactational amenorrhea). The effect depends on frequent suckling; as feeds space out, GnRH pulses return and ovulation can occur before the first menses. The same mechanism explains why a prolactinoma or a dopamine-blocking drug causes galactorrhea with amenorrhea and low LH and FSH, and why failure to lactate is often the first sign of postpartum pituitary infarction (Sheehan syndrome)."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormones of lactation","cols":["Hormone","Source","Role"],"rows":[["Estrogen","Placenta","Duct growth; lactotroph hyperplasia"],["Progesterone","Placenta","Lobuloalveolar growth; blocks milk secretion until delivery"],["Prolactin","Anterior pituitary (tonic dopamine inhibition)","Milk synthesis; surges with suckling"],["Oxytocin","Posterior pituitary (paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei)","Myoepithelial contraction and milk ejection; uterine contraction"],["hPL, cortisol, insulin","Placenta, adrenal, pancreas","Permissive for alveolar development and milk synthesis"]],"caption":"Prolactin makes milk; oxytocin moves milk that is already made."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman has high prolactin on day 5 after delivery but little milk, and ultrasound shows retained placental tissue. Why is milk delayed?","explain":"Retained trophoblast keeps secreting progesterone, which still blocks the secretory response of the alveolar cells. Prolactin is present, but secretory activation needs progesterone withdrawal.","check":"Why milk comes in after delivery."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-06-01-C07","obj":"RE-6.7","title":"Parturition as a positive feedback loop","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"For most of pregnancy, progesterone keeps the myometrium quiet: it limits gap junctions and oxytocin receptors and reduces excitability, while the collagen-rich cervix stays firm and closed. In humans, maternal serum progesterone does not fall before labor. Instead the myometrium undergoes functional progesterone withdrawal, through changes in progesterone receptor isoforms and local metabolism, while estrogen action increases."},{"type":"h","text":"Preparing the uterus"},{"type":"p","text":"Placental corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) rises steeply in the third trimester. It stimulates fetal ACTH, so the fetal adrenal makes more DHEA-S for placental estrogen synthesis and more cortisol, which in turn increases placental CRH and prostaglandin synthesis in the fetal membranes. Estrogen induces contraction-associated proteins: oxytocin receptors, which rise many-fold by term, gap junctions made of connexin 43, which let the myometrium contract as one unit, and prostaglandin-synthesizing enzymes. Plasma oxytocin changes little before labor; the uterus becomes far more sensitive to it."},{"type":"h","text":"How oxytocin and prostaglandins contract the uterus"},{"type":"p","text":"The oxytocin receptor is Gq-coupled. Phospholipase C forms IP3, which releases Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and Ca2+ also enters through L-type channels; Ca2+-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase and the muscle contracts. Oxytocin also stimulates the decidua and amnion to make prostaglandins. PGF2-alpha strengthens contractions, and PGE2 ripens the cervix by remodeling collagen and increasing hyaluronan and water, so the cervix softens, thins, and dilates. Blocking cyclooxygenase delays labor for this reason."},{"type":"h","text":"The Ferguson reflex"},{"type":"p","text":"As the presenting part descends, it stretches the cervix and upper vagina. Afferent signals travel through the spinal cord to the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei, which release more oxytocin. Stronger contractions push the fetus harder against the cervix, producing more stretch. In negative feedback the response cancels the stimulus; here the response amplifies it, driving the system to an endpoint. The loop ends only when delivery removes the stretch."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Positive feedback of labor","steps":["Presenting part stretches the cervix","Afferents reach the hypothalamus (Ferguson reflex)","Posterior pituitary releases more oxytocin","Oxytocin and prostaglandins raise myometrial Ca2+","Stronger contractions push the fetus against the cervix"],"caption":"Each turn of the loop strengthens the next until delivery removes the stimulus."},{"type":"p","text":"After delivery, continued oxytocin keeps the myometrium contracted. Its interlacing fibers compress the vessels of the placental bed, the main mechanism of postpartum hemostasis, so uterine atony from overdistension, prolonged labor, or retained placenta leads to postpartum hemorrhage. Overdistension also explains early labor in twin pregnancy and polyhydramnios, because stretch increases myometrial excitability."},{"type":"table","title":"The uterus during quiescence and at the onset of labor","cols":["Feature","Most of pregnancy","Onset of labor"],"rows":[["Progesterone action on myometrium","Dominant; keeps it quiet","Functionally withdrawn despite high serum levels"],["Estrogen action","Restrained","Increased; induces contraction-associated proteins"],["Oxytocin receptors","Few","Greatly increased"],["Gap junctions (connexin 43)","Few","Many; coordinated contraction"],["Prostaglandin synthesis","Low","High in decidua and membranes"],["Cervix","Firm, closed, collagen-rich","Softened, effaced, dilating"]],"caption":"Labor begins when the uterus becomes responsive, not mainly when oxytocin levels rise."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the stage. The basal layer of the endometrium survives each menses because straight arteries supply it, the tunica albuginea makes veno-occlusion possible, and the fetal adrenal fetal zone and fetal liver supply enzymes the placenta lacks. Biochemistry explains the hormones: missing CYP17 makes the placenta depend on fetal DHEA-S, lactose synthase links progesterone withdrawal to milk volume, and second messengers explain relaxation (NO and cGMP) and contraction (Gq, IP3, and Ca2+). Immunology adds secretory IgA in colostrum and maternal IgG transfer, covered in RE-11."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology meet at the interpretation of values. Pregnancy shifts the normal ranges for hemoglobin, creatinine, PaCO2, bicarbonate, fibrinogen, and blood pressure, so a finding is judged against pregnancy norms. Many disorders are these normal mechanisms in excess or failure: gestational diabetes when beta cells cannot meet hPL-driven resistance, venous thrombosis from protective hypercoagulability, hyperprolactinemic amenorrhea from the suckling pathway, and postpartum hemorrhage from uterine atony. Some values, such as hCG doubling time and screening cutoffs, vary with assay and guideline."}],"summary":["The luteal phase lasts about 14 days because the corpus luteum has a fixed lifespan without hCG, so cycle length varies through the follicular phase and ovulation occurs about 14 days before the next menses.","Estradiol builds the proliferative endometrium and, when sustained, triggers the LH surge; progesterone makes it secretory, and progesterone withdrawal causes spiral artery constriction and menses.","Erection is smooth muscle relaxation: NO activates guanylyl cyclase, cGMP lowers Ca2+, inflow fills the sinusoids, and veins are compressed against the tunica albuginea.","hCG acts on the LH receptor to keep the corpus luteum making progesterone until the placenta takes over at about 7 to 9 weeks.","The placenta lacks CYP17, so estriol requires fetal adrenal DHEA-S and fetal liver 16-alpha-hydroxylation; low estriol signals fetal adrenal, liver, or placental failure.","Pregnancy raises plasma volume more than red cell mass, raises cardiac output and GFR, lowers SVR, creatinine, and PaCO2, and increases coagulability.","hPL and other placental hormones cause insulin resistance that peaks in the third trimester; gestational diabetes appears when beta cells cannot compensate.","Maternal glucose crosses the placenta but insulin does not, so fetal hyperinsulinemia causes macrosomia and neonatal hypoglycemia.","Progesterone withdrawal at delivery starts milk secretion; suckling raises prolactin by lowering dopamine, releases oxytocin for ejection, and suppresses GnRH.","Labor is positive feedback: cervical stretch raises oxytocin (Ferguson reflex), and oxytocin and prostaglandins strengthen contractions until delivery."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pregnancy and Lactation"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Male Reproductive Physiology; The Female Reproductive System; Fertilization, Pregnancy, and Lactation"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third Month to Birth: The Fetus and Placenta"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract"}]},"RE-7":{"unit":"RE-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01","title":"Reproductive Hormones and the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Gonadal Axis","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how the hypothalamus, anterior pituitary, and gonads regulate one another through gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), luteinizing hormone (LH), follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), the sex steroids, inhibin, and prolactin. It covers why GnRH must pulse, how each gonadotropin acts on a different gonadal cell, how estradiol switches from negative to positive feedback, how binding proteins decide free hormone levels, what each steroid does in its target tissues, how puberty starts, and how prolactin shuts the axis down. Board questions usually change one link in the axis and ask you to predict every downstream hormone level, so the goal is to reason through feedback rather than memorize patterns.","prerequisites":["Hypothalamic-pituitary anatomy and the hypophyseal portal circulation","Peptide hormone signaling through Gs and Gq coupled receptors","Steroid hormone synthesis from cholesterol and nuclear receptor signaling","Negative and positive feedback in endocrine control","Histology of the testis and ovarian follicle"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive hormone biochemistry (RE-8)","how":"The enzymes split between theca and granulosa cells, and 5-alpha-reductase in target tissues, are the steroid pathway applied to specific cells."},{"to":"Menstrual cycle and reproductive processes (RE-6)","how":"The switch from negative to positive estradiol feedback described here is what times ovulation within the cycle."},{"to":"Endocrine system: pituitary and thyroid","how":"Pituitary adenomas, stalk compression, and primary hypothyroidism alter prolactin and therefore gonadal function."},{"to":"Metabolism and insulin resistance","how":"Insulin suppresses hepatic SHBG synthesis, linking obesity and insulin resistance to raised free testosterone."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal growth and bone","how":"Estrogen closes epiphyses and preserves bone mass in both sexes, so sex steroid deficiency causes tall stature or osteoporosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"GnRH pulse generator","def":"The network of hypothalamic neurons that releases GnRH in discrete bursts into the portal blood about every 1 to 2 hours."},{"term":"Gonadotroph","def":"The anterior pituitary cell that secretes both LH and FSH in response to GnRH."},{"term":"Receptor desensitization","def":"Loss of cellular response during continuous agonist exposure, through receptor uncoupling from signaling and reduced receptor number."},{"term":"Inhibin","def":"A peptide from Sertoli and granulosa cells (inhibin B) and the corpus luteum (inhibin A) that selectively suppresses pituitary FSH secretion."},{"term":"Androgen-binding protein (ABP)","def":"A Sertoli cell protein, induced by FSH, that binds testosterone and keeps its concentration high in the seminiferous tubules."},{"term":"Aromatase","def":"The enzyme (CYP19) that converts androgens to estrogens, androstenedione to estrone and testosterone to estradiol."},{"term":"Sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG)","def":"A liver glycoprotein that binds testosterone and dihydrotestosterone with high affinity and estradiol with lower affinity."},{"term":"Bioavailable testosterone","def":"The free plus albumin-bound fraction of testosterone, which can leave the capillary and enter target cells."},{"term":"5-alpha-reductase","def":"The enzyme that converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), a more potent ligand for the androgen receptor."},{"term":"Kisspeptin","def":"A hypothalamic peptide that stimulates GnRH neurons through the KISS1R receptor and is required for puberty and the LH surge."},{"term":"Thelarche, pubarche, menarche","def":"Onset of breast development, onset of pubic hair, and the first menstrual period."},{"term":"Hyperprolactinemia","def":"A serum prolactin above the reference range, commonly from pregnancy, dopamine-blocking drugs, a lactotroph adenoma, or loss of hypothalamic dopamine."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C01","obj":"RE-7.1","title":"Pulsatile GnRH and why continuous GnRH suppresses the axis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"GnRH is a 10 amino acid peptide made by a small population of neurons scattered mainly in the arcuate nucleus and preoptic area of the hypothalamus. Their axons end at the median eminence, where GnRH enters the hypophyseal portal capillaries and reaches the gonadotrophs at high concentration. Because GnRH has a half-life of only a few minutes, each burst produces a sharp rise and fall at the pituitary. In adults the bursts recur about every 1 to 2 hours, more often in the follicular phase and less often in the luteal phase, when progesterone slows the pulse generator."},{"type":"p","text":"The GnRH receptor on gonadotrophs is coupled to Gq. Binding activates phospholipase C, which produces inositol trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol; IP3 releases intracellular Ca2+, triggering exocytosis of stored LH and FSH, and protein kinase C drives transcription of the gonadotropin subunit genes. The gap between pulses lets the receptor and its signaling pathway recover. Pulse frequency also steers synthesis: faster pulses favor transcription of the LH beta subunit, and slower pulses favor FSH beta. Slow luteal pulses, together with the fall in inhibin A and estradiol as the corpus luteum regresses, help FSH rise first at the start of a new cycle, while faster pulses before ovulation favor LH."},{"type":"p","text":"When GnRH or a long-acting GnRH agonist is present continuously, gonadotrophs first release their stored LH and FSH, causing a flare of gonadotropins and sex steroids over the first one to two weeks. Constant occupancy then uncouples the receptor from Gq signaling and reduces receptor number, so the cells stop responding. LH and FSH fall, and testosterone or estradiol falls to castrate levels, a reversible state often called medical castration. Restoring physiologic pulses reactivates a dormant axis, which shows that the pattern of GnRH, not its amount, is the signal."},{"type":"flow","title":"Continuous GnRH exposure to hypogonadism","steps":["Continuous GnRH agonist occupies gonadotroph receptors","Stored LH and FSH released: transient flare","Receptors uncouple from Gq and are downregulated","LH and FSH secretion falls","Leydig, theca, and granulosa stimulation is lost","Testosterone or estradiol falls to castrate levels"],"caption":"The same agonist that stimulates when pulsed suppresses when constant; the flare comes first, suppression follows."},{"type":"p","text":"The pattern of GnRH delivery explains several disorders. In Kallmann syndrome, GnRH neurons fail to migrate from the olfactory placode into the hypothalamus during development, so patients have low LH, FSH, and sex steroids together with anosmia. In functional hypothalamic amenorrhea from energy deficit or stress, the pulse generator slows, giving low or inappropriately normal gonadotropins and low estradiol."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man receives a depot GnRH agonist. What happens to his serum testosterone in the first week and after one month?","explain":"Testosterone rises in the first week because stored LH is released (the flare). After about a month, desensitized gonadotrophs secrete little LH, so testosterone falls to castrate levels.","check":"The continuous GnRH flow and the paragraph on receptor desensitization."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C02","obj":"RE-7.2","title":"The male axis: Leydig and Sertoli cells and their separate feedback loops","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"LH and FSH act on different cells of the testis. LH binds a Gs-coupled receptor on Leydig cells in the interstitium; cAMP and protein kinase A increase the steroidogenic acute regulatory (StAR) protein, which moves cholesterol to the inner mitochondrial membrane, the rate-limiting step, where side-chain cleavage begins testosterone synthesis. FSH binds a Gs-coupled receptor on Sertoli cells inside the seminiferous tubules, where it stimulates androgen-binding protein (ABP), inhibin B, and aromatase, and supports the germ cells that Sertoli cells nurse."},{"type":"p","text":"Spermatogenesis needs both hormones. Testosterone diffuses from Leydig cells into the tubules, where ABP holds it at a concentration many times higher than in serum (often quoted as about 50 to 100 fold). Sertoli cells, not germ cells, carry the androgen receptor, so testosterone supports spermatogenesis by acting on Sertoli cells."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Two feedback loops of the male axis","steps":["Hypothalamus releases GnRH pulses","Pituitary secretes LH and FSH","LH drives Leydig cell testosterone","FSH drives Sertoli cell ABP and inhibin B","Testosterone and estradiol slow GnRH pulses and lower LH","Inhibin B lowers pituitary FSH selectively"],"caption":"Each gonadotropin is regulated mainly by the product of its own target cell."},{"type":"p","text":"Testosterone feeds back at both the hypothalamus, where it slows GnRH pulse frequency, and the pituitary, where it reduces the LH response. Part of this feedback in men is carried by estradiol formed by aromatization of testosterone. Because GnRH stimulates both gonadotropins, testosterone lowers FSH somewhat as well, but the selective brake on FSH is inhibin B, which acts directly on gonadotrophs. These separate loops let a lab panel localize damage, as the table shows."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting male axis labs","cols":["Situation","LH","FSH","Testosterone","Inhibin B"],"rows":[["Primary testicular failure (for example Klinefelter syndrome)","High","High","Low","Low"],["Selective Sertoli cell or germ cell damage","Normal","High","Normal","Low"],["Hypothalamic or pituitary failure","Low or inappropriately normal","Low or inappropriately normal","Low","Low"],["Exogenous testosterone","Low","Low","Serum high, intratesticular low","Low"],["Androgen insensitivity (receptor defect)","High","Normal or mildly high","High","Variable"]],"caption":"Gonadotropins are high when the testis fails and low when the hypothalamus or pituitary fails or is suppressed."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: exogenous testosterone and infertility","text":"A 28-year-old man using injected testosterone for muscle gain has small, soft testes and azoospermia. Serum testosterone is high, so negative feedback slows GnRH pulses and suppresses LH. Without LH, Leydig cells stop producing testosterone inside the testis, and intratesticular testosterone falls far below the level spermatogenesis requires even though serum testosterone is above normal. FSH and inhibin B are also suppressed, so Sertoli cell support fails. Testicular volume falls because the seminiferous tubules, which form most of the testis, lose their germ cells. The expected labs are high serum testosterone, low LH, low FSH, and low inhibin B."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C03","obj":"RE-7.3","title":"Two-cell ovarian steroidogenesis and the switch to positive feedback","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neither cell type in a follicle can make estradiol alone. Theca interna cells, outside the basement membrane, have LH receptors, access to LDL cholesterol from nearby capillaries, and the enzymes cholesterol side-chain cleavage, 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, and 17-alpha-hydroxylase with 17,20-lyase activity (CYP17). LH stimulation therefore produces androstenedione. Granulosa cells, an avascular layer inside the basement membrane, lack CYP17 but express aromatase when FSH stimulates them. Androstenedione diffuses inward, aromatase converts it to estrone, and 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase converts estrone to estradiol."},{"type":"table","title":"The two-cell model","cols":["Feature","Theca interna cell","Granulosa cell"],"rows":[["Location","Outside the basement membrane, vascularized","Inside the basement membrane, avascular"],["Gonadotropin receptor","LH","FSH; LH receptors added late in the dominant follicle"],["Key enzyme","CYP17 (17-alpha-hydroxylase, 17,20-lyase)","Aromatase (CYP19), induced by FSH"],["Main product","Androstenedione","Estradiol (and inhibin B)"]],"caption":"LH provides androgen substrate in theca cells; FSH provides the aromatase that converts it in granulosa cells."},{"type":"p","text":"As the dominant follicle grows, FSH induces LH receptors on its granulosa cells, so the follicle can keep growing while rising estradiol and inhibin B lower FSH and starve the smaller follicles. At low to moderate concentrations, estradiol exerts negative feedback, mainly lowering FSH. When estradiol stays above about 200 pg/mL for about 2 days, the hypothalamic and pituitary response reverses. Estradiol now acts through kisspeptin neurons to increase GnRH release and raises gonadotroph GnRH receptor number and sensitivity, so a large LH surge results, with a smaller FSH rise. A small rise in progesterone from luteinizing granulosa cells around the onset of the surge amplifies it."},{"type":"flow","title":"Follicular phase to ovulation","steps":["FSH induces aromatase and growth in the dominant follicle","Estradiol rises; negative feedback lowers FSH","Estradiol above about 200 pg/mL for about 2 days","Feedback switches to positive; GnRH and gonadotroph sensitivity rise","Midcycle LH surge","Oocyte completes meiosis I, arrests at metaphase II","Follicle ruptures about 34 to 36 hours after surge onset","Corpus luteum secretes progesterone, estradiol, and inhibin A"],"caption":"The follicle times its own ovulation: only a mature follicle makes enough estradiol for long enough to trigger the surge."},{"type":"p","text":"The LH surge also induces proteolytic enzymes and prostaglandins that weaken the follicle wall, and it converts granulosa and theca cells into luteal cells. Progesterone from the corpus luteum, together with estradiol and inhibin A, then slows GnRH pulses and suppresses LH and FSH, so the positive feedback state cannot recur in the luteal phase."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If FSH were absent but LH normal, what would happen to ovarian androgen and estradiol output?","explain":"Theca cells would still make androstenedione under LH, but granulosa cells would lack aromatase and follicles would not mature. Estradiol would be low and no LH surge would occur, while androgen output would be preserved or relatively high.","check":"The two-cell table and the first paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C04","obj":"RE-7.4","title":"Sex steroid transport, SHBG, and free testosterone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sex steroids are lipophilic and travel in plasma mostly bound to proteins. In men, about 1 to 3% of testosterone is free, roughly a third to a half is loosely bound to albumin, and the remainder is tightly bound to sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). Women have higher SHBG, so a larger share of their testosterone is SHBG-bound. SHBG binds DHT and testosterone with higher affinity than estradiol. Albumin binds with low affinity and releases hormone readily as blood passes through capillaries, so the free plus albumin-bound fraction, called bioavailable testosterone, determines tissue effect. SHBG-bound hormone is largely unavailable. Progesterone and cortisol are carried mainly by corticosteroid-binding globulin and albumin rather than SHBG."},{"type":"p","text":"SHBG is made in the liver, and its synthesis responds to hormones and metabolic state. Estrogen and thyroid hormone increase it; insulin and androgens suppress it. Hyperinsulinemia lowers SHBG directly, which is why obesity, insulin resistance, and polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) are associated with low SHBG."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that change SHBG","cols":["SHBG raised by","SHBG lowered by"],"rows":[["Estrogen (pregnancy, oral estrogen)","Androgens, including anabolic steroids"],["Hyperthyroidism","Insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia"],["Hepatic cirrhosis","Obesity"],["Aging in men","Hypothyroidism"],["Anorexia nervosa","Glucocorticoid and growth hormone excess"]],"caption":"Changes in SHBG alter free hormone fractions before the axis has time to compensate."},{"type":"p","text":"The consequence of an SHBG change depends on whether feedback can compensate. In a woman, ovarian and adrenal androgen output is not tightly regulated by serum testosterone, so a fall in SHBG raises free testosterone and can cause hirsutism and acne with a normal total testosterone. In a man with an intact axis, a rise in SHBG first lowers free testosterone; the hypothalamus senses this, LH rises, and total testosterone climbs until free testosterone returns toward normal. Because SHBG binds testosterone more tightly than estradiol, a rise in SHBG also increases the free estradiol to free testosterone ratio, which helps explain gynecomastia in cirrhosis and hyperthyroidism."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: normal total, high free testosterone","text":"A 24-year-old woman with obesity, acanthosis nigricans, and hirsutism has total testosterone in the upper normal range. Her fasting insulin is high. Insulin suppresses hepatic SHBG synthesis, so SHBG is low and a larger fraction of her testosterone is free. Free testosterone is therefore raised even though the total is normal, and hair follicles and sebaceous glands respond to the free hormone. Insulin also acts with LH on theca cells to increase androgen production. A total testosterone alone would underestimate her androgen exposure; free or bioavailable testosterone, or total testosterone with SHBG, reflects it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C05","obj":"RE-7.5","title":"Actions of testosterone, DHT, estrogen, and progesterone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone (DHT) act on the same intracellular androgen receptor, a ligand-activated transcription factor. DHT binds it with higher affinity and dissociates more slowly, so it is the more potent androgen. Which hormone acts in a tissue depends on whether that tissue expresses 5-alpha-reductase. The type 2 isoenzyme is concentrated in genital skin, the urogenital sinus and prostate, and scalp and beard hair follicles; the type 1 isoenzyme is found in sebaceous glands and the liver. Testosterone acts directly where 5-alpha-reductase is scarce, and some tissues respond to testosterone after aromatase converts it to estradiol."},{"type":"table","title":"Target tissue actions of sex steroids","cols":["Hormone","Main actions"],"rows":[["Testosterone","Fetal Wolffian ducts (epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles); spermatogenesis via Sertoli cells; muscle mass; libido; laryngeal growth and voice deepening; erythropoiesis; LH feedback"],["DHT","Fetal external genitalia (penis, scrotum) and prostate; adult prostate growth; male-pattern scalp hair loss; beard and body hair; sebum production"],["Estrogen","Proliferative endometrium and induction of progesterone receptors; breast duct growth; thin, watery, stretchy cervical mucus; vaginal epithelial maturation; epiphyseal closure and bone preservation in both sexes; raised hepatic SHBG, thyroxine-binding globulin, angiotensinogen, and clotting factors; higher HDL and lower LDL"],["Progesterone","Secretory endometrium and decidualization; thick, scant cervical mucus; myometrial quiescence; breast lobuloalveolar development; slower GnRH pulses; raised ventilation; basal body temperature rise of about 0.3 to 0.5 °C (0.5 to 0.9 °F)"]],"caption":"DHT acts where 5-alpha-reductase is abundant; testosterone acts directly elsewhere; several bone and feedback effects in men require aromatization to estradiol."},{"type":"p","text":"5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency shows the division clearly. A 46,XY infant has testes, normal anti-Mullerian hormone (so no uterus), and normal Wolffian derivatives because testosterone itself acts on the Wolffian ducts. The external genitalia, which need DHT, are undervirilized or appear female at birth, and the prostate is small. At puberty, the large rise in testosterone partly overcomes the defect and causes virilization of the genitalia and muscle, while scalp hair loss and acne are minimal. In complete androgen insensitivity, by contrast, the receptor itself fails, so neither testosterone nor DHT acts and Wolffian structures are absent."},{"type":"p","text":"Estrogen and progesterone act in sequence. Estrogen drives endometrial proliferation and induces progesterone receptors, so progesterone can act only on a primed endometrium; progesterone then converts it to a secretory lining suitable for implantation and opposes further proliferation. Unopposed estrogen, without cyclic progesterone, therefore causes endometrial hyperplasia. Progesterone raises the hypothalamic temperature set point, which is why the basal temperature rise confirms that ovulation has already occurred. Estrogen is also the main sex steroid that closes epiphyseal plates and restrains osteoclasts in both sexes, so men with aromatase or estrogen receptor deficiency remain tall with open epiphyses and low bone density despite normal testosterone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C06","obj":"RE-7.6","title":"Puberty as reactivation of the GnRH pulse generator","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The GnRH pulse generator is active in the fetus and again for several months after birth, a period called minipuberty, in which LH, FSH, and sex steroids rise briefly. Through childhood it is then held quiet by central inhibition, with gonadotropins low even though the pituitary and gonads can respond. Puberty begins when this restraint is lifted. Hypothalamic neurons that release kisspeptin and neurokinin B drive GnRH neurons through the KISS1R receptor, and loss of function mutations in kisspeptin signaling cause absent puberty. Adequate energy stores are permissive: leptin from adipose tissue signals that nutrition is sufficient, which is why severe undernutrition or very low body fat delays puberty."},{"type":"p","text":"The pulse generator wakes first during sleep. The earliest laboratory sign of puberty is the appearance of LH pulses in deep sleep, while daytime LH remains low; over the following years pulses spread across the whole day. Gonadal steroids then produce the physical changes. Adrenarche, the rise in adrenal androgens such as DHEA sulfate, is a separate process that is independent of GnRH and contributes to pubic and axillary hair."},{"type":"table","title":"Usual pubertal sequence","cols":["Order","Girls","Boys"],"rows":[["First sign","Thelarche (breast budding), about age 8 to 13","Testicular enlargement to 4 mL or more, about age 9 to 14"],["Next","Pubarche (pubic hair)","Pubarche and penile growth"],["Then","Growth spurt (peak height velocity, early in puberty)","Growth spurt (later in puberty than in girls)"],["Last","Menarche, about 2 to 3 years after thelarche","Spermarche, voice change, facial hair"]],"caption":"Boys start puberty about 2 years later and reach a higher peak height velocity, which explains most of the adult height difference."},{"type":"p","text":"In girls, ovarian estradiol stimulates breast ducts before it has built enough endometrium to produce withdrawal bleeding, so breast budding precedes menarche, and early cycles are often anovulatory because the positive feedback mechanism matures last. In boys, FSH-driven growth of the seminiferous tubules, which make up most of testicular volume, enlarges the testes before testosterone produces visible virilization. Pubarche can occasionally come first in girls when adrenarche is early."},{"type":"p","text":"Measuring LH separates disorders of pubertal timing. Precocious puberty is usually defined as onset before about age 8 in girls and 9 in boys. In central (GnRH-dependent) precocious puberty, the pulse generator has started early, so LH is pubertal and rises after GnRH stimulation. In peripheral (GnRH-independent) precocious puberty, sex steroids come from the gonads, adrenals, or an outside source, so feedback suppresses LH and FSH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-07-01-C07","obj":"RE-7.7","title":"Prolactin regulation and hyperprolactinemic hypogonadism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Prolactin is the only anterior pituitary hormone whose dominant hypothalamic control is inhibitory. Tuberoinfundibular dopamine neurons release dopamine into the portal blood, and dopamine acts on D2 receptors on lactotrophs. D2 is coupled to Gi, so it lowers cAMP and opens K+ channels, which reduces prolactin synthesis, secretion, and lactotroph proliferation. Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) and vasoactive intestinal peptide stimulate prolactin release, and estrogen increases lactotroph number and prolactin synthesis, which is why the pituitary enlarges in pregnancy. Prolactin regulates itself by short-loop feedback, raising hypothalamic dopamine release."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the normal signal is inhibitory, anything that blocks dopamine production, delivery, or action raises prolactin. The upper reference limit is about 20 to 25 ng/mL in women and somewhat lower in men, depending on the assay."},{"type":"table","title":"Mechanisms of hyperprolactinemia","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Lactotroph adenoma (prolactinoma)","Autonomous secretion; level roughly tracks tumor size"],["Pituitary stalk compression by a nonsecreting mass","Dopamine cannot reach lactotrophs; usually a modest rise"],["D2 receptor blocking drugs","Dopamine cannot act on lactotrophs"],["Pregnancy and nursing","Estrogen-driven lactotroph hyperplasia; suckling reflex"],["Primary hypothyroidism","Raised TRH stimulates lactotrophs"],["Chronic kidney disease","Reduced prolactin clearance"]],"caption":"Pregnancy is the most common physiologic cause of a high prolactin in a woman of reproductive age."},{"type":"p","text":"High prolactin suppresses GnRH pulse release, largely by inhibiting kisspeptin neurons that drive GnRH neurons. LH and FSH therefore fall or remain inappropriately normal, and estradiol or testosterone falls. This is secondary (central) hypogonadism, and it has the same physiology as the normal lactational suppression of ovulation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Prolactinoma to hypogonadism","steps":["Lactotroph adenoma secretes excess prolactin","Prolactin inhibits kisspeptin neurons","GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude fall","LH and FSH low or inappropriately normal","Estradiol or testosterone falls","Amenorrhea, infertility, low libido, bone loss"],"caption":"Hypogonadism in hyperprolactinemia is central: the gonads are normal but under-stimulated."},{"type":"p","text":"Women present with oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, infertility, and galactorrhea, because prolactin acts on breast tissue already primed by estrogen. Men present with low libido, erectile dysfunction, and infertility; galactorrhea is uncommon and gynecomastia may occur. Women usually notice menstrual change early and so are found with small tumors, whereas men often are diagnosed later with macroadenomas that cause headache or bitemporal hemianopia by compressing the optic chiasm. Low estrogen in either sex reduces bone density."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with amenorrhea has a mildly raised prolactin, a high TSH, and a low free T4. What links the thyroid result to her prolactin?","explain":"Low thyroid hormone removes feedback on the hypothalamus, so TRH rises. TRH stimulates lactotrophs as well as thyrotrophs, and the raised prolactin suppresses GnRH pulses, causing amenorrhea.","check":"The table of hyperprolactinemia mechanisms and the paragraph on prolactin control."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the wiring. GnRH neurons originate in the olfactory placode, which is why failure of their migration links hypogonadism with anosmia in Kallmann syndrome, and the portal circulation explains why stalk compression raises prolactin while it can lower the other anterior pituitary hormones. In the gonads, the basement membrane that separates theca from granulosa cells and the blood-testis barrier formed by Sertoli cells explain why steroid synthesis is split between cell types and why spermatogenesis depends on locally concentrated testosterone. Biochemistry adds the enzymes, aromatase and 5-alpha-reductase, that decide which steroid acts in each tissue, and the hepatic synthesis of SHBG that ties reproductive hormones to insulin, thyroid hormone, and liver disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows from feedback, so the level of a lesion can be read from whether gonadotropins move with or against the sex steroids. The limits are real: single hormone measurements miss pulsatility, total testosterone can mislead when SHBG changes, and reference ranges depend on age, sex, cycle phase, and assay, so values are read against the physiological state of the patient."}],"summary":["Pulsatile GnRH stimulates LH and FSH through Gq signaling; continuous GnRH causes a brief flare, then receptor desensitization and low gonadotropins and sex steroids.","Fast GnRH pulses favor LH and slow pulses favor FSH.","LH drives Leydig cell testosterone; FSH drives Sertoli cell ABP, inhibin B, and aromatase; testosterone mainly suppresses LH and inhibin B selectively suppresses FSH.","Exogenous testosterone suppresses LH and FSH, lowers intratesticular testosterone, and causes testicular atrophy with poor spermatogenesis.","Theca cells (LH, CYP17) make androstenedione; granulosa cells (FSH, aromatase) convert it to estradiol.","Estradiol above about 200 pg/mL for about 2 days switches feedback to positive and triggers the LH surge; ovulation follows about 34 to 36 hours after surge onset.","Free plus albumin-bound testosterone is bioavailable; estrogen and thyroid hormone raise SHBG, while insulin, obesity, and androgens lower it and raise the free fraction.","DHT drives external genitalia, prostate, and male-pattern hair loss; testosterone drives Wolffian ducts, spermatogenesis, muscle, and libido; estrogen closes epiphyses in both sexes.","Puberty begins with sleep-associated LH pulses as kisspeptin reactivates GnRH; girls usually progress thelarche, pubarche, growth spurt, menarche.","Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin; hyperprolactinemia suppresses GnRH and causes amenorrhea, galactorrhea, and central hypogonadism."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male; Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sexual differentiation, the male reproductive system, and the female reproductive system"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Steroid hormones and their transport"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Endocrine System, pituitary adenomas and hyperprolactinemia"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Hypothalamus and Pituitary Gland; Disorders of the Female and Male Reproductive Tracts"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Urogenital system, development of the genital ducts and external genitalia"}]},"RE-8":{"unit":"RE-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01","title":"Reproductive Hormone Biochemistry","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows sex steroids from cholesterol to their active forms and out through the liver, then covers the glycoprotein hormones and the chemistry of semen. Each topic is built around one enzyme step, one shared structure, or one clearance route, so a single defect explains a whole clinical picture: ambiguous genitalia in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, endometrial hyperplasia in obesity, hyperthyroidism in molar pregnancy, and feminization in cirrhosis. Board questions usually give the phenotype or the lab pattern and ask for the missing or overactive enzyme, so the goal is to read the pathway well enough to predict what accumulates and what goes missing.","prerequisites":["Cholesterol structure, LDL receptor uptake, and the steroid ring numbering (carbons 1 to 27)","Nuclear receptor signaling and Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A signaling","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and gonadotropin feedback (RE-7)","Sexual differentiation of the Wolffian and Mullerian ducts and external genitalia (RE-1)","Phase I and phase II hepatic biotransformation"],"connections":[{"to":"Reproductive hormones (RE-7)","how":"The enzymes in this chapter make the hormones whose secretion, transport on SHBG, and feedback RE-7 describes."},{"to":"Hormone synthesis and degradation (EN-8)","how":"The gonads share StAR, side-chain cleavage, 3-beta-HSD, and CYP17 with the adrenal cortex, so several enzyme defects affect cortisol and sex steroids together."},{"to":"Hepatic diseases and disorders (GI-13-C)","how":"Loss of hepatic steroid clearance and portosystemic shunting explain the endocrine signs of cirrhosis."},{"to":"Diseases of the placenta (RE-13-H)","how":"Hydatidiform mole and choriocarcinoma secrete enough hCG to stimulate the thyroid and the ovarian theca."},{"to":"Infectious diseases and hyperplasia of the prostate (RE-13-F)","how":"PSA is a product of prostatic epithelium, so it reflects prostate tissue mass and barrier integrity in BPH, prostatitis, and carcinoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"StAR (steroidogenic acute regulatory protein)","def":"A mitochondrial protein that transfers cholesterol from the outer to the inner mitochondrial membrane, the rate-limiting step of steroid synthesis."},{"term":"CYP11A1 (cholesterol side-chain cleavage enzyme)","def":"The inner mitochondrial enzyme, also called desmolase, that removes six carbons from cholesterol to form pregnenolone."},{"term":"CYP17","def":"A single endoplasmic reticulum enzyme with 17-alpha-hydroxylase and 17,20-lyase activities that converts 21-carbon steroids into 19-carbon androgens."},{"term":"Aromatase (CYP19)","def":"The enzyme that removes carbon 19 and aromatizes the A ring, converting androgens into estrogens."},{"term":"5-alpha-reductase","def":"The enzyme that reduces testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT); type 2 predominates in genital skin and prostate, type 1 in sebaceous skin and liver."},{"term":"Two-cell, two-gonadotropin model","def":"The division of ovarian estrogen synthesis between LH-driven theca cells, which make androgen, and FSH-driven granulosa cells, which aromatize it."},{"term":"Unopposed estrogen","def":"Estrogen exposure without adequate progesterone, which leaves the endometrium in continuous proliferation."},{"term":"Glycoprotein hormone","def":"A heterodimer of a common alpha subunit and a hormone-specific beta subunit, namely hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH."},{"term":"Conjugation","def":"Phase II attachment of glucuronic acid or sulfate to a steroid, making it water soluble for excretion in urine or bile."},{"term":"SHBG (sex hormone-binding globulin)","def":"A hepatic plasma protein that binds testosterone and DHT more tightly than estradiol."},{"term":"PSA (prostate-specific antigen)","def":"A kallikrein serine protease secreted by prostatic epithelium that liquefies the semen coagulum by cleaving semenogelin."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01-C01","obj":"RE-8.1","title":"From cholesterol to progesterone, androgens, and estrogens","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sex steroids are made on demand rather than stored, so secretion rate equals synthesis rate. Their substrate is cholesterol (27 carbons), taken up mainly from plasma LDL. Products are classified by carbon number: progestins have 21 carbons, androgens 19, and estrogens 18."},{"type":"p","text":"The rate-limiting step is transfer of cholesterol from the outer to the inner mitochondrial membrane by StAR, where CYP11A1 cleaves the side chain to form pregnenolone, the precursor of every steroid hormone. LH acts on Leydig and theca cells through Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A, acutely increasing StAR activity and, over hours to days, transcription of CYP11A1 and later enzymes."},{"type":"p","text":"In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, two enzymes decide the fate of pregnenolone. 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (3-beta-HSD) oxidizes the 3-hydroxyl group and moves the double bond from the B ring to the A ring (delta-5 to delta-4), making progesterone. CYP17 adds a hydroxyl at carbon 17 (17-alpha-hydroxylase), then cuts the bond between carbons 17 and 20 (17,20-lyase), leaving a 19-carbon androgen. Human 17,20-lyase prefers 17-hydroxypregnenolone, so the main gonadal route runs through dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA), which 3-beta-HSD converts to androstenedione."},{"type":"p","text":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (17-beta-HSD) reduces the 17-keto group, making testosterone from androstenedione and estradiol from estrone. Aromatase removes carbon 19 and aromatizes the A ring, making estradiol from testosterone and estrone from androstenedione, so every estrogen passes through an androgen first."},{"type":"table","title":"Enzymes of sex steroid synthesis","cols":["Enzyme","Reaction","Main sites"],"rows":[["StAR","Cholesterol into inner mitochondria (rate-limiting)","Gonads, adrenal cortex"],["CYP11A1 (side-chain cleavage)","Cholesterol to pregnenolone","All steroidogenic cells"],["3-beta-HSD","Pregnenolone to progesterone; DHEA to androstenedione","Gonads, adrenal, corpus luteum, placenta"],["CYP17","Pregnenolone to DHEA (hydroxylase, then lyase)","Leydig, theca, adrenal; not granulosa or placenta"],["17-beta-HSD","Androstenedione to testosterone; estrone to estradiol","Leydig (type 3), granulosa (type 1)"],["Aromatase (CYP19)","Testosterone to estradiol; androstenedione to estrone","Granulosa, placenta, adipose, brain, bone"]],"caption":"The enzyme set of each cell type determines its product."},{"type":"p","text":"Which enzymes a cell expresses decides what it secretes. Leydig cells secrete testosterone. In the follicle, theca cells have LH receptors and CYP17 but little aromatase, while avascular granulosa cells have FSH receptors and aromatase but no CYP17, so the two must cooperate. The vascularized corpus luteum takes up LDL cholesterol and makes large amounts of progesterone. The placenta lacks CYP17 and builds estrogens from fetal adrenal DHEA sulfate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Two-cell synthesis of estradiol in the follicle","steps":["LH acts on theca cells; StAR and CYP11A1 rise","Pregnenolone becomes DHEA through CYP17","3-beta-HSD converts DHEA to androstenedione","Androstenedione diffuses into the granulosa layer","FSH-induced aromatase makes estrone","17-beta-HSD type 1 converts estrone to estradiol"],"caption":"Granulosa cells cannot make androgen, so theca androgen supply limits follicular estradiol."},{"type":"p","text":"A block causes deficiency of everything downstream and diverts precursor into the branches still open. Defects in enzymes shared with the adrenal cortex also disturb cortisol and mineralocorticoids."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting the effect of each enzyme defect","cols":["Deficient enzyme","What is lost","Key consequence"],"rows":[["StAR or CYP11A1","Nearly all steroids","Adrenal insufficiency with salt wasting; 46,XY appear female"],["3-beta-HSD","Cortisol, aldosterone, potent sex steroids","Salt wasting; 46,XY undervirilized; 46,XX mildly virilized"],["CYP17","Cortisol and all sex steroids","Mineralocorticoid excess, hypertension, low K+; no puberty; 46,XY appear female"],["17-beta-HSD type 3","Testicular testosterone","46,XY undervirilized at birth, partial virilization at puberty"],["Aromatase","Estrogens","Maternal and 46,XX fetal virilization; tall stature from unfused epiphyses"]],"caption":"The top three rows are shared with the adrenal cortex."},{"type":"example","title":"Working through CYP17 deficiency","text":"A 16-year-old with female genitalia has primary amenorrhea, no breast development, hypertension, and hypokalemia. Without CYP17, pregnenolone can go only toward progesterone and the mineralocorticoid branch. Low cortisol raises ACTH, which drives production of 11-deoxycorticosterone, a mineralocorticoid that retains Na+ and wastes K+; volume expansion suppresses renin and aldosterone. No androgen means no estrogen, so puberty fails and LH and FSH are high. If the karyotype is 46,XY, no testosterone or DHT was made, but Sertoli cell AMH still removed the uterus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01-C02","obj":"RE-8.2","title":"5-alpha-reductase and dihydrotestosterone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone (DHT) act through the same intracellular androgen receptor, a nuclear receptor that binds androgen response elements on DNA. DHT binds the receptor with higher affinity and dissociates more slowly, so it gives a stronger, more sustained signal at the same concentration. DHT also cannot be aromatized, so its effects are purely androgenic. 5-alpha-reductase reduces the A ring double bond of testosterone to make DHT inside target cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Two isoenzymes matter. Type 2 predominates in genital skin, the prostate and urogenital sinus, and scalp and beard follicles; type 1 predominates in sebaceous glands, nongenital skin, and liver. In the male fetus, the Wolffian ducts lie beside the testis and respond to high local testosterone, forming the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles. The genital tubercle, urogenital folds, labioscrotal swellings, and urogenital sinus are farther away and need DHT to form the penis, scrotum, and prostate."},{"type":"p","text":"5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency is autosomal recessive and shows only in 46,XY individuals. The testes are normal, so Sertoli cell AMH removes the Mullerian ducts and Leydig cell testosterone builds normal Wolffian structures. With little DHT the external genitalia are ambiguous or appear female at birth, with a small phallus, bifid scrotum, perineoscrotal hypospadias, and a blind vaginal pouch, and the testes lie in the inguinal canals or labioscrotal folds."},{"type":"p","text":"At puberty, testosterone rises to adult levels, high enough to act on the androgen receptor directly and helped by type 1 activity in skin. The phallus grows, muscle mass increases, the voice deepens, and the testes may descend. DHT-dependent features stay weak: the prostate remains small, facial and body hair is sparse, temporal hair recession and acne are absent. Breast development does not occur because testosterone and estradiol are in normal male proportion. Labs show normal or high testosterone, normal or mildly raised LH, low DHT, and a high testosterone to DHT ratio, which widens after hCG stimulation."},{"type":"table","title":"5-alpha-reductase deficiency versus complete androgen insensitivity","cols":["Feature","5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency","Complete androgen insensitivity"],"rows":[["Karyotype and gonads","46,XY; testes","46,XY; testes"],["Defect","Cannot make DHT","Androgen receptor nonfunctional"],["Wolffian structures","Present","Absent"],["Mullerian structures","Absent (AMH)","Absent (AMH)"],["External genitalia at birth","Ambiguous or female","Female, short blind vagina"],["Puberty","Virilization; no breasts","Breast development from aromatized testosterone; scant pubic and axillary hair"],["Hormones","Normal or mildly raised LH; high testosterone to DHT ratio","High LH, high testosterone, high estradiol for a male"]],"caption":"Wolffian ducts show whether testosterone could act: present when only DHT is missing, absent when the receptor fails."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An adult man takes a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor for several months. Which tissues change, and which do not?","explain":"The prostate shrinks and serum PSA falls by about half, and scalp hair loss slows, because these depend on DHT. Libido, muscle mass, and spermatogenesis are largely preserved because they are testosterone actions, and testosterone rises slightly as less is converted.","check":"The type 2 tissue list and the testosterone-dependent features that persist in the deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01-C03","obj":"RE-8.3","title":"Peripheral aromatization, obesity, and unopposed estrogen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Aromatase is expressed outside the gonads, most importantly in adipose stromal cells (preadipocytes), and also in skin, bone, brain, and liver. Adipose aromatase converts circulating androstenedione, mainly from the adrenal cortex and, after menopause, the ovarian stroma, into estrone. Estrone is a weaker estrogen, but target tissues convert some of it to estradiol with 17-beta-HSD. Because the enzyme needs only a circulating precursor, estrone output scales with fat mass rather than ovarian function, and fractional conversion increases with age and obesity."},{"type":"table","title":"Main source of estrogen by physiological state","cols":["State","Main estrogen","Source"],"rows":[["Reproductive-age woman","Estradiol","Granulosa cells of the dominant follicle and corpus luteum"],["Postmenopausal woman","Estrone","Aromatization of adrenal and ovarian stromal androstenedione in fat"],["Pregnancy","Estriol (with estradiol)","Placenta, using fetal adrenal DHEA sulfate"],["Adult man","Estradiol","Mostly peripheral aromatization of testosterone; a small share from the testis"]],"caption":"Outside the reproductive years in women, and in men, most estrogen is made peripherally."},{"type":"p","text":"In the cycling endometrium, estrogen drives glandular and stromal proliferation through estrogen receptor alpha. Progesterone from the corpus luteum opposes it: it reduces estrogen receptor expression, induces 17-beta-HSD type 2, which converts estradiol to the weaker estrone, converts the glands to a secretory state, and, when it falls at the end of the cycle, triggers orderly shedding. Peripheral estrone is continuous and is not paired with progesterone. In obesity, especially with anovulation as in polycystic ovary syndrome or after menopause, the endometrium proliferates without interruption."},{"type":"flow","title":"Obesity to endometrial hyperplasia","steps":["Excess adipose tissue increases total aromatase","Adrenal androstenedione is converted to estrone","Insulin resistance lowers SHBG, raising free estrogen","No ovulation, so no luteal progesterone","Continuous estrogen-driven proliferation without shedding","Hyperplasia, then atypia and endometrioid carcinoma"],"caption":"Progression to atypical hyperplasia and endometrioid carcinoma involves acquired mutations such as PTEN loss on this estrogen-driven background."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically this appears as heavy or irregular bleeding, or postmenopausal bleeding, from a thickened unstable endometrium. Obesity is a major risk factor for endometrioid (type I) endometrial carcinoma, while serous (type II) carcinoma arises on an atrophic endometrium and is not estrogen driven. Peripheral estrogen also raises the risk of postmenopausal estrogen receptor positive breast cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"In men, adipose aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol. Estrogen stimulates ductal epithelium and periductal stroma in the breast, producing gynecomastia, which is true glandular tissue palpable beneath the areola, unlike the diffuse fat of pseudogynecomastia. Estradiol also exerts negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering LH and therefore testicular testosterone, while low SHBG from insulin resistance lowers total testosterone further."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01-C04","obj":"RE-8.4","title":"The glycoprotein hormones: shared alpha, specific beta","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH are heterodimers of two noncovalently joined, glycosylated subunits. The alpha subunit is identical in all four and is encoded by one gene; each beta subunit is encoded separately and determines which receptor the hormone activates. Neither subunit is active alone, so the free subunits have no hormonal effect. All four receptors are G protein-coupled receptors that signal mainly through Gs and cAMP."},{"type":"table","title":"The four glycoprotein hormones","cols":["Hormone","Source","Receptor and target cells","Main action"],"rows":[["LH","Pituitary gonadotropes","LH/hCG receptor; Leydig, theca, luteal cells","Androgen synthesis; ovulation; corpus luteum function"],["FSH","Pituitary gonadotropes","FSH receptor; Sertoli and granulosa cells","Spermatogenesis support; aromatase induction; follicle growth"],["TSH","Pituitary thyrotropes","TSH receptor; thyroid follicular cells","Thyroid hormone synthesis and release; thyroid growth"],["hCG","Placental syncytiotrophoblast","LH/hCG receptor","Rescues the corpus luteum to maintain progesterone in early pregnancy"]],"caption":"The beta subunit decides the receptor; hCG and LH share one receptor."},{"type":"p","text":"The hCG and LH beta subunits are highly similar, so hCG binds the LH receptor. Early in pregnancy this lets trophoblast hCG keep the corpus luteum secreting progesterone until the placenta takes over progesterone production at about 8 to 10 weeks. The hCG beta subunit also has a carboxy-terminal extension of about 24 amino acids carrying O-linked sugars rich in sialic acid, which slows clearance and gives hCG a half-life of roughly a day or more, compared with about 20 minutes for LH. Serum hCG roughly doubles every 2 days in early pregnancy and peaks at about 8 to 10 weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the alpha subunit is shared, an assay for it cannot distinguish the hormones. Pregnancy tests and tumor marker assays therefore use antibodies against beta-hCG, usually including its unique carboxy-terminal region, so they do not cross-react with LH."},{"type":"p","text":"Receptor specificity is high but not absolute. hCG is a weak agonist at the TSH receptor, many thousands of times less potent than TSH, which matters only when hCG is very high. In normal pregnancy the first-trimester hCG peak raises free T4 slightly and lowers TSH, the mirror image of the hCG curve. In a hydatidiform mole, especially a complete mole, or a choriocarcinoma, the abnormal trophoblast secretes far more hCG, and the result can be overt hyperthyroidism. The same excess drives the LH receptor on ovarian theca cells, producing bilateral theca lutein cysts, and hCG-secreting testicular germ cell tumors stimulate Leydig cell aromatase, causing gynecomastia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Molar pregnancy to hyperthyroidism","steps":["Proliferating molar trophoblast secretes very high hCG","hCG beta resembles TSH beta enough for weak binding","Mass action activates TSH receptors on thyrocytes","Gs and cAMP increase T4 and T3 secretion","Thyroid hormone suppresses pituitary TSH","Tachycardia, tremor, heat intolerance; high free T4, low TSH"],"caption":"The thyroid is driven from outside the pituitary axis, so TSH is suppressed while thyroid hormone is high, and the hyperthyroidism resolves as hCG falls."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A healthy woman at 10 weeks of gestation has a TSH slightly below the nonpregnant reference range. Is her pituitary failing?","explain":"No. hCG peaks near 10 weeks and weakly stimulates the TSH receptor, raising thyroid hormone a little and suppressing TSH by normal feedback. As hCG falls in the second trimester, TSH returns toward normal.","check":"The paragraph on hCG cross-activation of the TSH receptor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01-C05","obj":"RE-8.5","title":"Hepatic inactivation of sex steroids and the feminization of cirrhosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Steroids are lipid soluble and would be reabsorbed by the kidney if excreted unchanged, so the liver makes them water soluble in two phases. Phase I reactions, largely cytochrome P450 hydroxylations and 17-beta-HSD oxidation, reduce activity: estradiol is oxidized to estrone and hydroxylated to catechol estrogens, and testosterone is oxidized at carbon 17 and reduced in the A ring to androsterone and etiocholanolone. Phase II reactions then attach glucuronic acid (UDP-glucuronosyltransferases) or sulfate (sulfotransferases). The conjugates are excreted in urine, where androgen metabolites appear as 17-ketosteroids, or in bile."},{"type":"p","text":"Biliary estrogen conjugates can be deconjugated by bacterial beta-glucuronidase in the gut and reabsorbed, an enterohepatic circulation that returns part of the estrogen to the blood. Conjugates leave hepatocytes into bile or into plasma for renal excretion, so urinary metabolites reflect total production. Because the liver also makes SHBG, it controls both how much steroid is cleared and how much circulates free."},{"type":"p","text":"Cirrhosis shifts the balance toward estrogen through several mechanisms acting together. Reduced hepatocyte mass lowers estrogen inactivation. Portosystemic shunting carries adrenal androstenedione past the liver to adipose tissue and skin, where it is aromatized to estrone. SHBG usually rises, and because SHBG binds testosterone more tightly than estradiol, free testosterone falls more than free estradiol. In alcoholic cirrhosis, ethanol is also directly toxic to Leydig cells and suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary axis. The net result is a high ratio of free estrogen to free androgen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cirrhosis to hyperestrogenism","steps":["Hepatocyte loss and portosystemic shunting","Less hepatic inactivation and conjugation of estrogen","Androstenedione bypasses the liver and is aromatized peripherally","SHBG rises and binds testosterone preferentially","Free estrogen to free testosterone ratio rises","Feminizing skin, breast, and gonadal signs"],"caption":"Several small shifts in synthesis, clearance, and binding add up to relative estrogen excess."},{"type":"table","title":"Signs of hyperestrogenism in cirrhosis","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Gynecomastia","Estrogen stimulates breast ductal epithelium and stroma"],["Spider angiomas","Estrogen-related dilation of a central arteriole with radiating vessels, usually on the upper trunk and face"],["Palmar erythema","Estrogen-related vasodilation in the thenar and hypothenar skin"],["Testicular atrophy","Estrogen feedback lowers LH; low free testosterone; direct ethanol toxicity to Leydig cells"],["Loss of male hair pattern","Low free androgen at hair follicles; female pubic hair distribution"]],"caption":"Spider angiomas and palmar erythema also appear in pregnancy, another high-estrogen state, supporting the estrogen mechanism."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with cirrhosis has a normal total testosterone. Why might he still be hypogonadal?","explain":"Total testosterone includes the SHBG-bound fraction. With SHBG raised, more testosterone is bound and free testosterone, the active fraction, is low, while free estradiol is relatively preserved.","check":"The SHBG paragraph and the testicular atrophy row."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-08-01-C06","obj":"RE-8.6","title":"Chemistry of semen and the meaning of PSA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Semen is mostly accessory gland fluid; sperm from the testes and epididymis contribute only about 5% of its volume. Normal ejaculate volume is about 1.5 to 5 mL with a pH of about 7.2 to 8.0, which buffers the acidic vagina. Each gland adds its own chemical marker, so semen chemistry can localize a problem in the male tract."},{"type":"table","title":"Contributions to semen","cols":["Source","Share of volume","Key products and roles"],"rows":[["Seminal vesicles","About 60 to 70%","Fructose (sperm energy), prostaglandins, semenogelin (forms the coagulum)"],["Prostate","About 20 to 30%","PSA (liquefies the coagulum), citrate, zinc, acid phosphatase"],["Testes and epididymis","About 5%","Sperm"],["Bulbourethral glands","Small","Mucus that lubricates the urethra and neutralizes residual acidic urine before ejaculation"]],"caption":"Fructose marks seminal vesicle fluid; PSA, citrate, and zinc mark prostatic fluid."},{"type":"p","text":"Seminal vesicle fluid enters through the ejaculatory ducts and supplies fructose, which sperm use for glycolysis. Semenogelin forms a gel coagulum immediately after ejaculation that holds the sperm. PSA, a kallikrein-family serine protease secreted by prostatic luminal epithelium under androgen control, cleaves semenogelin and liquefies the coagulum within about 15 to 30 minutes, freeing motile sperm. Prostatic citrate chelates calcium and contributes to buffering, and zinc has antibacterial activity and stabilizes sperm chromatin."},{"type":"p","text":"PSA is secreted into the gland lumen, separated from blood by the epithelium, a basal cell layer, and a basement membrane. Only a small amount normally reaches serum, where most is bound to alpha-1-antichymotrypsin. Serum PSA rises whenever there is more prostate tissue or a breach of this barrier, so it is specific to the prostate but not to cancer. It rises in benign prostatic hyperplasia (more epithelium), acute prostatitis (inflammation disrupts the epithelium), prostatic infarction, after instrumentation, and in carcinoma, where the disorganized glands and absent basal layer let PSA leak directly into the stroma and capillaries. Because prostate growth is DHT dependent, reducing DHT lowers PSA, linking this section to C02."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing obstruction from semen chemistry","text":"A man has azoospermia, ejaculate volume of 0.8 mL, acidic pH, and absent fructose. Fructose comes only from the seminal vesicles, so its absence means their fluid is missing. Without that alkaline majority, the low volume is mostly acidic prostatic fluid, which explains the pH. Two lesions fit: obstruction of the ejaculatory ducts, or congenital absence of the vas deferens, often with absent or hypoplastic seminal vesicles, as seen with CFTR mutations. Compare a man with azoospermia but normal volume, pH, and fructose: the seminal vesicles and ejaculatory ducts are intact, so the problem lies in the testis or the epididymis and vas above the seminal vesicles."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and embryology explain the developmental disorders. One cholesterol-derived pathway feeds the adrenal, testis, ovary, and placenta, and each tissue's enzyme set decides its output, so shared enzymes (StAR, CYP11A1, 3-beta-HSD, CYP17) cause combined adrenal and gonadal disease while peripheral enzymes (5-alpha-reductase, aromatase) cause isolated reproductive phenotypes. The Wolffian ducts, the external genitalia, and the prostate differ in which androgen they need, so the anatomy of a 46,XY infant reveals which step failed. The same logic applies to the glycoprotein hormones: one shared alpha subunit and similar beta subunits explain both normal corpus luteum rescue and the thyroid and ovarian effects of trophoblastic disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology connect peripheral metabolism to disease. Adipose aromatase, hepatic clearance, and SHBG binding all change the ratio of free estrogen to free androgen without any change in the gonads, which is why obesity, cirrhosis, and menopause share features such as gynecomastia or endometrial hyperplasia. Total hormone levels can mislead when SHBG changes, and PSA reflects prostate tissue and barrier integrity rather than malignancy itself."}],"summary":["StAR transfer of cholesterol into mitochondria is rate limiting; CYP11A1 makes pregnenolone, the precursor of every steroid, and LH stimulates both through cAMP.","3-beta-HSD makes progesterone and androstenedione, CYP17 converts 21-carbon steroids to 19-carbon androgens, 17-beta-HSD makes testosterone and estradiol, and aromatase makes estrogens from androgens.","Theca cells (LH, CYP17) make androstenedione and granulosa cells (FSH, aromatase) convert it to estradiol; the placenta lacks CYP17 and uses fetal DHEA sulfate.","DHT binds the androgen receptor more strongly than testosterone and is required for the external genitalia and prostate; Wolffian ducts need only testosterone.","5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency gives a 46,XY infant ambiguous genitalia with normal Wolffian ducts, virilization at puberty, and a high testosterone to DHT ratio.","Adipose aromatase turns androstenedione into estrone, the main postmenopausal estrogen; without progesterone this causes endometrial hyperplasia, and in men gynecomastia and low LH.","hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH share one alpha subunit; beta subunits give specificity, so assays measure beta-hCG and very high hCG can activate TSH and LH receptors.","The liver inactivates sex steroids and conjugates them to glucuronides and sulfates; cirrhosis raises the free estrogen to androgen ratio through reduced clearance, shunting to aromatase, and higher SHBG.","Seminal vesicles supply fructose and semenogelin; the prostate supplies PSA, citrate, and zinc, and PSA liquefies the coagulum.","Serum PSA rises with more prostate tissue or barrier disruption, so BPH, prostatitis, and carcinoma all raise it."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Reproductive and Hormonal Functions of the Male; Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Male and female reproductive physiology, steroid hormone synthesis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Female Genital Tract (endometrial hyperplasia and gestational trophoblastic disease); The Lower Urinary Tract and Male Genital System (prostate)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, section on cirrhosis"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Male Reproductive Tract"}]},"RE-9":{"unit":"RE-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01","title":"Reproductive Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Reproductive System (REPR)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"Reproduction runs on specific nutrients and fuels, and this chapter follows each one to its mechanism. It covers folate in DNA synthesis and neural tube closure, the iron cost of pregnancy, lactose and immune proteins in milk, glucose transfer across the placenta, zinc and vitamin A in sexual and embryonic development, the energy signal that permits GnRH pulsatility, and the cholesterol supply for sex steroids. Board questions typically describe a deficiency, an excess, or a lab pattern in a mother, fetus, newborn, or athlete and ask you to predict the consequence or name the step involved. Each answer follows from knowing what the nutrient does at the molecular level and when in development it is needed.","prerequisites":["Nucleotide synthesis and the role of one-carbon carriers","Iron absorption, transport, and storage (ferroportin, transferrin, ferritin, hepcidin)","Placental structure and the barrier between maternal and fetal blood","Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and GnRH pulsatility (RE-6)","Steroidogenic enzyme pathway from cholesterol to estradiol (RE-8)"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology","how":"The same folate and iron mechanisms that affect pregnancy explain megaloblastic and microcytic anemias in any patient."},{"to":"Embryology","how":"Folate and retinoic acid act on neurulation and neural crest patterning, which explains the timing and type of birth defects."},{"to":"Endocrinology","how":"Placental hormones create maternal insulin resistance, and fetal insulin drives the growth seen in diabetic pregnancy."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Secretory IgA in milk is the classic example of passive mucosal immunity transferred from mother to infant."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal system","how":"Estrogen loss in hypothalamic amenorrhea raises osteoclast activity and lowers bone density."}],"terms":[{"term":"Tetrahydrofolate (THF)","def":"The fully reduced, active form of folate that carries one-carbon units for nucleotide and amino acid metabolism."},{"term":"Thymidylate synthase","def":"The enzyme that methylates dUMP to dTMP using 5,10-methylene-THF, which it oxidizes to dihydrofolate."},{"term":"Neuropore","def":"An open end of the closing neural tube; the cranial neuropore closes at about day 25 and the caudal neuropore at about day 27 to 28."},{"term":"Ferritin","def":"The intracellular iron storage protein whose serum level reflects body iron stores unless inflammation raises it."},{"term":"Physiologic (dilutional) anemia of pregnancy","def":"A fall in hemoglobin concentration because plasma volume expands more than red cell mass, with normal iron stores."},{"term":"Alpha-lactalbumin","def":"A milk whey protein that binds galactosyltransferase and redirects it to make lactose from glucose."},{"term":"Secretory IgA","def":"Dimeric IgA bound to secretory component that is transported across epithelium into secretions and resists digestion."},{"term":"Human placental lactogen","def":"A placental hormone, also called human chorionic somatomammotropin, that raises maternal insulin resistance as pregnancy advances."},{"term":"Macrosomia","def":"Excessive fetal growth, commonly defined as birth weight above about 4,000 to 4,500 g."},{"term":"Retinoic acid receptor (RAR)","def":"A nuclear receptor that pairs with RXR and, when bound by retinoic acid, controls transcription of patterning genes such as Hox genes."},{"term":"Energy availability","def":"Dietary energy intake minus exercise energy expenditure, the energy left for all other body functions."},{"term":"StAR (steroidogenic acute regulatory protein)","def":"The protein that moves cholesterol from the outer to the inner mitochondrial membrane, the rate-limiting step of steroidogenesis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C01","obj":"RE-9.1","title":"Folate in thymidylate and purine synthesis, and neural tube defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Folate is a water-soluble B vitamin; folic acid is its synthetic, oxidized form. To work, it must be reduced by dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), using NADPH, to tetrahydrofolate (THF). THF carries single carbon units at different oxidation levels (methyl, methylene, formyl), and the cell spends these units on two DNA building blocks: thymidylate and the purine ring. Body folate stores last only a few months, so intake must be continuous."},{"type":"p","text":"Thymidylate synthase transfers a methylene group from 5,10-methylene-THF to dUMP and then reduces it to a methyl group, forming dTMP. The reducing power comes from the folate itself, so the reaction leaves dihydrofolate (DHF), which must be reduced again by DHFR before it can carry another carbon. This makes thymidylate synthesis the step most sensitive to folate supply and explains why DHFR inhibitors (methotrexate in human cells, trimethoprim in bacteria) stop DNA synthesis even when folate is present. In de novo purine synthesis, two transformylase steps take carbons from 10-formyl-THF to build carbons 2 and 8 of the ring on the way to IMP."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The thymidylate folate cycle","steps":["THF picks up a carbon from serine","5,10-methylene-THF donates it to dUMP","dTMP forms; folate is oxidized to DHF","DHFR and NADPH reduce DHF back to THF"],"caption":"Each dTMP made costs one reduction by DHFR, so blocking DHFR or lacking folate halts thymidylate supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Folate also links to vitamin B12. Methionine synthase uses B12 to move the methyl group from 5-methyl-THF to homocysteine, making methionine and freeing THF. Without B12, folate is trapped as 5-methyl-THF (the methyl trap), so both deficiencies impair DNA synthesis and raise homocysteine, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid."},{"type":"p","text":"When dTMP runs short, the dUTP to dTTP ratio rises and DNA polymerase inserts uracil. Repair enzymes excise it repeatedly but cannot fill the gaps without dTTP, so strands break and cells stall in S phase or die. Rapidly dividing tissues suffer first: marrow (megaloblastic anemia, hypersegmented neutrophils) and, in the embryo, the neuroepithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"In week 3 the notochord induces ectoderm to form the neural plate. Its folds rise and fuse first near the future cervical region at about day 22, then zip toward both ends. The cranial neuropore closes at about day 25 and the caudal neuropore at about day 27 to 28, often before a pregnancy is recognized, so folate status at conception is what matters. Failure of cranial closure causes anencephaly, and failure of caudal closure causes spina bifida with meningocele or myelomeningocele. Open defects leak alpha-fetoprotein into amniotic fluid and maternal serum, and amniotic acetylcholinesterase confirms an open lesion. Neural tube defects are multifactorial: folate deficiency, reduced-activity MTHFR variants, folate-antagonist or certain antiepileptic drugs (valproate), maternal pregestational diabetes, and obesity all raise risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Folate forms and what they supply","cols":["Folate form","Carbon unit","Product"],"rows":[["5,10-methylene-THF","Methylene (reduced to methyl in the reaction)","dTMP from dUMP (thymidylate synthase)"],["10-formyl-THF","Formyl","Carbons 2 and 8 of the purine ring"],["5-methyl-THF","Methyl","Methionine from homocysteine (requires B12)"]],"caption":"Shortage of any form slows DNA synthesis; the thymidylate step fails first."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on methotrexate takes high-dose folic acid supplements. Will thymidylate synthesis recover?","explain":"No. Folic acid is the oxidized form and needs DHFR to become THF, and thymidylate synthase turns THF into DHF with every dTMP it makes. With DHFR blocked, neither supplement nor recycled folate can be reduced, so the folate pool collapses into DHF.","check":"The first two paragraphs and the folate cycle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C02","obj":"RE-9.2","title":"Iron requirement of pregnancy and dilutional versus iron deficiency anemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pregnancy needs about 1 g of iron beyond ordinary needs, most of it in the second half. The maternal red cell mass expands, the fetus and placenta build their own iron stores and hemoglobin, and basal losses continue. Dietary iron rarely covers this, so the mother draws on her stores, and many women begin pregnancy with low ones."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate iron cost of a singleton pregnancy","cols":["Use","Iron (about)"],"rows":[["Expansion of maternal red cell mass","450 to 500 mg"],["Fetus and placenta","300 mg"],["Basal losses (gut, skin, urine)","200 mg"],["Total","About 1,000 mg"]],"caption":"Part of the red cell iron returns to stores after delivery, but blood lost at delivery is not recovered."},{"type":"p","text":"The body adapts by raising absorption. Hepcidin, the liver hormone that degrades the iron exporter ferroportin, falls in the second and third trimesters, so enterocytes release more iron into plasma. The placenta takes up maternal transferrin through transferrin receptors on the syncytiotrophoblast and exports iron to fetal transferrin. Transfer runs one way and favors the fetus, so the newborn is rarely anemic, but severe maternal deficiency still reduces fetal iron stores."},{"type":"p","text":"Plasma volume rises by about 40 to 50% because renin, angiotensin, and aldosterone activity increase and sodium and water are retained, while red cell mass rises by only about 20 to 30% under erythropoietin. Hemoglobin concentration therefore falls by dilution, most noticeably in the second trimester, even when iron is adequate. Lower viscosity eases placental perfusion, so this physiologic anemia is an adaptation, not a disease."},{"type":"p","text":"True iron deficiency follows a fixed sequence. Ferritin, which reflects storage iron, falls first. Serum iron and transferrin saturation then fall, and hemoglobin synthesis becomes iron-limited, so new cells come out small and pale, the MCV drops, and the RDW widens as microcytes mix with older normal cells. Two pregnancy effects complicate the labs. Estrogen raises hepatic transferrin synthesis, so TIBC rises in every pregnancy and cannot diagnose deficiency on its own, and MCV normally rises a few femtoliters, so a normal MCV can hide early deficiency. Ferritin is therefore the deciding test, read with care because inflammation raises it as an acute phase reactant."},{"type":"table","title":"Physiologic dilution versus iron deficiency in pregnancy","cols":["Finding","Dilutional anemia","Iron deficiency anemia"],"rows":[["Hemoglobin","Mildly low, rarely below about 10.5 g/dL","Low, often progressive"],["MCV","Normal (about 80 to 100 fL)","Low once deficiency is established"],["Ferritin","Normal","Low (below about 30 ng/mL), the earliest change"],["Transferrin saturation","Normal or mildly low","Low"],["TIBC","Raised (estrogen effect)","Raised further"],["RDW","Normal","High"],["Mechanism","Plasma volume outpaces red cell mass","Iron demand exceeds intake and stores"]],"caption":"Ferritin separates the two; TIBC rises in both, and MCV changes late."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading pregnancy iron studies","text":"At 26 weeks, hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL, MCV 88 fL, ferritin 9 ng/mL, transferrin saturation 10%, and TIBC high. The normal MCV first suggests dilution, but ferritin of 9 ng/mL means stores are empty. MCV has not yet fallen because microcytes are only beginning to enter a circulation of older normocytic cells, and the normal pregnancy rise in MCV masks the change. The high TIBC adds nothing, since estrogen raises it in all pregnancies. The diagnosis is early iron deficiency. If ferritin had been 60 ng/mL with the same hemoglobin and MCV, the picture would fit dilution. A low MCV with normal ferritin would point instead to another cause, such as thalassemia trait."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C03","obj":"RE-9.3","title":"Lactose synthesis and the composition of human milk","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lactose (galactose beta-1,4 glucose) is made in the Golgi lumen of mammary alveolar cells. Glucose enters the cell through GLUT1, and part of it is converted to UDP-glucose and then, by UDP-galactose 4-epimerase, to UDP-galactose, so the gland needs no dietary galactose. Both substrates move into the Golgi."},{"type":"p","text":"Lactose synthase has two subunits. The catalytic one, beta-1,4-galactosyltransferase, normally adds galactose to N-acetylglucosamine on glycoproteins and has very low affinity for free glucose. The regulatory subunit, alpha-lactalbumin, binds the enzyme and lowers its Km for glucose roughly a thousandfold, so the complex joins UDP-galactose to glucose to make lactose. Alpha-lactalbumin is made only in the lactating gland. Prolactin induces it, and high progesterone suppresses it during pregnancy, so copious milk production (lactogenesis II) begins only when progesterone falls after delivery of the placenta."},{"type":"p","text":"Lactose cannot cross the Golgi or secretory vesicle membranes, so it holds water osmotically inside the vesicles. Lactose is therefore the main osmole of milk and sets its volume, so milk stays isosmotic with plasma and lactose concentration changes little as volume rises or falls."},{"type":"table","title":"Mature human milk compared with cow milk (approximate, per dL)","cols":["Component","Human milk","Cow milk"],"rows":[["Lactose","About 7 g","About 4.7 g"],["Total protein","About 0.9 to 1.2 g","About 3.3 g"],["Whey to casein ratio","About 60:40, whey dominant","About 20:80, casein dominant"],["Main whey proteins","Alpha-lactalbumin, lactoferrin, secretory IgA, lysozyme","Beta-lactoglobulin (absent in human milk)"],["Fat","About 3.5 to 4 g","About 3.5 to 4 g"],["Vitamin D","Low","Low unless fortified"],["Vitamin K","Low","Higher than human milk"]],"caption":"Human milk is high in lactose and low in protein, with a whey-dominant protein mix that is easy to digest."},{"type":"p","text":"Colostrum, made in the first days, is richer in protein and secretory IgA and lower in lactose. Secretory IgA comes from plasma cells in the breast that originated in maternal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (the enteromammary pathway), so it targets microbes the mother has met. The polymeric immunoglobulin receptor carries dimeric IgA across the epithelium, and the retained secretory component protects it from proteolysis in the infant gut. There it blocks microbial adherence without activating complement or inflammation. Lactoferrin binds the iron that bacteria need, and lysozyme hydrolyzes bacterial peptidoglycan."},{"type":"p","text":"Milk is poor in two fat-soluble vitamins. Low vitamin D leaves an infant with little sun exposure at risk of rickets. Vitamin K is low in milk, crosses the placenta poorly, and is not yet supplied by gut bacteria, so the newborn has little to gamma-carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S. The result is vitamin K deficiency bleeding, with a prolonged PT and normal platelet count."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C04","obj":"RE-9.4","title":"Placental glucose transfer and the fetus of a hyperglycemic mother","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Glucose is the main fuel of the fetus, which makes little of its own. It crosses the syncytiotrophoblast by facilitated diffusion through GLUT1 on both the maternal-facing microvillous membrane and the fetal-facing basal membrane. The process is carrier-mediated and saturable but needs no energy, so the flux depends on the concentration gradient, and fetal glucose runs at about 70 to 80% of maternal glucose. Maternal insulin, a peptide hormone with no transport route across the placenta, stays on the maternal side."},{"type":"p","text":"As pregnancy advances, human placental lactogen, placental growth hormone, progesterone, and cortisol make the mother progressively insulin resistant. This diverts glucose toward the fetus. A mother whose beta cells cannot raise insulin output enough becomes hyperglycemic (gestational diabetes), and pregestational diabetes produces the same fetal exposure."},{"type":"flow","title":"Maternal hyperglycemia to neonatal hypoglycemia","steps":["Maternal glucose rises; maternal insulin cannot cross","Glucose crosses by GLUT1; fetal glucose rises","Fetal beta cells hypertrophy and oversecrete insulin","Fetal insulin drives fat, glycogen, and organ growth","Cord clamped: glucose supply stops, insulin stays high","Neonatal hypoglycemia in the first hours"],"caption":"Glucose crosses and insulin does not, so the fetus answers maternal hyperglycemia with its own insulin."},{"type":"p","text":"Fetal insulin acts as a growth hormone. It promotes fat deposition, hepatic glycogen, and growth of insulin-sensitive organs, producing macrosomia with hepatomegaly and cardiomegaly that can include interventricular septal hypertrophy. The brain is not insulin dependent, so the head grows normally while the shoulders and trunk enlarge, which explains the risk of shoulder dystocia. Hyperinsulinemia raises fetal metabolic rate and oxygen consumption, and the relative hypoxemia stimulates erythropoietin, causing polycythemia and later hyperbilirubinemia. Insulin also antagonizes cortisol's induction of surfactant synthesis in type II pneumocytes, so respiratory distress is more likely at a given gestational age."},{"type":"p","text":"At birth the maternal glucose supply ends abruptly, but the hyperplastic beta cells keep secreting insulin, which suppresses glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, so glucose falls within hours. Timing distinguishes the other effects of diabetic pregnancy. Hyperglycemia during organogenesis in the first trimester causes structural malformations such as neural tube defects, conotruncal heart defects, and caudal regression, while hyperglycemia in the second and third trimesters causes macrosomia. Maternal vascular disease can instead reduce placental perfusion and cause growth restriction."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A mother has high plasma insulin but normal glucose because of insulin resistance. Will her fetus be hyperinsulinemic?","explain":"No. Maternal insulin does not cross the placenta, and fetal beta cells respond only to fetal glucose, which follows maternal glucose. Normal maternal glucose means normal fetal insulin.","check":"The first paragraph and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C05","obj":"RE-9.5","title":"Zinc deficiency and vitamin A excess in sexual and embryonic development","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Zinc is a cofactor for hundreds of enzymes, including DNA and RNA polymerases, alkaline phosphatase, carbonic anhydrase, and superoxide dismutase, and it forms the zinc finger DNA-binding domains of many transcription factors. Each nuclear steroid receptor, including the androgen and estrogen receptors, binds DNA through two zinc fingers. Because zinc is needed for cell division, protein synthesis, and hormone signaling, deficiency hits tissues that grow fast or depend on steroid action."},{"type":"p","text":"Zinc deficiency causes hypogonadism with low testosterone, impaired spermatogenesis, and delayed sexual maturation, together with growth failure. The classic descriptions came from adolescent boys in the Middle East whose diets were high in phytate, which binds zinc and blocks its absorption. Zinc is required for Leydig cell steroidogenesis and for the germinal epithelium, and the testis and prostate hold some of the highest zinc concentrations in the body. Other features follow from the same roles: poor wound healing, hypogeusia (reduced taste), alopecia, impaired cell-mediated immunity, diarrhea, and a periorificial and acral dermatitis. The inherited form, acrodermatitis enteropathica, is an autosomal recessive defect in the intestinal zinc transporter ZIP4 (SLC39A4). Acquired causes include malabsorption, chronic diarrhea, alcoholism, and cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin A is the opposite problem: harm from excess. Retinol is oxidized to retinal and then, irreversibly, to retinoic acid, the form that acts as a morphogen. All-trans retinoic acid binds the retinoic acid receptor (RAR), which pairs with the retinoid X receptor (RXR) on retinoic acid response elements and controls genes such as the Hox genes that specify position along the body axis. Embryos keep retinoic acid in precise gradients by making it in some tissues and degrading it with CYP26 in others. Excess preformed vitamin A or a retinoid drug such as isotretinoin (13-cis retinoic acid) floods those gradients during organogenesis, roughly weeks 3 to 8, and misdirects the hindbrain and the cranial neural crest cells that migrate into the pharyngeal arches and the cardiac outflow tract."},{"type":"table","title":"Zinc deficiency compared with vitamin A excess","cols":["Feature","Zinc deficiency","Vitamin A or retinoid excess"],"rows":[["Molecular basis","Loss of zinc enzymes and zinc finger proteins","Overactivation of RAR/RXR and Hox signaling"],["Main target","Gonads, skin, gut, growth","Cranial neural crest and hindbrain during organogenesis"],["Reproductive or developmental result","Hypogonadism, low sperm count, delayed puberty","Craniofacial, cardiac, thymic, and CNS defects"],["Other signs","Dermatitis, alopecia, hypogeusia, poor healing","Spontaneous abortion"],["Classic setting","Phytate-rich diet, malabsorption, ZIP4 mutation","Isotretinoin or high-dose preformed vitamin A in early pregnancy"]],"caption":"Zinc deficiency removes function; retinoid excess adds signaling at the wrong time and place."},{"type":"p","text":"Retinoid embryopathy therefore has a recognizable pattern. Craniofacial defects include small, absent, or low-set ears, micrognathia, and cleft palate. Cardiac defects are conotruncal, such as tetralogy of Fallot, transposition of the great arteries, and interrupted aortic arch. Thymic hypoplasia reflects the third pharyngeal pouch, and CNS defects include hydrocephalus, microcephaly, and cerebellar malformation. Beta-carotene does not cause this, because its cleavage to retinal is regulated by vitamin A status."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C06","obj":"RE-9.6","title":"Low energy availability, leptin, and hypothalamic amenorrhea","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Reproduction is energetically expensive, and the hypothalamus permits it only when fuel is sufficient. Energy availability is dietary intake minus exercise energy expenditure, the energy left over for every other body function. When it falls too low, whether from intense training, restricted eating, an eating disorder, or very low body fat, the reproductive axis is switched off before other systems are threatened."},{"type":"p","text":"Leptin is the main signal. Adipocytes secrete it in proportion to fat mass, and it also drops quickly with an acute energy deficit. GnRH neurons lack leptin receptors, so leptin acts on upstream hypothalamic neurons, largely in the arcuate nucleus, that in turn regulate the kisspeptin neurons driving GnRH release. Low leptin, together with high ghrelin, raised cortisol (CRH inhibits GnRH), low insulin and IGF-1, and low T3, slows GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude. People with congenital leptin deficiency have hypogonadotropic hypogonadism that leptin replacement reverses, which shows the signal is necessary."},{"type":"flow","title":"Low energy availability to bone loss","steps":["Energy intake falls below expenditure","Leptin falls; cortisol rises; insulin and IGF-1 fall","Kisspeptin drive drops; GnRH pulses slow","LH and FSH pulses fade; no dominant follicle","Low estradiol, anovulation, amenorrhea","Bone resorption rises and formation falls","Low bone density and stress fractures"],"caption":"The reproductive axis shuts down to save energy, and the bone loss is the cost of low estrogen."},{"type":"p","text":"With slow GnRH pulses, LH pulses are too infrequent to support follicle growth, so no dominant follicle forms, estradiol stays low, and ovulation and menses stop. This is functional hypothalamic amenorrhea: a hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with low or inappropriately normal LH and FSH, low estradiol, and normal prolactin. The endometrium is thin, so a progestin challenge produces little or no withdrawal bleeding. Men show the same suppression as low testosterone."},{"type":"p","text":"Estrogen normally restrains bone resorption. It lowers osteoblast and stromal cell production of RANKL, raises osteoprotegerin, and shortens osteoclast lifespan. Without it, resorption rises, and low IGF-1 and insulin reduce bone formation at the same time, so bone balance turns negative from both sides. In adolescents this also prevents reaching peak bone mass, a deficit that may not fully recover. The combination of low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, and low bone density is the female athlete triad, now viewed as part of relative energy deficiency in sport (RED-S)."},{"type":"table","title":"Labs that place amenorrhea in the axis","cols":["Cause","LH and FSH","Estradiol","Other clue"],"rows":[["Hypothalamic (low energy availability)","Low or normal","Low","Low leptin, normal prolactin, low BMI or heavy training"],["Hyperprolactinemia","Low or normal","Low","High prolactin"],["Primary ovarian insufficiency","High, FSH especially","Low","Ovary fails to respond"],["Polycystic ovary syndrome","LH often high relative to FSH","Normal","Androgen excess"]],"caption":"Low gonadotropins with low estradiol place the problem above the ovary."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-REPR-09-01-C07","obj":"RE-9.7","title":"Cholesterol supply and the StAR rate-limiting step in gonadal steroidogenesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Steroid-producing cells store very little hormone, so output depends on how fast cholesterol reaches the first enzyme. Gonads and placenta can make cholesterol from acetyl-CoA through HMG-CoA reductase, but in humans most of their substrate comes from circulating LDL. LDL binds the LDL receptor through apolipoprotein B-100, enters by clathrin-mediated endocytosis, and is delivered to lysosomes, where acid lipase frees cholesterol. HDL cholesterol taken up through scavenger receptor B1 contributes a smaller share. Cholesterol not used at once is stored as cholesteryl ester in lipid droplets and released by a hormone-sensitive cholesteryl ester hydrolase."},{"type":"flow","title":"LDL cholesterol to pregnenolone in a Leydig or theca cell","steps":["LH binds its Gs-coupled receptor; cAMP and PKA rise","LDL receptors import LDL; lysosomes free cholesterol","Stored cholesteryl esters are hydrolyzed","Cholesterol reaches the outer mitochondrial membrane","StAR moves it to the inner membrane (rate-limiting)","CYP11A1 cleaves the side chain to pregnenolone"],"caption":"LH speeds both cholesterol supply and its delivery into mitochondria."},{"type":"p","text":"The side-chain cleavage enzyme CYP11A1 sits on the inner mitochondrial membrane, but cholesterol is poorly soluble and moves slowly across the aqueous space between the membranes. StAR moves it from the outer to the inner membrane, and this transfer, not the enzyme reaction, sets the overall rate. LH, acting through cAMP and protein kinase A, increases StAR within minutes by phosphorylating it and by inducing new StAR synthesis, which is why the acute steroid response depends on ongoing protein synthesis. Over longer periods LH also raises LDL receptor and CYP11A1 expression."},{"type":"table","title":"Where steroidogenic cells get cholesterol","cols":["Cell","Main cholesterol source","Main product"],"rows":[["Leydig cell","LDL plus de novo synthesis; LH raises uptake","Testosterone"],["Theca cell","LDL from its blood supply; LH-driven","Androstenedione"],["Granulosa cell before ovulation","Little LDL (avascular, inside the basement membrane)","Estradiol from theca androgen"],["Corpus luteum","Mainly LDL, available once vessels invade","Progesterone and estradiol"],["Placenta","Maternal LDL","Progesterone; estrogens only from fetal (and maternal) DHEA-S"]],"caption":"Access to blood-borne LDL explains why granulosa cells make large amounts of progesterone only after luteinization."},{"type":"p","text":"The placenta is an incomplete steroid organ. It takes up maternal LDL and converts cholesterol to pregnenolone and progesterone, using the related protein MLN64 rather than StAR, but lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP17). It cannot make androgens and must use DHEA-S from the fetal adrenal, 16-hydroxylated in the fetal liver, to make estriol, so maternal estriol reflects the fetal adrenal and liver as well as the placenta."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of StAR causes congenital lipoid adrenal hyperplasia. Cholesterol accumulates in lipid droplets and damages adrenal and gonadal cells, so almost no steroids are made: adrenal insufficiency in all patients and female external genitalia in 46,XY individuals. Loss of LDL receptors in familial hypercholesterolemia, by contrast, largely spares steroid output because de novo synthesis and HDL uptake compensate."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does StAR deficiency nearly abolish steroid synthesis while LDL receptor deficiency barely affects it?","explain":"StAR controls the single route of cholesterol into the inner mitochondrial membrane, the only place CYP11A1 can act. LDL receptors are one of several supply routes; de novo synthesis and HDL can replace them.","check":"The StAR paragraph and the final paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each nutrient in this chapter acts through a mechanism taught in another discipline. Folate and iron are biochemistry and hematology applied to pregnancy. Folate and retinoic acid act at defined windows of embryology, neurulation in weeks 3 and 4 and neural crest migration during organogenesis, so the defects they cause are predictable from timing. Glucose transfer and fetal insulin connect placental physiology to endocrine pathology, and secretory IgA in milk links lactation to mucosal immunology."},{"type":"p","text":"Energy and cholesterol show how supply controls hormone output. The hypothalamus reads leptin and other metabolic signals before allowing GnRH pulses, and steroidogenic cells depend on StAR to move cholesterol where CYP11A1 can act. Limits apply: neural tube defects are multifactorial and not all are folate related, ferritin can be falsely normal with inflammation, and reference ranges for hemoglobin, ferritin, and milk composition differ among sources, so values here are approximate."}],"summary":["Folate, reduced by DHFR to THF, supplies the one-carbon unit for dTMP and carbons 2 and 8 of purines; thymidylate synthase oxidizes folate to DHF each time it acts.","Thymidylate shortage causes uracil misincorporation and strand breaks in dividing cells, and the neural tube must close by about day 28, so folate must be adequate at conception.","Pregnancy needs about 1 g of extra iron; plasma volume rises about 40 to 50% and red cell mass about 20 to 30%, causing dilutional anemia.","Ferritin separates iron deficiency (low) from dilution (normal); TIBC rises in all pregnancies, and MCV falls only after stores are gone.","Lactose synthase is galactosyltransferase redirected by alpha-lactalbumin; lactose is the osmole that sets milk volume.","Human milk is high in lactose, low in protein, whey dominant, and rich in secretory IgA, lactoferrin, and lysozyme, but low in vitamins D and K.","Glucose crosses the placenta by GLUT1 and insulin does not, so maternal hyperglycemia causes fetal hyperinsulinemia, macrosomia, polycythemia, delayed surfactant, and neonatal hypoglycemia.","Zinc deficiency causes hypogonadism and delayed puberty; excess retinoids disrupt RAR and Hox signaling and cranial neural crest, causing craniofacial, conotruncal, thymic, and CNS defects.","Low energy availability and low leptin slow GnRH pulses, producing hypothalamic amenorrhea with low estrogen and bone loss.","Gonads and placenta take cholesterol mainly from LDL; StAR transfer into the inner mitochondrial membrane is the rate-limiting step, and LH accelerates it through cAMP."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Folic acid and one-carbon metabolism; nucleotide metabolism; cholesterol and steroid metabolism"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pregnancy and Lactation; Female Physiology Before Pregnancy and Female Hormones"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Fertilization, pregnancy, and lactation; the female reproductive system"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third to eighth weeks: the embryonic period; birth defects and prenatal diagnosis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases; Diseases of Infancy and Childhood"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Female Reproductive Tract; Disorders of the Endocrine Pancreas"}]}});
